window.FK={"video":"AmUuXtVWZb4","duration":31134,"title":"Frankenstein","ann":[{"id":1,"sec":"Letter 1","p":[1,4],"start":14.6,"t":203.3,"from":"You will rejoice to hear that no disaster has …","to":"… effected by an undertaking such as mine.","q":"an enterprise which you have regarded with such evil forebodings","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Opens w/ danger warning: sister fears what he chases","b":"The very first sentence warns that ambition may end in disaster. Walton's 'enterprise' mirrors Victor's later 'undertaking'; both men ignore family worry.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Epistolary novel: told through letters. The dated header ('St. Petersburgh, Dec. 11th, 17—') hides the year with a dash, a period convention that made fiction feel like a real, discreet document."},{"id":2,"sec":"Letter 1","p":[5,6],"start":203.7,"t":307.4,"from":"These reflections have dispelled the agitation with which I …","to":"… into the channel of their earlier bent.","q":"a point on which the soul may fix its intellectual eye","tag":"Character","m":"Walton = obsessed w/ one goal, like Victor","b":"Walton finds peace only in a 'steady purpose'. This single-minded focus is the exact trait that ruins Victor, so Walton is set up as Victor's double.","lt":"Literature","l":"He wanted to be a poet 'where Homer and Shakespeare are consecrated' and failed. Mary Shelley lived among Romantic poets (Percy Shelley, Lord Byron), who prized genius and glory."},{"id":3,"sec":"Letter 1","p":[7,8],"start":307.8,"t":415.4,"from":"Six years have passed since I resolved on my …","to":"… sustain my own, when theirs are failing.","q":"I voluntarily endured cold, famine, thirst, and want of sleep","tag":"Character","m":"Self-discipline → isolation; trains alone","b":"Walton trains his body and studies at night, alone. Victor will also work through sleepless nights; isolation plus ambition is the novel's danger formula.","lt":"History","l":"Real Arctic explorers of the era were searching for the Northwest Passage, a sea route over North America. Expeditions like Franklin's (1845) ended in disaster, much as Margaret fears."},{"id":4,"sec":"Letter 1","p":[9,13],"start":415.8,"t":514.6,"from":"This is the most favourable period for travelling in …","to":"R. Walton","q":"I have no ambition to lose my life","tag":"Irony","m":"Irony: says he's careful, yet risks all","b":"Walton insists he is not reckless, yet he heads into the unknown 'emergencies' of the pole. Readers learn to doubt narrators who reassure too much.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Note the semicolons and dashes ('furs—a dress which…'). Shelley uses the dash to add an explanation mid-sentence, the way we'd use parentheses today."},{"id":5,"sec":"Letter 2","p":[14,17],"start":515.0,"t":665.7,"from":"How slowly the time passes here, encompassed as I …","to":"… me to endeavour to regulate my mind.","q":"I have no friend, Margaret","tag":"Theme","m":"THEME: loneliness / need for a companion","b":"Walton's longing for a friend who will 'sympathise' with him previews the Creature's demand for a companion. Every major character in the book craves connection.","lt":"Psych","l":"Psychologists call this the need to belong (Baumeister & Leary, 1995): humans need close bonds the way they need food. Loneliness raises health risks as much as smoking."},{"id":6,"sec":"Letter 2","p":[18,19],"start":666.1,"t":878.1,"from":"Well, these are useless complaints; I shall certainly find …","to":"… and sympathy which otherwise he would command.","q":"he is madly desirous of glory","tag":"Character","m":"Lieutenant = glory-seeker; Walton too","b":"Walton describes his lieutenant as hungry for glory but quickly corrects himself; the description fits Walton better. The master is kind but 'uneducated', showing Walton's class bias.","lt":"Language","l":"'To word my phrase more characteristically' is self-correction (epanorthosis), a rhetorical device where a speaker revises their words mid-sentence for precision."},{"id":7,"sec":"Letter 2","p":[20,21],"start":878.5,"t":997.1,"from":"Yet do not suppose, because I complain a little …","to":"… unvisited regions I am about to explore.","q":"a love for the marvellous, a belief in the marvellous","tag":"Theme","m":"Romantic spirit: science + wonder","b":"Walton admits his practical science is mixed with a love of the 'marvellous'. Victor's science will be driven by the same mix of reason and fantasy (alchemy).","lt":"Literature","l":"He mentions Coleridge's 'Ancient Mariner' here: an albatross-killing sailor cursed at sea. Shelley actually heard Coleridge recite it as a child, and it previews a cursed voyage."},{"id":8,"sec":"Letter 2","p":[22,23],"start":997.5,"t":1041.6,"from":"But to return to dearer considerations. Shall I meet …","to":"Your affectionate brother, Robert Walton","q":"Remember me with affection, should you never hear from me again","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Possible death hinted = suspense","b":"The letter closes on the chance he may die. Shelley builds suspense before the main story starts; the letters are a 'frame' around Victor's tale.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'Should you never hear' is an inverted conditional (= 'if you should never hear'). It's formal; modern English still uses it: 'Should you need help, call me.'"},{"id":9,"sec":"Letter 3","p":[24,27],"start":1042.0,"t":1102.1,"from":"I write a few lines in haste to say …","to":"… renovating warmth which I had not expected.","q":"floating sheets of ice that continually pass us","tag":"Setting","m":"ICE = danger, isolation, being 'frozen'","b":"Ice will frame the whole novel: it opens and closes in the Arctic, and Victor confronts the Creature on a glacier. Ice suggests emotional coldness and isolation.","lt":"Science","l":"Polar sea ice moves with currents and wind; ships could be 'beset' (trapped) for months. The Open Polar Sea that Walton hopes for was a real but false 1800s theory."},{"id":10,"sec":"Letter 3","p":[28,32],"start":1102.5,"t":1176.8,"from":"No incidents have hitherto befallen us that would make …","to":"R.W.","q":"What can stop the determined heart and resolved will of man?","tag":"Theme","m":"Hubris: man vs. nature (rhetorical Q)","b":"Walton's rhetorical question shows hubris, the excessive pride that tragic heroes have. Nature (the ice) will answer him by trapping his ship.","lt":"Grammar","l":"A rhetorical question is asked for effect, not an answer. Notice the chain 'Wherefore not? … Why not …? What can stop …?' Repeating questions builds emotional momentum."},{"id":11,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[33,37],"start":1177.2,"t":1269.5,"from":"So strange an accident has happened to us that …","to":"… among the distant inequalities of the ice.","q":"a being which had the shape of a man, but apparently of gigantic stature","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"1st sighting of the Creature (not named)","b":"Our first glimpse of the Creature comes before we know anything about him: a giant crossing the ice. Shelley starts at the end of the story and works back.","lt":"Language","l":"'In medias res' means starting in the middle of the action. Many films do this too, opening on a dramatic moment and then flashing back."},{"id":12,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[38,40],"start":1269.9,"t":1365.6,"from":"This appearance excited our unqualified wonder. We were, as …","to":"… you to perish on the open sea.”","q":"He was not, as the other traveller seemed to be, a savage inhabitant","tag":"Character","m":"Contrast: 'savage' giant vs. 'European'","b":"The crew judges the two travelers instantly by appearance. The Creature is assumed 'savage', and the man is trusted because he looks European. This prejudice of appearance is the novel's core injustice.","lt":"History","l":"Words like 'savage' reflect 18th-century colonial attitudes that ranked people by origin. The book will question who is truly 'savage'."},{"id":13,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[41,43],"start":1366.0,"t":1457.8,"from":"On perceiving me, the stranger addressed me in English, …","to":"… a little soup, which restored him wonderfully.","q":"will you have the kindness to inform me whither you are bound?","tag":"Character","m":"Victor won't board unless ship goes north = obsession","b":"Nearly dead, the stranger still cares only about where the ship is headed. His obsession outweighs his survival instinct, the first sign of how consumed Victor is.","lt":"Language","l":"'Whither' = to where; 'whence' = from where; 'thither' = to there. These archaic words survive in phrases like 'whither thou goest'."},{"id":14,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[44,45],"start":1458.2,"t":1533.7,"from":"Two days passed in this manner before he was …","to":"… the ice in so strange a vehicle.","q":"I never saw a more interesting creature","tag":"Language","m":"Walton calls VICTOR a 'creature'","b":"Walton calls Victor a 'creature' long before we meet Victor's Creature. The shared word blurs the line between creator and creation.","lt":"Language","l":"'Creature' comes from Latin creatura, 'a thing created'. Shelley plays on the root: everyone is someone's creature."},{"id":15,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[46,52],"start":1534.1,"t":1596.8,"from":"His countenance instantly assumed an aspect of the deepest …","to":"… you have benevolently restored me to life.”","q":"the dæmon, as he called him","tag":"Symbol","m":"'dæmon' = Victor's label for the Creature","b":"Victor names his pursuer a 'dæmon' (demon), judging him before we hear his side. Watch which names Victor uses (fiend, wretch, devil) and how they shape our view.","lt":"Language","l":"Greek 'daimon' meant a spirit or inner guiding power, not necessarily evil. The 'æ' is a ligature (a and e joined), an older spelling."},{"id":16,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[53,54],"start":1597.2,"t":1643.8,"from":"Soon after this he inquired if I thought that …","to":"… any new object should appear in sight.","q":"a new spirit of life animated the decaying frame of the stranger","tag":"Theme","m":"Revenge gives Victor life (dark irony)","b":"Victor revives only when he hears his enemy may still live. Revenge has become his reason to exist. 'Animated' echoes how he once gave life to dead matter.","lt":"Psych","l":"Obsessive revenge can become someone's whole identity. Psychologists note that rumination on revenge prolongs pain instead of relieving it."},{"id":17,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[55,56],"start":1644.2,"t":1702.3,"from":"Such is my journal of what relates to this …","to":"… possessed as the brother of my heart.","q":"I begin to love him as a brother","tag":"Theme","m":"Walton finally finds the friend he wanted","b":"Walton's wish from Letter 2 comes true, but with a broken man. Friendship and brotherhood are central to the novel: Clerval and Victor, and the Creature's longing for a companion.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Aristotle described the highest friendship as one built on shared virtue, not usefulness or pleasure. Walton and Victor bond over shared ambition. Is that virtue?"},{"id":18,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[57,60],"start":1702.7,"t":1858.6,"from":"I shall continue my journal concerning the stranger at …","to":"… will dash the cup from your lips!”","q":"Have you drunk also of the intoxicating draught?","tag":"Symbol","m":"Knowledge = a drug/poison (metaphor)","b":"Victor compares Walton's thirst for knowledge to a dangerous drink. The 'cup' metaphor warns that ambition intoxicates and blinds.","lt":"Literature","l":"This echoes Genesis: eating the fruit of knowledge brings the Fall. The subtitle 'The Modern Prometheus' refers to the Titan who stole fire (forbidden knowledge) for humans and was punished."},{"id":19,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[61,63],"start":1859.0,"t":1956.1,"from":"Such words, you may imagine, strongly excited my curiosity; …","to":"… lost everything and cannot begin life anew.”","q":"I have lost everything and cannot begin life anew","tag":"Character","m":"Victor = tragic hero, beyond hope","b":"Victor presents himself as someone already ruined, which makes us read his story as a tragedy whose ending is known. His 'friend, the most noble' is Clerval.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Greek tragedy the hero falls through a flaw (hamartia). Victor's flaw is his unchecked ambition. Keep a list of moments where he could have chosen differently."},{"id":20,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[64,66],"start":1956.5,"t":2034.8,"from":"As he said this his countenance became expressive of …","to":"… voice whose varied intonations are soul-subduing music.","q":"Such a man has a double existence","tag":"Theme","m":"'Double' = theme of doubles/doppelgangers","b":"Victor's 'double existence', suffering yet uplifted by nature, introduces the idea of doubles. Walton/Victor and Victor/Creature mirror one another.","lt":"Science","l":"Romantics believed nature could heal the mind. Modern research supports this: time in nature lowers stress hormones (cortisol) and improves mood."},{"id":21,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[67,68],"start":2035.2,"t":2156.9,"from":"Yesterday the stranger said to me, “You may easily …","to":"… the events of which it is composed.”","q":"may not be a serpent to sting you, as mine has been","tag":"Symbol","m":"Serpent = Eden; knowledge brings the Fall","b":"Victor warns that seeking knowledge stung him like a serpent. This tells us his tale is a cautionary one, told to change Walton's course.","lt":"Literature","l":"The serpent tempts Eve in Genesis. Shelley's epigraph is from Milton's 'Paradise Lost', which the Creature will later read and compare himself to."},{"id":22,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[69,70],"start":2157.3,"t":2214.2,"from":"You may easily imagine that I was much gratified …","to":"… will perceive how irrevocably it is determined.”","q":"how irrevocably it is determined","tag":"Theme","m":"Fate vs. free will: Victor blames destiny","b":"Victor claims his fate is fixed. As you read, ask whether events were really destined or whether Victor chose them. He often blames 'destiny' for his own decisions.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Determinism holds that every event is caused by prior events; free will holds that people choose. Victor uses fate as an excuse, a habit psychologists call an external locus of control."},{"id":23,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[71,71],"start":2214.6,"t":2283.8,"from":"He then told me that he would commence his …","to":"… vessel on its course and wrecked it—thus!","q":"frightful the storm which embraced the gallant vessel on its course and wrecked it","tag":"Narration","m":"FRAME ends → Victor's story begins (nested)","b":"Walton will write down Victor's words, and later Victor will relate the Creature's words. The novel is a set of nested stories, each with a narrator we must judge for reliability.","lt":"Grammar","l":"The sentence ends with a dash and 'thus!' The punctuation acts out the shipwreck, breaking the sentence the way the storm breaks the ship."},{"id":24,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[72,73],"start":2284.2,"t":2397.0,"from":"I am by birth a Genevese, and my family …","to":"… world again through his credit and assistance.","q":"I am by birth a Genevese","tag":"Narration","m":"Victor's narration begins: wealthy, respected family","b":"Victor stresses his family's honor and status. He had every advantage, so his downfall can't be blamed on a bad start, unlike the Creature's.","lt":"History","l":"Geneva was an independent Protestant republic run by elected councils ('syndics'). Mary Shelley wrote the novel near Geneva in 1816 at Lake Geneva with Byron."},{"id":25,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[74,75],"start":2397.4,"t":2480.3,"from":"Beaufort had taken effectual measures to conceal himself, and …","to":"… a pittance scarcely sufficient to support life.","q":"her courage rose to support her in her adversity","tag":"Character","m":"Caroline = strong, caring woman (ideal)","b":"Caroline Beaufort works with her hands to support her dying father. Shelley's women are often self-sacrificing caregivers; notice how passive most of them become later.","lt":"History","l":"'Plaited straw' (braiding straw for hats) was common low-paid work for poor women. Mary Shelley's mother, Mary Wollstonecraft, argued women needed education and economic independence."},{"id":26,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[76,77],"start":2480.7,"t":2626.4,"from":"Several months passed in this manner. Her father grew …","to":"… as a restorative for her weakened frame.","q":"He came like a protecting spirit to the poor girl","tag":"Theme","m":"Rescue/protection pattern (father figure)","b":"Alphonse rescues Caroline, and she in turn rescues Elizabeth. The family repeatedly adopts the helpless. That makes Victor's abandonment of his own 'child' even more shocking.","lt":"Language","l":"'Like a protecting spirit' is a simile (comparison using like/as). Note the big age gap; such marriages were common in that era."},{"id":27,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[78,79],"start":2626.8,"t":2828.3,"from":"From Italy they visited Germany and France. I, their …","to":"… a celestial stamp in all her features.","q":"I was their plaything and their idol","tag":"Theme","m":"Parents' duty to child → contrast w/ Creature","b":"Victor says his parents knew they owed duties 'towards the being to which they had given life'. Hold onto this; he will completely fail that duty toward the being HE gives life.","lt":"Psych","l":"Attachment theory (John Bowlby) says children who receive steady affection develop secure bonds. The Creature becomes a case study of what happens without it."},{"id":28,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[80,81],"start":2828.7,"t":2951.2,"from":"The peasant woman, perceiving that my mother fixed eyes …","to":"… of all my occupations and my pleasures.","q":"Elizabeth Lavenza became the inmate of my parents","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth adopted (1831 ed.) = 'gift'","b":"Elizabeth is chosen for her beauty and taken in. In the 1831 edition she is adopted; in the 1818 edition she is Victor's cousin. Note that she is valued for looks, while the Creature is rejected for his.","lt":"Language","l":"'Inmate' here simply means 'one who lives in a house'. Today it means a prisoner. This is semantic shift: words narrow or change meaning over time."},{"id":29,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[82,82],"start":2951.6,"t":3023.1,"from":"Everyone loved Elizabeth. The passionate and almost reverential attachment …","to":"… death she was to be mine only.","q":"looked upon Elizabeth as mine","tag":"Theme","m":"Elizabeth = possession ('mine') → objectified","b":"Victor treats Elizabeth as a 'possession of my own'. This possessive love foreshadows his self-centered thinking, and 'till death' is grim foreshadowing.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Feminist critics point out that the women in Frankenstein are passive and often objectified. Mary Wollstonecraft (Shelley's mother) argued women were raised to be ornaments rather than equals."},{"id":30,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[83,84],"start":3023.5,"t":3195.2,"from":"We were brought up together; there was not quite …","to":"… sepulchre from the hands of the infidels.","q":"more deeply smitten with the thirst for knowledge","tag":"Character","m":"Victor = 'thirst for knowledge' (key trait)","b":"Elizabeth admires nature's beauty while Victor wants to know its causes. Their contrast sets art and feeling against analysis and control.","lt":"Language","l":"'Thirst for knowledge' is a metaphor that treats knowledge as something to drink. Compare the 'intoxicating draught' in Letter 4; Shelley keeps returning to the image."},{"id":31,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[85,86],"start":3195.6,"t":3275.4,"from":"No human being could have passed a happier childhood …","to":"… sense, the physical secrets of the world.","q":"the physical secrets of the world","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor wants nature's SECRETS = power","b":"Victor admits a 'violent' temper and says he was drawn to the 'secrets of heaven and earth', not languages or politics. Secrets and hidden knowledge drive him.","lt":"Language","l":"'Metaphysical' means beyond the physical (soul, being, reality); 'physical' means the material world. Victor uses both, blurring science and philosophy."},{"id":32,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[87,88],"start":3275.8,"t":3408.4,"from":"Meanwhile Clerval occupied himself, so to speak, with the …","to":"… swept away all my hopes and joys.","q":"it became the torrent which, in its course, has swept away all my hopes and joys","tag":"Symbol","m":"Metaphor: passion = river → torrent","b":"Clerval cares about people and morals; Victor cares about secrets. Victor's extended metaphor of a stream that grows into a destructive torrent shows how small obsessions grow.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Extended metaphor: one comparison developed across a whole sentence (source → river → torrent → swept away). Try spotting the stages and labelling them in the margin."},{"id":33,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[89,90],"start":3408.8,"t":3523.9,"from":"Natural philosophy is the genius that has regulated my …","to":"… continued to read with the greatest avidity.","q":"Cornelius Agrippa","tag":"Allusion","m":"Agrippa = alchemy; father's dismissal = turning point","b":"Victor blames his father for calling Agrippa 'sad trash' without explaining why. He shifts blame onto others, a pattern you'll keep seeing.","lt":"History","l":"Cornelius Agrippa (1486–1535) was a real German occultist. Alchemists sought the philosopher's stone and the elixir of life, quests that resemble Victor's goal."},{"id":34,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[91,92],"start":3524.3,"t":3627.6,"from":"When I returned home my first care was to …","to":"… and rashly and ignorantly I had repined.","q":"Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus","tag":"Allusion","m":"Old 'magic' science vs. modern science","b":"Victor prefers the grand promises of alchemy to modern science's slow, modest progress. He wants power, not just understanding.","lt":"Science","l":"Newton said he was like a boy picking up pebbles 'whilst the great ocean of truth lay all undiscovered before me'. Real science is humble and incremental; Victor wants shortcuts."},{"id":35,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[93,94],"start":3628.0,"t":3745.6,"from":"But here were books, and here were men who …","to":"… again changed the current of my ideas.","q":"the elixir of life","tag":"Theme","m":"Goal: banish disease / make man invulnerable","b":"Victor seeks the elixir of life and the glory it would bring. Even as a teen his goal mixes helping humanity with personal fame.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Today, anti-aging and life-extension research is a billion-dollar industry. The ethical question, who benefits and at what cost, is the same one Victor ignores."},{"id":36,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[95,96],"start":3746.0,"t":3899.1,"from":"When I was about fifteen years old we had …","to":"… foundations, and so worthy of my consideration.","q":"I beheld a stream of fire issue from an old and beautiful oak","tag":"Symbol","m":"LIGHTNING = power of nature / spark of life","b":"Lightning destroys the oak, showing nature's terrifying power. Electricity becomes Victor's new fascination and hints at how he'll 'spark' life.","lt":"Science","l":"Galvanism: in the 1780s Luigi Galvani made dead frog legs twitch with electricity; in 1803 his nephew Aldini 'animated' a hanged man's corpse in London. Shelley knew of these experiments."},{"id":37,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[97,98],"start":3899.5,"t":3966.4,"from":"Thus strangely are our souls constructed, and by such …","to":"… had decreed my utter and terrible destruction.","q":"Destiny was too potent, and her immutable laws had decreed my utter and terrible destruction","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor blames DESTINY (personified) again","b":"Victor nearly turns to safe mathematics but claims destiny overruled him. He keeps framing his choices as fate, which takes away his responsibility.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Personification: Destiny is 'her' with 'laws'. Writers personify abstract forces to make them feel like characters. Here it's an excuse."},{"id":38,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[99,100],"start":3966.8,"t":4096.3,"from":"When I had attained the age of seventeen my …","to":"… hope of meeting you in another world.”","q":"the first misfortune of my life occurred","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Caroline dies of scarlet fever: an 'omen'","b":"Caroline catches scarlet fever while nursing Elizabeth and dies. Her dying wish is for Victor and Elizabeth to marry. Victor's grief over death may feed his wish to conquer it.","lt":"Science","l":"Scarlet fever (a streptococcal infection) killed many children before antibiotics. Today penicillin cures it in days. Mary Shelley herself lost babies and her mother to illness."},{"id":39,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[101,102],"start":4096.7,"t":4218.7,"from":"She died calmly, and her countenance expressed affection even …","to":"… my sweet Elizabeth in some degree consoled.","q":"the void that presents itself to the soul","tag":"Theme","m":"Death & grief: motivation to defeat death?","b":"Victor describes grief's emptiness. This loss is a turning point: soon after, he goes off to study how life and death work.","lt":"Psych","l":"The Kübler-Ross model describes grief stages (denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance). Some readers see Victor's project as extreme 'bargaining' with death."},{"id":40,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[103,104],"start":4219.1,"t":4288.7,"from":"She indeed veiled her grief and strove to act …","to":"… chained to the miserable details of commerce.","q":"not to be chained to the miserable details of commerce","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval wants education, held back by father","b":"Elizabeth hides her grief to comfort others. Clerval is kept from university by his merchant father. Note who gets education: Victor easily, Clerval with difficulty, Elizabeth never.","lt":"History","l":"In the 1700s only wealthy men attended university. 'Liberal education' meant broad learning (languages, history, philosophy) fit for a free ('liber') man."},{"id":41,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[105,106],"start":4289.1,"t":4397.4,"from":"We sat late. We could not tear ourselves away …","to":"… would, indeed, have been folly to repent.","q":"I was now alone","tag":"Theme","m":"Leaves home → isolation begins","b":"Victor leaves the warm family circle for Ingolstadt. Physical distance from family will become emotional isolation. He even admits he was bad at meeting new people.","lt":"Psych","l":"Starting college is a known period of loneliness and risk for mental-health struggles; support networks matter. Victor will cut his off completely."},{"id":42,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[107,108],"start":4397.8,"t":4468.9,"from":"I had sufficient leisure for these and many other …","to":"… spent your time in studying such nonsense?”","q":"the Angel of Destruction, which asserted omnipotent sway over me","tag":"Theme","m":"Blames 'Chance/Angel of Destruction'","b":"Victor corrects himself: not 'chance' but an 'Angel of Destruction' led him to M. Krempe. Again he narrates his life as cursed rather than chosen.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Em dashes ('Chance—or rather…') let a speaker interrupt and correct himself. Shelley's narrators do this often, showing them reshaping their story as they tell it."},{"id":43,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[109,110],"start":4469.3,"t":4547.9,"from":"I replied in the affirmative. “Every minute,” continued M. …","to":"… chemistry the alternate days that he omitted.","q":"these fancies which you have so greedily imbibed are a thousand years old","tag":"Character","m":"Krempe mocks alchemy = modern science","b":"M. Krempe bluntly dismisses Victor's alchemists. He's right about the science, but his rudeness pushes Victor away. Victor judges him by his looks, the very thing he'll condemn in others.","lt":"History","l":"The Scientific Revolution (1500s–1700s) replaced alchemy with chemistry. Lavoisier, the 'father of modern chemistry', named oxygen and hydrogen in the 1770s–80s."},{"id":44,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[111,112],"start":4548.3,"t":4680.4,"from":"I returned home not disappointed, for I have said …","to":"… he had hitherto been out of town.","q":"a repulsive countenance","tag":"Irony","m":"Victor judges Krempe by APPEARANCE (irony!)","b":"Victor rejects Krempe partly because he's 'squat' and 'repulsive'. This is the same appearance-based judgment that dooms the Creature, and Victor is guilty of it from the start.","lt":"Psych","l":"The 'halo effect' is a bias where we assume good-looking people are also good, smart or kind. The reverse (the 'horns effect') makes us judge unattractive people harshly."},{"id":45,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[113,114],"start":4680.8,"t":4802.8,"from":"Partly from curiosity and partly from idleness, I went …","to":"… the invisible world with its own shadows.”","q":"they can command the thunders of heaven, mimic the earthquake","tag":"Theme","m":"Waldman: modern science = godlike power","b":"Waldman's lecture praises modern scientists who 'penetrate into the recesses of nature'. His language of power and command ignites Victor's ambition.","lt":"Science","l":"Scientists of the era did 'command the thunders': Benjamin Franklin's kite experiment (1752) and the lightning rod showed electricity could be captured and controlled."},{"id":46,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[115,116],"start":4803.2,"t":4983.4,"from":"Such were the professor’s words—rather let me say such …","to":"… concerning the books I ought to procure.","q":"one thought, one conception, one purpose","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor's obsession is born (tricolon)","b":"This is the moment Victor's obsession takes over: he will 'pioneer a new way' and unfold 'the deepest mysteries of creation'. He speaks of himself in the third person ('the soul of Frankenstein'), as if he were a hero in a legend.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Tricolon: a list of three parallel items ('one thought, one conception, one purpose') for rhythm and emphasis. Speeches use it often: 'government of the people, by the people, for the people.'"},{"id":47,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[117,119],"start":4983.8,"t":5067.8,"from":"“I am happy,” said M. Waldman, “to have gained …","to":"… to me; it decided my future destiny.","q":"it decided my future destiny","tag":"Character","m":"Waldman = mentor; Victor's turning point","b":"Kind Waldman welcomes Victor as a 'disciple'. Unlike Krempe, he is gentle, and that is what wins Victor over. Mentors shape us, but Victor will soon have no one to check him.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Waldman says a chemist who studies only one field is a 'sorry' one. Today 'interdisciplinary' science (bioethics, for example) exists to keep scientists from Victor's tunnel vision."},{"id":48,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[120,121],"start":5068.2,"t":5250.3,"from":"From this day natural philosophy, and particularly chemistry, in …","to":"… an incident happened that protracted my stay.","q":"became nearly my sole occupation","tag":"Character","m":"Total focus: 2 yrs, no visits home","b":"Victor studies so hard that he never goes home in two years and makes discoveries that earn the university's esteem. His talent is real; the problem is that he has no balance.","lt":"Psych","l":"'Flow' (Csikszentmihalyi) is the joy of total absorption in a task. Healthy flow ends; Victor's becomes compulsion. Burnout research shows what happens when it doesn't stop."},{"id":49,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[122,122],"start":5250.7,"t":5414.2,"from":"One of the phenomena which had peculiarly attracted my …","to":"… reserved to discover so astonishing a secret.","q":"Whence, I often asked myself, did the principle of life proceed?","tag":"Theme","m":"KEY QUESTION: where does life come from?","b":"Victor studies death (decay, corpses, charnel-houses) to understand life. He is 'surprised' that 'I alone' should discover the secret. Note the arrogance.","lt":"Science","l":"Scientists in the 1810s debated 'vitalism', the belief that life needs a special spark beyond chemistry. Today we know life is chemistry, yet we still can't build a cell from scratch."},{"id":50,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[123,124],"start":5414.6,"t":5512.5,"from":"Remember, I am not recording the vision of a …","to":"… by one glimmering and seemingly ineffectual light.","q":"Remember, I am not recording the vision of a madman","tag":"Narration","m":"Unreliable narrator insists he's sane","b":"Victor insists he's telling the truth. When narrators protest too much, readers grow suspicious. Remember that Walton is transcribing a feverish, grieving man.","lt":"Literature","l":"Compare Poe's 'The Tell-Tale Heart' (1843), whose narrator begins by insisting he is not mad. That is the classic unreliable-narrator opening."},{"id":51,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[125,126],"start":5512.9,"t":5676.8,"from":"I see by your eagerness and the wonder and …","to":"… collecting and arranging my materials, I began.","q":"Learn from me, if not by my precepts, at least by my example","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Moral: dangerous knowledge; ignorance = bliss?","b":"Victor speaks directly to Walton: the man who believes 'his native town to be the world' is happier than one who aspires 'to become greater than his nature will allow'. This is the novel's central warning. He also decides to make the being eight feet tall to make the work easier: a shortcut with huge consequences.","lt":"Ethics","l":"The 'precautionary principle' says not to move forward with a powerful technology until its risks are understood. It is debated today with AI, gene editing (CRISPR) and nuclear power."},{"id":52,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[127,128],"start":5677.2,"t":5843.8,"from":"No one can conceive the variety of feelings which …","to":"… brought my work near to a conclusion.","q":"A new species would bless me as its creator and source","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor wants to be GOD/father, worshipped","b":"Victor imagines grateful creatures who would owe him everything: 'No father could claim the gratitude of his child so completely.' He wants worship, not a relationship. Note also where his materials come from: the dissecting room and slaughter-house.","lt":"History","l":"In the 1800s 'resurrection men' robbed graves to sell bodies to anatomy schools. Britain's Anatomy Act of 1832 was passed partly in response to the Burke and Hare murders."},{"id":53,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[129,130],"start":5844.2,"t":5926.1,"from":"The summer months passed while I was thus engaged, …","to":"… habit of my nature, should be completed.","q":"my eyes were insensible to the charms of nature","tag":"Theme","m":"Cut off from NATURE + FAMILY = warning sign","b":"In a gorgeous summer, Victor sees nothing. For Romantic writers, losing your connection to nature means losing your humanity. He also puts off his feelings 'until the great object… should be completed.'","lt":"Literature","l":"Wordsworth's 'The world is too much with us' (1807) laments people who are 'out of tune' with nature, busy 'getting and spending'. Victor is the extreme case."},{"id":54,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[131,132],"start":5926.5,"t":6006.7,"from":"I then thought that my father would be unjust …","to":"… and your looks remind me to proceed.","q":"America would have been discovered more gradually","tag":"Theme","m":"Moral: no pursuit worth destroying affection","b":"Victor steps outside his story to moralize: any study that weakens your affections is 'unlawful'. He argues that slower progress (Greece, Caesar, Mexico, Peru) would have spared suffering.","lt":"History","l":"He points to the Spanish conquest of the Aztec (Mexico) and Inca (Peru) empires (1519–1533), driven by ambition and greed, which destroyed whole civilizations. It is a real-world example of ambition without ethics."},{"id":55,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[133,133],"start":6007.1,"t":6096.7,"from":"My father made no reproach in his letters and …","to":"… these when my creation should be complete.","q":"The leaves of that year had withered","tag":"Setting","m":"Seasons pass = time & health wasted","b":"Shelley marks time through the seasons Victor ignores. His body deteriorates ('emaciated', nervous, feverish) while his 'creation' nears completion. He wastes away as it gains life.","lt":"Language","l":"Pathetic fallacy: nature reflects a character's mood. Here the withering leaves reflect Victor's decline."},{"id":56,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[134,135],"start":6097.1,"t":6181.4,"from":"It was on a dreary night of November that …","to":"… his shrivelled complexion and straight black lips.","q":"It was on a dreary night of November","tag":"Setting","m":"★ CREATION SCENE: gothic setting","b":"The creation is famously brief: rain, a dying candle, 1 a.m., and a 'dull yellow eye' opening. Victor chose 'beautiful' features, but together they are horrid. Beauty in parts does not make a beautiful whole.","lt":"Literature","l":"Mary Shelley said this scene came first, from a waking nightmare in June 1816 during Byron's ghost-story contest. This chapter was the seed of the whole novel."},{"id":57,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[136,136],"start":6181.8,"t":6337.5,"from":"The different accidents of life are not so changeable …","to":"… which I had so miserably given life.","q":"the beauty of the dream vanished, and breathless horror and disgust filled my heart","tag":"Theme","m":"Instant rejection: abandons creation","b":"After two years of work, Victor flees the moment his creation is alive. He rejects it purely for its looks. This is the novel's original sin: abandonment.","lt":"Psych","l":"Parental rejection research shows children who feel rejected are more likely to become hostile and aggressive. Psychologists call this the rejection-aggression link."},{"id":58,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[137,139],"start":6337.9,"t":6431.2,"from":"Oh! No mortal could support the horror of that …","to":"… poured from a black and comfortless sky.","q":"it became a thing such as even Dante could not have conceived","tag":"Allusion","m":"Creature reaches for Victor; V. runs","b":"The Creature comes to Victor's bed, 'one hand stretched out', smiling, perhaps reaching like a baby. Victor reads it as a threat and runs. We only get Victor's interpretation.","lt":"Literature","l":"Dante's 'Inferno' (1300s) describes the monsters of Hell. Victor also quotes Coleridge's 'Ancient Mariner' ('a frightful fiend / Doth close behind him tread'), the same poem Walton cited."},{"id":59,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[140,143],"start":6431.6,"t":6513.0,"from":"I continued walking in this manner for some time, …","to":"… at the very moment of my alighting!”","q":"Like one who, on a lonely road","tag":"Allusion","m":"Coleridge quote = guilt follows him","b":"Victor's state is captured by Coleridge's lines about being stalked by a fiend. Then, ironically, his kind friend Clerval appears, the opposite of a fiend.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Block quotations of poetry keep their line breaks and are set apart from prose. In essays, quote 1–3 lines inline with slashes (/) and longer passages as an indented block."},{"id":60,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[144,145],"start":6513.4,"t":6602.6,"from":"Nothing could equal my delight on seeing Clerval; his …","to":"… you left my father, brothers, and Elizabeth.”","q":"a voyage of discovery to the land of knowledge","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval = healing friend; balanced learner","b":"Clerval's arrival restores Victor's calm. Clerval's father finally let him study; his 'voyage of discovery' is a healthy contrast to Victor's and Walton's dangerous voyages.","lt":"Language","l":"Clerval quotes the Dutch schoolmaster in 'The Vicar of Wakefield' (Goldsmith, 1766), using humor. Allusions show a character's education and personality."},{"id":61,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[146,148],"start":6603.0,"t":6723.6,"from":"“Very well, and very happy, only a little uneasy …","to":"… for joy and ran down to Clerval.","q":"you look as if you had been watching for several nights","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor hides the truth (secrecy theme)","b":"Clerval notices Victor's illness immediately, but Victor won't explain. Secrecy is Victor's pattern: he never tells anyone about the Creature until it's far too late.","lt":"Psych","l":"Keeping a big secret increases stress and isolation; studies by Michael Slepian found that thinking about secrets, not just hiding them, harms well-being."},{"id":62,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[149,151],"start":6724.0,"t":6798.5,"from":"We ascended into my room, and the servant presently …","to":"… furiously and fell down in a fit.","q":"I imagined that the monster seized me; I struggled furiously and fell down in a fit","tag":"Character","m":"Victor's breakdown: hysteria, then collapse","b":"Victor laughs uncontrollably, then collapses. His mind can't hold what he's done. Notice the shift from 'creature' to 'monster' in his vocabulary.","lt":"Science","l":"Sleep deprivation and extreme stress can cause hallucinations and paranoia. Victor has gone months with little sleep, so his 'spectre' may be partly a symptom."},{"id":63,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[152,153],"start":6798.9,"t":6857.6,"from":"Poor Clerval! What must have been his feelings? A …","to":"… kindest action that he could towards them.","q":"This was the commencement of a nervous fever which confined me for several months","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval nurses him = true friendship","b":"Clerval gives up his whole winter of study to nurse Victor and hides the severity from Victor's family. Compare the care Victor receives with the care his creation never gets.","lt":"History","l":"'Nervous fever' was a vague diagnosis for illnesses with mental symptoms, sometimes typhus and sometimes a breakdown. Before modern medicine, nursing was often the only treatment."},{"id":64,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[154,155],"start":6858.0,"t":6937.2,"from":"But I was in reality very ill, and surely …","to":"… I was attacked by the fatal passion.","q":"The form of the monster on whom I had bestowed existence was for ever before my eyes","tag":"Theme","m":"Guilt haunts him, but still won't confess","b":"Victor raves about the monster in his fever, but recovers in spring. Nature heals him again. The Creature, meanwhile, is out alone in the world. Where is he?","lt":"Grammar","l":"'On whom I had bestowed existence': a preposition before 'whom' is formal style. Casual English would be 'who I'd given life to'. Formal writing avoids ending with a preposition."},{"id":65,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[156,161],"start":6937.6,"t":7028.5,"from":"“Dearest Clerval,” exclaimed I, “how kind, how very good …","to":"… it is from your cousin, I believe.”","q":"How shall I ever repay you?","tag":"Character","m":"Victor asks forgiveness of Clerval, not creature","b":"Victor feels remorse toward Clerval for wasting his winter, yet none toward the being he abandoned. His moral concern only reaches people he sees as his own.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Philosopher Peter Singer writes about the 'expanding circle', the idea that moral progress means extending concern beyond our own group. Victor's circle is very small."},{"id":66,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[162,164],"start":7028.9,"t":7104.6,"from":"Clerval then put the following letter into my hands. …","to":"… this intelligence soon in your own handwriting.","q":"one word from you, dear Victor, is necessary to calm our apprehensions","tag":"Narration","m":"Elizabeth's letter = another narrator","b":"Elizabeth's letter adds another voice, a letter inside a story inside letters. Her tone is warm and domestic, a contrast with Victor's dark obsession.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Nested quotation: in British style the outer quote uses one set of marks and the inner quote another ('…\"…\"…'). Each new paragraph of a long quotation opens with a quote mark but doesn't close the previous one."},{"id":67,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[165,166],"start":7105.0,"t":7283.9,"from":"“Get well—and return to us. You will find a …","to":"… of the dignity of a human being.","q":"does not include the idea of ignorance and a sacrifice of the dignity of a human being","tag":"Character","m":"Justine introduced; Swiss 'equality' of classes","b":"Elizabeth introduces Justine Moritz, a servant welcomed into the family. Elizabeth says Geneva's republic treats lower classes with more dignity than France or England. Remember Justine. She becomes crucial.","lt":"History","l":"The French Revolution (1789) began partly over class inequality. Republics like Geneva claimed to be more equal. Mary's father, William Godwin, wrote against aristocratic privilege."},{"id":68,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[167,168],"start":7284.3,"t":7367.6,"from":"“Justine, you may remember, was a great favourite of …","to":"… but other trials were reserved for her.","q":"one glance from Justine could dissipate it","tag":"Allusion","m":"Justine: loyal, kind, Caroline's imitator","b":"Justine adored Caroline and imitated her. Elizabeth shows that Justine is gentle and devoted. Shelley is setting her up as purely innocent before what happens later.","lt":"Literature","l":"Ariosto's 'Orlando Furioso' (1516) is an Italian epic whose heroine Angelica drives knights mad with love. Characters' reading reveals their education."},{"id":69,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[169,170],"start":7368.0,"t":7499.9,"from":"“One by one, her brothers and sister died; and …","to":"… little girl of five years of age.","q":"She was a Roman Catholic; and I believe her confessor confirmed the idea","tag":"Character","m":"Justine's mother cruel → blames her","b":"Justine's mother neglected her and later blamed her for her siblings' deaths. Justine's life is a pattern of unfair blame, foreshadowing her trial. William appears here: sweet, dimpled, rosy.","lt":"History","l":"Geneva was a Protestant (Calvinist) city; Elizabeth's comment reflects Protestant suspicion of Catholic confession. Religious tension shaped Swiss life for centuries."},{"id":70,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[171,172],"start":7500.3,"t":7563.8,"from":"“Now, dear Victor, I dare say you wish to …","to":"… of yourself; and, I entreat you, write!","q":"one line—one word will be a blessing to us","tag":"Theme","m":"Family begs for contact = Victor neglects","b":"Elizabeth's gossip about Geneva marriages is light and normal. Her repeated plea, 'write!', shows how Victor has neglected the family who love him.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Dashes and repetition ('one line—one word') mimic urgent speech. Writers use these to add emotion; in formal essays use them sparingly."},{"id":71,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[173,176],"start":7564.2,"t":7751.6,"from":"“Dear, dear Elizabeth!” I exclaimed, when I had read …","to":"… to another would only impress more deeply.","q":"I could never persuade myself to confide in him","tag":"Theme","m":"Secrecy again: won't confide in Clerval","b":"Victor recovers and introduces Clerval to the professors, but the sight of his lab instruments sickens him. Henry senses something and removes them, yet Victor still won't share his secret.","lt":"Psych","l":"Avoidance (staying away from reminders of trauma) is a core symptom of PTSD. It brings short-term relief but keeps the fear alive."},{"id":72,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[177,178],"start":7752.0,"t":7830.7,"from":"M. Krempe was not equally docile; and in my …","to":"… subject that was so annoying to me.","q":"I assure you he has outstript us all","tag":"Irony","m":"Praise = torture (dramatic irony)","b":"Krempe praises Victor loudly, but every word about his scientific skill is torture to Victor. We know why; Krempe doesn't. That is dramatic irony.","lt":"Language","l":"'D—n the fellow!': writers once censored swear words with dashes. 'Outstript' is an older spelling of 'outstripped' (surpassed)."},{"id":73,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[179,180],"start":7831.1,"t":7975.5,"from":"Clerval had never sympathised in my tastes for natural …","to":"… came its beauty compensated for its dilatoriness.","q":"the oriental languages","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval studies languages: human connection","b":"Clerval studies Persian, Arabic and Sanskrit. Victor joins him to escape science. Languages connect people; Victor's science isolated him. They are opposite paths.","lt":"History","l":"In the early 1800s Europeans studied 'oriental' (Middle Eastern and Asian) languages partly for trade and the British Empire in India. The term 'Orientalism' (Edward Said, 1978) critiques how the West imagined the East."},{"id":74,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[181,182],"start":7975.9,"t":8096.6,"from":"The month of May had already commenced, and I …","to":"… throw them off, with an invincible burden.","q":"the flowers of spring bloomed in the hedges","tag":"Setting","m":"Nature heals → Victor at peace (briefly)","b":"On a walking tour with Clerval, Victor feels free again. Spring and friendship bring him back to life. Enjoy it; it's the calm before the storm.","lt":"Literature","l":"Walking tours were a Romantic tradition: Wordsworth and Coleridge walked across England and the Alps. Mary and Percy Shelley walked across war-torn France in 1814."},{"id":75,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[183,184],"start":8097.0,"t":8154.6,"from":"Henry rejoiced in my gaiety, and sincerely sympathised in …","to":"… with feelings of unbridled joy and hilarity.","q":"I bounded along with feelings of unbridled joy and hilarity","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Peak happiness → disaster next ch.","b":"Chapter 6 ends at Victor's happiest moment. Shelley sets up a sharp reversal: the next chapter brings terrible news from Geneva.","lt":"Literature","l":"Peripeteia (Aristotle): a sudden reversal of fortune. Writers often place one right after a character's happiest moment so the fall hits harder."},{"id":76,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[185,187],"start":8155.0,"t":8218.8,"from":"On my return, I found the following letter from …","to":"… to convey to you the horrible tidings.","q":"your eye skims over the page to seek the words","tag":"Narration","m":"Father's letter: delays the bad news","b":"Alphonse knows Victor will skim ahead for the bad news, and so do we. Shelley uses the letter to build dread before the reveal.","lt":"Language","l":"Second-person address ('your eye skims') pulls the reader into the scene. It's a technique writers use to create immediacy: 'You open the door…'"},{"id":77,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[188,190],"start":8219.2,"t":8281.0,"from":"“William is dead!—that sweet child, whose smiles delighted and …","to":"… time, but that he did not return.","q":"William is dead!","tag":"Theme","m":"★ William murdered = Creature's 1st victim","b":"Victor's youngest brother is murdered. Three short exclamations (dead, sweet child, murdered) show the father's shock. The 'innocent child' victim makes the crime feel monstrous.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Short declarative sentences after long ones create impact. Notice how the rhythm shifts from the father's careful preamble to 'William is dead!'"},{"id":78,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[191,192],"start":8281.4,"t":8345.5,"from":"“This account rather alarmed us, and we continued to …","to":"… God! I have murdered my darling child!’","q":"I have murdered my darling child!","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth blames herself (guilt spreads)","b":"Elizabeth blames herself because she let William wear the miniature. Victor is the one truly responsible, yet it's the innocent who carry the guilt.","lt":"Psych","l":"'Survivor's guilt' is the irrational self-blame people feel after a loved one's death, common in grief. Elizabeth and later Justine both take on blame that isn't theirs."},{"id":79,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[193,194],"start":8345.9,"t":8411.0,"from":"“She fainted, and was restored with extreme difficulty. When …","to":"… cruel, miserable death of her youngest darling!","q":"a very valuable miniature that she possessed of your mother","tag":"Symbol","m":"Miniature (portrait of Caroline) = key clue","b":"The missing locket portrait of Caroline is assumed to be the motive. Keep track of it; it becomes the evidence that frames Justine.","lt":"History","l":"Portrait miniatures were tiny painted portraits worn in lockets before photography (invented around 1839). They were keepsakes of loved ones, often of the dead."},{"id":80,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[195,201],"start":8411.4,"t":8483.7,"from":"“Come, Victor; not brooding thoughts of vengeance against the …","to":"… he read the account of my misfortune.","q":"not with hatred for your enemies","tag":"Theme","m":"Father: forgive, don't seek revenge","b":"Alphonse asks Victor to come home with 'peace and gentleness', not vengeance. Victor will ignore this advice, and revenge will consume the rest of his life.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Many ethical traditions teach that revenge 'festers' the wound, from 'turn the other cheek' to Stoicism. Psychology agrees: forgiveness is linked to lower stress and better health."},{"id":81,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[202,204],"start":8484.1,"t":8564.2,"from":"“I can offer you no consolation, my friend,” said …","to":"… must reserve that for his miserable survivors.”","q":"A sod covers his gentle form, and he knows no pain","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval's comfort: pity the living","b":"Clerval reflects that William is at peace and the survivors are the ones to pity. That turns out to be truer than he knows: Victor will suffer far more than anyone.","lt":"Language","l":"'Sod' (grass and soil) is a metonymy for the grave: one associated thing stands in for another. Similar examples: 'the crown' for the monarch, 'the White House' for the president."},{"id":82,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[205,206],"start":8564.6,"t":8639.4,"from":"Clerval spoke thus as we hurried through the streets; …","to":"… although I was unable to define them.","q":"dreading a thousand nameless evils that made me tremble","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Dread on journey: senses something worse","b":"Victor hesitates as he nears home, sensing that more disasters are coming. His anxiety is vague but grows stronger.","lt":"Psych","l":"Anticipatory anxiety (fear of a future threat you can't name) is often worse than the threat itself. Researchers find uncertainty is more stressful than certain bad news."},{"id":83,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[207,209],"start":8639.8,"t":8720.6,"from":"I remained two days at Lausanne, in this painful …","to":"… and, more than all, thy lovely lake!","q":"the palaces of nature","tag":"Allusion","m":"Byron quote; nature calms Victor","b":"The Alps soothe Victor. 'The palaces of nature' quotes Byron's 'Childe Harold's Pilgrimage', a nod to Byron, who was at Lake Geneva with Mary in 1816.","lt":"Literature","l":"The Sublime: Romantic writers described awe-inspiring nature (mountains, storms) that is beautiful and terrifying at once. Edmund Burke defined it in 1757."},{"id":84,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[210,211],"start":8721.0,"t":8814.7,"from":"Yet, as I drew nearer home, grief and fear …","to":"… large drops, but its violence quickly increased.","q":"I was destined to become the most wretched of human beings","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Prophetic dread + approaching storm","b":"Victor 'prophesies' his misery and a storm approaches. Shelley uses weather to mirror his inner turmoil and to herald the Creature.","lt":"Language","l":"Pathetic fallacy again: the storm matches the mood. Note the verbs: 'advanced', 'clouded', 'increased'. The storm is described like an attacking army."},{"id":85,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[212,213],"start":8815.1,"t":8986.6,"from":"I quitted my seat, and walked on, although the …","to":"… He soon reached the summit, and disappeared.","q":"a vast sheet of fire","tag":"Setting","m":"Lightning reveals the Creature (callback ch.2)","b":"A lightning flash reveals the Creature near where William died. Lightning, which first inspired Victor's science, now exposes its consequence. The Creature climbs Mont Salève with superhuman speed.","lt":"Science","l":"Thunder echoing off mountains is reverberation: sound reflecting off surfaces. You see lightning before you hear thunder because light travels about 900,000 times faster than sound."},{"id":86,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[214,215],"start":8987.0,"t":9074.8,"from":"I remained motionless. The thunder ceased; but the rain …","to":"… destroy all that was dear to me.","q":"nearly in the light of my own vampire, my own spirit let loose from the grave","tag":"Symbol","m":"Creature = Victor's 'vampire'/own spirit","b":"Victor instantly decides the Creature is the murderer and calls it 'my own spirit let loose', admitting the Creature is part of himself. Many critics read it as Victor's dark double.","lt":"Literature","l":"The same 1816 contest at Lake Geneva produced John Polidori's 'The Vampyre' (1819), the first modern vampire story in English and an ancestor of Dracula."},{"id":87,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[216,217],"start":9075.2,"t":9169.8,"from":"Day dawned; and I directed my steps towards the …","to":"… to attend their usual hour of rising.","q":"These reflections determined me, and I resolved to remain silent","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor chooses SILENCE: moral failure","b":"Victor could tell the authorities but decides no one would believe him. This choice of silence will cost Justine her life. Mark it as a key decision.","lt":"Ethics","l":"The 'bystander effect' describes people who know something is wrong but don't act. Victor is worse than a bystander: he alone holds the truth."},{"id":88,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[218,219],"start":9170.2,"t":9291.3,"from":"Six years had elapsed, passed in a dream but …","to":"… father, and here I named my cousin.","q":"Six years had elapsed, passed in a dream but for one indelible trace","tag":"Setting","m":"Mother's portrait: grief + guilt at home","b":"Victor returns to his childhood home after six years. The painting of Caroline weeping by her father's coffin echoes the grief now in the house.","lt":"Language","l":"'Indelible' means impossible to erase (in-, not; delere, to delete). The same Latin root gives us 'delete'."},{"id":89,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[220,222],"start":9291.7,"t":9354.1,"from":"“She most of all,” said Ernest, “requires consolation; she …","to":"… of so frightful, so appalling a crime?”","q":"Justine Moritz, who was so amiable, and fond of all the family","tag":"Irony","m":"Justine accused (dramatic irony)","b":"Ernest reveals that Justine has been accused. We know the Creature did it; the family doesn't. Dramatic irony makes the trial agonizing to read.","lt":"History","l":"Before forensic science, trials relied heavily on witnesses and circumstantial evidence. Fingerprinting wasn't used in court until about 1900, and DNA evidence not until the 1980s."},{"id":90,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[223,225],"start":9354.5,"t":9435.7,"from":"“Justine Moritz! Poor, poor girl, is she the accused? …","to":"… measure by her extreme confusion of manner.","q":"the poor girl confirmed the suspicion in a great measure by her extreme confusion of manner","tag":"Theme","m":"Justine's nervousness → seen as guilt","b":"Justine's distress is read as guilt. People judge by outward signs. Compare how the Creature is judged by his looks.","lt":"Psych","l":"Research shows people are bad at detecting lies from behavior; nervousness looks like guilt to observers. Innocent people under pressure often appear 'suspicious'."},{"id":91,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[226,230],"start":9436.1,"t":9503.8,"from":"This was a strange tale, but it did not …","to":"… sincerely hope, that she will be acquitted.”","q":"I know the murderer. Justine, poor, good Justine, is innocent","tag":"Character","m":"Victor knows truth but won't explain it","b":"Victor insists Justine is innocent but refuses to say why. His half-truth helps no one. His father hopes the court will acquit her.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Commas set off an appositive phrase ('poor, good Justine') that renames a noun. They work like a pause that adds detail about the same person."},{"id":92,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[231,232],"start":9504.2,"t":9618.9,"from":"This speech calmed me. I was firmly convinced in …","to":"… the sad death of my little William.”","q":"My tale was not one to announce publicly","tag":"Theme","m":"Fear of being called MAD → stays silent","b":"Victor chooses his reputation over Justine's life. He tells himself that the evidence won't convict her. Is this reasonable caution or cowardice? Make a note of your view.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Whistleblowers face this dilemma: speaking up risks your reputation or career, but silence lets harm continue. Laws now protect some whistleblowers for this reason."},{"id":93,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[233,235],"start":9619.3,"t":9670.3,"from":"“She is innocent, my Elizabeth,” said I, “and that …","to":"… shall prevent the slightest shadow of partiality.”","q":"rely on the justice of our laws","tag":"Irony","m":"Father trusts justice → it will fail","b":"Alphonse tells Elizabeth to trust the justice system. That is painful irony, because the system is about to condemn an innocent woman.","lt":"History","l":"Wrongful convictions still happen: the US Innocence Project has helped free over 200 people through DNA evidence. Mistaken eyewitnesses and false confessions are leading causes."},{"id":94,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[236,237],"start":9670.7,"t":9809.1,"from":"We passed a few sad hours until eleven o’clock, …","to":"… affection seemed to attest her utter guiltlessness.","q":"During the whole of this wretched mockery of justice I suffered living torture","tag":"Theme","m":"Trial = 'mockery of justice'","b":"Victor watches the trial as a 'mockery'. He frames himself as the one suffering, while Justine faces execution. Notice how often his narration centers on his own pain.","lt":"Language","l":"Oxymoron: 'living torture' combines contrasting ideas. Shelley often pairs life and death words, fitting a novel about bringing the dead to life."},{"id":95,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[238,239],"start":9809.5,"t":9909.9,"from":"The trial began, and after the advocate against her …","to":"… spoke in an audible although variable voice.","q":"Several strange facts combined against her","tag":"Theme","m":"Circumstantial evidence piles up","b":"Justine was out all night near where the body was found, and the miniature was in her pocket. Each fact is innocent alone but damning together. The Creature planted the evidence.","lt":"Science","l":"Circumstantial evidence requires inference; direct evidence (like an eyewitness) does not. Combining several weak clues can feel convincing even when it's wrong, a form of confirmation bias."},{"id":96,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[240,241],"start":9910.3,"t":10015.7,"from":"“God knows,” she said, “how entirely I am innocent. …","to":"… the picture she could give no account.","q":"how entirely I am innocent","tag":"Character","m":"Justine's calm, honest defense","b":"Justine explains where she was: searching for William, locked out, sheltering in a barn. Her explanation is plain and believable, but she can't explain the picture.","lt":"Language","l":"Her speech uses formal legal register ('adduced', 'favourable interpretation'). Register is the level of formality we choose for a situation, as in a courtroom versus texting."},{"id":97,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[242,243],"start":10016.1,"t":10079.8,"from":"“I know,” continued the unhappy victim, “how heavily and …","to":"… would pledge my salvation on my innocence.”","q":"I would pledge my salvation on my innocence","tag":"Theme","m":"Truth isn't enough without proof","b":"Justine's honesty can't overcome the planted evidence. Shelley shows that innocence alone doesn't guarantee justice.","lt":"Ethics","l":"'Innocent until proven guilty' (presumption of innocence) is a principle of modern law. Notice how the burden of proof seems to fall on Justine here."},{"id":98,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[244,245],"start":10080.2,"t":10218.8,"from":"Several witnesses were called who had known her for …","to":"… much do I esteem and value her.”","q":"so much do I esteem and value her","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth testifies = courage","b":"Elizabeth bravely speaks up when other witnesses are too afraid. She is the only one who publicly defends Justine; Victor, who knows the truth, stays silent.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Inversion for emphasis: 'so much do I esteem her' instead of 'I esteem her so much'. Moving the adverb to the front adds weight, as in 'Never have I seen…'"},{"id":99,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[246,247],"start":10219.2,"t":10313.9,"from":"A murmur of approbation followed Elizabeth’s simple and powerful …","to":"… were all black, and Justine was condemned.","q":"they were all black, and Justine was condemned","tag":"Symbol","m":"Black ballots = guilty verdict","b":"Elizabeth's defense backfires: the crowd sees Justine as ungrateful. The verdict comes through black ballots, unanimous and final.","lt":"History","l":"Voting with black and white balls goes back to ancient Athens. It's the origin of 'blackballing', rejecting someone from a club."},{"id":100,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[248,249],"start":10314.3,"t":10371.9,"from":"I cannot pretend to describe what I then felt. …","to":"… home, and Elizabeth eagerly demanded the result.","q":"words cannot convey an idea of the heart-sickening despair","tag":"Narration","m":"Justine 'confessed', Victor stunned","b":"Victor hears that Justine confessed and wonders if he's mad. Note his 'inexpressibility' claim: he says words fail, then describes it anyway.","lt":"Language","l":"The 'inexpressibility topos' (saying something is beyond words) is an ancient rhetorical device. It emphasizes intensity, and people still use it: 'I can't even describe it.'"},{"id":101,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[250,252],"start":10372.3,"t":10446.8,"from":"“My cousin,” replied I, “it is decided as you …","to":"… to me, yet I could not refuse.","q":"all judges had rather that ten innocent should suffer than that one guilty should escape","tag":"Irony","m":"Twists Blackstone's ratio (bitter irony)","b":"Victor bitterly reverses a famous legal principle to show how unjust the court is. Elizabeth asks how she can ever believe in human goodness again.","lt":"History","l":"Blackstone's ratio (1760s): 'better that ten guilty persons escape than that one innocent suffer'. It is a cornerstone of English and American law, and Victor flips it on purpose."},{"id":102,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[253,256],"start":10447.2,"t":10522.3,"from":"We entered the gloomy prison chamber and beheld Justine …","to":"… for a moment, but your own confession.”","q":"her hands were manacled, and her head rested on her knees","tag":"Setting","m":"Prison: Justine in chains","b":"The gloomy prison and the image of chains create a vivid picture of the innocent punished. Justine throws herself at Elizabeth's feet. Note the class difference.","lt":"Art","l":"Gothic fiction loves prisons, dungeons and confinement, which symbolize being trapped by fate or society. Look for other enclosed spaces in the novel."},{"id":103,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[257,258],"start":10522.7,"t":10607.6,"from":"“I did confess, but I confessed a lie. I …","to":"… I am to suffer ignominy and death.”","q":"I confessed, that I might obtain absolution","tag":"Theme","m":"False confession under pressure","b":"Justine confessed a lie because her confessor threatened her with damnation. She was pressured until she almost believed she was 'the monster'. The word choice connects her to the Creature.","lt":"Psych","l":"False confessions are real: about 25% of DNA exonerations involved one. Coercion, exhaustion and fear lead innocent people to confess (Kassin's research)."},{"id":104,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[259,260],"start":10608.0,"t":10668.4,"from":"“Oh, Justine! Forgive me for having for one moment …","to":"… in patience to the will of heaven!”","q":"Learn from me, dear lady, to submit in patience to the will of heaven","tag":"Character","m":"Justine accepts death (passive virtue)","b":"Justine echoes Victor's 'learn from me' but means the opposite: humble acceptance, not ambition. She is a model of passive, saintly virtue.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Compare Socrates, who accepted an unjust death sentence (399 BCE) rather than flee, saying it was right to obey the law. Is accepting injustice noble or tragic?"},{"id":105,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[261,263],"start":10668.8,"t":10750.5,"from":"During this conversation I had retired to a corner …","to":"… by you, dear lady, and your cousin.”","q":"felt not, as I did, such deep and bitter agony","tag":"Character","m":"Victor claims HIS pain > Justine's (selfish)","b":"Victor claims that he suffers more than Justine, who faces execution. This is one of the clearest examples of his self-centeredness. Readers often lose sympathy for him here.","lt":"Psych","l":"Egocentrism is the inability to see beyond your own perspective. Victor reframes every tragedy around himself, which is a red flag for an unreliable narrator."},{"id":106,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[264,265],"start":10750.9,"t":10837.6,"from":"Thus the poor sufferer tried to comfort others and …","to":"… and be happy, and make others so.”","q":"I, the true murderer, felt the never-dying worm alive in my bosom","tag":"Allusion","m":"Victor admits guilt (to us, not court)","b":"Victor calls himself 'the true murderer' but still says nothing. Justine comforts others, Victor only suffers. Innocence and guilt are clearly contrasted.","lt":"Literature","l":"'The worm that dieth not' comes from the Bible (Mark 9:48), describing the torments of hell. Shelley uses biblical allusion to show eternal guilt."},{"id":107,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[266,268],"start":10838.0,"t":10975.1,"from":"And on the morrow Justine died. Elizabeth’s heart-rending eloquence …","to":"… first hapless victims to my unhallowed arts.","q":"the first hapless victims to my unhallowed arts","tag":"Theme","m":"Justine executed; 2 victims of V.'s science","b":"Justine is executed. Victor calls William and Justine victims of his 'unhallowed arts', admitting responsibility, though he still blames the Creature for acting.","lt":"Language","l":"'Unhallowed' means unholy (hallow = make holy, as in Halloween, 'All Hallows' Eve'). Victor admits his science crossed a sacred line."},{"id":108,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[269,270],"start":10975.5,"t":11085.2,"from":"Nothing is more painful to the human mind than, …","to":"… was my only consolation—deep, dark, deathlike solitude.","q":"solitude was my only consolation—deep, dark, deathlike solitude","tag":"Theme","m":"Isolation deepens (alliteration d-d-d)","b":"After Justine's death Victor shuts himself away. He chooses isolation, the same condition that will drive the Creature to violence.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Alliteration: repeating initial consonant sounds ('deep, dark, deathlike'). It's a sound device that adds rhythm and makes the phrase stick."},{"id":109,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[271,272],"start":11085.6,"t":11169.7,"from":"My father observed with pain the alteration perceptible in …","to":"… endeavour to hide myself from his view.","q":"Do you think, Victor,","tag":"Character","m":"Father: grieve but don't neglect duties","b":"Alphonse argues that excessive grief is selfish because it stops you from helping others. He's right, but he doesn't know the guilt behind Victor's despair.","lt":"Psych","l":"Complicated grief is grief that doesn't ease with time and disrupts daily life. Guilt makes grief harder to resolve, which is Victor's exact situation."},{"id":110,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[273,274],"start":11170.1,"t":11358.1,"from":"About this time we retired to our house at …","to":"… avenge the deaths of William and Justine.","q":"I wished to see him again, that I might wreak the utmost extent of abhorrence on his head","tag":"Theme","m":"Revenge begins; suicidal thoughts on lake","b":"Victor rows alone at night and considers drowning himself, but stays for his family. His hatred of the Creature grows, and revenge starts to define him.","lt":"Science","l":"Lake Geneva's curfew (gates closing at ten) was real: walled cities locked their gates at night for defense. Geneva's walls were only torn down in the 1850s."},{"id":111,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[275,276],"start":11358.5,"t":11527.4,"from":"Our house was the house of mourning. My father’s …","to":"… not change places with such a wretch.”","q":"he walks about the world free, and perhaps respected","tag":"Theme","m":"Elizabeth loses faith in humanity","b":"Elizabeth sees the world as full of wolves now. Her line about the murderer walking free 'and perhaps respected' unknowingly describes Victor himself.","lt":"Language","l":"Situational and dramatic irony together: Elizabeth's words accidentally point to the person beside her. The reader sees what she can't."},{"id":112,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[277,278],"start":11527.8,"t":11612.1,"from":"I listened to this discourse with the extremest agony. …","to":"… been near to rob me of her.","q":"I, not in deed, but in effect, was the true murderer","tag":"Theme","m":"Responsibility: deed vs. effect","b":"Victor distinguishes direct action from causing an outcome. It's a key question for essays: is Victor guilty of what his creation does?","lt":"Ethics","l":"Moral responsibility for consequences is debated in tech today: are social media companies, or AI makers, responsible for harms their products cause? This is the same 'in effect' question."},{"id":113,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[279,280],"start":11612.5,"t":11693.9,"from":"Thus not the tenderness of friendship, nor the beauty …","to":"… changed in those savage and enduring scenes.","q":"The wounded deer dragging its fainting limbs","tag":"Symbol","m":"Wounded deer simile: suffering alone","b":"Victor compares himself to a wounded deer dying alone. He then heads to the Alps for comfort in the 'savage and enduring' mountains.","lt":"Literature","l":"The wounded deer is a classic image of a lonely sufferer, used by Shakespeare ('As You Like It') and the poet William Cowper ('I was a stricken deer that left the herd')."},{"id":114,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[281,282],"start":11694.3,"t":11846.6,"from":"I performed the first part of my journey on …","to":"… and its tremendous _dôme_ overlooked the valley.","q":"Mont Blanc, the supreme and magnificent Mont Blanc","tag":"Setting","m":"Mont Blanc = sublime nature","b":"The journey to Chamounix shows the sublime Alps: glaciers, avalanches, Mont Blanc. Shelley drew on her own 1816 tour of the area.","lt":"Literature","l":"Percy Shelley wrote the poem 'Mont Blanc' on the same 1816 trip. The French words ('aiguilles', needles or sharp peaks; 'dôme') are mountaineering terms still used today."},{"id":115,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[283,284],"start":11847.0,"t":11942.3,"from":"A tingling long-lost sense of pleasure often came across …","to":"… came and blessed the giver of oblivion.","q":"maternal Nature bade me weep no more","tag":"Theme","m":"Nature as MOTHER: comforts Victor","b":"Nature is personified as a mother comforting Victor. That is ironic: Victor 'gave birth' without a mother, unnaturally. Nature heals what science broke, at least for a moment.","lt":"Science","l":"'Mother Nature' is an ancient personification (Greek Gaia). White noise and nature sounds really do help sleep; the rushing Arve acting as 'a lullaby' fits modern sleep science."},{"id":116,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[285,286],"start":11942.7,"t":12147.3,"from":"I spent the following day roaming through the valley. …","to":"… destroy the solitary grandeur of the scene.","q":"the presence of another would destroy the solitary grandeur of the scene","tag":"Setting","m":"Goes up glacier ALONE (isolation)","b":"Victor goes up Montanvert without a guide because he wants solitude. The glacier is cold, vast and lonely, the perfect stage for meeting the Creature.","lt":"Science","l":"Glaciers are rivers of ice that move slowly downhill under their own weight. The Mer de Glace ('Sea of Ice') near Chamonix is real, and has retreated about 2 km since Shelley's day due to climate change."},{"id":117,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[287,288],"start":12147.7,"t":12299.3,"from":"The ascent is precipitous, but the path is cut …","to":"… his morrow; Nought may endure but mutability!","q":"Nought may endure but mutability!","tag":"Allusion","m":"P. Shelley's 'Mutability': only change lasts","b":"Victor quotes 'Mutability', a poem by Percy Shelley (Mary's husband): the only constant is change. It fits a man whose life has been overturned.","lt":"Language","l":"'Mutability' means changeability (Latin mutare, to change). Related words include mutation, mutate and commute. The poem's idea parallels the Buddhist concept of impermanence."},{"id":118,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[289,290],"start":12299.7,"t":12478.4,"from":"It was nearly noon when I arrived at the …","to":"… words expressive of furious detestation and contempt.","q":"its unearthly ugliness rendered it almost too horrible for human eyes","tag":"Character","m":"★ Victor meets Creature on glacier","b":"The Creature bounds across the ice toward Victor. Victor describes him as 'it' and focuses only on his ugliness, then explodes in rage.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Pronouns reveal attitudes: Victor uses 'it', denying the Creature personhood. Track when characters say 'he' versus 'it' for the Creature; it's great essay evidence."},{"id":119,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[291,292],"start":12478.8,"t":12560.6,"from":"“Devil,” I exclaimed, “do you dare approach me? And …","to":"… with the blood of your remaining friends.”","q":"Begone, vile insect!","tag":"Character","m":"Victor's rage vs. Creature's calm speech","b":"Victor screams insults; the Creature replies calmly and eloquently. For the first time we hear the Creature speak, and he sounds more reasonable than his maker.","lt":"Language","l":"Contrast in dialogue reveals character. The Creature uses balanced, logical sentences and conditionals ('If you will comply… but if you refuse…'). Which of them sounds like the monster?"},{"id":120,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[293,296],"start":12561.0,"t":12681.5,"from":"“Abhorred monster! Fiend that thou art! The tortures of …","to":"… happy, and I shall again be virtuous.”","q":"I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend","tag":"Theme","m":"★ KEY QUOTE: nurture made him a monster","b":"The Creature's central argument: he was born good, and suffering made him evil. 'I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel.' He compares himself to characters from Paradise Lost.","lt":"Psych","l":"Nature vs. nurture: are we shaped by birth or experience? Rousseau argued humans are born good and corrupted by society. This line is the novel's clearest statement of that idea."},{"id":121,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[297,298],"start":12681.9,"t":12832.4,"from":"“Begone! I will not hear you. There can be …","to":"… will, destroy the work of your hands.”","q":"Oh, praise the eternal justice of man!","tag":"Irony","m":"Creature's sarcasm: human justice hypocrisy","b":"The Creature points out that humans condemn murder yet Victor would 'destroy your own creature' with a clear conscience. His sarcasm exposes human hypocrisy.","lt":"Language","l":"Verbal irony and sarcasm: saying the opposite of what you mean. 'Praise the eternal justice of man' actually condemns it. Tone of voice carries sarcasm in speech, while in writing context must do it."},{"id":122,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[299,300],"start":12832.8,"t":12931.8,"from":"“Why do you call to my remembrance,” I rejoined, …","to":"… the author of your own speedy ruin.”","q":"Cursed be the day, abhorred devil, in which you first saw light!","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature asks Victor to HEAR his story","b":"Victor curses the Creature's birth, the way Job curses his birthday in the Bible. The Creature asks only to be heard, and says Victor's choice will decide whether he lives harmlessly or becomes a scourge.","lt":"Ethics","l":"The right to be heard ('audi alteram partem', hear the other side) is a basic principle of fair justice. Justine got a trial; the Creature asks for the same."},{"id":123,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[301,301],"start":12932.2,"t":13010.9,"from":"As he said this he led the way across …","to":"… had lighted, he thus began his tale.","q":"compassion confirmed my resolution","tag":"Narration","m":"Victor agrees to listen: Creature's tale begins","b":"Victor feels compassion for the first time and realizes he owes his creation duties. The hut, the fire and a story by the fire set up the novel's innermost narrative.","lt":"Literature","l":"This is the center of the 'Chinese box' or nested structure: Walton → Victor → Creature. The Creature's story sits at the heart of the novel, both literally and morally."},{"id":124,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[302,303],"start":13011.3,"t":13165.2,"from":"“It is with considerable difficulty that I remember the …","to":"… all sides, I sat down and wept.","q":"I sat down and wept","tag":"Character","m":"Creature's birth: confused senses, weeps","b":"The Creature describes waking with confused senses, cold, hungry and alone. He is like a newborn abandoned in the woods. His first act of self-expression is crying.","lt":"Science","l":"Infants really do take months to sort their senses. William James called a baby's world 'one great blooming, buzzing confusion.' The Creature describes exactly that."},{"id":125,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[304,305],"start":13165.6,"t":13283.3,"from":"“Soon a gentle light stole over the heavens and …","to":"… from me frightened me into silence again.","q":"the uncouth and inarticulate sounds which broke from me frightened me into silence again","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature tries to sing like birds","b":"The Creature delights in the moon and birdsong and tries to imitate them. His gentle wonder contrasts with how others see him.","lt":"Science","l":"Language acquisition: babies babble before they speak and learn by imitation. The Creature has an adult body but goes through infant development stages."},{"id":126,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[306,307],"start":13283.7,"t":13404.9,"from":"“The moon had disappeared from the night, and again, …","to":"… on the ground and sank into sleep.","q":"I distinguished the insect from the herb","tag":"Theme","m":"Learns by observation (empiricism)","b":"The Creature learns through experience: he finds fire, learns it burns, and figures out how to keep it alive. His mind develops step by step through his senses.","lt":"History","l":"John Locke's 'tabula rasa' (blank slate, 1690) held that the mind starts empty and is filled by sense experience. The Creature's education reads like Locke's theory in action."},{"id":127,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[308,309],"start":13405.3,"t":13521.0,"from":"“It was morning when I awoke, and my first …","to":"… cold damp substance that covered the ground.","q":"I uncovered it, and a gentle breeze quickly fanned it into a flame","tag":"Symbol","m":"FIRE = Prometheus; gift & danger","b":"The Creature discovers fire: it gives warmth and light, and it burns him. Fire is the symbol of Prometheus (the novel's subtitle), with knowledge that helps and hurts.","lt":"Science","l":"Combustion needs heat, fuel and oxygen (the fire triangle). Fanning works because it adds oxygen. The Creature discovers basic chemistry by trial and error."},{"id":128,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[310,311],"start":13521.4,"t":13716.3,"from":"“It was about seven in the morning, and I …","to":"… agreeable asylum from the snow and rain.","q":"I perceived a small hut","tag":"Theme","m":"1st human contact: old man flees","b":"The shepherd flees in terror at the sight of him, and villagers attack him with stones. His first lessons about humans are violence and rejection, based purely on looks.","lt":"Psych","l":"Children learn social behavior through how others treat them (Bandura's social learning theory). The Creature's early 'lessons' teach him that humans are hostile."},{"id":129,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[312,313],"start":13716.7,"t":13812.3,"from":"“Here, then, I retreated and lay down happy to …","to":"… of the cottage it was tolerably warm.","q":"from the barbarity of man","tag":"Irony","m":"'barbarity of MAN': irony, humans = savage","b":"The Creature calls humans barbaric, reversing Walton's 'savage' label in Letter 4. He sleeps in a 'kennel', treated like an animal.","lt":"Language","l":"'Barbarian' comes from the Greek 'barbaros', meaning someone whose speech sounds like 'bar-bar' (a foreigner). It shows how labels of 'savage' often reflect the speaker's prejudice."},{"id":130,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[314,315],"start":13812.7,"t":14039.3,"from":"“Being thus provided, I resolved to reside in this …","to":"… the window, unable to bear these emotions.","q":"they were a mixture of pain and pleasure","tag":"Theme","m":"Watches De Laceys: learns emotion","b":"Through a chink in the wall he sees the old man play music and the girl weep. He feels empathy, a complex emotion, for the first time.","lt":"Psych","l":"Empathy is feeling what others feel. Mirror neurons are linked to it; we feel others' emotions by simulating them. The Creature's empathy proves he is deeply human."},{"id":131,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[316,317],"start":14039.7,"t":14146.5,"from":"“Soon after this the young man returned, bearing on …","to":"… morning, directed his steps across the fields.","q":"his eyes and attitude expressed the utmost sadness and despondency","tag":"Character","m":"Felix & Agatha introduced (sad, poor)","b":"The young man (Felix) and girl (Agatha) work hard and seem sad. The Creature watches them like a student and learns their routines.","lt":"Language","l":"The Creature is an outsider observer, like an anthropologist doing 'participant observation'. Writers use outsider narrators to make ordinary human life look strange and new."},{"id":132,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[318,319],"start":14146.9,"t":14220.2,"from":"“Night quickly shut in, but to my extreme wonder, …","to":"… and retired, as I conjectured, to rest.”","q":"I knew nothing of the science of words or letters","tag":"Theme","m":"Reading = a mystery to him","b":"The Creature sees Felix read aloud but doesn't understand it yet. Literacy will become his path to humanity, and to pain.","lt":"History","l":"Literacy rates in early 1800s Europe were low; many poor people couldn't read. Literacy was a marker of class and power, a theme also seen in Frederick Douglass's narrative (1845)."},{"id":133,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[320,322],"start":14220.6,"t":14310.7,"from":"“I lay on my straw, but I could not …","to":"… he rewarded them by his benevolent smiles.","q":"I longed to join them, but dared not","tag":"Theme","m":"Longs to join family; fears rejection","b":"The Creature admires the cottagers' gentleness and love for the old man. He wants to belong, but past violence makes him afraid.","lt":"Psych","l":"Social anxiety: fear of rejection based on past experience. The Creature's caution is learned, not innate."},{"id":134,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[323,324],"start":14311.1,"t":14420.0,"from":"“They were not entirely happy. The young man and …","to":"… man when they reserved none for themselves.","q":"If such lovely creatures were miserable","tag":"Theme","m":"Cottagers poor, sacrifice food for father","b":"The Creature realizes the family is poor and hungry; the young ones give their food to their father. He learns self-sacrifice by watching it.","lt":"History","l":"Rural poverty and food scarcity were common in early-1800s Europe. 1816, the year Shelley wrote Frankenstein, was the 'Year Without a Summer' (after the Tambora volcano), with famine across Europe."},{"id":135,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[325,327],"start":14420.4,"t":14494.1,"from":"“This trait of kindness moved me sensibly. I had …","to":"… repairing the cottage and cultivating the garden.","q":"I abstained and satisfied myself with berries, nuts, and roots","tag":"Character","m":"Creature stops stealing; secretly helps","b":"When he realizes stealing hurts them, he stops and secretly collects firewood for them. This is moral reasoning: he chooses kindness without being taught rules.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Kohlberg's stages of moral development: the Creature reasons from empathy (others' pain), not fear of punishment. That is an advanced moral stage."},{"id":136,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[328,329],"start":14494.5,"t":14699.8,"from":"“By degrees I made a discovery of still greater …","to":"… especially when he addressed the old man.","q":"This was indeed a godlike science","tag":"Theme","m":"Language = 'godlike science'","b":"The Creature discovers that words cause feelings. He calls language 'godlike', which parallels Victor calling his science godlike. Their ambitions differ: one wants connection, the other power.","lt":"Language","l":"Linguists call this pragmatics, how words act on listeners. Speech act theory (J.L. Austin) argues words DO things: promise, hurt, comfort."},{"id":137,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[330,331],"start":14700.2,"t":14828.1,"from":"“I could mention innumerable instances which, although slight, marked …","to":"… to my eyes had made me acquainted.","q":"which knowledge might enable me to make them overlook the deformity of my figure","tag":"Theme","m":"Plan: master language to be accepted","b":"The Creature hopes words can overcome his looks. He believes eloquence can defeat prejudice. Watch whether he's right.","lt":"Psych","l":"First impressions form in about a tenth of a second (Willis & Todorov, 2006). Overcoming a negative first impression takes much more effort than making a good one."},{"id":138,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[332,333],"start":14828.5,"t":14901.1,"from":"“I had admired the perfect forms of my cottagers—their …","to":"… comfort increased daily as the season advanced.","q":"I viewed myself in a transparent pool","tag":"Symbol","m":"Sees his reflection: self-hatred","b":"The Creature sees his reflection and is horrified to realize he is 'the monster'. He has learned to see himself through human eyes.","lt":"Literature","l":"This reverses the myth of Narcissus, who fell in love with his reflection in a pool. The Creature is repulsed by his own reflection, the first step in internalized self-hatred."},{"id":139,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[334,335],"start":14901.5,"t":14977.2,"from":"“The old man, leaning on his son, walked each …","to":"… then understand the signification of these terms.","q":"good spirit, wonderful","tag":"Irony","m":"Cottagers call him 'good spirit' (irony)","b":"The cottagers call their invisible helper a 'good spirit'. Unseen, the Creature is judged by his actions and seen as good. Seen, he would be judged by his looks.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Philosopher John Rawls proposed a 'veil of ignorance': judge fairly without knowing who people are. Invisible, the Creature is judged justly, which shows how looks distort judgment."},{"id":140,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[336,337],"start":14977.6,"t":15074.2,"from":"“My thoughts now became more active, and I longed …","to":"… deserved better treatment than blows and execration.","q":"It was as the ass and the lap-dog","tag":"Allusion","m":"Aesop fable: wrong way to seek affection","b":"The Creature compares his clumsy voice to the donkey in Aesop's fable, who copied a lap-dog's affection and got beaten. He worries his good intentions won't be enough.","lt":"Literature","l":"Aesop's fables (ancient Greece) teach morals through animals. In 'The Ass and the Lapdog', a donkey imitates a pet dog's affection and is beaten for it. The moral: you can't be what you're not."},{"id":141,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[338,338],"start":15074.6,"t":15132.3,"from":"“The pleasant showers and genial warmth of spring greatly …","to":"… rays of hope and anticipations of joy.”","q":"Happy, happy earth!","tag":"Setting","m":"Spring = hope (Creature's happiest moment)","b":"Spring brings hope; 'the past was blotted from my memory'. Like Victor at the end of Chapter 6, this happiness comes right before disaster.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Epizeuxis: repeating a word immediately ('Happy, happy') for emotional emphasis. Compare 'Never, never, never' in King Lear."},{"id":142,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[339,341],"start":15132.7,"t":15199.7,"from":"“I now hasten to the more moving part of …","to":"… music when someone tapped at the door.","q":"have made me what I am","tag":"Narration","m":"Creature signals a turning point","b":"The Creature warns that these events 'made me what I am'. He frames his story as cause and effect, the opposite of Victor's 'destiny' explanation.","lt":"Psych","l":"Developmental psychology studies how experiences shape personality. The Creature explains himself through experience; Victor explains himself through fate. Which is more convincing?"},{"id":143,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[342,343],"start":15200.1,"t":15322.3,"from":"“It was a lady on horseback, accompanied by a …","to":"… he raised her and embraced her affectionately.","q":"a thick black veil","tag":"Character","m":"Safie arrives (veiled stranger)","b":"A veiled woman arrives on horseback and says 'Felix'. This is Safie, whose arrival transforms the family.","lt":"History","l":"Veils were worn by many women in the Ottoman Empire, and also by European women for travel and mourning. Safie's veil marks her as foreign and mysterious."},{"id":144,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[344,345],"start":15322.7,"t":15438.0,"from":"“I soon perceived that although the stranger uttered articulate …","to":"… that purpose, but found it utterly impossible.","q":"as the sun dissipates the morning mists","tag":"Theme","m":"Language barrier: Safie can't speak French","b":"Safie doesn't speak French, so she, like the Creature, must learn the language. This is a perfect parallel: the Creature will learn alongside her.","lt":"Language","l":"Sign language and gesture are natural first bridges between languages. A pidgin is a simplified shared language that develops when groups without a common tongue must communicate."},{"id":145,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[346,347],"start":15438.4,"t":15498.1,"from":"“The next morning Felix went out to his work, …","to":"… him the greatest delight by her music.","q":"the Arabian sat at the feet of the old man","tag":"Theme","m":"Music = universal language","b":"Safie plays guitar and sings, moving everyone, including the Creature, to tears. Music communicates where words cannot.","lt":"Science","l":"Studies of music across 300+ cultures (Mehr et al., 2019) find universal patterns: lullabies and dance songs are recognizable worldwide. Music truly crosses languages."},{"id":146,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[348,349],"start":15498.5,"t":15562.5,"from":"“The days now passed as peaceably as before, with …","to":"… in the first village which I entered.","q":"she and I improved rapidly in the knowledge of language","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature learns via Safie's lessons","b":"The Creature learns French by eavesdropping on Safie's lessons, a secret classroom. He never goes out in daylight for fear of attack.","lt":"Science","l":"'Comprehensible input' (Stephen Krashen): we learn languages by hearing speech we can mostly understand. The Creature learns by listening, the way children do."},{"id":147,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[350,352],"start":15562.9,"t":15664.9,"from":"“My days were spent in close attention, that I …","to":"… the hapless fate of its original inhabitants.","q":"wept with Safie over the hapless fate of its original inhabitants","tag":"History","m":"Learns history via Volney's 'Ruins'","b":"Felix teaches from Volney's 'Ruins of Empires', a history of civilizations. The Creature weeps over the fate of Native Americans, showing empathy for other outsiders.","lt":"History","l":"Volney's 'Ruins of Empires' (1791) was a radical book arguing that empires fall through tyranny and inequality. Its account of colonial conquest shaped Romantic political thought."},{"id":148,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[353,354],"start":15665.3,"t":15754.0,"from":"“These wonderful narrations inspired me with strange feelings. Was …","to":"… poverty, of rank, descent, and noble blood.","q":"Was man, indeed, at once so powerful, so virtuous and magnificent, yet so vicious and base?","tag":"Theme","m":"Human nature = contradictory (good+evil)","b":"The Creature is shocked that humans can be both noble and evil. He also learns about property, wealth, rank and class, the structures that exclude him.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Philosophers debate whether humans are basically good (Rousseau) or selfish (Hobbes: life is 'nasty, brutish and short'). The Creature sees evidence for both."},{"id":149,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[355,356],"start":15754.4,"t":15851.8,"from":"“The words induced me to turn towards myself. I …","to":"… the sensations of hunger, thirst, and heat!","q":"sorrow only increased with knowledge","tag":"Theme","m":"Knowledge brings pain (Ecclesiastes)","b":"The Creature learns he has no family, money or name, so by society's rules he is nothing. Knowledge makes him miserable, echoing Victor's warning about knowledge.","lt":"Literature","l":"Ecclesiastes 1:18: 'He that increaseth knowledge increaseth sorrow.' Both Victor and the Creature find that knowing more makes them suffer more."},{"id":150,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[357,358],"start":15852.2,"t":15955.3,"from":"“Of what a strange nature is knowledge! It clings …","to":"… human being to another in mutual bonds.","q":"It clings to the mind when it has once seized on it like a lichen on the rock","tag":"Symbol","m":"Simile: knowledge = lichen (can't remove)","b":"Knowledge can't be unlearned. The Creature wishes he could forget. He learns about family bonds, parents and children, and realizes he has none.","lt":"Science","l":"Lichen is a symbiosis of a fungus and an alga (or cyanobacteria). It clings to bare rock and can live for centuries. That makes it an apt image for knowledge you can't scrape off."},{"id":151,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[359,360],"start":15955.7,"t":16030.9,"from":"“But where were my friends and relations? No father …","to":"… an innocent, half-painful self-deceit, to call them).”","q":"No father had watched my infant days, no mother had blessed me with smiles and caresses","tag":"Theme","m":"★ 'No father… no mother': abandoned child","b":"The Creature realizes he has no parents and no childhood. Compare this to Victor's loving childhood in Chapter 1. The contrast is deliberate. Then he asks the big question: 'What was I?'","lt":"Psych","l":"Children raised without consistent caregivers (as in Romanian orphanages studied in the 1990s) show lasting emotional effects. Early care shapes the developing brain."},{"id":152,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[361,362],"start":16031.3,"t":16093.3,"from":"“Some time elapsed before I learned the history of …","to":"… accompanied by a moderate fortune, could afford.","q":"The name of the old man was De Lacey","tag":"Character","m":"De Laceys were rich in Paris → fell","b":"The cottagers were once a wealthy, respected Paris family. Their fall from fortune parallels the novel's many falls, and shows that misfortune can strike the virtuous.","lt":"History","l":"Wealthy families could lose everything through political persecution in pre-Revolutionary and Revolutionary France. Shelley sets this in a world of unjust governments."},{"id":153,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[363,364],"start":16093.7,"t":16209.0,"from":"“The father of Safie had been the cause of …","to":"… would fully reward his toil and hazard.","q":"He was a Turkish merchant","tag":"Theme","m":"Safie's father unjustly imprisoned","b":"Safie's father is condemned, the Creature suspects, because of his religion and wealth, not his guilt. Injustice by prejudice again, like Justine and the Creature.","lt":"History","l":"The Ottoman Empire (Turkey) and Christian Europe had centuries of conflict and trade. Muslims in Europe often faced religious prejudice, a backdrop that shapes this subplot."},{"id":154,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[365,366],"start":16209.4,"t":16267.6,"from":"“The Turk quickly perceived the impression that his daughter …","to":"… time she gently deplored her own fate.","q":"Felix was too delicate to accept this offer","tag":"Character","m":"Felix helps Turk; Safie = 'reward'?","b":"Felix rescues the merchant out of justice, then falls for Safie. The father offers his daughter as payment, treating her as property, like Victor's 'gift' of Elizabeth.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Arranged marriages and daughters as 'payment' reflect a time when women had few legal rights. Mary Wollstonecraft's 'A Vindication of the Rights of Woman' (1792) attacked this."},{"id":155,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[367,368],"start":16268.0,"t":16366.8,"from":"“I have copies of these letters, for I found …","to":"… rank in society was enchanting to her.","q":"women were allowed to take a rank in society","tag":"Character","m":"Safie = independent woman (mother's lessons)","b":"Safie's Christian Arab mother taught her to aspire to independence of mind. Safie is the most independent woman in the novel; she later travels alone across Europe.","lt":"History","l":"Mary Shelley's mother, Wollstonecraft, argued women should be educated as rational beings. Safie's mother's teaching echoes Wollstonecraft's ideas directly."},{"id":156,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[369,371],"start":16367.2,"t":16452.4,"from":"“The day for the execution of the Turk was …","to":"… the divine airs of her native country.","q":"Felix had procured passports in the name of his father, sister, and himself","tag":"History","m":"Felix's prison-break plan","b":"Felix helps the Turk escape the night before his execution. His virtue involves breaking an unjust law. Is that right? The novel suggests yes.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Civil disobedience means breaking unjust laws on principle. Thoreau (1849) and Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) argue there's a duty to disobey unjust laws."},{"id":157,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[372,373],"start":16452.8,"t":16563.8,"from":"“The Turk allowed this intimacy to take place and …","to":"… De Lacey and Agatha by this proceeding.","q":"he had formed far other plans","tag":"Theme","m":"Turk betrays Felix = ingratitude","b":"The Turk secretly plans to break his promise. Felix turns himself in to free his family. Ingratitude is one of the cruelest acts in this novel; the Creature notes it carefully.","lt":"Language","l":"'Far other plans' is an older construction meaning 'very different plans'. Modern English would say 'quite different plans'."},{"id":158,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[374,376],"start":16564.2,"t":16641.8,"from":"“He did not succeed. They remained confined for five …","to":"… now infused new life into his soul.","q":"condemned them to a perpetual exile","tag":"Theme","m":"De Laceys exiled & poor for doing right","b":"The family loses its fortune and is exiled for helping an innocent man. Doing good was punished, which complicates the Creature's lessons about virtue.","lt":"History","l":"Exile was a common punishment for political offenses. Victor Hugo spent 19 years in exile from France, and Napoleon was exiled twice (Elba, then St Helena)."},{"id":159,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[377,378],"start":16642.2,"t":16701.1,"from":"“When the news reached Leghorn that Felix was deprived …","to":"… which had not yet arrived at Leghorn.","q":"The generous nature of Safie was outraged by this command","tag":"Character","m":"Safie defies father (agency)","b":"Safie refuses her father's command and protests. Unlike Elizabeth or Justine, she acts on her own judgment.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'Expostulate' means to argue strongly in protest. Formal Latin-root verbs like this (ex- + postulare, to demand) were typical of educated 19th-century prose."},{"id":160,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[379,380],"start":16701.5,"t":16795.2,"from":"“When alone, Safie resolved in her own mind the …","to":"… safety at the cottage of her lover.”","q":"A residence in Turkey was abhorrent to her","tag":"Character","m":"Safie travels alone to find Felix","b":"Safie escapes with jewels and money, travels across Europe with a servant who dies, and still finds Felix. She is brave, resourceful and self-directed.","lt":"History","l":"Women travelling alone was rare and risky then. Mary Wollstonecraft travelled through Scandinavia with her baby in 1795 and wrote a travel book about it, an inspiration for Safie."},{"id":161,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[381,383],"start":16795.6,"t":16895.3,"from":"“Such was the history of my beloved cottagers. It …","to":"… friends were employed in their ordinary occupations.","q":"Paradise Lost","tag":"Allusion","m":"3 books: Paradise Lost, Plutarch, Werter","b":"The Creature finds three books that shape his worldview. Each teaches him something different about emotion, virtue and his own origin.","lt":"Literature","l":"Paradise Lost (Milton, 1667) is an epic of Satan's fall and Adam's creation. Plutarch's Lives (around 100 CE) are biographies of Greek and Roman leaders. The Sorrows of Young Werther (Goethe, 1774) tells of a sensitive young man's despair."},{"id":162,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[384,385],"start":16895.7,"t":17035.5,"from":"“I can hardly describe to you the effect of …","to":"… but I was unable to solve them.","q":"Who was I? What was I? Whence did I come? What was my destination?","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Identity questions (existential)","b":"Werther teaches him about feeling and death. Then he asks the deepest questions: who, what, where from, where to. These are the questions of every human being.","lt":"Grammar","l":"A series of short rhetorical questions creates urgency. In philosophy these are existential questions; existentialists like Sartre argued we must create our own meaning."},{"id":163,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[386,387],"start":17035.9,"t":17229.1,"from":"“The volume of Plutarch’s Lives which I possessed contained …","to":"… bitter gall of envy rose within me.","q":"Many times I considered Satan as the fitter emblem of my condition","tag":"Allusion","m":"Creature = Adam? No, Satan (envy)","b":"Like Adam he was created alone, but Adam had a loving Creator. Like Satan he feels envy at others' bliss. Victor, by abandoning him, has turned an Adam into a Satan.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Paradise Lost, Satan says 'Myself am Hell' and envies Adam and Eve's happiness in Eden. The Creature identifies with Satan's alienation, not his evil."},{"id":164,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[388,389],"start":17229.5,"t":17394.1,"from":"“Another circumstance strengthened and confirmed these feelings. Soon after …","to":"… months should have added to my sagacity.","q":"I discovered some papers in the pocket of the dress which I had taken from your laboratory","tag":"Theme","m":"Reads Victor's journal = learns truth","b":"The Creature finds Victor's lab journal in the coat he took and learns exactly how he was made and how Victor was disgusted by him. It's the cruelest kind of knowledge.","lt":"Psych","l":"Discovering you were unwanted is devastating. Adoption researchers note that how origin stories are told to children deeply affects their self-worth."},{"id":165,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[390,391],"start":17394.5,"t":17499.0,"from":"“Several changes, in the meantime, took place in the …","to":"… bitterness of my heart I cursed him.","q":"no Eve soothed my sorrows nor shared my thoughts","tag":"Theme","m":"No Eve: Creature needs a companion","b":"Adam had Eve; the Creature has no one. 'Where was mine?' His Creator abandoned him, and he curses Victor. This sets up his demand for a female companion.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Genesis 2:18 God says 'It is not good that the man should be alone' and creates Eve. The Creature asks for the same mercy God showed Adam."},{"id":166,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[392,393],"start":17499.4,"t":17652.8,"from":"“Autumn passed thus. I saw, with surprise and grief, …","to":"… means be tolerated by my younger protectors.","q":"if in the absence of his children I could gain the good will and mediation of the old De Lacey","tag":"Theme","m":"Plan: approach BLIND De Lacey first","b":"The Creature's plan is clever: the blind father can't see his face and will judge only his words. It's a test of whether character can win over prejudice.","lt":"Ethics","l":"'Blind' auditions: orchestras that started auditioning musicians behind screens (from the 1970s) hired far more women. Removing sight removes bias, exactly the Creature's logic."},{"id":167,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[394,395],"start":17653.2,"t":17741.9,"from":"“One day, when the sun shone on the red …","to":"… I approached the door of their cottage.","q":"with renewed determination I approached the door of their cottage","tag":"Character","m":"Courage: Creature approaches cottage","b":"The old man plays a sad song. The Creature nearly loses courage but gathers his strength. This is the most suspenseful moment of his story.","lt":"Psych","l":"Rejection sensitivity is the anxious expectation of rejection. People who have been rejected often freeze before reaching out; the Creature's hesitation is deeply human."},{"id":168,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[396,400],"start":17742.3,"t":17802.8,"from":"“I knocked. ‘Who is there?’ said the old man. …","to":"… interview, when the old man addressed me.","q":"I am a traveller in want of a little rest","tag":"Character","m":"Creature speaks politely to De Lacey","b":"The Creature introduces himself with perfect manners. Without his appearance, he is simply a polite traveller, and De Lacey welcomes him warmly.","lt":"Language","l":"Polite forms ('you would greatly oblige me if…') use conditional mood to soften requests. Linguists call this negative politeness: avoiding imposing on the listener."},{"id":169,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[401,405],"start":17803.2,"t":17867.6,"from":"‘By your language, stranger, I suppose you are my …","to":"… are good and amiable, do not despair.’","q":"the hearts of men, when unprejudiced by any obvious self-interest, are full of brotherly love and charity","tag":"Irony","m":"De Lacey: people are good (irony!)","b":"De Lacey believes people are basically kind. He is about to be proven tragically wrong by his own son.","lt":"Ethics","l":"This reflects Enlightenment optimism about human nature. Compare Anne Frank: 'In spite of everything, I still believe that people are really good at heart.' Is the novel optimistic or pessimistic?"},{"id":170,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[406,410],"start":17868.0,"t":17926.3,"from":"“‘They are kind—they are the most excellent creatures in …","to":"“‘Near this spot.’","q":"a fatal prejudice clouds their eyes","tag":"Theme","m":"★ 'Fatal prejudice clouds their eyes'","b":"The Creature describes his whole problem: people see 'a detestable monster' instead of a kind friend. It's a statement about prejudice itself.","lt":"History","l":"Prejudice based on appearance includes racism and discrimination against people with disabilities or disfigurements. Some critics read the Creature as a symbol of any marginalized group."},{"id":171,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[411,413],"start":17926.7,"t":17994.2,"from":"“The old man paused and then continued, ‘If you …","to":"… I do not feel for your misfortunes.’","q":"I am blind and cannot judge of your countenance","tag":"Theme","m":"Blind man = only one to see truly","b":"De Lacey, who can't see, is the only person who judges the Creature fairly. There's a paradox: the blind man sees the truth that sighted people miss.","lt":"Literature","l":"The wise blind figure is ancient: Tiresias, the blind prophet in Oedipus Rex, sees the truth others can't. Shelley uses the same paradox."},{"id":172,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[414,416],"start":17994.6,"t":18057.0,"from":"“‘How can I thank you, my best and only …","to":"… desert me in the hour of trial!’","q":"From your lips first have I heard the voice of kindness directed towards me","tag":"Theme","m":"1st kindness ever: from a blind man","b":"This is the first time anyone has spoken kindly to him. Then the family returns, and he begs De Lacey: 'Save and protect me!'","lt":"Psych","l":"Research finds a single supportive adult can change a struggling child's life ('Harvard Center on the Developing Child'). De Lacey nearly becomes that person."},{"id":173,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[417,418],"start":18057.4,"t":18127.5,"from":"“‘Great God!’ exclaimed the old man. ‘Who are you?’","to":"… general tumult escaped unperceived to my hovel.”","q":"Who can describe their horror and consternation on beholding me?","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Rejected by De Laceys: turning point","b":"Agatha faints, Safie flees, and Felix beats him. The Creature could have torn Felix apart but refrains. This rejection is the turning point that breaks his hope.","lt":"Psych","l":"Neuroscientist Naomi Eisenberger found social rejection activates the same brain areas as physical pain. 'Hurt feelings' are literally pain."},{"id":174,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[419,420],"start":18127.9,"t":18214.0,"from":"“Cursed, cursed creator! Why did I live? Why, in …","to":"… have sat down and enjoyed the ruin.","q":"I, like the arch-fiend, bore a hell within me","tag":"Allusion","m":"Creature becomes Satan: rage begins","b":"Now he truly identifies with Satan ('the arch-fiend'). His rage turns outward: he wants to destroy. Notice the shift from victim to avenger.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Paradise Lost, Satan says 'Which way I fly is Hell; myself am Hell.' Hell as an inner state, not a place, is a powerful idea the Creature borrows."},{"id":175,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[421,423],"start":18214.4,"t":18313.0,"from":"“But this was a luxury of sensation that could …","to":"… my representations win him to my party.","q":"I did not believe my errors to be irretrievable","tag":"Character","m":"Still hopeful: plans to try again","b":"Even after the beating, he blames his own timing and hopes to try again. His persistence is heartbreaking.","lt":"Psych","l":"Self-blame after rejection is common; people often think 'I did it wrong' rather than 'they were unfair'. That protects hope but harms self-esteem."},{"id":176,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[424,425],"start":18313.4,"t":18377.4,"from":"“These thoughts calmed me, and in the afternoon I …","to":"… cannot describe the agony of this suspense.","q":"I cannot describe the agony of this suspense","tag":"Narration","m":"Suspense: cottagers don't come out","b":"He waits all morning and the cottage stays silent. Shelley stretches the suspense.","lt":"Language","l":"Pacing slows in suspense scenes: short events stretched across longer descriptions. Filmmakers do the same with slow motion and lingering shots."},{"id":177,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[426,428],"start":18377.8,"t":18451.9,"from":"“Presently two countrymen passed by, but pausing near the …","to":"… and let me fly from this place.’","q":"Take possession of your tenement and let me fly from this place","tag":"Theme","m":"De Laceys flee forever","b":"Felix breaks the lease and leaves. His sister and Safie 'will never recover from their horror'. The Creature's one hope is gone forever.","lt":"Language","l":"'Tenement' originally meant any rented dwelling; today it suggests a run-down apartment building. This is another example of words changing meaning over time."},{"id":178,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[429,430],"start":18452.3,"t":18531.6,"from":"“Felix trembled violently as he said this. He and …","to":"… moon had sunk to commence my operations.","q":"I placed a variety of combustibles around the cottage","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature's 1st destructive act (no harm to ppl)","b":"In rage he destroys the garden and prepares to burn the cottage. It's destruction of property, not people, at least for now.","lt":"Psych","l":"Displaced aggression: anger aimed at something other than its true cause. He can't punish the family, so he attacks objects."},{"id":179,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[431,432],"start":18532.0,"t":18592.5,"from":"“As the night advanced, a fierce wind arose from …","to":"… and sought for refuge in the woods.","q":"licked it with their forked and destroying tongues","tag":"Symbol","m":"Burns cottage: fire = destruction","b":"Fire, which once gave him warmth and comfort, now destroys. The Promethean gift turns violent.","lt":"Language","l":"Personification and metaphor: flames 'licked' with 'forked tongues', like serpents. It links the fire back to the serpent of Eden."},{"id":180,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[433,434],"start":18592.9,"t":18693.2,"from":"“And now, with the world before me, whither should …","to":"… other being that wore the human form.","q":"I learned from your papers that you were my father","tag":"Theme","m":"Seeks Victor: 'my father,' 'my creator'","b":"The Creature turns to his only possible family: Victor. He seeks justice from the one being who owes him care.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Parental responsibility is a legal and moral duty. Laws on child abandonment and child support exist because society agrees parents owe their children care."},{"id":181,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[435,436],"start":18693.6,"t":18836.2,"from":"“My travels were long and the sufferings I endured …","to":"… sun, which bestowed such joy upon me.","q":"dared to be happy","tag":"Setting","m":"Spring brings brief joy again","b":"Travelling to Geneva at night, he suffers in winter, but spring makes him briefly happy again. Nature still moves him.","lt":"Science","l":"Seasonal affective disorder (SAD) shows light and seasons affect mood; spring sunlight raises serotonin. The Creature's spring joy is biologically real."},{"id":182,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[437,438],"start":18836.6,"t":18960.4,"from":"“I continued to wind among the paths of the …","to":"… me; my pulses paused, and I fainted.","q":"I vowed eternal hatred and vengeance to all mankind","tag":"Theme","m":"Saves girl → SHOT. Vows revenge on all","b":"He rescues a drowning girl, and her companion shoots him. This is the final betrayal: his good deed is punished, and he swears eternal vengeance.","lt":"Ethics","l":"'No good deed goes unpunished' is an ironic saying. Good Samaritan laws now protect people who help in emergencies from being sued, recognizing helpers shouldn't be harmed."},{"id":183,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[439,440],"start":18960.8,"t":19015.3,"from":"“For some weeks I led a miserable life in …","to":"… not made for the enjoyment of pleasure.","q":"I was not made for the enjoyment of pleasure","tag":"Theme","m":"Wounded; joy becomes a 'mockery'","b":"With a bullet in his shoulder, he concludes he wasn't made for happiness. His hatred is now settling into his identity.","lt":"Psych","l":"Learned helplessness (Martin Seligman): after repeated bad outcomes, people stop believing they can change anything. The Creature has stopped expecting good."},{"id":184,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[441,443],"start":19015.7,"t":19081.4,"from":"“But my toils now drew near a close, and …","to":"… be so desolate in this peopled earth.","q":"this little creature was unprejudiced","tag":"Theme","m":"Hopes a CHILD won't be prejudiced","b":"He sees a boy and reasons that a child is too young to have learned prejudice. He plans to raise the boy as a friend. His logic is innocent, but his methods are not.","lt":"Psych","l":"Research suggests prejudice is learned: babies show some preferences early, but stronger bias develops through social learning. The Creature's theory is partly right."},{"id":185,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[444,448],"start":19081.8,"t":19144.2,"from":"“Urged by this impulse, I seized on the boy …","to":"… revenge; you shall be my first victim.’","q":"My papa is a syndic—he is M. Frankenstein","tag":"Theme","m":"The boy is WILLIAM Frankenstein","b":"The child screams 'monster' and names his father: Frankenstein. The coincidence turns the Creature's hope into revenge. Notice that the child already has the prejudice he hoped wouldn't exist.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Dashes in dialogue show a speaker rushing, breaking off or panicking. William's speech is fragmented: 'My papa is a syndic—he is M. Frankenstein—he will punish you.'"},{"id":186,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[449,451],"start":19144.6,"t":19217.2,"from":"“The child still struggled and loaded me with epithets …","to":"… to one expressive of disgust and affright.","q":"I grasped his throat to silence him, and in a moment he lay dead at my feet","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Creature kills William; takes locket","b":"The murder is quick, and the Creature feels 'hellish triumph': he can create desolation too. He takes the miniature of Caroline and is moved, then enraged, by her beauty.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Is the Creature fully responsible? Courts consider 'mitigating circumstances', factors like abuse or provocation that reduce blame without excusing a crime."},{"id":187,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[452,453],"start":19217.6,"t":19279.8,"from":"“Can you wonder that such thoughts transported me with …","to":"… affection from thine eyes; my beloved, awake!’","q":"Awake, fairest, thy lover is near","tag":"Character","m":"Finds sleeping Justine in barn","b":"He finds Justine asleep and whispers to her like a lover. He longs for the affection he knows she would never give him.","lt":"Language","l":"'Thy' and 'thine' are archaic second-person forms (informal 'you'). Ironically, they were once used for intimacy, as here, and also in prayer to God."},{"id":188,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[454,455],"start":19280.2,"t":19384.2,"from":"“The sleeper stirred; a thrill of terror ran through …","to":"… same defects. This being you must create.”","q":"This being you must create","tag":"Theme","m":"Frames Justine; DEMANDS a female mate","b":"He plants the miniature on Justine, deliberately framing her: 'not I, but she, shall suffer.' Then he makes his demand: create a female companion like himself.","lt":"Ethics","l":"This is the novel's ethical dilemma: should Victor create a second being? Critics debate whether the Creature's request is just, or whether creating a mate risks a 'race of devils'."},{"id":189,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[456,459],"start":19384.6,"t":19459.0,"from":"The being finished speaking and fixed his looks upon …","to":"… torture me, but I will never consent.”","q":"you may torture me, but I will never consent","tag":"Character","m":"Victor refuses at first","b":"Victor refuses, fearing 'joint wickedness'. His refusal is based on fear of what two Creatures might do.","lt":"Science","l":"This echoes debates on de-extinction and genetic engineering: should scientists create new species? What if they reproduce? Ecologists warn of invasive species."},{"id":190,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[460,461],"start":19459.4,"t":19553.3,"from":"“You are in the wrong,” replied the fiend; “and …","to":"… but presently he calmed himself and proceeded—","q":"I am malicious because I am miserable","tag":"Theme","m":"★ 'Malicious because miserable'","b":"The Creature's thesis in one line: his evil is caused by his misery. Treat him well, and he'd be good. He threatens to 'desolate' Victor's heart if refused.","lt":"Psych","l":"The frustration–aggression hypothesis (Dollard, 1939): blocked goals and suffering lead to aggression. Criminologists study how deprivation and exclusion predict violence."},{"id":191,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[462,463],"start":19553.7,"t":19653.6,"from":"“I intended to reason. This passion is detrimental to …","to":"… saw my change of feeling and continued,","q":"I would make peace with the whole kind!","tag":"Theme","m":"One kindness = peace with all mankind","b":"For one creature's love, he'd forgive everyone. Victor starts to feel he does owe his creation some happiness, and his position begins to change.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Hyperbole and repetition ('a hundred and a hundredfold') show intense emotion. Italic 'you' shows emphasis; in speech this would be vocal stress."},{"id":192,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[464,465],"start":19654.0,"t":19748.2,"from":"“If you consent, neither you nor any other human …","to":"… argue the point, for I cannot consent.”","q":"I will go to the vast wilds of South America","tag":"Character","m":"Promises exile to S. America; vegetarian","b":"He promises to leave humanity forever and live peacefully on acorns and berries. He is vegetarian and gentle by nature, which undercuts the 'monster' label.","lt":"History","l":"Percy Shelley wrote pamphlets promoting vegetarianism (1813). South America represented untamed wilderness to Europeans; Humboldt's explorations there (1799–1804) were famous."},{"id":193,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[466,467],"start":19748.6,"t":19818.5,"from":"“How inconstant are your feelings! But a moment ago …","to":"… was yet in my power to bestow.","q":"How inconstant are your feelings!","tag":"Character","m":"Victor wavers: sympathy vs. disgust","b":"Victor feels sympathy when he listens, but disgust when he looks. Sight overpowers reason, the same pattern as everyone else in the novel.","lt":"Psych","l":"Cognitive dissonance (Festinger, 1957): holding two conflicting beliefs causes discomfort. Victor flips between pity and hatred because he can't hold both."},{"id":194,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[468,469],"start":19818.9,"t":19875.1,"from":"“You swear,” I said, “to be harmless; but have …","to":"… events from which I am now excluded.”","q":"I shall feel the affections of a sensitive being and become linked to the chain of existence","tag":"Theme","m":"Wants to be 'linked to chain of existence'","b":"The Creature argues that companionship will make him virtuous; isolation is what made him vicious. He wants to belong to the community of living things.","lt":"History","l":"The 'Great Chain of Being' was a medieval and Renaissance idea ranking all things from God to rocks. The Creature has no place in the chain, which is a profound exclusion."},{"id":195,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[470,471],"start":19875.5,"t":19946.9,"from":"I paused some time to reflect on all he …","to":"… who will accompany you in your exile.”","q":"I consent to your demand","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor AGREES to make a female","b":"Victor agrees, with conditions: the pair must leave Europe forever. He reasons that justice requires it. Note it as a moment of moral growth that he will later reverse.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Contract ethics: promises create obligations. When Victor breaks this promise later, is he justified by future risk, or is he betraying a just agreement?"},{"id":196,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[472,474],"start":19947.3,"t":20065.1,"from":"“I swear,” he cried, “by the sun, and by …","to":"… depart, depart, and leave me in darkness.”","q":"by the sun, and by the blue sky of heaven, and by the fire of love that burns my heart","tag":"Character","m":"Creature swears an oath (polysyndeton)","b":"The Creature swears by nature and love. Victor, alone on the mountain, despairs, and the stars seem to mock him.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Polysyndeton: repeating 'and' ('by the sun, and by… and by…') to slow down and add solemnity. The opposite is asyndeton (leaving out 'and'): 'I came, I saw, I conquered.'"},{"id":197,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[475,476],"start":20065.5,"t":20153.1,"from":"These were wild and miserable thoughts, but I cannot …","to":"… had to me the reality of life.","q":"I resolved to dedicate myself to my most abhorred task","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Victor returns home; dreads the task","b":"Victor returns home haggard and resolves to do the work to save his family. But he dreads it, and that dread will grow into procrastination and fear.","lt":"Language","l":"A 'siroc' (sirocco) is a hot Mediterranean wind from the Sahara. Shelley uses vivid, exotic vocabulary to describe Victor's mental storm."},{"id":198,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[477,478],"start":20153.5,"t":20277.6,"from":"Day after day, week after week, passed away on …","to":"… father, calling me aside, thus addressed me,","q":"I could not collect the courage to recommence my work","tag":"Character","m":"Victor procrastinates: dread & delay","b":"Weeks pass and Victor can't start. His avoidance of responsibility is a pattern: he delays, hides, and hopes problems vanish.","lt":"Psych","l":"Procrastination is often emotional avoidance, not laziness: we delay tasks that trigger negative feelings (Tim Pychyl's research). Victor's dread is extreme procrastination."},{"id":199,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[479,481],"start":20278.0,"t":20363.1,"from":"“I am happy to remark, my dear son, that …","to":"… poignant misery which you appear to feel.”","q":"considering yourself as bound in honour to Elizabeth","tag":"Character","m":"Father thinks V. doesn't want to marry E.","b":"Alphonse misreads Victor's gloom as reluctance to marry Elizabeth. Because Victor keeps secrets, everyone misunderstands him.","lt":"Language","l":"Misunderstanding drives plots in comedy and tragedy alike. Shakespeare's plots (e.g. Romeo and Juliet) often hinge on information one character has and another lacks."},{"id":200,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[482,483],"start":20363.5,"t":20451.2,"from":"“My dear father, reassure yourself. I love my cousin …","to":"… I conjure you, with confidence and sincerity.”","q":"My future hopes and prospects are entirely bound up in the expectation of our union","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor says he loves Elizabeth","b":"Victor insists he loves Elizabeth. Alphonse proposes an immediate wedding. Victor's secret makes the happy plan feel ominous.","lt":"History","l":"Marrying a cousin or adopted sibling was legal and fairly common in 1800s Europe; Charles Darwin married his first cousin. Attitudes have shifted since."},{"id":201,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[484,485],"start":20451.6,"t":20592.0,"from":"I listened to my father in silence and remained …","to":"… an end to my slavery for ever.","q":"some accident might meanwhile occur to destroy him","tag":"Character","m":"Victor wishes problem would vanish","b":"Victor wants to finish his task first, or hopes the monster will simply die. Wishful thinking replaces action again.","lt":"Psych","l":"Magical thinking is believing a problem will resolve itself without action. It's a common coping strategy that rarely works."},{"id":202,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[486,487],"start":20592.4,"t":20691.3,"from":"These feelings dictated my answer to my father. I …","to":"… my task or to contemplate its progress?","q":"I expressed a wish to visit England","tag":"Theme","m":"Goes to England (hides real reason)","b":"Victor lies about his reasons for travelling; he needs English scientists' knowledge. Clerval will join him. Victor even hopes Henry will shield him from the monster.","lt":"Science","l":"Britain was a world center of science around 1800: the Royal Society, Humphry Davy's electrochemistry, and the Royal Institution's public lectures drew crowds."},{"id":203,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[488,489],"start":20691.7,"t":20781.2,"from":"To England, therefore, I was bound, and it was …","to":"… family from the danger of his machinations.","q":"the fiend would follow me and exempt my family from the danger of his machinations","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Victor assumes he's the only target ✗","b":"Victor assumes the monster will follow him and leave his family alone. He thinks only of himself as the target, a fatal misjudgment.","lt":"Psych","l":"Egocentric bias: overestimating your own centrality to events. Victor's misreading of the Creature's threats will cost Clerval and Elizabeth their lives."},{"id":204,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[490,491],"start":20781.6,"t":20857.0,"from":"It was in the latter end of September that …","to":"… was to occupy me whilst they endured.","q":"my eyes were fixed and unobserving","tag":"Setting","m":"Can't enjoy scenery (vs. Clerval)","b":"Victor travels through beautiful scenery but sees nothing, consumed by his task. Compare Chapter 4, where obsession blinded him to nature too.","lt":"Language","l":"'Bourne' means destination or limit. Hamlet's 'undiscovered country from whose bourn no traveller returns' (death) uses the same word, a subtle death hint."},{"id":205,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[492,493],"start":20857.4,"t":20994.7,"from":"After some days spent in listless indolence, during which …","to":"… river and populous towns occupy the scene.","q":"Alas, how great was the contrast between us!","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval joyful vs. Victor gloomy","b":"Clerval is 'alive to every new scene'; Victor is dead to them. Clerval represents the person Victor could have been.","lt":"Science","l":"The Rhine Gorge (castles, vineyards, cliffs) is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The Romantic 'Rhine journey' became popular partly through writers like Byron and Shelley."},{"id":206,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[494,494],"start":20995.1,"t":21138.0,"from":"We travelled at the time of the vintage and …","to":"… of the mountains of our own country.”","q":"even I was pleased","tag":"Setting","m":"Rhine journey: brief peace","b":"Floating down the Rhine during the harvest, even Victor feels some pleasure. Clerval's speech about the friendly spirit of the place shows his poetic soul.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Repetition of 'even I' emphasizes how unlikely his pleasure is. Writers repeat a phrase to stress contrast or surprise."},{"id":207,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[495,496],"start":21138.4,"t":21208.8,"from":"Clerval! Beloved friend! Even now it delights me to …","to":"… or any interest Unborrow’d from the eye.","q":"He was a being formed in the","tag":"Allusion","m":"Tribute to Clerval + Wordsworth quote","b":"Victor praises Clerval and quotes Wordsworth's 'Tintern Abbey' about loving nature for itself. The past tense hints that Clerval is gone.","lt":"Literature","l":"'Tintern Abbey' (1798) by Wordsworth celebrates nature's power to restore the mind. Victor uses it to describe Clerval, who lives in harmony with nature as Victor cannot."},{"id":208,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[497,500],"start":21209.2,"t":21276.8,"from":"And where does he now exist? Is this gentle …","to":"… river was too gentle to aid us.","q":"And where does he now exist?","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Victor mourns Clerval (death foreshadowed)","b":"Victor breaks off his tale to mourn Henry, revealing in advance that Clerval will die. We now read the rest of the journey with dread.","lt":"Language","l":"Prolepsis (a flash-forward) reveals a future event early. It shifts suspense from 'what happens?' to 'how does it happen?'"},{"id":209,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[501,502],"start":21277.2,"t":21330.8,"from":"Our journey here lost the interest arising from beautiful …","to":"… and the Tower famed in English history.","q":"I first saw the white cliffs of Britain","tag":"Setting","m":"Arrive in England: history tour","b":"Arriving in England, they see sites of English history (Tilbury, Greenwich, St Paul's, the Tower). Clerval loves history; Victor sees only his task.","lt":"History","l":"Tilbury Fort is where Queen Elizabeth I gave her famous speech to troops facing the Spanish Armada (1588): 'I have the heart and stomach of a king.'"},{"id":210,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[503,504],"start":21331.2,"t":21423.8,"from":"London was our present point of rest; we determined …","to":"… those names filled my soul with anguish.","q":"I saw an insurmountable barrier placed between me and my fellow men","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor isolated by guilt in London","b":"In London Victor avoids people. His guilt creates a 'barrier sealed with blood' between him and humanity, the same isolation the Creature feels.","lt":"Psych","l":"Guilt and shame differ: guilt says 'I did something bad'; shame says 'I am bad' (Brené Brown). Shame isolates. Victor feels both."},{"id":211,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[505,506],"start":21424.2,"t":21558.8,"from":"But in Clerval I saw the image of my …","to":"… with which Nature adorns her chosen dwelling-places.","q":"in Clerval I saw the image of my former self","tag":"Theme","m":"Clerval = Victor's former self (double)","b":"Victor sees his younger, curious self in Clerval. Clerval plans to work in India, using languages to help. His ambition is healthy and outward-looking.","lt":"History","l":"The British East India Company ruled much of India by 1800. Clerval's plan to 'assist the progress of European colonization' reflects attitudes we now critique as imperialist."},{"id":212,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[507,508],"start":21559.2,"t":21616.5,"from":"We had arrived in England at the beginning of …","to":"… stately deer were all novelties to us.","q":"the majestic oaks, the quantity of game, and the herds of stately deer","tag":"Setting","m":"Travel north via Windsor","b":"They plan a scenic route north. Windsor's forest delights them. These travel sections slow the story before the crisis.","lt":"History","l":"Windsor Castle has been a royal residence for over 900 years; Windsor Great Park was a royal hunting forest. Mary Shelley lived near Windsor while writing parts of her work."},{"id":213,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[509,510],"start":21616.9,"t":21751.4,"from":"From thence we proceeded to Oxford. As we entered …","to":"… pitiable to others and intolerable to myself.","q":"But I am a blasted tree; the bolt has entered my soul","tag":"Symbol","m":"'Blasted tree' = callback to lightning oak","b":"At Oxford, Victor calls himself a 'blasted tree', recalling the oak destroyed by lightning in Chapter 2. He has been struck down by the same force he once admired.","lt":"History","l":"Oxford sided with King Charles I during the English Civil War (1642–1651). The king made it his capital; Parliament's victory led to his execution in 1649."},{"id":214,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[511,512],"start":21751.8,"t":21856.2,"from":"We passed a considerable period at Oxford, rambling among …","to":"… which that terrible scene was thus associated.","q":"The latter name made me tremble","tag":"Theme","m":"Trauma triggered by a word (Chamounix)","b":"Even a mention of Chamounix makes Victor flee. Small reminders trigger his terror.","lt":"Psych","l":"Trauma triggers: sensory reminders (words, sounds, places) can bring back fear and distress. This is a recognized feature of post-traumatic stress."},{"id":215,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[513,514],"start":21856.6,"t":21941.4,"from":"From Derby, still journeying northwards, we passed two months …","to":"… which also he forsakes for other novelties.","q":"I could now almost fancy myself among the Swiss mountains","tag":"Setting","m":"Lake District = like Switzerland","b":"The Lake District reminds Victor of home. Clerval enjoys new friends here; Victor notes Clerval's restless love of novelty.","lt":"Literature","l":"Cumberland and Westmorland (the Lake District) were home to the 'Lake Poets': Wordsworth, Coleridge and Southey. The area is now a national park."},{"id":216,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[515,516],"start":21941.8,"t":22067.9,"from":"We had scarcely visited the various lakes of Cumberland …","to":"… arrive at the termination of my journey.","q":"But I was impatient to arrive at the termination of my journey","tag":"Character","m":"Edinburgh: Clerval happy, Victor anxious","b":"In Edinburgh, Clerval enjoys the city while Victor is impatient. He wants to finish the task, but also dreads it.","lt":"History","l":"Edinburgh was called the 'Athens of the North' for its Scottish Enlightenment thinkers: David Hume, Adam Smith and James Hutton (father of geology)."},{"id":217,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[517,518],"start":22068.3,"t":22142.4,"from":"We left Edinburgh in a week, passing through Coupar, …","to":"… which I cannot do in your absence.”","q":"I had rather be with you","tag":"Character","m":"Last time V. sees Clerval alive","b":"Victor leaves Clerval in Perth to work alone. Clerval's sweet farewell, that he feels at home only with Victor, is heartbreaking given what follows.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'I had rather' is an older form of 'I would rather'. It survives in some formal and dialect English and appears often in Shakespeare."},{"id":218,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[519,520],"start":22142.8,"t":22207.2,"from":"Having parted from my friend, I determined to visit …","to":"… mainland, which was about five miles distant.","q":"I determined to visit some remote spot of Scotland and finish my work in solitude","tag":"Setting","m":"Orkney island: remote, barren, isolated","b":"Victor chooses a barren island in the Orkneys to work alone, repeating the isolation of Ingolstadt. The landscape is bleak and the people poor.","lt":"Science","l":"Orkney is an archipelago off northern Scotland with harsh weather and few trees. It is also home to Skara Brae, a 5,000-year-old Neolithic village."},{"id":219,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[521,522],"start":22207.6,"t":22300.0,"from":"On the whole island there were but three miserable …","to":"… to the roarings of the giant ocean.","q":"The thatch had fallen in, the walls were unplastered","tag":"Setting","m":"Squalid hut = Victor's new lab","b":"Victor's new lab is a squalid hut. He compares Switzerland's gentle lakes with the 'roarings of the giant ocean', with nature now hostile and wild.","lt":"Language","l":"Contrast (antithesis) between settings: Switzerland is gentle like 'a lively infant'; the sea is a roaring 'giant'. Shelley uses setting to show Victor's state of mind."},{"id":220,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[523,525],"start":22300.4,"t":22406.8,"from":"In this manner I distributed my occupations when I …","to":"… made my heart sicken in my bosom.","q":"obscure forebodings of evil that made my heart sicken in my bosom","tag":"Theme","m":"2nd creation = done in horror (not passion)","b":"This time Victor works with full awareness of the horror. Without passion's 'blindness', the work disgusts him. Contrast this with the excitement of Chapter 4.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Informed consent and full awareness of consequences are basic research ethics. Victor now sees clearly what he's doing, which makes his doubt more significant."},{"id":221,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[526,527],"start":22407.2,"t":22570.6,"from":"I sat one evening in my laboratory; the sun …","to":"… the existence of the whole human race.","q":"whose selfishness had not hesitated to buy its own peace at the price, perhaps, of the existence of the whole human race","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor's reasoning: risk to humanity","b":"Victor imagines the female might refuse the bargain, or the pair might breed 'a race of devils'. He decides he can't risk the future of humanity for his own peace.","lt":"Science","l":"Biologists worry about releasing engineered organisms that can reproduce (e.g. gene drives in mosquitoes). Victor's fear of a self-replicating species is a real modern concern."},{"id":222,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[528,529],"start":22571.0,"t":22636.6,"from":"I trembled and my heart failed within me, when, …","to":"… howl of devilish despair and revenge, withdrew.","q":"tore to pieces the thing on which I was engaged","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor DESTROYS the female","b":"Seeing the Creature's grin at the window, Victor tears apart the half-made female. The Creature watches his only hope destroyed and howls in despair.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Is breaking a promise ever right? Kant says lying and promise-breaking are always wrong; utilitarians say do what produces the best outcome. Victor chooses the outcome. Do you agree?"},{"id":223,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[530,531],"start":22637.0,"t":22696.1,"from":"I left the room, and locking the door, made …","to":"… a person landed close to my house.","q":"a person landed close to my house","tag":"Setting","m":"Isolation + silence = suspense","b":"Victor vows never to resume the work. Alone in the silence, he hears oars, and someone lands. Shelley builds horror through sound.","lt":"Language","l":"Sound imagery creates suspense in horror: footsteps, creaking doors, silence. Films use a technique called 'diegetic sound' to make audiences feel what characters hear."},{"id":224,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[532,534],"start":22696.5,"t":22780.3,"from":"In a few minutes after, I heard the creaking …","to":"… hunger; do you dare destroy my hopes?”","q":"do you dare destroy my hopes?","tag":"Character","m":"Creature recounts his suffering to follow V.","b":"The Creature has followed Victor across Europe, enduring cold and hunger, only to watch his hope be destroyed. His fury is understandable.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Anaphora: repeating 'I have…' at the start of clauses ('I have dwelt… I have endured…'). Speakers use it to build emotional intensity, as in Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'I have a dream.'"},{"id":225,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[535,537],"start":22780.7,"t":22838.6,"from":"“Begone! I do break my promise; never will I …","to":"… your words will only exasperate my rage.”","q":"you believe yourself miserable, but I can make you so wretched","tag":"Theme","m":"Power reversal: Creature calls V. 'Slave'","b":"'You are my creator, but I am your master; obey!' Power flips: the creation now controls the creator.","lt":"Literature","l":"Hegel's master–slave dialectic (1807) argues that masters depend on their slaves and roles can reverse. The Creature and Victor dramatize this power struggle."},{"id":226,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[538,539],"start":22839.0,"t":22928.1,"from":"The monster saw my determination in my face and …","to":"… beneath words. Leave me; I am inexorable.”","q":"Shall each man","tag":"Theme","m":"'Each beast has his mate, and I alone?'","b":"The Creature's anguish: every man and animal has a mate, but he is denied one. This is his strongest argument about the cruelty of total isolation.","lt":"Science","l":"Social isolation harms social animals: Harry Harlow's monkey studies (1950s–60s) showed isolated monkeys developed severe behavioral problems. Isolation damages any social creature."},{"id":227,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[540,543],"start":22928.5,"t":23042.8,"from":"“It is well. I go; but remember, I shall …","to":"… before my enemy without a bitter struggle.","q":"I shall be with you on your wedding-night","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"★ THREAT: 'with you on your wedding-night'","b":"The Creature's most famous threat. Victor assumes it means HE will die that night, never imagining Elizabeth is the target. It is another self-centered misreading.","lt":"Language","l":"Ambiguity: the threat can be read more than one way. Victor's misinterpretation is a classic tragic error, and readers often guess the truth before he does."},{"id":228,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[544,545],"start":23043.2,"t":23152.5,"from":"The night passed away, and the sun rose from …","to":"… yet distinct and oppressive as a reality.","q":"the words of the fiend rang in my ears like a death-knell","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor thinks of himself as target","b":"Victor walks the beach thinking of the threat; he even wishes he could leave humanity, like the Creature. The 'death-knell' simile points to his own funeral.","lt":"History","l":"A death knell is a bell rung to announce a death. Church bells marked deaths and funerals in villages, a custom that gave us the phrase 'death knell' for the end of anything."},{"id":229,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[546,547],"start":23152.9,"t":23295.5,"from":"The sun had far descended, and I still sat …","to":"… in cleaning and arranging my chemical apparatus.","q":"determined to throw them into the sea that very night","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval's letter; V. disposes of remains","b":"Clerval writes asking Victor to rejoin him. Victor gathers the female's remains in a basket with stones to sink them at sea.","lt":"Science","l":"Disposing of biological material at sea was common then. Today strict regulations control disposal of biological and hazardous waste to protect ecosystems."},{"id":230,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[548,549],"start":23295.9,"t":23428.7,"from":"Nothing could be more complete than the alteration that …","to":"… in a short time I slept soundly.","q":"I slept soundly","tag":"Theme","m":"Out at sea: Victor sleeps in boat","b":"After sinking the remains, Victor feels relieved and falls asleep in the boat. His carelessness puts him at the mercy of the winds.","lt":"Language","l":"'Keel' is the bottom spine of a boat. Many everyday idioms come from sailing: 'even keel' (balanced), 'learn the ropes', 'by and large', 'taken aback'."},{"id":231,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[550,551],"start":23429.1,"t":23559.7,"from":"I do not know how long I remained in …","to":"… line of high land towards the south.","q":"I found that the wind was northeast","tag":"Setting","m":"Lost at sea, nature controls him","b":"Victor wakes far from land, without a compass, at nature's mercy. The man who wanted to control nature now cannot even steer a boat.","lt":"Science","l":"Before GPS, sailors navigated by compass, stars and 'dead reckoning' (estimating position from speed, time and heading). Without a compass Victor is truly lost."},{"id":232,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[552,553],"start":23560.1,"t":23640.6,"from":"Almost spent, as I was, by fatigue and the …","to":"… bounding with joy at my unexpected escape.","q":"How mutable are our feelings","tag":"Theme","m":"Wants to live after wishing to die","b":"Facing death at sea, Victor discovers he wants to live. Our 'clinging love' of life persists even in misery. He lands on an unknown shore.","lt":"Psych","l":"Self-preservation instinct is deep: even people in despair often fight to survive when in danger. This is part of why crisis support saves lives."},{"id":233,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[554,556],"start":23641.0,"t":23712.5,"from":"As I was occupied in fixing the boat and …","to":"… of Englishmen to receive strangers so inhospitably.”","q":"Surely it is not the custom of Englishmen to receive strangers so inhospitably","tag":"Irony","m":"Victor met with hostility (like Creature!)","b":"The townspeople crowd around Victor with angry faces. For once Victor is the stranger received with hostility, just as the Creature always is.","lt":"History","l":"Hospitality to strangers was a sacred duty in many cultures, as in ancient Greece's 'xenia'. Its breakdown signals danger in literature."},{"id":234,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[557,560],"start":23712.9,"t":23782.0,"from":"“I do not know,” said the man, “what the …","to":"… who was found murdered here last night.”","q":"it is the custom of the Irish to hate villains","tag":"Setting","m":"Ireland! Victor accused of murder","b":"Victor is in Ireland, not England, and he's taken to the magistrate over a murder. The irony is that Victor is suspected just as Justine was.","lt":"History","l":"In 1800 the Act of Union joined Ireland to Great Britain. Irish–English tensions were high, which colors the exchange about 'the custom of Englishmen'."},{"id":235,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[561,562],"start":23782.4,"t":23842.0,"from":"This answer startled me, but I presently recovered myself. …","to":"… relate, in proper detail, to my recollection.","q":"I was innocent; that could easily be proved","tag":"Irony","m":"V. thinks innocence = easy to prove (cf. Justine)","b":"Victor assumes his innocence can easily be proven, the same naïve faith his father once had for Justine. We know how that went.","lt":"Language","l":"Dramatic irony again: the reader recalls Justine's fate while Victor confidently expects justice. Shelley deliberately rhymes these scenes."},{"id":236,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[563,564],"start":23842.4,"t":23956.4,"from":"I was soon introduced into the presence of the …","to":"… black mark of fingers on his neck.","q":"the black mark of fingers on his neck","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Strangled young man: same as William","b":"Witnesses describe a young man strangled with finger marks on his neck. This is the Creature's signature. Victor (and we) start to fear who it is.","lt":"Science","l":"Forensic pathologists today study bruise patterns, like finger marks, to identify how a person died. The first forensic medicine textbooks appeared in the early 1800s."},{"id":237,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[565,567],"start":23956.8,"t":24028.4,"from":"The first part of this deposition did not in …","to":"… shore where the corpse was afterwards found.","q":"I remembered the murder of my brother","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor's agitation = looks guilty","b":"Victor's agitation makes him look guilty, just as Justine's confusion did. A woman saw a boat with one man near the shore.","lt":"Psych","l":"Observers misread emotional distress as evidence of guilt; studies show juries are influenced by defendants' demeanor. It's the same error that condemned Justine."},{"id":238,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[568,569],"start":24028.8,"t":24078.7,"from":"Another woman confirmed the account of the fishermen having …","to":"… place where I had deposited the corpse.","q":"Another woman confirmed the account","tag":"Theme","m":"Circumstantial evidence vs. Victor","b":"Testimony about the wind and his boat builds a case against Victor. Circumstantial evidence piles up, mirroring Justine's trial.","lt":"Language","l":"'Deposition' (formal witness testimony) and 'apothecary' (pharmacist) are period legal and medical terms. Note the use of '——' to hide a town's name, like dashes hiding dates."},{"id":239,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[570,571],"start":24079.1,"t":24177.5,"from":"Mr. Kirwin, on hearing this evidence, desired that I …","to":"… but you, Clerval, my friend, my benefactor—”","q":"Have my murderous machinations deprived you also, my dearest Henry, of life?","tag":"Theme","m":"★ CLERVAL MURDERED (3rd victim)","b":"The body is Clerval. Victor throws himself on it, calling himself the murderer. This is the Creature's revenge for the destroyed female, matching companion for companion.","lt":"Ethics","l":"'Eye for an eye' (lex talionis) is retributive justice. The Creature takes Victor's companion in exchange for his own. It is revenge in perfect symmetry."},{"id":240,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[572,574],"start":24177.9,"t":24267.3,"from":"The human frame could no longer support the agonies …","to":"… of the wheel, continually renewed the torture?","q":"like the turning of the wheel, continually renewed the torture","tag":"Symbol","m":"Fever: 'wheel' of torture","b":"Victor collapses into a two-month fever, raving that he murdered William, Justine and Clerval. He wonders why he can't die.","lt":"History","l":"The 'breaking wheel' was a medieval torture device. The 'wheel of fortune' (rota fortunae) was a medieval symbol of fate's endless ups and downs."},{"id":241,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[575,576],"start":24267.7,"t":24342.9,"from":"But I was doomed to live and in two …","to":"… that I had heard during my sufferings.","q":"surrounded by gaolers, turnkeys, bolts, and all the miserable apparatus of a dungeon","tag":"Setting","m":"Victor wakes in PRISON (like Justine)","b":"Victor wakes in a prison cell, exactly where Justine spent her last days. His fate keeps mirroring the victims of his silence.","lt":"Language","l":"'Gaol' is the old British spelling of 'jail' (both pronounced 'jail'). 'Turnkey' is a jailer who keeps the keys. Spelling variants often survive in British English."},{"id":242,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[577,580],"start":24343.3,"t":24416.8,"from":"I replied in the same language, with a feeble …","to":"… my mind with the force of reality.","q":"I am sorry that I am still alive to feel this misery and horror","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor wishes he were dead","b":"The nurse is cold and indifferent. Victor wishes he'd died. His life now feels like a dream with no reality.","lt":"Psych","l":"Depersonalization and derealization (feeling that life is unreal or dreamlike) are common responses to extreme trauma and stress."},{"id":243,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[581,582],"start":24417.2,"t":24492.5,"from":"As the images that floated before me became more …","to":"… visits were short and with long intervals.","q":"no one was near me who soothed me with the gentle voice of love","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor alone, unloved (now like Creature)","b":"Victor has no one to comfort him. For the first time he experiences what the Creature always endured: illness and suffering without love.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Empathy through experience: we often understand others' pain only once we feel it. Victor now lives the Creature's condition, but does he learn from it?"},{"id":244,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[583,585],"start":24492.9,"t":24558.4,"from":"One day, while I was gradually recovering, I was …","to":"… comfort which I am capable of receiving.”","q":"on the whole earth there is no comfort which I am capable of receiving","tag":"Character","m":"Mr. Kirwin: kind magistrate","b":"Mr. Kirwin, the magistrate, is kind and visits Victor. Unlike Justine's judges, he treats Victor humanely, though Victor rejects all comfort.","lt":"History","l":"A magistrate was a local judge handling minor cases and preliminary hearings. Kirwin's fairness contrasts with the Geneva court that condemned Justine."},{"id":245,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[586,588],"start":24558.8,"t":24620.4,"from":"“I know that the sympathy of a stranger can …","to":"… were, by some fiend across your path.”","q":"placed, as it were, by some fiend across your path","tag":"Irony","m":"Kirwin says 'by some fiend' (literal!)","b":"Kirwin says it was as if 'some fiend' placed the body in Victor's path. He means it figuratively, but it's literally true.","lt":"Language","l":"Literal vs. figurative meaning: Kirwin uses an idiom without realizing it's exactly what happened. Writers create irony by making figures of speech literally true."},{"id":246,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[589,591],"start":24620.8,"t":24687.6,"from":"As Mr. Kirwin said this, notwithstanding the agitation I …","to":"… whose murder I am now to lament?”","q":"whose murder I am now to lament?","tag":"Theme","m":"V. expects more deaths","b":"Kirwin found letters showing who Victor is and wrote to Geneva. Victor immediately assumes another family member has died.","lt":"Psych","l":"Hypervigilance, constantly expecting danger, develops after repeated trauma. Victor's mind now jumps to catastrophe."},{"id":247,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[592,596],"start":24688.0,"t":24743.9,"from":"“Your family is perfectly well,” said Mr. Kirwin with …","to":"… welcome instead of inspiring such violent repugnance.”","q":"someone, a friend, is come to visit you","tag":"Character","m":"V. thinks the visitor is the monster","b":"Told that a visitor has come, Victor thinks it's the monster and panics. Kirwin reads the panic as guilt.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'Is come' uses 'be' as the helping verb for 'come', an older perfect tense ('he is come' = 'he has come'). It survives in the Christmas carol 'Joy to the World, the Lord is come.'"},{"id":248,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[597,601],"start":24744.3,"t":24828.1,"from":"“My father!” cried I, while every feature and every …","to":"… seems to pursue you. And poor Clerval—”","q":"You travelled to seek happiness, but a fatality seems to pursue you","tag":"Character","m":"Father arrives: comfort & 'fatality'","b":"Alphonse arrives and Victor's anguish turns to joy. Even his father now speaks of a 'fatality' pursuing him, reinforcing Victor's belief in doom.","lt":"Language","l":"'Fatality' here means fate or doom, not a death. The word's main meaning has narrowed over time to 'a death in an accident'."},{"id":249,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[602,605],"start":24828.5,"t":24942.1,"from":"The name of my unfortunate and murdered friend was …","to":"… me and my destroyer in its ruins.","q":"wishing for some mighty revolution that might bury me and my destroyer in its ruins","tag":"Theme","m":"Wants to die w/ the Creature","b":"Victor wishes for a catastrophe that would destroy him and the Creature together. Their fates are now tied: creator and creation, joined in destruction.","lt":"Literature","l":"Samson brings down the temple on himself and his enemies (Judges 16). Victor's wish for mutual destruction echoes this biblical image, and foreshadows the ending."},{"id":250,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[606,607],"start":24942.5,"t":25050.4,"from":"The season of the assizes approached. I had already …","to":"… saw them in my chamber at Ingolstadt.","q":"The season of the assizes approached","tag":"Theme","m":"Trial: acquitted (alibi in Orkneys)","b":"Victor is acquitted because witnesses prove he was in the Orkneys at the time. Unlike Justine, he's freed, perhaps thanks to his class and connections.","lt":"History","l":"The assizes were periodic courts held in county towns by travelling judges. They lasted in England and Wales until 1972."},{"id":251,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[608,609],"start":25050.8,"t":25172.7,"from":"My father tried to awaken in me the feelings …","to":"… and day preyed upon my wasted frame.","q":"I was a shattered wreck—the shadow of a human being","tag":"Character","m":"Victor = 'skeleton', a shadow","b":"Victor is physically wasted, 'a mere skeleton'. Ironically he now resembles a corpse, like the materials he once used.","lt":"Language","l":"Metaphor ('a shattered wreck') and hyperbole ('a mere skeleton') show his decline. Notice how often Victor describes himself in ship or wreck imagery, echoing Walton's frame."},{"id":252,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[610,611],"start":25173.1,"t":25344.1,"from":"Still, as I urged our leaving Ireland with such …","to":"… mind is by its structure peculiarly susceptible.","q":"a sense of security, a feeling that a truce was established","tag":"Theme","m":"Leaves Ireland; brief calm at sea","b":"Sailing from Ireland at night, Victor feels a brief 'truce' with fate. Calm moments in this novel always come before disaster.","lt":"History","l":"Havre-de-Grâce (now Le Havre) is a major French port founded by King Francis I in 1517. It was the main gateway between France and England and America."},{"id":253,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[612,613],"start":25344.5,"t":25435.0,"from":"The voyage came to an end. We landed, and …","to":"… prove to me the futility of pride.","q":"he endeavoured to prove to me the futility of pride","tag":"Character","m":"Father misreads V.'s despair as pride","b":"In Paris, Alphonse thinks Victor is ashamed of being accused. He keeps misunderstanding because Victor keeps hiding the truth.","lt":"Language","l":"'Futility' means uselessness (from Latin futilis, leaky, as in a vessel that can't hold water). Words often carry hidden metaphors in their roots."},{"id":254,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[614,615],"start":25435.4,"t":25545.4,"from":"“Alas! My father,” said I, “how little do you …","to":"… relieved the burden of my mysterious woe.","q":"Justine, poor unhappy Justine, was as innocent as I","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor: 'I murdered them' (half-confession)","b":"Victor blurts out that he is the murderer of William, Justine and Henry, but never explains. His half-confessions relieve him without helping anyone.","lt":"Psych","l":"Confession can relieve the psychological burden of secrets (James Pennebaker's research on expressive writing). Victor confesses just enough to feel better, not enough to act."},{"id":255,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[616,618],"start":25545.8,"t":25611.4,"from":"Upon this occasion my father said, with an expression …","to":"… suffered me to speak of my misfortunes.","q":"I am not mad","tag":"Narration","m":"'I am not mad': V.'s sanity doubted","b":"His father thinks he's raving; Victor insists he's sane, as he did to Walton. Others' disbelief isolates him further.","lt":"History","l":"In the 1800s 'madness' could get people locked in asylums like Bedlam (London). Fear of being called mad was real, and it's why Victor stays silent."},{"id":256,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[619,622],"start":25611.8,"t":25688.2,"from":"As time passed away I became more calm; misery …","to":"… not totally void of comfort and tranquillity.","q":"sufficient for me was the consciousness of them","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth's letter: 'do you love another?'","b":"Victor learns to hide his feelings. Elizabeth writes, worried that he loves someone else and is marrying her only out of duty.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Inversion: 'sufficient for me was the consciousness' puts the complement first for emphasis. Normal order would be 'the consciousness was sufficient for me'."},{"id":257,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[623,625],"start":25688.6,"t":25790.0,"from":"“Yet I fear that the same feelings now exist …","to":"… with simple truth—Do you not love another?","q":"Do you not love another?","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth: honest & selfless","b":"Elizabeth offers Victor his freedom if he loves someone else. She puts his happiness before her own, and she is braver about honesty than Victor ever is.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Elizabeth asks for 'simple truth', the honesty Victor never gives. Transparency in relationships is linked to trust and satisfaction."},{"id":258,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[626,627],"start":25790.4,"t":25901.6,"from":"“You have travelled; you have spent several years of …","to":"… mine, I shall need no other happiness.","q":"if I see but one smile on your lips","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth asks only for his happiness","b":"Elizabeth tells Victor not to come if it would cause him pain; one smile would be enough for her. Her selflessness contrasts with Victor's self-absorption.","lt":"Language","l":"'But' here means 'only' ('if I see but one smile'). This older meaning survives in phrases like 'all but' and 'but a moment'."},{"id":259,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[628,630],"start":25902.0,"t":25991.9,"from":"This letter revived in my memory what I had …","to":"… guilt which would pursue me until death.","q":"I will be with you on your wedding-night!","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Victor remembers threat: thinks of HIMSELF","b":"Victor recalls the threat and calls it 'my sentence', still assuming he'll be the victim. He prepares to fight to the death.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Italics in a quotation mark repeated words remembered, or emphasis. In handwriting, show emphasis by underlining, the traditional proofreader's mark for italics."},{"id":260,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[631,632],"start":25992.3,"t":26131.4,"from":"Sweet and beloved Elizabeth! I read and reread her …","to":"… entreat, and I know you will comply.”","q":"but the apple was already eaten","tag":"Allusion","m":"'Apple already eaten' = Fall from Eden","b":"Victor's dreams of happiness are ruined by the 'apple': his forbidden knowledge. He promises to tell Elizabeth a 'dreadful secret' the day after the wedding.","lt":"Literature","l":"The apple and the angel with a flaming sword come from Genesis 3 and Paradise Lost: Adam and Eve are driven from Eden after eating forbidden fruit. Victor sees himself as fallen."},{"id":261,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[633,634],"start":26131.8,"t":26190.2,"from":"In about a week after the arrival of Elizabeth’s …","to":"… the multitude of miseries that overcame me.","q":"a real insanity possessed me","tag":"Character","m":"Victor's mood swings (rage ↔ despair)","b":"Back in Geneva, Victor alternates between fury and numbness. Elizabeth is also changed: thinner, sadder, but gentler.","lt":"Psych","l":"Emotional dysregulation, intense swings between anger and numbness, is common after severe trauma and grief."},{"id":262,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[635,638],"start":26190.6,"t":26251.3,"from":"Elizabeth alone had the power to draw me from …","to":"… death, to the happiness of my cousin.”","q":"I will consecrate myself, in life or death, to the happiness of my cousin","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"'in life or death': wedding set","b":"Victor says he will dedicate himself to Elizabeth 'in life or death'. The phrasing is ominous.","lt":"Language","l":"Double meanings: 'consecrate' (make sacred) is used for weddings and for burials. Shelley chooses words that work for both marriage and death."},{"id":263,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[639,640],"start":26251.7,"t":26328.4,"from":"“My dear Victor, do not speak thus. Heavy misfortunes …","to":"… I imagined, the seal to my fate.","q":"thus put, as I imagined, the seal to my fate","tag":"Theme","m":"Wedding in 10 days = 'seal to my fate'","b":"Elizabeth urges them to cling to what remains. Victor agrees to marry in ten days, believing he's sealing his own death. He is wrong about whose.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'As I imagined' is a parenthetical aside admitting his own error in hindsight. Retrospective narrators often signal mistakes this way."},{"id":264,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[641,642],"start":26328.8,"t":26405.2,"from":"Great God! If for one instant I had thought …","to":"… no trace but deep and everlasting regret.","q":"If for one instant I had thought what might be the hellish intention of my fiendish adversary","tag":"Narration","m":"Victor's hindsight: 'If I had known…'","b":"Victor, telling the story later, says he would have exiled himself if he'd understood the threat. Retrospective narration lets him (and us) see what he couldn't.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Third conditional: 'If I had thought…, I would have banished…' describes an unreal past and regret. Structure: if + past perfect, would have + past participle."},{"id":265,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[643,644],"start":26405.6,"t":26496.2,"from":"Preparations were made for the event, congratulatory visits were …","to":"… occurrence which no accident could possibly prevent.","q":"an occurrence which no accident could possibly prevent","tag":"Irony","m":"'No accident could prevent': irony","b":"Everyone treats the wedding as certain. Victor hides his anxiety, carries pistols and a dagger, and prepares to fight. The reader dreads what's coming.","lt":"Language","l":"Tempting fate is a common literary setup: when characters say 'nothing can go wrong', readers expect disaster. Horror films use this trope constantly."},{"id":266,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[645,647],"start":26496.6,"t":26598.7,"from":"Elizabeth seemed happy; my tranquil demeanour contributed greatly to …","to":"… invader who should wish to enslave it.","q":"a presentiment of evil pervaded her","tag":"Setting","m":"Wedding day: boat trip, Elizabeth uneasy","b":"After the wedding they sail across Lake Geneva to Evian. Elizabeth senses danger. The scenery is lovely, but the mood is anxious.","lt":"History","l":"Evian is a French spa town on Lake Geneva, famous today for its bottled mineral water. Shelley sets this final happy journey on the same lake where she wrote the book."},{"id":267,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[648,649],"start":26599.1,"t":26666.2,"from":"I took the hand of Elizabeth. “You are sorrowful, …","to":"… How happy and serene all nature appears!”","q":"What a divine day! How happy and serene all nature appears!","tag":"Irony","m":"Elizabeth's 'divine day' = dramatic irony","b":"Elizabeth tries to cheer them both by pointing out the beauty around them. Her joy hurts to read because we suspect what's coming.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Exclamatory sentences ('What a divine day!') express strong emotion. In formal writing they're rare; in dialogue they reveal a character's feelings."},{"id":268,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[650,652],"start":26666.6,"t":26748.5,"from":"Thus Elizabeth endeavoured to divert her thoughts and mine …","to":"… me and cling to me for ever.","q":"those cares and fears revive which soon were to clasp me and cling to me for ever","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Sunset landing = doom approaching","b":"The sun sets as they land. The day's beauty ends, and Victor's fears return 'for ever'. Darkness falls literally and figuratively.","lt":"Language","l":"Personification: cares and fears 'clasp' and 'cling' like hands. Shelley foreshadows the Creature's grasp, which will soon take Elizabeth."},{"id":269,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[653,655],"start":26748.9,"t":26823.2,"from":"It was eight o’clock when we landed; we walked …","to":"… or that of my adversary was extinguished.","q":"my right hand grasped a pistol which was hidden in my bosom","tag":"Theme","m":"V. armed, guards HIMSELF, not Elizabeth","b":"Victor patrols with a pistol, ready to defend himself. He's focused on his own battle, not on protecting Elizabeth.","lt":"History","l":"Pistols of the era (flintlocks) fired one shot and took time to reload. Victor's single shot will be useless against the Creature's speed."},{"id":270,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[656,659],"start":26823.6,"t":26918.3,"from":"Elizabeth observed my agitation for some time in timid …","to":"… repeated, and I rushed into the room.","q":"What is it that agitates you, my dear Victor? What is it you fear?","tag":"Theme","m":"★ V. sends Elizabeth away ALONE = fatal","b":"Victor sends Elizabeth to bed alone so he can search for the Creature. It's the fatal mistake: he leaves her unprotected. Then he hears a scream.","lt":"Psych","l":"Tunnel vision under threat: stress narrows attention to one danger, and people miss other obvious risks. Victor is so sure he's the target that he leaves her alone."},{"id":271,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[660,661],"start":26918.7,"t":27020.7,"from":"Great God! Why did I not then expire! Why …","to":"… had ceased to issue from her lips.","q":"The murderous mark of the fiend’s grasp was on her neck","tag":"Theme","m":"★ ELIZABETH MURDERED (4th victim)","b":"Elizabeth lies dead across the bed, strangled. The threat was never about Victor's life; it was about making him suffer as the Creature suffers, alone.","lt":"Art","l":"Henry Fuseli's painting 'The Nightmare' (1781) shows a woman sprawled across a bed with a demon. Many critics think Shelley's image of Elizabeth echoes it; Fuseli knew Mary's mother."},{"id":272,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[662,663],"start":27021.1,"t":27092.2,"from":"While I still hung over her in the agony …","to":"… different directions among the woods and vines.","q":"the pale yellow light of the moon illuminate the chamber","tag":"Symbol","m":"Creature grins at window; V. fires & misses","b":"The Creature grins at the window, pointing at the corpse. Victor fires and misses, and the Creature escapes into the lake. Others think Victor imagined him.","lt":"Language","l":"The 'yellow' light echoes the Creature's 'dull yellow eye' on the night of his creation. Shelley links colors and images across the novel."},{"id":273,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[664,665],"start":27092.6,"t":27183.6,"from":"I attempted to accompany them and proceeded a short …","to":"… return to Geneva with all possible speed.","q":"my father even now might be writhing under his grasp","tag":"Character","m":"Fears for father & Ernest; rushes home","b":"Victor collapses, then realizes his father and Ernest may be next. Fear finally drives him to action.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Modal verbs ('might be writhing', 'might be dead') express possibility. Here they build a chain of feared scenarios."},{"id":274,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[666,667],"start":27184.0,"t":27302.7,"from":"There were no horses to be procured, and I …","to":"… words, what remains of my hideous narration.","q":"one by one, my friends were snatched away; I was left desolate","tag":"Narration","m":"Victor speeds up the story (summary)","b":"Victor tells Walton he must summarize: he's exhausted. Narrative pace accelerates toward the end.","lt":"Language","l":"Narrative pacing: summary covers lots of time quickly; scene slows down for detail. Victor shifts to summary as his strength fails."},{"id":275,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[668,669],"start":27303.1,"t":27392.2,"from":"I arrived at Geneva. My father and Ernest yet …","to":"… a solitary cell had been my habitation.","q":"For they had called me mad","tag":"Theme","m":"Father dies of grief; V. in asylum","b":"Alphonse dies of grief days later. Victor is locked in a cell as mad for months. Everything and everyone is gone.","lt":"History","l":"Asylums of the era often used solitary confinement and restraints. Reformers like Philippe Pinel in France (1790s) pushed for more humane 'moral treatment'."},{"id":276,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[670,671],"start":27392.6,"t":27455.6,"from":"Liberty, however, had been a useless gift to me, …","to":"… authority for the apprehension of the murderer.","q":"at the same time awakened to revenge","tag":"Theme","m":"Revenge = only reason to live","b":"Freed, Victor awakens only to revenge. His passion for discovery has transformed into a passion for destruction.","lt":"Psych","l":"Revenge fantasies can give purpose after loss, but research (Carlsmith, 2008) shows taking revenge usually makes people feel worse, not better."},{"id":277,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[672,673],"start":27456.0,"t":27519.2,"from":"The magistrate listened to me with attention and kindness. …","to":"… and never deviating into invective or exclamation.","q":"never deviating into invective or exclamation","tag":"Theme","m":"Finally tells a magistrate (too late)","b":"Victor finally tells the truth to an official, calmly and precisely. If he'd done this after William's murder, Justine might have lived.","lt":"Language","l":"'Invective' means insulting, abusive language. Victor strips his speech of emotion to be believed, the same strategy the Creature used with De Lacey."},{"id":278,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[674,676],"start":27519.6,"t":27614.6,"from":"The magistrate appeared at first perfectly incredulous, but as …","to":"… or what region he may now inhabit.”","q":"no one can conjecture to what place he has wandered","tag":"Character","m":"Magistrate believes, but can't help","b":"The magistrate half-believes but says catching a creature who lives in glaciers and caves is impossible.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Even when people are believed, institutions may lack the means to act. It's an issue in real justice systems with limited resources."},{"id":279,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[677,678],"start":27615.0,"t":27665.6,"from":"“I do not doubt that he hovers near the …","to":"… should make up your mind to disappointment.”","q":"he may be hunted like the chamois and destroyed as a beast of prey","tag":"Theme","m":"V. wants creature hunted like an animal","b":"Victor wants his creation hunted down like an animal. He completely denies the Creature's humanity.","lt":"Science","l":"The chamois is a goat-antelope of the Alps, agile on steep cliffs. It was hunted almost to local extinction in places, and its skin gave us the word 'shammy' cloth."},{"id":280,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[679,682],"start":27666.0,"t":27753.9,"from":"“That cannot be; but all that I can say …","to":"… meditate on some other mode of action.","q":"how ignorant art thou in thy pride of wisdom!","tag":"Irony","m":"V. calls magistrate proud (irony!)","b":"Victor admits revenge is 'a vice' but 'the devouring and only passion of my soul'. He accuses the magistrate of 'pride of wisdom', which is exactly Victor's own sin.","lt":"Psych","l":"Projection (Freud) is seeing your own flaws in others. Victor accuses others of pride, ignorance and monstrousness, the qualities critics see in him."},{"id":281,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[683,685],"start":27754.3,"t":27827.0,"from":"My present situation was one in which all voluntary …","to":"… die and leave my adversary in being.","q":"But revenge kept me alive; I dared not die and leave my adversary in being","tag":"Theme","m":"Revenge sustains Victor (like Creature)","b":"Victor leaves Geneva forever. Revenge is all that keeps him alive. He and the Creature are now driven by the same single passion.","lt":"Literature","l":"Captain Ahab in 'Moby-Dick' (1851) chases the white whale out of revenge, destroying himself and his crew. Both he and Victor are consumed by obsessive pursuit."},{"id":282,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[686,687],"start":27827.4,"t":27956.3,"from":"When I quitted Geneva my first labour was to …","to":"… feel the despair that now torments me.”","q":"And I call on you, spirits of the dead","tag":"Theme","m":"Vow at family graves","b":"At the graves of William, Elizabeth and his father, Victor swears revenge, calling on spirits. He makes a sacred ritual of vengeance.","lt":"Literature","l":"Hamlet swears revenge after his father's ghost appears. Revenge tragedy is a genre (Shakespeare, Seneca) where vengeance destroys the avenger too."},{"id":283,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[688,689],"start":27956.7,"t":28018.4,"from":"I had begun my adjuration with solemnity and an …","to":"… determined to live, and I am satisfied.”","q":"I am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have determined to live, and I am satisfied","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature WANTS Victor to chase him","b":"The Creature laughs and whispers that he's glad Victor will live. He wants Victor to suffer, and to stay connected to him through hatred.","lt":"Psych","l":"Toxic bonds: hatred can bind people as tightly as love. Psychologists note that obsessive conflict keeps people emotionally attached to each other."},{"id":284,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[690,692],"start":28018.8,"t":28157.3,"from":"I darted towards the spot from which the sound …","to":"… few drops that revived me, and vanish.","q":"he fled with more than mortal speed","tag":"Theme","m":"Chase begins: Rhône → Black Sea → Russia","b":"The chase begins: from the Rhône, across the Mediterranean and the Black Sea, through Tartary and Russia. Victor believes spirits help him with food and rain.","lt":"History","l":"'Tartary' was a vague European name for central and northern Asia (Siberia, Mongolia, Central Asia). Old maps labelled huge regions with it."},{"id":285,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[693,694],"start":28157.7,"t":28290.1,"from":"I followed, when I could, the courses of the …","to":"… as the ardent desire of my soul.","q":"as the mechanical impulse of some power of which I was unconscious","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor becomes a machine of revenge","b":"Victor says he pursues 'more as a task enjoined by heaven' than by desire. He has become an instrument, almost a creature himself, driven by forces outside his will.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Victor again denies free will: he's 'mechanical', driven by 'some power'. Philosophers call this an excuse of determinism. Is he responsible for choosing revenge?"},{"id":286,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[695,696],"start":28290.5,"t":28365.0,"from":"What his feelings were whom I pursued I cannot …","to":"… of my tedious toil and horrible pilgrimage!","q":"My reign is not yet over","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature leaves messages + food (guides V.)","b":"The Creature carves taunting messages and even leaves food so Victor won't die. He leads Victor north deliberately, making him feel the cold and suffering he has felt.","lt":"Psych","l":"The Creature reverses roles: now he is the 'parent' providing food and Victor the dependent. It's a cruel mirror of the care Victor never gave."},{"id":287,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[697,699],"start":28365.4,"t":28473.6,"from":"As I still pursued my journey to the northward, …","to":"… gibe, to meet and grapple with him.","q":"the snows thickened and the cold increased","tag":"Setting","m":"Victor reaches the frozen sea","b":"Victor follows the Creature north to the frozen ocean. The story has come full circle to the icy setting of the opening letters.","lt":"Literature","l":"Circular or frame structure: the novel ends where it began. Dante's Hell is also icy at its deepest point (Cocytus); ice there means total isolation from love."},{"id":288,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[700,701],"start":28474.0,"t":28605.1,"from":"Some weeks before this period I had procured a …","to":"… and revenge, I prepared for my journey.","q":"Some weeks before this period I had procured a sledge and dogs","tag":"Setting","m":"Dogsled chase (callback to Letter 4)","b":"Victor trades for a sledge and dogs and gains ground. Now we understand the sledges Walton's crew saw: these are the two travelers from Letter 4.","lt":"History","l":"Dog sledding was developed by Arctic peoples like the Inuit and Chukchi thousands of years ago. European explorers learned it from them, often too late."},{"id":289,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[702,704],"start":28605.5,"t":28722.8,"from":"I exchanged my land-sledge for one fashioned for the …","to":"… emotions that oppressed me, I wept aloud.","q":"I wept aloud","tag":"Theme","m":"Sees Creature on ice → weeps","b":"When Victor finally sees the Creature nearby, he weeps with emotion. Is it rage, hope, or the closeness of his own 'child'? The feeling is strangely intimate.","lt":"Psych","l":"Intense emotions can be mixed: tears can come from joy, rage or relief. The Victor–Creature bond is so intense that even hatred feels intimate."},{"id":290,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[705,706],"start":28723.2,"t":28812.8,"from":"But this was not the time for delay; I …","to":"… thus preparing for me a hideous death.","q":"I was left drifting on a scattered piece of ice","tag":"Setting","m":"Ice breaks: Victor stranded","b":"Just as Victor nears his enemy, the ice cracks and a sea opens between them. Nature again defeats human will.","lt":"Science","l":"Sea ice breaks up through ocean swells and temperature changes. Floating ice floes can drift for months; Arctic explorers sometimes survived for months on them."},{"id":291,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[707,708],"start":28813.2,"t":28956.9,"from":"In this manner many appalling hours passed; several of …","to":"… hover near and direct the steel aright.","q":"thrust your sword into his heart","tag":"Narration","m":"V. asks Walton to KILL the Creature","b":"Victor's story ends where Walton found him. He asks Walton to swear to kill the Creature if Victor dies, passing his revenge on like an inheritance.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Victor tries to recruit Walton into his vendetta. Cycles of revenge passed down across generations appear in feuds worldwide (e.g. the Hatfields and McCoys)."},{"id":292,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[709,712],"start":28957.3,"t":29054.4,"from":"You have read this strange and terrific story, Margaret; …","to":"… but on this point he was impenetrable.","q":"Such a monster has, then, really existence!","tag":"Narration","m":"Back to Walton: letters as PROOF","b":"Walton returns as narrator. He says Felix and Safie's letters and the monster's sighting prove Victor's story true. Victor refuses to share how he made the Creature.","lt":"Language","l":"Authentication devices are 'evidence' inside fiction (letters, documents) that make a story feel real. Shelley stacks proofs to make the impossible believable."},{"id":293,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[713,715],"start":29054.8,"t":29163.8,"from":"“Are you mad, my friend?” said he. “Or whither …","to":"… almost as imposing and interesting as truth.","q":"Would you also create for yourself and the world a demoniacal enemy?","tag":"Theme","m":"V. guards the secret (finally learns?)","b":"Victor refuses to share his method; he has learned that some knowledge is too dangerous to spread. He also edits Walton's notes to 'correct' his story.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Dual-use research: knowledge that can help or harm (e.g. virus engineering). Scientists still debate whether to publish dangerous findings."},{"id":294,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[716,717],"start":29164.2,"t":29314.0,"from":"Our conversations are not always confined to his own …","to":"… I fell, never, never again to rise.”","q":"until I fell, never, never again to rise","tag":"Allusion","m":"V. compares himself to fallen archangel","b":"Victor compares himself to 'the archangel who aspired to omnipotence', Satan. Earlier the Creature compared himself to Satan. Both now see themselves as fallen.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Paradise Lost, Satan's fall comes from pride ('Better to reign in Hell than serve in Heaven'). Victor's ambition to be a creator-god is that same aspiring pride."},{"id":295,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[718,719],"start":29314.4,"t":29449.1,"from":"Must I then lose this admirable being? I have …","to":"… will be fulfilled and I may die.”","q":"I must pursue and destroy the being to whom I gave existence","tag":"Character","m":"Walton longs to keep V. as friend","b":"Walton has finally found his friend and is about to lose him. Victor says he can't start again; his only remaining purpose is destruction.","lt":"Psych","l":"Anticipatory grief means mourning someone before they die. Walton experiences this as Victor fades."},{"id":296,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[720,723],"start":29449.5,"t":29525.0,"from":"I write to you, encompassed by peril and ignorant …","to":"… Heaven bless you and make you so!","q":"I am surrounded by mountains of ice which admit of no escape","tag":"Setting","m":"Ship trapped in ice: crew in danger","b":"Walton's ship is trapped in the ice and his crew fears death. His own ambition has now endangered everyone, like Victor's.","lt":"History","l":"Shackleton's Endurance (1915) was trapped and crushed by Antarctic ice; remarkably, all 28 men survived. The wreck was found in 2022, 3,000 m deep."},{"id":297,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[724,726],"start":29525.4,"t":29590.2,"from":"My unfortunate guest regards me with the tenderest compassion. …","to":"… you, yet I cannot forbear recording it.","q":"I almost dread a mutiny caused by this despair","tag":"Theme","m":"Crew wants to go home; mutiny?","b":"Victor encourages Walton while the crew grows desperate. Walton fears mutiny. Leaders' ambitions versus followers' lives become the question.","lt":"History","l":"Mutinies were real dangers at sea: the famous mutiny on HMS Bounty (1789) saw sailors set their captain adrift. Henry Hudson's crew did the same to him in 1611."},{"id":298,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[727,728],"start":29590.6,"t":29686.4,"from":"We are still surrounded by mountains of ice, still …","to":"… I would instantly direct my course southwards.","q":"I would instantly direct my course southwards","tag":"Theme","m":"Crew demands: turn back if freed","b":"The sailors ask Walton to promise to return south if the ice frees them. This tests whether Walton will repeat Victor's mistake.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Leaders' duty of care: a captain is responsible for the lives of the crew. Modern ethics in exploration, medicine and business stress weighing others' safety above personal goals."},{"id":299,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[729,730],"start":29686.8,"t":29835.9,"from":"This speech troubled me. I had not despaired, nor …","to":"… to turn their backs on the foe.”","q":"Return as heroes who have fought and conquered","tag":"Character","m":"V.'s speech: 'Be heroes! Don't turn back'","b":"Dying Victor gives a rousing speech urging the crew to continue for glory. Even now he values ambition above life; he hasn't fully learned his lesson.","lt":"Language","l":"Persuasive rhetoric uses pathos (emotion), ethos (credibility) and logos (logic). Victor uses pathos: shame, honor and glory. Identify each appeal in his speech."},{"id":300,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[731,733],"start":29836.3,"t":29889.3,"from":"He spoke this with a voice so modulated to …","to":"… willingly continue to endure their present hardships.","q":"I had rather die than return shamefully, my purpose unfulfilled","tag":"Character","m":"Walton torn: glory vs. crew's lives","b":"The crew is moved but unconvinced. Walton says he'd rather die than return defeated. He is at the same crossroads Victor once faced.","lt":"Psych","l":"Sunk cost fallacy: continuing something because you've already invested so much, even when quitting is wiser. Walton and Victor both struggle with it."},{"id":301,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[734,738],"start":29889.7,"t":30001.8,"from":"The die is cast; I have consented to return …","to":"… “because they will soon return to England.”","q":"The die is cast; I have consented to return if we are not destroyed","tag":"Theme","m":"★ WALTON TURNS BACK (learns lesson)","b":"Walton agrees to turn back. He calls it cowardice, but readers see wisdom. He chooses his crew's lives over glory, the choice Victor never made.","lt":"History","l":"'The die is cast' (alea iacta est) is what Julius Caesar said crossing the Rubicon in 49 BCE, a decision with no return. Walton ironically uses it for his retreat."},{"id":302,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[739,742],"start":30002.2,"t":30064.4,"from":"“Alas! Yes; I cannot withstand their demands. I cannot …","to":"… had certainly not many hours to live.","q":"You may give up your purpose, but mine is assigned to me by heaven","tag":"Character","m":"V. refuses to give up; collapses","b":"Victor insists he must continue, but collapses. The surgeon says he has only hours to live. His obsession outlasts his body.","lt":"Language","l":"Victor again says his purpose is 'assigned' by heaven, using the passive voice to avoid responsibility. Notice: 'is assigned to me' (passive) versus 'I chose' (active)."},{"id":303,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[743,744],"start":30064.8,"t":30220.4,"from":"His sentence was pronounced, and I could only grieve …","to":"… I may still be misled by passion.","q":"I dare not ask you to do what I think right, for I may still be misled by passion","tag":"Theme","m":"V. dying: admits he may be wrong","b":"Victor reviews his life. He says he was right to refuse to make a female, since his duty to humanity came first, but admits his judgment may be clouded by passion.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Victor weighs duties: to his creature versus to humanity. Philosophers call this a conflict of obligations. Do you agree he chose correctly?"},{"id":304,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[745,746],"start":30220.8,"t":30284.0,"from":"“That he should live to be an instrument of …","to":"… gentle smile passed away from his lips.","q":"Seek happiness in tranquillity and avoid ambition","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor's last words: 'avoid ambition'… or not?","b":"Victor's final advice: avoid ambition, 'even if it be only the apparently innocent one of distinguishing yourself in science'. Then he adds that another may succeed where he failed. He contradicts himself until the end.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Is ambition good or dangerous? Society celebrates innovators like Edison and Musk, yet also warns of 'moving fast and breaking things'. Victor's final words capture this tension."},{"id":305,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[747,749],"start":30284.4,"t":30351.3,"from":"Margaret, what comment can I make on the untimely …","to":"… incomplete without this final and wonderful catastrophe.","q":"what comment can I make on the untimely extinction of this glorious spirit?","tag":"Character","m":"Walton mourns Victor; hears a noise","b":"Walton grieves Victor and calls him 'glorious'. He still admires him. Then a sound from the cabin interrupts: there is one more scene.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Rhetorical questions in grief express what can't be answered. Walton's questions mirror Victor's earlier 'Why did I not then expire!'"},{"id":306,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[750,751],"start":30351.7,"t":30419.4,"from":"I entered the cabin where lay the remains of …","to":"… the wildest rage of some uncontrollable passion.","q":"gigantic in stature, yet uncouth and distorted in its proportions","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Creature mourns over Victor's body","b":"The Creature hangs over Victor's coffin, grieving. Walton sees him with his own eyes, and he can't bear to look at his face.","lt":"Art","l":"The image of a mourner over a body echoes the Pietà (Mary holding Jesus). The Creature mourning his creator is a reversed parent–child image."},{"id":307,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[752,753],"start":30419.8,"t":30491.7,"from":"“That is also my victim!” he exclaimed. “In his …","to":"… pause of the tempest of his passion.","q":"Oh, Frankenstein! Generous and self-devoted being!","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature asks Victor's pardon (too late)","b":"The Creature calls Victor 'my victim' and asks him for pardon. His grief seems real. Even after everything, he wanted his creator's love.","lt":"Psych","l":"Children who are mistreated often still long for their parent's love and approval. The Creature grieving his abuser is psychologically realistic."},{"id":308,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[754,755],"start":30492.1,"t":30556.2,"from":"“Your repentance,” I said, “is now superfluous. If you …","to":"… torture such as you cannot even imagine.","q":"My heart was fashioned to be susceptible of love and sympathy","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature: crimes tortured him too","b":"Walton says repentance is too late. The Creature replies that he suffered more than Victor did; every murder tortured him. His heart was made for love.","lt":"Language","l":"'Fashioned' means made or shaped. The Creature was literally fashioned by Victor's hands. The word links his heart to his physical making."},{"id":309,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[756,757],"start":30556.6,"t":30700.1,"from":"“After the murder of Clerval I returned to Switzerland, …","to":"… your malignity is withdrawn from your power.”","q":"It is not pity that you feel","tag":"Character","m":"Walton accuses Creature of hypocrisy","b":"The Creature explains that Victor's hope for happiness drove him to kill Elizabeth. Walton, influenced by Victor, angrily accuses him of false grief.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Can a murderer truly feel remorse? Restorative justice programs bring offenders and victims together, and many offenders do express genuine remorse."},{"id":310,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[758,759],"start":30700.5,"t":30876.4,"from":"“Oh, it is not thus—not thus,” interrupted the being. …","to":"… boils at the recollection of this injustice.","q":"I, the miserable and the abandoned, am an abortion, to be spurned at, and kicked, and trampled on","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Creature: Victor's sins also deserve blame","b":"The Creature asks why only he is called a criminal when Felix drove him away and a man shot him after he saved a girl. He doesn't excuse himself, but demands that others share the blame.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Shared responsibility: when harm emerges from many people's actions, who is to blame? Sociologists call this a 'diffusion of responsibility'."},{"id":311,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[760,761],"start":30876.8,"t":31027.6,"from":"“But it is true that I am a wretch. …","to":"… can I find rest but in death?","q":"where can I find rest but in death?","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature admits guilt; plans own death","b":"The Creature fully admits his crimes: 'I am a wretch.' He has no hope left; death is his only consolation.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Anaphora again: 'I have murdered… I have strangled…' The repeated 'I have' is a full confession, the confession Victor never gave."},{"id":312,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[762,764],"start":31028.0,"t":31128.0,"from":"“Farewell! I leave you, and in you the last …","to":"… waves and lost in darkness and distance.","q":"He was soon borne away by the waves and lost in darkness and distance","tag":"Theme","m":"END: Creature leaves to burn himself","b":"The Creature vows to build a funeral pyre at the North Pole and leaves on an ice raft. We never see him die. The ending is open: darkness, distance, unfinished.","lt":"Literature","l":"An open (ambiguous) ending lets readers decide. Modern adaptations, from the 1931 film to Guillermo del Toro's 2025 'Frankenstein', keep reimagining the Creature's fate."}],"quiz":[{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"What is the married name of the sister Walton writes to?","o":["Mrs Lavenza, of Geneva","Mrs Saville","Mrs Clerval, of Archangel","Mrs Walton, of London"],"a":1,"e":"Every letter is addressed 'To Mrs. Saville, England'. Her first name is Margaret."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"Besides a passage near the pole, what does Walton hope to discover?","o":["A new kind of whale","A lost Viking colony","What attracts the needle","A cure for scurvy"],"a":2,"e":"He hopes to 'discover the wondrous power which attracts the needle', meaning magnetism."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"Walton longs for a friend. What does he want that friend to do with his plans?","o":["Write them down for Margaret to read","Keep them secret from the crew","Pay for the voyage out of his own fortune","Approve or amend them"],"a":3,"e":"He wants a man 'to approve or amend my plans'. He finds him in Victor."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Structure","q":"Why does the frame make Victor's story a 'second-hand' account?","o":["Walton writes it down","Margaret edits it for print","Victor wrote it as a book","The creature retells it to Walton"],"a":0,"e":"Walton records it each night 'as nearly as possible in his own words'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"How far away was the giant on the dog sledge when the crew saw him?","o":["Several leagues","About half a mile","Right beside the ship","Just out of rifle shot"],"a":1,"e":"'We perceived a low carriage… at the distance of half a mile.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Theme","q":"What makes Victor decide to tell Walton his story?","o":["Walton offers to hunt the creature","Walton promises to take him home","Walton threatens to put him ashore","Walton is repeating his mistake"],"a":3,"e":"'You seek for knowledge and wisdom, as I once did.'"},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Character","q":"Where does Caroline first see the infant Elizabeth (1831 text)?","o":["In an orphanage in Milan","On a ship returning from Naples","At a convent in Geneva","In a poor cottage by Lake Como"],"a":3,"e":"She finds 'five hungry babes' in a peasant's cot; Elizabeth stands out among them."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Theme","q":"Victor calls Elizabeth 'mine to protect, love, and cherish'. Which word is most revealing?","o":["'Cherish'","'Protect'","'Love'","'Mine'"],"a":3,"e":"He treats her as his possession, a 'pretty present' rather than an equal."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Irony","q":"His parents felt duty to 'the being to which they had given life'. Why is this ironic?","o":["He resents their care","Victor fails that duty","His parents neglected him","He was adopted"],"a":1,"e":"Victor later gives life to a being and abandons it at once."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Character","q":"Victor sought 'the secrets of heaven and earth'. What absorbed Clerval?","o":["The laws of electricity and magnetism","The history and secrets of the alchemists","The geology and glaciers of the Alps","The moral relations of things"],"a":3,"e":"Clerval was occupied 'with the moral relations of things'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Plot","q":"Where does Victor first find a volume of Cornelius Agrippa?","o":["His father's library","An inn near Thonon","Waldman's study","A Geneva bookshop"],"a":1,"e":"Bad weather kept the family at the inn near 'the baths near Thonon'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Symbol","q":"After the oak is blasted, what theory does a visitor explain to Victor?","o":["The circulation of the blood","Electricity and galvanism","The transmutation of metals","The motion of the planets"],"a":1,"e":"A 'man of great research' explains 'electricity and galvanism'."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Plot","q":"On her deathbed, what does Caroline ask Elizabeth to do?","o":["Marry Victor on the day of her funeral","Enter a convent and pray for her soul","Mother the younger children","Look after Alphonse and nobody else"],"a":2,"e":"'You must supply my place to my younger children.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"What does Krempe say Victor must do with his studies?","o":["Continue with Agrippa","Study only mathematics","Give up science","Begin entirely anew"],"a":3,"e":"'My dear sir, you must begin your studies entirely anew.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"Waldman says modern scientists 'have indeed performed…' what?","o":["Miracles","Great harm","Little of note","Wonders of gold"],"a":0,"e":"'These philosophers… have indeed performed miracles.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"Victor contrasts the happy man who thinks his town 'the world' with which man?","o":["One who has no home or town of his own","One who spends his nights studying the stars","One who travels to see the whole world","One who overreaches"],"a":3,"e":"'Than he who aspires to become greater than his nature will allow.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Plot","q":"Why did Victor build the creature 'of a gigantic stature'?","o":["To frighten his rivals","To make it strong enough to serve him","Tiny parts slowed him","Large corpses were easier to find"],"a":2,"e":"'The minuteness of the parts formed a great hindrance to my speed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Plot","q":"Which seasons pass while Victor works on the creature?","o":["Spring and summer only","Winter, spring and summer","A single winter","Summer and autumn"],"a":1,"e":"'Winter, spring, and summer passed away during my labours.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"At what hour does the creature open its eye?","o":["Noon","Midnight exactly","One in the morning","Dawn"],"a":2,"e":"'It was already one in the morning; the rain pattered dismally against the panes.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"Where does Victor take refuge after fleeing his bedroom?","o":["Clerval's inn","The university chapel","The courtyard below","The riverbank"],"a":2,"e":"'I took refuge in the courtyard belonging to the house which I inhabited.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"How long does Victor's nervous fever confine him?","o":["Several months","Several days","Two years","One night"],"a":0,"e":"'This was the commencement of a nervous fever which confined me for several months.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"Why did Justine come to live with the Frankensteins?","o":["She was an orphan from Naples","She was hired from an agency","She was Elizabeth's cousin","Her mother couldn't bear her"],"a":3,"e":"'Through a strange perversity, her mother could not endure her.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"What does Victor find in studying Eastern writers with Clerval?","o":["Scientific facts","Consolation","Ancient formulas","Nothing of value"],"a":1,"e":"He 'found not only instruction but consolation in the works of the orientalists'."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Plot","q":"How long is the walking tour at the end of Chapter 6?","o":["Three months","A single afternoon","A whole year","A fortnight"],"a":3,"e":"'We passed a fortnight in these perambulations.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Plot","q":"According to Alphonse's letter, what was on William's neck?","o":["A bruise from a thrown stone","A murderer's finger marks","The torn ribbon of the missing miniature","A deep burn from a rope"],"a":1,"e":"'The print of the murder's finger was on his neck.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Character","q":"Why does Elizabeth blame herself for William's death?","o":["She left him alone in the woods","She let him wear the miniature","She refused to search for him","She sent him out to play"],"a":1,"e":"'He had teased her to let him wear a very valuable miniature.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Plot","q":"What convinces Victor the creature killed William?","o":["A lightning flash revealing it","Justine's tearful confession in the cell","A giant footprint in the mud by the lake","A letter left pinned to William's coat"],"a":0,"e":"'A flash of lightning illuminated the object, and discovered its shape plainly to me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"Which two threats from her confessor drove Justine to confess?","o":["Torture and prison","The loss of her wages","Exile from Geneva","Excommunication and hell"],"a":3,"e":"'He threatened excommunication and hell fire in my last moments.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Character","q":"Which deed of Justine's does Elizabeth stress in court?","o":["Refusing a rich suitor","Nursing Caroline","Saving William once","Teaching the children"],"a":1,"e":"'She nursed Madame Frankenstein, my aunt, in her last illness.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Character","q":"'The tortures of the accused did not equal mine.' What does this reveal?","o":["Victor's guilt is small","Victor's faith in the court","Victor's love for Justine","Victor's self-pity"],"a":3,"e":"Even as an innocent girl faces death, Victor centres his own suffering."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Theme","q":"Victor calls himself 'the true murderer'. Of whom?","o":["Clerval","Caroline","William and Justine","Elizabeth"],"a":2,"e":"By this point only William and Justine have died because of his creation."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Setting","q":"At Chamounix, what does Victor call sleep?","o":["The thief of time","The giver of oblivion","The image of death","The friend of the poor"],"a":1,"e":"'Sleep crept over me… I felt it as it came and blessed the giver of oblivion.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Character","q":"After which event do men seem to Elizabeth 'monsters thirsting for each other's blood'?","o":["Caroline's death","Justine's execution","William's funeral","Victor's departure"],"a":1,"e":"Justine's death destroyed her faith in justice."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Setting","q":"Where is Victor sitting when the creature approaches?","o":["On the open glacier","At the summit of Mont Blanc","In a recess of the rock","In the hut by a fire"],"a":2,"e":"'I remained in a recess of the rock, gazing on this wonderful and stupendous scene.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Theme","q":"'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.' Which side of the debate is this?","o":["Nurture","Nature","Fate","Religion"],"a":0,"e":"He argues that his circumstances, not his making, corrupted him."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Allusion","q":"Why 'the fallen angel' and not Adam? Because Victor drives him from joy…","o":["For disobedience","For no misdeed","For his ugliness","For his pride"],"a":1,"e":"'Whom thou drivest from joy for no misdeed.' Unlike Satan, he did nothing to deserve his fall."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Close reading","q":"Which sense first overwhelms the newborn creature?","o":["Cold","Light","Smell","Sound"],"a":1,"e":"'A stronger light pressed upon my nerves, so that I was obliged to shut my eyes.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What lures the creature into the village cottage?","o":["The sound of music","Milk and cheese at windows","A fire on the hearth","A crying child"],"a":1,"e":"'The milk and cheese that I saw placed at the windows of some of the cottages, allured my appetite.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Symbol","q":"The creature's Promethean discovery comes by chance. How?","o":["Beggars left a fire","He rubs sticks together","Lightning strikes a tree","A shepherd shows him"],"a":0,"e":"'I found a fire which had been left by some wandering beggars.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Character","q":"Why does the creature stop taking the cottagers' food?","o":["He was caught","Felix set a trap","It caused them pain","He disliked their food"],"a":2,"e":"'When I found that in doing this I inflicted pain on the cottagers, I abstained.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Theme","q":"Why can't the creature at first learn the cottagers' words?","o":["Words don't match visible things","They speak too softly for him to hear","They speak only German among themselves","He is too far away from the window"],"a":0,"e":"Their words had no 'apparent connection with visible objects'."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Close reading","q":"Seeing his reflection, what does the creature say he did not yet know?","o":["That he could speak","That he was alive","How tall he was","Deformity's fatal effects"],"a":3,"e":"'I did not yet entirely know the fatal effects of this miserable deformity.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Character","q":"How well can Safie and the cottagers understand each other at first?","o":["Only through Felix","Perfectly, in French","Not at all","Through Latin"],"a":2,"e":"'She was neither understood by nor herself understood the cottagers.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Context","q":"What did Volney's 'Ruins of Empires' give the creature?","o":["A view of history","A map to Geneva","A guide to science","A book of prayers"],"a":0,"e":"'Through this work I obtained a cursory knowledge of history.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Theme","q":"Learning what society values, what does the creature ask himself?","o":["'Why am I tall?'","'And what was I?'","'How can I be rich?'","'Where is my father?'"],"a":1,"e":"'And what was I? Of my creation and creator I was absolutely ignorant.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"In whose names did Felix get the passports for the escape?","o":["His family's","False names","Safie's","The Turk's"],"a":0,"e":"'Felix had procured passports in the name of his father, sister, and himself.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Theme","q":"Why did the Turk secretly oppose Safie's marriage?","o":["Felix was poor","He'd promised her elsewhere","Safie was too young","Felix was a Christian"],"a":3,"e":"'He loathed the idea that his daughter should be united to a Christian.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"What made Safie's attendant useful on the journey?","o":["She carried money enough for the journey","She spoke Turkish","She was an old friend of Felix's family","She was armed and could protect them"],"a":1,"e":"She was 'a native of Leghorn, but who understood the common language of Turkey'."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"What language were the creature's three books written in?","o":["Latin, the language of scholars","The cottagers' language","German, the language of the country","Greek, the language of Plutarch"],"a":1,"e":"'The books were written in the language, the elements of which I had acquired at the cottage.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"How does the creature explain his French to De Lacey?","o":["He says a village priest taught him","He says Felix taught him in secret","A French family raised him","He says he grew up in the streets of Paris"],"a":2,"e":"'I was educated by a French family and understand that language only.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"What does Agatha do when she sees the creature?","o":["Attacks him","Runs to the village","Faints","Screams for help"],"a":2,"e":"'Agatha fainted, and Safie, unable to attend to her friend, rushed out.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What does the creature destroy before firing the cottage?","o":["The garden","The well","The cow","The hovel"],"a":0,"e":"He 'destroyed every vestige of cultivation in the garden'."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Quote","q":"After being shot: 'This was then the reward of my…' what?","o":["Benevolence","Strength","Curiosity","Folly"],"a":0,"e":"'This was then the reward of my benevolence! I had saved a human being from destruction.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What does William threaten to do if the creature won't release him?","o":["Run to Justine","Bite him","Tell his papa","Call the police"],"a":2,"e":"'Let me go, or I will tell my papa.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Character","q":"What does the creature say he will NOT do 'in my dying moments' if granted a mate?","o":["Return to Geneva","Curse his maker","Fear death","Pray to God"],"a":1,"e":"'In my dying moments I shall not curse my maker.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Theme","q":"'Am I not shunned and hated by all mankind?' What does the creature ask next?","o":["Where in the world he ought to go now","Why Victor ever chose to make him at all","Why he should pity man","Whether God made Victor as Victor made him"],"a":2,"e":"'Tell me why I should pity man more than he pities me?'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Setting","q":"What will the creature and his mate make their bed of?","o":["Furs","Moss and snow","Straw","Dried leaves"],"a":3,"e":"'We shall make our bed of dried leaves; the sun will shine on us as on man.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"How does Victor present his wish to visit England?","o":["Through Elizabeth","Telling the whole truth","Hiding the true reason","By letter from Paris"],"a":2,"e":"'Concealing the true reasons of this request, I clothed my desires under a guise.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Character","q":"Clerval says the Rhine's spirit suits man better than those who…","o":["Sail the seas","Guard the castles","Pile the glacier","Rule the cities"],"a":2,"e":"'More in harmony with man than those who pile the glacier.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Theme","q":"Why does Victor find the idea of being followed 'soothing'?","o":["He can trap the creature","He wants the company","It means he'll be killed","It keeps his family safe"],"a":3,"e":"'Soothing inasmuch as it supposed the safety of my friends.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Language","q":"In London, what does Victor say 'had come over my existence'?","o":["A curse","A frost","A shadow","A blight"],"a":3,"e":"'But a blight had come over my existence.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"How many huts stand on Victor's Orkney island?","o":["One","Three","Five","Twelve"],"a":1,"e":"'On the whole island there were but three miserable huts.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Theme","q":"Why is Victor afraid to wander away from the islanders?","o":["He might claim his mate","He might get lost on the moors and starve","The tide might cut him off on the rocks","The islanders might rob his hut"],"a":0,"e":"'Lest when alone he should come to claim his companion.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Setting","q":"What is happening outside as Victor reflects in his lab?","o":["The moon is rising","A storm is breaking","Snow is falling","The sun is rising"],"a":0,"e":"'The sun had set, and the moon was just rising from the sea.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor shout back at the wedding-night threat?","o":["Kill me now instead of my family","Be sure you're safe yourself","I will warn Elizabeth tonight","I will never marry while you live"],"a":1,"e":"'Villain! Before you sign my death-warrant, be sure that you are yourself safe.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"Why can't Victor tell where the wind is driving him?","o":["It is too foggy","He is asleep","He has no compass","The stars are hidden"],"a":2,"e":"'I had no compass with me and was so slenderly acquainted with the geography.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"Where was Clerval's body taken after it was found?","o":["The church","The inn","An old woman's cottage","The magistrate's house"],"a":2,"e":"'They instantly carried it to the cottage of an old woman near the spot.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Character","q":"Why did Kirwin seldom visit Victor in prison?","o":["He was away on business in Dublin","He secretly thought Victor guilty of murder","He avoided a murderer's ravings","He was ill himself with a fever"],"a":2,"e":"He 'did not wish to be present at the agonies and miserable ravings of a murderer'."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Theme","q":"What saves Victor from Justine's fate?","o":["A confession","A bribe","An alibi","A pardon"],"a":2,"e":"'It being proved that I was on the Orkney Islands at the hour the body… was found.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"Since when does Elizabeth say their union was planned?","o":["Their infancy","Victor's return","Caroline's death","Last autumn"],"a":0,"e":"'Our union had been the favourite plan of your parents ever since our infancy.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Allusion","q":"After 'the apple was already eaten', what does Victor see bared to drive him from hope?","o":["Satan's claws","Death's scythe","God's sword","The angel's arm"],"a":3,"e":"'The apple was already eaten, and the angel's arm bared to drive me from all hope.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor say his secret will do to Elizabeth?","o":["Make her ill","Make her leave him","Chill her with horror","Make her laugh"],"a":2,"e":"'When revealed to you, it will chill your frame with horror.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"What was Victor doing when Elizabeth screamed?","o":["Talking to the innkeeper","Writing a letter","Searching the house","Sleeping downstairs"],"a":2,"e":"He was 'walking up and down the passages of the house and inspecting every corner'."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"How soon after the news did Alphonse die?","o":["The same night","Within a few days","After many months","After a year"],"a":1,"e":"'In a few days he died in my arms.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Theme","q":"What does the magistrate say about the creature's powers?","o":["They defy his efforts","They pose no threat to Geneva now","They can be tamed with patience","They are made up by Victor's sick mind"],"a":0,"e":"'The creature of whom you speak appears to have powers which would put all my exertions to defiance.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"'Revenge kept me alive; I dared not die and leave…' what?","o":["My work unfinished","Elizabeth unburied","My adversary living","My father alone"],"a":2,"e":"'I dared not die and leave my adversary in being.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What food does one of the creature's messages direct Victor to?","o":["A dead hare","A loaf of bread","A frozen fish","A sack of grain"],"a":0,"e":"'You will find near this place… a dead hare; eat and be refreshed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"On what date did the passage south open for Walton's ship?","o":["11 September","9 September","5 September","1 October"],"a":0,"e":"'On the 11th the passage towards the south became perfectly free.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"How does the creature leave Walton's ship?","o":["Up the main mast and over the side","In one of the ship's lifeboats","Out the cabin window","Over the bow onto the ice"],"a":2,"e":"'He sprang from the cabin-window as he said this, upon the ice raft.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"Which 'apparently innocent' ambition does Victor warn Walton against?","o":["Wealth from trade","Glory in war","Power in politics","Fame in science"],"a":3,"e":"'Even if it be only the apparently innocent one of distinguishing yourself in science and discoveries.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Which narrator speaks to us without anyone retelling?","o":["Safie","The creature","Victor","Walton"],"a":3,"e":"Walton writes his own letters; Victor's and the creature's stories reach us through him."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Allusion","q":"In some myths Prometheus also moulded humans from clay. Why does this fit Victor?","o":["He is also chained","He is also a Titan","He also makes a man","He also steals fire"],"a":2,"e":"Prometheus as maker (plasmator) as well as fire-thief: Victor builds a human form."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"How does Mary Shelley say the idea came to her in 1831?","o":["A waking dream","A newspaper story","A lecture by Galvani","A visit to a graveyard"],"a":0,"e":"She describes seeing, with 'shut eyes', 'the pale student of unhallowed arts kneeling beside the thing he had put together'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which event most directly turns the creature to violence?","o":["Seeing his own reflection in the pool","Reading Paradise Lost and seeing himself as Satan","Leaving the forest near Ingolstadt","The De Laceys' rejection"],"a":3,"e":"After Felix beats him and the family flees, he declares 'everlasting war against the species'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Which line most clearly makes the creature Victor's dark mirror?","o":["'I am alone'","'I too can create desolation'","'I am thy creature'","'Make me happy'"],"a":1,"e":"After killing William, he claims a creator's power of his own."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Plot","q":"Which death is NOT caused, even indirectly, by the creature?","o":["Elizabeth's","Alphonse's","Caroline's","Justine's"],"a":2,"e":"Caroline dies of scarlet fever before the creature exists."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"'Destiny was too potent.' What do critics say Victor's talk of fate shows?","o":["He is being completely honest","He is truly cursed by a higher power","He is a deeply religious man","He evades responsibility"],"a":3,"e":"He blames fate for what his own choices caused."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"What does the Arctic frame suggest about Victor and Walton?","o":["A wish to be the first to see the creature","A shared love of cold and empty places","A secret fear of the open sea","Ambition past natural limits"],"a":3,"e":"Both push into a place hostile to human life in pursuit of glory."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Setting","q":"Put Walton's letters in order of where they were written.","o":["London, St. Petersburgh, Archangel, at sea","St. Petersburgh, Archangel, at sea, in the ice","Archangel, St. Petersburgh, at sea, in the ice","St. Petersburgh, at sea, Archangel, in the ice"],"a":1,"e":"Letter 1 is from St. Petersburgh (Dec.), Letter 2 from Archangel (March), Letter 3 at sea (July), Letter 4 in the ice (August)."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Close reading","q":"In Letter 1, how does Walton picture the pole, against what he knows?","o":["A waste where ships are lost","A sea choked with whales","A frozen seat of desolation","A warm land of eternal light"],"a":3,"e":"He tries 'in vain' to believe it is 'the seat of frost and desolation' and imagines 'a country of eternal light'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"Which goal does Walton NOT give for his voyage?","o":["Treading land never visited","Finding the source of life","A passage near the pole","The secret of the magnet"],"a":1,"e":"His goals are geographic and magnetic. Seeking 'the principle of life' is Victor's goal, which is the parallel the novel wants you to notice."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Connection","q":"Walton says a steady purpose is 'a point on which the soul may fix its intellectual eye'. Whose life does this most closely mirror?","o":["Victor's single-minded creation","Clerval's study of languages","Alphonse's public service","Elizabeth's care of the family"],"a":0,"e":"Victor later says he 'seemed to have lost all soul or sensation but for this one pursuit'. Walton's 'steady purpose' is the same trait."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"What books made up Uncle Thomas's whole library?","o":["Poems of Homer and Shakespeare","Treatises on navigation and magnets","Histories of voyages of discovery","Works of the alchemists"],"a":2,"e":"The library was 'a history of all the voyages made for purposes of discovery'. Walton's formation through reading parallels Victor's Agrippa and the Creature's three books."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Connection","q":"Walton's father forbade him a seafaring life; Victor's father dismissed Agrippa as 'sad trash'. What do both moments share?","o":["A father encourages a dangerous path","A father is proved right immediately","A father's word fails to stop a son's obsession","A father dies before the son can obey"],"a":2,"e":"Neither father's warning or dismissal stops the son. The obsession grows anyway."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Sequence","q":"Which happened FIRST in Walton's life?","o":["He hired the master in Archangel","He failed as a poet","He inherited his cousin's fortune","He sailed on a Greenland whaler"],"a":1,"e":"He failed as a poet, then inherited the fortune that turned him back to exploring. The training and hiring came later."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"What rank was Walton offered on the whaler after serving as under-mate?","o":["A share in the profits","The command of the ship","The second dignity in the vessel","Ship's surgeon"],"a":2,"e":"'My captain offered me the second dignity in the vessel.' This shows his competence and his pride."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Close reading","q":"'My courage and my resolution is firm; but my hopes fluctuate.' What does this reveal about Walton?","o":["He is about to abandon the voyage","He is determined yet privately insecure","He doubts his crew's courage","He fears his sister's disapproval"],"a":1,"e":"He wants 'some encouraging voice' to answer him. Firm will and shaky confidence sit side by side."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"When does Walton plan to set sail from Archangel?","o":["August","March","June","December"],"a":2,"e":"'I do not intend to sail until the month of June.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Theme","q":"In Letter 2, what does Walton say writing his thoughts down cannot replace?","o":["A captain's log kept for the Admiralty","Margaret's advice, sent by every post","A friend whose eyes reply to his","A proper education in languages"],"a":2,"e":"Writing is 'a poor medium for the communication of feeling'. He wants 'a man… whose eyes would reply to mine'. The Creature later longs for the same thing."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Language","q":"Walton says his daydreams lack 'keeping', using a painters' term. What does he mean?","o":["Bright and vivid colouring","A frame to set them off properly","Enough light and shadow to be seen at night","Harmony between the parts"],"a":3,"e":"In painting, 'keeping' means the parts are in harmony. His grand dreams are out of proportion, and he needs a friend 'to regulate my mind'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"Until what age did Walton 'run wild on a common'?","o":["Ten","Twenty-eight","Seventeen","Fourteen"],"a":3,"e":"'For the first fourteen years of my life I ran wild on a common and read nothing but our Uncle Thomas' books.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"Which description fits Walton's lieutenant, not the master?","o":["Gave his farm to a rival in love","Madly desirous of advancement in his profession","Famous for mild discipline","Silent as a Turk and wholly uneducated"],"a":1,"e":"The lieutenant wants glory and promotion. The master is the gentle, generous, uneducated one."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Irony","q":"Why does Walton find the master's noble story hard to admire fully?","o":["His ignorant carelessness","He was paid handsomely to give up the lady","He boasts of the deed too often","The lady later regretted her choice and wrote to him"],"a":0,"e":"'A kind of ignorant carelessness attends him' and 'detracts from the interest and sympathy'. Walton values cultivation even in goodness."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"Why did the Russian lady beg the master to spare her?","o":["Her father had lost his fortune","She was already married","She feared the sea","She loved another, poorer man"],"a":3,"e":"She confessed 'that she loved another, but that he was poor'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Allusion","q":"Walton links his love of 'the dangerous mysteries of ocean' to which poet's work?","o":["Coleridge","Milton","Shakespeare","Homer"],"a":0,"e":"He credits 'the most imaginative of modern poets', meaning Coleridge and the Ancient Mariner."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Allusion","q":"Why is 'I shall kill no albatross' an uneasy promise?","o":["Killing an albatross is a crime under Russian law","Walton shot an albatross on his whaling voyages","Sailors believe eating albatross brings fair winds","A careless act brought the Mariner's curse"],"a":3,"e":"In Coleridge's poem a thoughtless killing brings a curse on the whole crew. The novel is about consequences that start with one rash act."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"Which two drives does Walton say are 'intertwined in all my projects'?","o":["Hard labour and a love of the marvellous","Duty to his family and loyalty to his country","Greed for fortune and fear of failure","Faith in science and faith in Providence together"],"a":0,"e":"He is 'practically industrious… but besides this there is a love for the marvellous'. Victor has the same mix."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"Walton asks Margaret to 'remember me with affection, should you never hear from me again'. Why does this matter structurally?","o":["He plans to settle permanently in Russia","His letters may be her only news of him","He has secretly decided to join Victor's chase","He expects to be imprisoned for debts in England"],"a":1,"e":"The whole novel arrives as Walton's papers. The letters are the only proof of his fate."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Close reading","q":"In Letter 3, Walton promises to be 'cool, persevering, and prudent', then writes 'But success shall crown my endeavours'. What does the shift show?","o":["He is gently mocking his sister's fears","His crew has forced him to press on against his will","He has just sighted the pole and is celebrating","His caution gives way to ambition"],"a":3,"e":"He reassures Margaret, then his 'swelling heart' takes over. It is the same pattern as Victor's."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Setting","q":"How does Walton explain the unexpected warmth in Letter 3?","o":["A warm current from Africa","Hot springs near the ice","The midnight sun on the ice","Southern gales blowing them north"],"a":3,"e":"'The southern gales… breathe a degree of renovating warmth which I had not expected.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"What does Letter 3 call the voyage's only incidents so far?","o":["Two stiff gales and a leak","An attack by a whale that stove in a boat","A collision with an iceberg that nearly sank them","A mutiny over wages and a fever among the men"],"a":0,"e":"They are 'accidents which experienced navigators scarcely remember to record'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"What first made the crew believe land was nearer than they thought?","o":["A giant on a dog sledge","Smoke rising from the horizon to the east","A tree trunk floating among the ice floes","Seabirds circling the ship for days"],"a":0,"e":"'This apparition seemed to denote that it was not, in reality, so distant as we had supposed.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Sequence","q":"What happened between the crew seeing the giant and finding Victor?","o":["The ice broke up in the night","The giant came alongside and asked for food","A storm drove the ship far to the south","Walton fell ill and the lieutenant took command"],"a":0,"e":"'Before night the ice broke and freed our ship'. Victor drifted to them on a fragment of ice by morning."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"The sailors first took the giant to be what?","o":["The ghost of a drowned whaling captain","A Russian fur trader lost on the ice","A polar bear standing upright in the fog","An island savage"],"a":3,"e":"Victor was 'not, as the other traveller seemed to be, a savage inhabitant of some undiscovered island, but a European'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"Who first speaks to Victor for the ship, saying the captain 'will not allow you to perish'?","o":["Walton","The lieutenant","The master","A common sailor"],"a":2,"e":"'The master said, \"Here is our captain, and he will not allow you to perish on the open sea.\"'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"Why did Victor faint when first carried below?","o":["A swinging block of ice struck him as he was lifted","He caught sight of the giant again through a porthole","He left the fresh air","One of the sailors said the word 'dÃ¦mon' aloud"],"a":2,"e":"'As soon as he had quitted the fresh air he fainted', so they brought him back on deck."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"Which expression does Walton say Victor's eyes usually hold?","o":["Wildness, even madness","Childlike wonder","Cold contempt","Calm wisdom"],"a":0,"e":"'His eyes have generally an expression of wildness, and even madness'. Kindness alone lights up his face."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"Who asks Victor why he came so far on the ice?","o":["The ship's doctor","The lieutenant","Walton","The master"],"a":1,"e":"'Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had come so far upon the ice.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor asks whether the breaking ice 'had destroyed the other sledge'. What does his reaction to the answer suggest?","o":["He fears losing his prey","He hopes the giant has drowned so that he can rest","He wants to warn the giant that the ship is near","He is indifferent and only asks out of politeness"],"a":0,"e":"The possibility that his quarry survived gives him 'a new spirit of life'. The chase is all he lives for."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"What does Walton say Victor does when anyone except Walton enters his cabin?","o":["He asks about the sledge","He pretends to sleep","He appears uneasy","He becomes eloquent"],"a":2,"e":"'Is very silent and appears uneasy when anyone except myself enters his cabin.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Irony","q":"Walton writes 'I said… that I should find no friend on the wide ocean; yet I have found' one. Why is this tragic?","o":["The friend is a liar","The friend is the monster","The friend he finds is dying and broken","The friend leaves at the next port"],"a":2,"e":"Walton's one wish is granted in a man who 'cannot begin life anew'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Language","q":"Walton says Victor's speech flows with 'unparalleled eloquence'. Why does the novel stress this?","o":["It warns us he is persuasive","It explains why he speaks English with an accent","It proves he was trained as a poet at Ingolstadt","It shows that Walton's own English is clumsy"],"a":0,"e":"Both Victor and the Creature are eloquent. The reader must judge two persuasive speakers."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Symbol","q":"'Do you share my madness? Have you drunk also of the intoxicating draught?' What is the 'madness'?","o":["Hatred of the creature that killed his family","Love for a woman he can never have","Terror of the Arctic cold and the endless ice","Craving glory through knowledge"],"a":3,"e":"It is triggered by Walton saying one life is a small price for knowledge and 'dominion… over the elemental foes of our race'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Close reading","q":"What does Walton's phrase 'dominion… over the elemental foes of our race' reveal?","o":["He wants to defeat the Creature","He means fighting disease","He sees science as conquering nature","He wants to rule other nations"],"a":2,"e":"Nature is the 'foe' to be mastered. Victor too 'pursued nature to her hiding-places'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Quote","q":"Who calls people 'unfashioned creatures, but half made up' without a wiser friend?","o":["Walton","Victor","The Creature","Clerval"],"a":1,"e":"Victor says it to Walton in Letter 4. The irony is that he 'fashioned' a creature and denied it any companion."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"What does Victor say Walton has that he has lost?","o":["A loyal crew who would follow him anywhere","Hope, and the world before him","A loving sister who writes to him by every post","Youth, health and the strength to endure the cold"],"a":1,"e":"'You have hope, and the world before you, and have no cause for despair. But I—I have lost everything.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Language","q":"Walton says a man like Victor has 'a double existence'. What are the two sides?","o":["Crushing misery, and a soul lifted by nature","Public fame and private shame","His life in Geneva and his life at sea","Science and poetry"],"a":0,"e":"He may be 'overwhelmed by disappointments', yet in himself he is 'like a celestial spirit that has a halo around him'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"What does Walton say makes Margaret 'somewhat fastidious'?","o":["Her strict religious upbringing in England","Her lifelong fear of the sea and of sailors","Her comfortable marriage to Mr. Saville","Books and retirement from the world"],"a":3,"e":"'You have been tutored and refined by books and retirement from the world.' Margaret is the novel's silent final reader."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"Victor hopes Walton's wishes won't be 'a serpent to sting you, as mine has been'. What does Victor see his own knowledge as?","o":["A cure no one wanted","A temptation that brought his fall","A gift wasted by others","A punishment for his father's sins"],"a":1,"e":"The serpent echoes Eden. Knowledge sought for glory becomes the source of the fall, as in Paradise Lost."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Structure","q":"Why does Victor say the wild Arctic is the right place to tell his tale?","o":["Walton's men need a story to pass the long nights","He may die before the ship reaches any land","No one on the ice can report him to the magistrates","Wild nature makes marvels believable"],"a":3,"e":"'Many things will appear possible in these wild and mysterious regions which would provoke the laughter' elsewhere."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Theme","q":"Victor says his tale has 'internal evidence of the truth'. Why does the novel need this claim?","o":["Margaret has demanded proof in her letters","Walton is a magistrate and must have evidence","The original letters were lost at sea","Only the story proves the Creature"],"a":3,"e":"Like Victor at Justine's trial, the story rests on being believed, which is a recurring problem in the book."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"How will Walton record Victor's story?","o":["The lieutenant will take it down in shorthand","From memory, once the voyage is over","Victor will write it in his own journal","Nightly, in Victor's own words"],"a":3,"e":"'I have resolved every night… to record, as nearly as possible in his own words, what he has related.'"},{"s":"Letters","t":"Structure","q":"Who is the intended final reader of the whole novel inside the story?","o":["The Geneva magistrate","Captain Walton's crew","Margaret Saville","Elizabeth Lavenza"],"a":2,"e":"Everything, including Victor's and the Creature's narratives, reaches us through Walton's letters to his sister."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Close reading","q":"'Frightful the storm which embraced the gallant vessel on its course and wrecked it—thus!' Who is the 'gallant vessel'?","o":["The Creature","Walton's ship","Margaret","Victor"],"a":3,"e":"Walton imagines Victor as a fine ship wrecked by a storm. It is a metaphor, but it also warns about his own voyage."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Theme","q":"Which trait does Walton share with the Creature, rather than with Victor?","o":["A gift for chemistry and anatomy","A murderous temper when crossed","A longing for a friend","A loving family waiting at home"],"a":2,"e":"'I have no friend, Margaret' anticipates the Creature's 'I am alone and miserable; man will not associate with me'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"What drove Walton's choice of ship's master over other mariners?","o":["His knowledge of magnets","His experience in polar seas","His low price","His reputation for kindness to his crew"],"a":3,"e":"Walton has 'an intense distaste to the usual brutality exercised on board ship'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Context","q":"Walton credits Margaret's 'gentle and feminine fosterage' with what?","o":["A hatred of brutality on board","His skill in navigation and the stars","The money he used to hire the ship","His father's ban on going to sea"],"a":0,"e":"The novel quietly shows women's gentleness shaping men. Elizabeth plays the same role for Victor and Clerval."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Character","q":"Why did Beaufort hide in Lucerne rather than accept help?","o":["He owed money to Alphonse and was ashamed to face him","Pride: he couldn't bear poverty where he'd been grand","He wanted his daughter to learn to earn her own living","He was wanted by the Geneva magistrates for fraud"],"a":1,"e":"He was 'of a proud and unbending disposition' and could not live 'in poverty and oblivion' where he had been distinguished."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Setting","q":"On which river did Beaufort's mean street stand?","o":["The Reuss","The Rhône","The Rhine","The Arve"],"a":0,"e":"Alphonse found 'the house, which was situated in a mean street near the Reuss'."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Sequence","q":"Which comes LAST in Caroline Beaufort's story?","o":["She plaits straw to earn a pittance","She becomes Alphonse's wife","Alphonse places her with a relation in Geneva","Her father dies in her arms"],"a":1,"e":"She marries Alphonse 'two years after' he placed her with a relation in Geneva."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Connection","q":"Caroline is rescued kneeling by her father's coffin. Where does this exact image appear again?","o":["In a painting over the library mantelpiece","In the scene of Justine's trial","On the miniature William was wearing","In Victor's nightmare after the creation"],"a":0,"e":"In Chapter 7, the picture over the mantel shows 'Caroline Beaufort in an agony of despair, kneeling by the coffin of her dead father'."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does Victor say his father needed to 'approve highly to love strongly'?","o":["Caroline had to prove herself to his family first","He had once been hurt by someone unworthy","He was a syndic and judged people for a living","He thought all love must be earned by obedience"],"a":1,"e":"'Perhaps during former years he had suffered from the late-discovered unworthiness of one beloved.'"},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Theme","q":"How does Victor say his parents saw their duty to him?","o":["To guide him to happiness or misery","To prepare him to serve the republic","To make him a famous man of science","To find him a rich and suitable wife"],"a":0,"e":"They saw him as a creature 'whose future lot it was in their hands to direct to happiness or misery'. Victor reverses this with his own creation."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Connection","q":"Victor's parents saw him as 'the innocent and helpless creature bestowed on them by Heaven'. Why is this phrase pointed?","o":["Victor never feels grateful to them","Victor abandons his own helpless creature","Victor later becomes a priest","Victor was adopted like Elizabeth"],"a":1,"e":"The language of parental duty to a 'helpless creature' becomes the Creature's case against Victor."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Character","q":"Why was Caroline drawn to the poor cottages near Como?","o":["Alphonse ordered her to inspect his tenants","She was writing a book on Italian peasants","She was looking for a girl to adopt","She remembered being rescued when poor"],"a":3,"e":"Charity was 'a necessity, a passion—remembering what she had suffered, and how she had been relieved'."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Plot","q":"Where was Alphonse when Caroline found Elizabeth?","o":["In Naples","In Lucerne","In Milan","In Geneva"],"a":2,"e":"'One day, when my father had gone by himself to Milan, my mother… visited this abode.'"},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Character","q":"How many children were in the peasant family's cottage?","o":["Three","Five","Four","Seven"],"a":1,"e":"They were 'distributing a scanty meal to five hungry babes'. Four were their own and one was Elizabeth."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Context","q":"Why was Elizabeth's father's property confiscated?","o":["He fought for Italy's liberty against Austria","He was bankrupted like Beaufort","He refused to pay taxes to the pope","He married a German without permission"],"a":0,"e":"He was one of the patriots who 'exerted himself to obtain the liberty of his country' and 'became the victim of its weakness'."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Language","q":"Which comparison is NOT used for the young Elizabeth?","o":["A shrine-dedicated lamp","Lighter than the chamois of the hills","A creature fairer than a cherub","A garden rose among brambles"],"a":0,"e":"The 'shrine-dedicated lamp' image comes in Chapter 2, describing her soul in the home."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Close reading","q":"Elizabeth looked 'of a distinct species, a being heaven-sent'. What contrast does this set up?","o":["Beauty seen as heavenly, ugliness as evil","Rich families set against the poor peasants","The Italians set against the sober Swiss","Faith in heaven set against modern science"],"a":0,"e":"The novel judges by appearance. Elizabeth's looks win her a home; the Creature's lose him every chance."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Plot","q":"Who decided that the peasants should give Elizabeth up?","o":["Elizabeth's German relatives asked for it","A Milan court ordered it","Alphonse paid them a large sum","They consulted their village priest"],"a":3,"e":"'They consulted their village priest, and the result was that Elizabeth Lavenza became the inmate of my parents' house.'"},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Quote","q":"'I have a pretty present for my Victor.' Who says it?","o":["Caroline","The peasant woman","Elizabeth","Alphonse"],"a":0,"e":"His mother says it playfully the night before Elizabeth arrives, and Victor takes it 'literally'."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor's literal reading of 'a pretty present' reveal?","o":["He is secretly jealous of Elizabeth","He is frightened of strangers in the house","He sees her as his possession","He had wanted a baby brother instead"],"a":2,"e":"He 'looked upon Elizabeth as mine—mine to protect, love, and cherish'. Possessive love runs through his relationships."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Character","q":"What did Victor and Elizabeth call each other?","o":["Sister and brother","Nicknames from Clerval's plays","Cousin","Their first names only"],"a":2,"e":"'We called each other familiarly by the name of cousin.'"},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"What in the phrase 'till death she was to be mine only' proves bitterly true?","o":["She dies on their wedding night","Elizabeth never marries anyone at all","Victor dies first, far away in the Arctic","Elizabeth outlives Victor by many years"],"a":0,"e":"The Creature kills Elizabeth on the wedding night, so she is his only 'till death'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Character","q":"Which pairing correctly matches person and passion in Chapter 2?","o":["Elizabeth: appearances; Victor: causes","Elizabeth: chemistry; Victor: Italian poetry","Clerval: alchemy; Victor: knightly romances","Elizabeth: politics; Clerval: natural philosophy"],"a":0,"e":"'While my companion contemplated… the magnificent appearances of things, I delighted in investigating their causes.'"},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Plot","q":"How much younger is Victor's first brother, Ernest?","o":["Three years","One year","Twelve years","Seven years"],"a":3,"e":"'On the birth of a second son, my junior by seven years, my parents gave up… their wandering life.'"},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Setting","q":"How far is the house at Belrive from Geneva?","o":["Rather more than a league","A day's journey","Ten leagues","Half a mile"],"a":0,"e":"'A campagne on Belrive, the eastern shore of the lake, at the distance of rather more than a league from the city.'"},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says of himself, 'My temper was sometimes violent, and my passions vehement'. Where did this energy go?","o":["Into an eager desire to learn","Into rebelling against his father","Into Clerval's knightly plays","Into fights with schoolfellows"],"a":0,"e":"'They were turned not towards childish pursuits but to an eager desire to learn.' His later science draws on that same violent energy."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Character","q":"Which two inquiries does Victor say drew him?","o":["Chivalry and the history of Geneva","Outward substance and nature's inner spirit","Languages and the codes of governments","Moral relations and men's actions"],"a":1,"e":"Whether 'the outward substance of things or the inner spirit of nature and the mysterious soul of man', all of them 'secrets of heaven and earth'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Character","q":"What was Clerval's 'hope and dream'?","o":["To become a professor of chemistry","To marry Elizabeth Lavenza","Fame as a gallant benefactor of mankind","To take over his father's business"],"a":2,"e":"He hoped to be 'one among those whose names are recorded in story as the gallant and adventurous benefactors of our species'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Connection","q":"Clerval and Victor both want glory as benefactors of mankind. What separates them?","o":["Victor never cared for glory","Clerval had no talent for science","Clerval's father supported him","Elizabeth made doing good Clerval's aim"],"a":3,"e":"Elizabeth 'unfolded to him the real loveliness of beneficence and made the doing good the end and aim of his soaring ambition'. Victor wants the glory without that aim."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Character","q":"Without Elizabeth, what does Victor say he might have become?","o":["A poet like Walton","A merchant like Clerval's father","Sullen in study and rough by nature","A soldier in foreign service"],"a":2,"e":"'I might have become sullen in my study, rough through the ardour of my nature, but that she was there to subdue me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Theme","q":"Victor says misfortune changed his 'visions of extensive usefulness' into what?","o":["A wish to serve the poor","Gloomy, narrow reflections on self","A burning thirst for revenge","A fear of all science"],"a":1,"e":"The change is from public benefit to self-absorption, the opposite of Clerval."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says his ruling passion arose 'from ignoble and almost forgotten sources'. What does 'ignoble' suggest?","o":["The cause was secret and magical","The cause was his father's crime","The cause was royal and grand","The cause was small and unworthy"],"a":3,"e":"A chance book at an inn becomes the start of a torrent of ruin."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Sequence","q":"Put these in order: (a) oak destroyed, (b) Agrippa found, (c) mathematics taken up, (d) elixir of life sought.","o":["b, d, a, c","a, b, c, d","d, b, c, a","b, a, d, c"],"a":0,"e":"Agrippa at 13, then the alchemists' elixir, then the lightning at 15, then he turns to mathematics."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Theme","q":"Victor imagines a better response from his father about Agrippa. What would it have been?","o":["Explaining why modern science had replaced him","Forbidding the book and burning it","Reading Agrippa together to test him","Sending Victor to study in Ingolstadt early"],"a":0,"e":"If his father had explained that the ancient powers 'were chimerical, while those of the former were real', Victor would have thrown Agrippa aside."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Irony","q":"Victor blames his father's 'cursory glance' for his ruin. Why is this ironic?","o":["His father was secretly a man of science","He gives his creature even less","Victor never opens Agrippa's books again","His father turns out to be right about Agrippa"],"a":1,"e":"He faults his father's brief attention while he himself abandons his creature at first sight."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Allusion","q":"Newton felt like a child picking up shells by 'the great and unexplored ocean of truth'. How does Victor use this?","o":["To mock Walton's voyage","To praise Newton above all others","To explain why he chose the sea","To show even great men knew little"],"a":3,"e":"Modern philosophers seemed to him like 'tyros', so he turned to the bolder alchemists."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor pictures nature as a citadel with fortifications. What does this metaphor imply?","o":["God guards nature from humans","Knowledge must be stormed and conquered","Science is a slow building project","Nature is a peaceful home to be loved"],"a":1,"e":"'The fortifications and impediments that seemed to keep human beings from entering the citadel of nature.' Nature becomes something to besiege."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Character","q":"Why was Victor 'self-taught' in his favourite studies?","o":["Elizabeth hid his books","Geneva had no schools","His tutors were all alchemists","His father was not scientific"],"a":3,"e":"'My father was not scientific, and I was left to struggle with a child's blindness.'"},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Theme","q":"Which aim did Victor rank as 'an inferior object'?","o":["Raising spirits","Wealth","Glory","Banishing disease"],"a":1,"e":"'Wealth was an inferior object, but what glory would attend the discovery!'"},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Character","q":"When his incantations to raise spirits failed, whom did Victor blame?","o":["The lack of proper tools","The alchemists' errors","His father's interference","His own inexperience"],"a":3,"e":"He blamed 'my own inexperience and mistake' rather than 'a want of skill or fidelity in my instructors'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Setting","q":"From where did the great storm of Victor's fifteenth year advance?","o":["From over Mont Blanc","From behind the mountains of Jura","From the south over Salêve","From across the lake at Copêt"],"a":1,"e":"'It advanced from behind the mountains of Jura.'"},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Plot","q":"How far from the house was the oak struck by lightning?","o":["About half a league","At the edge of the lake","In the next village","About twenty yards"],"a":3,"e":"It 'stood about twenty yards from our house'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Connection","q":"The blasted oak returns later as Victor's image of himself. Which line?","o":["'I am a wreck of humanity'","'I am a blasted tree'","'I was benevolent and good'","'I am the fallen angel'"],"a":1,"e":"In Chapter 19 he says 'I am a blasted tree; the bolt has entered my soul'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does the visitor's theory of electricity make Victor give up science altogether?","o":["His father forbade the subject after the storm","It frightened him away from storms for good","It proved that alchemy was actually dangerous","His heroes fell, so nothing seemed knowable"],"a":3,"e":"Seeing his 'lords of my imagination' overthrown, 'it seemed to me as if nothing would or could ever be known'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor call natural history after the storm?","o":["A garden of wonders","A deformed and abortive creation","The foundation of all knowledge","A game for children"],"a":1,"e":"He set it down 'as a deformed and abortive creation', words that foreshadow how he will see his own creature."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Theme","q":"What 'lesson' does Victor say the guardian angel tried to teach him?","o":["To trust his father's judgement over his books","Evil with study, happiness without it","To seek God first and knowledge only afterwards","To study with patience, humility and method"],"a":1,"e":"'I was to be taught to associate evil with their prosecution, happiness with their disregard.'"},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Theme","q":"Chapter 2 ends with Victor blaming 'Destiny'. Why do critics question this?","o":["Walton disagrees aloud","Destiny is never mentioned again","Elizabeth blames destiny too","His own choices drove his ruin"],"a":3,"e":"By blaming 'immutable laws' he dodges responsibility for repeated free decisions."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Irony","q":"Caroline dies because she nursed Elizabeth. What does the narrator call this act?","o":["A sacrifice demanded by her husband","An imprudence fatal to her preserver","A mistake made by the doctors","A duty she performed unwillingly"],"a":1,"e":"'Elizabeth was saved, but the consequences of this imprudence were fatal to her preserver.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Plot","q":"On which day of Elizabeth's illness did Caroline fall sick?","o":["After a fortnight","The first night","The third day","The day Victor left"],"a":2,"e":"'On the third day my mother sickened.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Close reading","q":"Caroline says resigning herself to death 'cheerfully' is what she will 'endeavour' to do. What does 'endeavour' admit?","o":["She is certain of heaven and unafraid","It is hard; she is not truly ready to go","She wants Victor to stay home","She blames Elizabeth for her death"],"a":1,"e":"She says 'is it not hard to quit you all?' Her calm takes effort."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor say begins only 'when the lapse of time proves the reality of the evil'?","o":["Elizabeth's role as mother","The actual bitterness of grief","His fear of strangers","His interest in reviving the dead"],"a":1,"e":"First comes disbelief. 'Then the actual bitterness of grief commences.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Connection","q":"Caroline's death is the first loss Victor wants to undo. Which later hope does it most anticipate?","o":["Renewing life where death had begun decay","Making a mate for the Creature","Becoming rich from his discovery","Avenging William's murder"],"a":0,"e":"In Chapter 4 he hopes to 'renew life where death had apparently devoted the body to corruption'."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Plot","q":"Why did Victor delay leaving for Ingolstadt after his mother died?","o":["Leaving the house of mourning felt like sacrilege","Elizabeth fell ill again with the scarlet fever","The roads to Bavaria were closed by heavy snow","His father forbade him to travel until the spring"],"a":0,"e":"'It appeared to me sacrilege so soon to leave the repose, akin to death, of the house of mourning.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"What did Elizabeth do with her own grief?","o":["She blamed herself publicly for Caroline's death","She shut herself away in her room for months","She turned to religion and spent her days in prayer","She veiled it to comfort the others"],"a":3,"e":"'She indeed veiled her grief and strove to act the comforter to us all.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"How did Clerval's father view his son's ambitions?","o":["As a phase he would outgrow","As a credit to the family","As a reason to send him abroad","As idleness leading to ruin"],"a":3,"e":"'He saw idleness and ruin in the aspirations and ambition of his son.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Close reading","q":"Leaving home, Victor feels 'invincible repugnance to new countenances'. Why is this ironic later?","o":["He becomes a famous lecturer adored by his students","He later marries a stranger from a foreign country","He builds a being whose face repels all","He grows to love company and society at Ingolstadt"],"a":2,"e":"He fears new faces, then makes a face no one, including himself, can bear."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"What did Elizabeth ask of Victor as he left?","o":["That he come home by Christmas","That he write often","That he avoid alchemy","That he look after Clerval"],"a":1,"e":"She came 'to renew her entreaties that I would write often'."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Language","q":"Victor calls the force that led him to Krempe 'the Angel of Destruction'. What is the effect?","o":["It praises the university as a holy, protected place","It mocks his father's simple religious beliefs","It shifts blame from Victor to fate","It shows how deeply he came to love and trust Krempe"],"a":2,"e":"Again he frames chance as destiny, as in Chapter 2."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor mentions his alchemists to Krempe 'carelessly, and partly in contempt'. Contempt for what?","o":["For the professor and his modern science","For his father's opinion","For the alchemists themselves","For his own ignorance"],"a":0,"e":"He still thinks modern science small. His tone invites Krempe's scorn."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Quote","q":"'In what desert land have you lived, where no one was kind enough to inform you…?' Who says it?","o":["Krempe","Clerval","Alphonse","Waldman"],"a":0,"e":"Krempe mocks Victor's 'exploded systems and useless names'."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Plot","q":"On which days would Waldman lecture on chemistry?","o":["Only during the summer term, by private arrangement","Every evening at eight in his own laboratory","The days Krempe did not lecture","Every Sunday afternoon in the great hall"],"a":2,"e":"Waldman would lecture 'the alternate days that he omitted'."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"Why had Victor not yet met Waldman?","o":["He lectured only in Latin","He was ill with fever","He refused all students","He had been out of town"],"a":3,"e":"'M. Waldman, whom I had never seen, as he had hitherto been out of town.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"What was striking about Waldman's hair?","o":["Entirely white and long","Grey at the temples, nearly black behind","Black and flowing like the Creature's","He was completely bald"],"a":1,"e":"'A few grey hairs covered his temples, but those at the back of his head were nearly black.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Theme","q":"Waldman says modern scientists' hands 'seem only made to dabble in dirt'. What is his point?","o":["Humble methods have performed miracles","Chemistry is beneath gentlemen","Students must clean the laboratory","Science is dirty and immoral"],"a":0,"e":"They 'promise very little' yet 'have indeed performed miracles'."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Plot","q":"Which achievement does Waldman credit to modern science?","o":["Finding the elixir of life that banishes disease","Raising the spirits of the dead by experiment","Turning base metals into gold in the crucible","Discovering how the blood circulates"],"a":3,"e":"'They have discovered how the blood circulates, and the nature of the air we breathe.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Close reading","q":"'They… mock the invisible world with its own shadows.' What is Waldman describing?","o":["Ghost stories told to children","Painting with light and dark","Science imitating unseen forces","Religious visions of heaven"],"a":2,"e":"Science now does what magic only promised, commanding thunder and mimicking earthquakes."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Language","q":"'I felt as if my soul were grappling with a palpable enemy.' What is happening to Victor?","o":["He is furious at Waldman for mocking Agrippa","His old ambition is fighting to return","He is fighting off sleep in a warm lecture hall","He is suffering a fit of the nervous fever again"],"a":1,"e":"'One by one the various keys were touched which formed the mechanism of my being.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Structure","q":"Victor writes 'exclaimed the soul of Frankenstein' about himself. What does the third person suggest?","o":["He is speaking to Walton aloud","His ambition feels like a separate force","He is quoting his father","He has forgotten his own name"],"a":1,"e":"He narrates his younger self from outside, as if possessed."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Plot","q":"What happens the night after Waldman's lecture?","o":["Victor cannot close his eyes","Victor burns his alchemy books","Victor writes to Elizabeth","Victor visits a charnel-house"],"a":0,"e":"'I closed not my eyes that night. My internal being was in a state of insurrection and turmoil.'"},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"How did Waldman's manner differ at home and at lectures?","o":["Exactly the same everywhere","Stricter and colder at home","More mild and kind at home","Shy in public, bold at home"],"a":2,"e":"In public 'a certain dignity'. At home 'the greatest affability and kindness'."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Theme","q":"Waldman says the labours of genius, 'however erroneously directed', usually do what?","o":["Destroy the very man of genius who undertook them","Bring down the anger of the church on the university","Turn to mankind's solid advantage","Lead their authors into madness and despair"],"a":2,"e":"'The labours of men of genius, however erroneously directed, scarcely ever fail in ultimately turning to the solid advantage of mankind.' The novel tests this claim."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"What does Victor hide from Waldman in their private talk?","o":["The enthusiasm driving his plans","His family's wealth","His knowledge of Agrippa","His dislike of Krempe"],"a":0,"e":"He speaks modestly 'without letting escape… any of the enthusiasm which stimulated my intended labours'. It is his first secret."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Quote","q":"'A man would make but a very sorry chemist if he attended to that department… alone.' What does Waldman recommend?","o":["Chemistry before all else, and nothing besides","Every branch, including mathematics","Medicine and anatomy, which lead to chemistry","Philosophy and poetry, to balance the mind"],"a":1,"e":"Breadth, 'if your wish is to become really a man of science and not merely a petty experimentalist'."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Character","q":"Victor eventually values Krempe because of what?","o":["His kindness to new students","His secret interest in alchemy","His friendship with Waldman","Sound sense beneath bad manners"],"a":3,"e":"He found 'a great deal of sound sense and real information, combined… with a repulsive physiognomy'. Victor can look past ugliness in a professor but not in his creature."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"Why does Victor say science, unlike other studies, never ends?","o":["It is too difficult to master","The books are endless","There is continual food for discovery","Professors keep changing their minds"],"a":2,"e":"'In other studies you go as far as others have gone… in a scientific pursuit there is continual food for discovery and wonder.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says 'a mind of moderate capacity' focused on one study 'must infallibly' excel. What does this suggest about him?","o":["He studied many different subjects at once","Focus, not genius, drove him","He secretly believes he is the greatest genius alive","He lacked any real ambition at first"],"a":1,"e":"He attributes his success to obsessive focus on 'one object of pursuit'."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Plot","q":"Just as Victor planned to return to Geneva, what kept him?","o":["Clerval's unexpected arrival at the university","His interest in the principle of life","A request from Waldman to assist with lectures","A return of the fever that had followed his mother's death"],"a":1,"e":"He thought of returning home 'when an incident happened that protracted my stay': the question of where life comes from."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"Victor says many discoveries are near 'if cowardice or carelessness did not restrain our inquiries'. Why is this troubling?","o":["He blames Waldman's carelessness","He treats moral caution as cowardice","He means poor equipment","He admits he is a coward"],"a":1,"e":"What restrains others is called cowardice, so ethics becomes weakness."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Plot","q":"Which branch of study did Victor turn to in order to find the source of life?","o":["Botany and the growth of plants in the spring","Electricity alone, as the storm had taught him","Astronomy and the motions of the heavens","Physiology and anatomy"],"a":3,"e":"'I determined thenceforth to apply myself more particularly to those branches of natural philosophy which relate to physiology.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Close reading","q":"'A churchyard was to me merely the receptacle of bodies deprived of life.' What does this attitude enable?","o":["A healthy lack of superstition only","Respect for the dead","Coldly using the dead as material","Comfort after his mother's death"],"a":2,"e":"His father's rationalism leaves him without reverence, ready to rob graves."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Sequence","q":"Which does Victor describe studying LAST before his discovery?","o":["The science of anatomy","The structure of the human frame","The change from death to life","The natural decay of the body"],"a":2,"e":"He studied anatomy, then decay, then examined 'the change from life to death, and death to life', until 'a sudden light broke in'."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Language","q":"How does Victor describe the moment of discovery?","o":["A storm clearing at once","A sudden light breaking from darkness","A voice speaking in a dream","A slow dawn over many years"],"a":1,"e":"'From the midst of this darkness a sudden light broke in upon me.' Light imagery recalls Prometheus."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor is 'surprised… that I alone should be reserved to discover so astonishing a secret'. What does 'reserved' imply?","o":["He was saving it for later","He feels chosen by destiny","He was too shy to share it","He kept it a secret"],"a":1,"e":"He sees himself as singled out. This is pride dressed as fate."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Structure","q":"Victor breaks off his story to address Walton directly. Why at this point?","o":["To rest his voice before the hardest part","To ask Walton's advice about what he should have done","To warn him off the same knowledge","Because the crew interrupt the story with a question"],"a":2,"e":"'Learn from me, if not by my precepts, at least by my example, how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"What was Victor's first hesitation about using his power?","o":["Whether the discovery would make him wealthy","Whether the experiment was morally right at all","A man, or something simpler?","Whether he ought first to tell his father about it"],"a":2,"e":"'I doubted at first whether I should attempt the creation of a being like myself, or one of simpler organization.' His doubt was technical, not moral."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Irony","q":"Victor makes the creature gigantic for what reason?","o":["To frighten his enemies","To make it strong enough to protect him","The small parts slowed his work","Because the bones he found were large"],"a":2,"e":"'As the minuteness of the parts formed a great hindrance to my speed.' Haste, not design, makes the Creature monstrous."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Close reading","q":"'Life and death appeared to me ideal bounds, which I should first break through.' What does 'ideal' mean here?","o":["Imaginary rather than real limits","Perfect and beautiful","Holy and untouchable","Practical and useful"],"a":0,"e":"He thinks of the boundary of death as only an idea that can be crossed."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor imagine his new species will give him?","o":["Eternal youth for himself and for Elizabeth","Political power and a seat among the syndics of Geneva","Wealth and lands across the Swiss cantons","Gratitude above any child's"],"a":3,"e":"'No father could claim the gratitude of his child so completely as I should deserve theirs.' He wants worship, not responsibility."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Plot","q":"Which grim practice does Victor confess to using in his work?","o":["Bribing gravediggers","Torturing living animals","Murdering a beggar","Stealing from Waldman's lab"],"a":1,"e":"He 'tortured the living animal to animate the lifeless clay'."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Allusion","q":"'To animate the lifeless clay' recalls which story?","o":["Pygmalion and his statue","Pandora's creation","The golem of Prague","God forming Adam from dust"],"a":3,"e":"Clay echoes Genesis and Paradise Lost, the Creature's own key texts."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Close reading","q":"'I seemed to have lost all soul or sensation but for this one pursuit.' What is the irony?","o":["Victor gains a soul through science","The Creature has no sensation","Victor forgets his own name","Victor becomes soulless while giving life"],"a":3,"e":"In creating life he drains himself of humanity."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Close reading","q":"'Often did my human nature turn with loathing from my occupation.' What does this admission show?","o":["He saw it was wrong and went on anyway","He hated chemistry from the start","He was ill from the smell","He was forced to do it"],"a":0,"e":"Disgust is overridden by 'an eagerness which perpetually increased'."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"What season-long beauty did Victor ignore while working?","o":["Snow lying unusually late on the distant Alps","A rich harvest and vintage","A royal procession passing through the town","A great comet seen in the skies over Ingolstadt"],"a":1,"e":"'Never did the fields bestow a more plentiful harvest or the vines yield a more luxuriant vintage.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Character","q":"How did Victor's father respond in his letters to the silence?","o":["He stopped writing letters in return","He threatened to come to Ingolstadt himself","He asked more about his work","He scolded him sharply for his ingratitude"],"a":2,"e":"'My father made no reproach… and only took notice of my silence by inquiring into my occupations more particularly.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"Victor says a perfect human should never let what disturb his tranquillity?","o":["Fear of God's judgement on his studies","Passion or a passing desire","Love for a woman, unless she shares his aims","Grief for the dead, however deep it may be"],"a":1,"e":"'A human being in perfection ought always to preserve a calm and peaceful mind.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Allusion","q":"Which disaster does Victor list among the results of ignoring domestic affection?","o":["The destruction of Mexico and Peru","The burning of London","The Black Death","The fall of Rome to the Goths"],"a":0,"e":"He names Greece enslaved, Caesar, America discovered too fast, and 'the empires of Mexico and Peru'."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says the study that weakens affection is 'unlawful, that is to say, not befitting the human mind'. Whose law is meant?","o":["The law of Geneva","The law of the church","The university's rules","The law of human nature"],"a":3,"e":"He explains 'unlawful' as not befitting the human mind, a moral law and not a legal one."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Plot","q":"How long did Victor's labours continue, by the seasons?","o":["One summer only, in a single fit of labour","Autumn and winter, while the snow kept him in","Winter, spring and summer","Two full years without any pause at all"],"a":2,"e":"'Winter, spring, and summer passed away during my labours.'"},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Close reading","q":"'The fall of a leaf startled me, and I shunned my fellow creatures as if I had been guilty of a crime.' Why the word 'guilty'?","o":["He has stolen equipment from the university","His conscience knows it is wrong","He owes money all over the town to tradesmen","He has already killed someone to get materials"],"a":1,"e":"His body behaves like a criminal's before any crime is known."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Close reading","q":"'With an anxiety that almost amounted to agony.' What is Victor's mood just BEFORE the creature wakes?","o":["Joyful certainty that the work would succeed","Calm scientific detachment and quiet pride","Agonised anxiety","Exhausted indifference after two years of labour"],"a":2,"e":"The horror comes after; even beforehand, triumph is mixed with agony."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Symbol","q":"The creature wakes as Victor's candle is 'nearly burnt out'. What does the dying light suggest?","o":["The night is nearly over and morning will bring hope","Victor's own life force is spent","Victor has run out of money for his work","The experiment used fire rather than electricity"],"a":1,"e":"The 'half-extinguished light' mirrors Victor's ruined health at the moment of creation."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Close reading","q":"'His limbs were in proportion, and I had selected his features as beautiful. Beautiful! Great God!' What does the repetition show?","o":["Sarcasm aimed at Walton's eager curiosity","Shock at the gap between plan and result","Wonder at the beauty of the creature's face","Pride that he chose each feature so well"],"a":1,"e":"The exclamation turns from intention to horror in one word."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Character","q":"Which detail of the creature's face is NOT in Victor's description?","o":["Red, glowing eyes","Watery eyes almost the colour of their sockets","Straight black lips","A shrivelled complexion"],"a":0,"e":"His eyes are 'watery', 'almost of the same colour as the dun-white sockets'. Glowing red eyes are a film invention."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Theme","q":"'The different accidents of life are not so changeable as the feelings of human nature.' What is Victor explaining?","o":["Why accidents happen in laboratories","Why he changed his career","How longing turned to disgust at once","How the creature's mood changed"],"a":2,"e":"After two years of ardour, 'the beauty of the dream vanished'."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"After fleeing the creature, how does Victor try to sleep?","o":["Fully clothed on his bed","In Waldman's laboratory","In the courtyard on a bench","At an inn across the town"],"a":0,"e":"He 'threw myself on the bed in my clothes, endeavouring to seek a few moments of forgetfulness'."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Connection","q":"In the nightmare, Elizabeth becomes Caroline's corpse. Which later event does the dream predict most precisely?","o":["Elizabeth dying on her wedding night","Clerval's body found in Ireland","Justine's execution for murder","William strangled in Plainpalais"],"a":0,"e":"The kiss of love turning into death foreshadows the wedding night."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature wakes Victor by lifting the bed curtain and grinning. How could this be read differently from Victor's view?","o":["As a planned attack on the man who made it","As a demand for food and warmth after its birth","As an attempt to steal Victor's notes and secrets","As a newborn seeking its parent"],"a":3,"e":"His outstretched hand and 'inarticulate sounds' fit a baby reaching for its father. Victor reads menace."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor see by the light of the moon?","o":["Clerval arriving on the coach","His mother's shroud and grave-worms","Elizabeth walking in Ingolstadt","The creature at his bedside"],"a":3,"e":"'By the dim and yellow light of the moon… I beheld the wretch.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor call the creature that night, as he listens for its approach?","o":["A living statue","A fallen angel","A newborn child","A demoniacal corpse"],"a":3,"e":"He fears 'the approach of the demoniacal corpse to which I had so miserably given life'."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Close reading","q":"'He might have spoken, but I did not hear.' What does this admission reveal?","o":["The creature was born deaf and dumb","The creature spoke in a foreign tongue","Victor's fever had left him partly deaf","He never let it speak"],"a":3,"e":"Victor admits he ran before the creature could communicate."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Theme","q":"'Dreams that had been my food and pleasant rest… were now become a hell to me.' What is the key change?","o":["His dream became his torment","He stopped eating for days","He began to dream of his mother","He could no longer sleep at all"],"a":0,"e":"The overthrow was 'so rapid… so complete'."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Setting","q":"What does Victor see at dawn from the courtyard?","o":["Mont Blanc catching the first light in the distance","The steeple clock at six","The creature crouched on a rooftop opposite","The coach from Switzerland coming up the street"],"a":1,"e":"'The church of Ingolstadt, its white steeple and clock, which indicated the sixth hour.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Allusion","q":"Victor quotes the Ancient Mariner as he walks. What is the frightful fiend in his mind?","o":["The ghost of the albatross","The creature he has abandoned","The memory of his mother","Professor Krempe's scorn"],"a":1,"e":"Like the Mariner he 'turns no more his head' because he knows something follows him."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Irony","q":"Clerval says 'How fortunate that you should be here at the very moment of my alighting!' Why is this ironic?","o":["Victor was about to leave Ingolstadt for ever","Victor had forgotten Clerval was coming","Clerval had come to arrest him","Victor is only there fleeing in terror"],"a":3,"e":"It looks like a welcome, but Victor is wandering in panic."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Character","q":"What does Clerval's arrival bring back to Victor's thoughts?","o":["His old love of the alchemists and their dreams","His father, Elizabeth and home","Waldman's lecture and his vow to unfold creation","His terror of the creature, now loose in the town"],"a":1,"e":"'His presence brought back to my thoughts my father, Elizabeth, and all those scenes of home.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"How does Victor explain his thin, pale looks to Clerval?","o":["Poor food in Ingolstadt","A recent fever","Long study without rest","Grief for his mother"],"a":2,"e":"'I have lately been so deeply engaged in one occupation that I have not allowed myself sufficient rest.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor throws open his door 'as children are accustomed to do when they expect a spectre'. What does the simile show?","o":["Joy at returning home after a night outside","Childish dread with bravado","Anger at the creature for invading his rooms","Calm and manly courage in the face of danger"],"a":1,"e":"The man of science behaves like a frightened child."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor do on finding his rooms empty?","o":["Claps his hands for joy","Searches the whole house","Faints from relief","Calls for the police"],"a":0,"e":"'When I became assured that my enemy had indeed fled, I clapped my hands for joy.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Language","q":"Victor calls the creature 'my enemy' on finding it gone. Why is the word significant?","o":["Clerval had used the word first on the stairs","It had already attacked him in the night","He judges it hostile before it acts","It had stolen his notes before running away"],"a":2,"e":"The creature has harmed no one, yet Victor has already cast it as an enemy."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor imagine just before falling into a fit?","o":["The spectre seizing him","Krempe laughing at him in front of the students","Elizabeth's corpse standing in the doorway","His father's angry letter lying on the table"],"a":0,"e":"'I thought I saw the dreaded spectre glide into the room… I imagined that the monster seized me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Character","q":"Why did Clerval come to believe Victor's ravings had a real cause?","o":["Victor showed him the empty laboratory upstairs","Waldman told him what Victor had been working on","He kept returning to one subject","The creature had been seen by people in the town"],"a":2,"e":"'The pertinacity with which I continually recurred to the same subject persuaded him.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Symbol","q":"What does Victor first notice with pleasure on recovering?","o":["Young buds replacing fallen leaves","Clerval asleep in a chair","A letter on the table","Snow on the rooftops"],"a":0,"e":"'I perceived that the fallen leaves had disappeared and that the young buds were shooting forth.' Nature's renewal mirrors his own."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Theme","q":"What does Clerval ask as 'repayment' for nursing Victor all winter?","o":["That Victor pay for his studies","That Victor share his secret","That Victor visit Geneva with him","That Victor get well quickly"],"a":3,"e":"'You will repay me entirely if you do not discompose yourself, but get well as fast as you can.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"What has Victor been forbidden to do during his illness, according to Elizabeth?","o":["Write or hold a pen","Receive visitors except Clerval","Read his chemistry books","Leave his bed for any reason"],"a":0,"e":"'You are forbidden to write—to hold a pen; yet one word from you… is necessary.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Close reading","q":"Elizabeth's letter is long, chatty and full of gossip. Why might Shelley place it right after the creation?","o":["It explains exactly how the creature escaped","It warns Victor that Justine cannot be trusted","It reveals that Elizabeth already knows the secret","Domestic calm against his horror"],"a":3,"e":"It shows the gentle home Victor's secret will soon destroy."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"Why does Elizabeth fear Ernest will become 'an idler'?","o":["He spends all his allowance on gambling at cards","He wants to become a poet, like Walton once did","He has fallen in with bad company in the town","He hates study and loves the outdoors"],"a":3,"e":"'His time is spent in the open air, climbing the hills or rowing on the lake.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"Why won't the family let Ernest enter foreign service yet?","o":["Not until Victor comes home","Not until Alphonse dies","Not until Elizabeth marries","Not until he finishes university"],"a":0,"e":"'We cannot part with him, at least until his elder brother returns to us.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Close reading","q":"Elizabeth says their 'contented hearts are regulated by the same immutable laws' as the lake and mountains. What is the irony?","o":["The family home is about to be sold to pay debts","The lake is about to flood the house at Belrive","Elizabeth secretly hates the mountains and lake","Victor has already defied nature"],"a":3,"e":"Victor's science has defied 'immutable laws', and the calm home is about to break."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"What was Justine's position among Madame Moritz's four children?","o":["The third","The eldest","The youngest","The only daughter"],"a":0,"e":"'Madame Moritz, her mother, was a widow with four children, of whom Justine was the third.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"Which parent had favoured Justine?","o":["Her mother","Neither parent","Her father","Her stepfather"],"a":2,"e":"'This girl had always been the favourite of her father, but… her mother could not endure her.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Context","q":"Elizabeth claims Geneva's republican institutions produce what?","o":["Simpler manners, less class division","Stronger and more loyal armies than the monarchies","Stricter rules to keep servants in their place","Greater wealth and privilege for the old nobility"],"a":0,"e":"There 'is less distinction between the several classes of its inhabitants'. Shelley quietly praises republics."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Context","q":"Elizabeth says being a servant in Geneva does NOT include what?","o":["Living in the family home","Ignorance and loss of dignity","Learning to read","Wages and holidays"],"a":1,"e":"It 'does not include the idea of ignorance and a sacrifice of the dignity of a human being'."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Irony","q":"Caroline gave Justine a better education than she 'at first intended'. How does this connect to the trial?","o":["She defends herself brilliantly in Latin","Her good character fails to save her","She is trained in law and acts as her own advocate","She writes letters to every judge before the trial"],"a":1,"e":"Character witnesses and education count for nothing against the planted evidence."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"How did Justine show gratitude to Caroline?","o":["With lavish speeches of thanks","By naming a child after her","Through her eyes, not words","By refusing all wages"],"a":2,"e":"'I do not mean that she made any professions… but you could see by her eyes that she almost adored her protectress.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"What surprising habit did Justine pick up from Caroline?","o":["Reading alchemy in secret","Praying in Italian","Visiting the poor in Como","Imitating her phrases and manners"],"a":3,"e":"She 'endeavoured to imitate her phraseology and manners, so that even now she often reminds me of her'."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Plot","q":"Who nursed Caroline during her final illness, besides the family?","o":["The village priest","Elizabeth only","Justine","A hired nurse"],"a":2,"e":"Justine 'had attended her during her illness with the most anxious affection'."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Plot","q":"What happened to Justine's brothers and sister?","o":["They died one by one","They emigrated to France","They became servants in Geneva","They joined Ernest in the army"],"a":0,"e":"'One by one, her brothers and sister died; and her mother… was left childless.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Plot","q":"When did Madame Moritz die?","o":["The day Victor left for Ingolstadt","At the start of the last winter","On the day William was born","Shortly after Caroline died"],"a":1,"e":"'She died on the first approach of cold weather, at the beginning of this last winter.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"How did grief change Justine's manner?","o":["It made her careless of her duties in the house","It made her bitter, withdrawn and silent","It made her sharp-tongued with the other servants","It gave her softness and mildness"],"a":3,"e":"'Grief had given softness and a winning mildness to her manners, which had before been remarkable for vivacity.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"Elizabeth writes warmly about 'little darling William'. Why is the portrait painful to reread?","o":["He will steal the miniature and run away with it","He will grow up to be a cruel young man","He will be sent away to school in Lausanne","He will be the first victim"],"a":3,"e":"The details of his blue eyes and dimples make his murder in Chapter 7 more shocking."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"Which Geneva gossip is NOT in Elizabeth's letter?","o":["Manon's marriage to a banker","Louis Manoir's misfortunes","Clerval's engagement","Miss Mansfield's engagement"],"a":2,"e":"Clerval is in Ingolstadt. The gossip is about the Mansfields, Manon and Manoir."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"Who is M. Duvillard?","o":["A rich banker who married Manon","Justine's confessor","Victor's old schoolfellow","Elizabeth's music teacher"],"a":0,"e":"'Her ugly sister, Manon, married M. Duvillard, the rich banker, last autumn.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"What is unusual about Madame Tavernier, Louis Manoir's intended?","o":["A cousin of Elizabeth","A widow much older than him","An Englishwoman like Miss Mansfield","A former servant of the Frankensteins"],"a":1,"e":"'She is a widow, and much older than Manoir; but she is very much admired.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Plot","q":"How long after reading Elizabeth's letter can Victor leave his room?","o":["Another fortnight","By midsummer","Three months","The next morning"],"a":0,"e":"'In another fortnight I was able to leave my chamber.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says Waldman's praise felt like instruments 'to be afterwards used in putting me to a slow and cruel death'. What does this image reveal?","o":["Waldman secretly wants to harm him","He is afraid of surgery","Science itself now tortures him","He expects to be executed"],"a":2,"e":"His own discipline has become a torture chamber in his mind."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Irony","q":"Waldman takes Victor's discomfort as what?","o":["Modesty","Rudeness","Illness","Boredom"],"a":0,"e":"'Not guessing the real cause, he attributed my feelings to modesty.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does Victor never confide in Clerval, though he loves him?","o":["Telling would fix the memory deeper","Clerval never once asked him what was wrong","He had sworn an oath of silence to Waldman","Clerval would report him to the magistrates"],"a":0,"e":"He feared 'the detail to another would only impress more deeply' the memory."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Quote","q":"'Young men should be diffident of themselves… I was myself when young; but that wears out in a very short time.' Who says this?","o":["Krempe","Alphonse","Clerval","Waldman"],"a":0,"e":"Krempe's 'eulogy on himself' turns the talk away from Victor."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"What life plan lies behind Clerval's oriental studies?","o":["Translating the Bible","Teaching at Ingolstadt","Writing a history of Persia","An enterprising career in the East"],"a":3,"e":"'Resolved to pursue no inglorious career, he turned his eyes toward the East.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"How does Victor's way of reading the orientalists differ from Clerval's?","o":["He translates them carefully into German for practice","He reads them more deeply and critically than Clerval","He reads them aloud to Elizabeth in his letters","He reads for meaning, not mastery"],"a":3,"e":"'I did not, like him, attempt a critical knowledge of their dialects… I read merely to understand their meaning.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor contrasts the oriental writers with 'the manly and heroical poetry of Greece and Rome'. What does he value in the former?","o":["Soothing melancholy and elevating joy","Battles and noble heroes","Hard philosophical argument","Lessons in science"],"a":0,"e":"'Their melancholy is soothing, and their joy elevating.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Plot","q":"Why did Victor stay in Ingolstadt so long after recovering?","o":["Waldman needed his help with the winter lectures","His father told him to stay until he had graduated","He was afraid of making the long journey alone","To avoid leaving Clerval alone"],"a":3,"e":"His return was delayed 'from an unwillingness to leave Clerval in a strange place'."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Plot","q":"How long was the walking tour with Clerval?","o":["Two days","A month","A fortnight","All summer"],"a":2,"e":"'We passed a fortnight in these perambulations.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Theme","q":"What does Clerval teach Victor again on their walk?","o":["To forgive his father for dismissing Agrippa","Love of nature and children's faces","To speak and write Persian like a native scholar","To forget his creation entirely and for ever"],"a":1,"e":"'He again taught me to love the aspect of nature, and the cheerful faces of children.'"},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Sequence","q":"Which event comes LAST in Chapter 6?","o":["Victor writes to Elizabeth","Victor introduces Clerval to the professors","Clerval begins Persian studies","Peasants dancing as they return"],"a":3,"e":"They 'returned to our college on a Sunday afternoon: the peasants were dancing'. It is a moment of joy just before William's death."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Alphonse writes, 'even now your eye skims over the page to seek the words'. What does this show about him?","o":["He suspects Victor already knows what has happened","He knows his son well","He has written the news on the last page on purpose","He is angry that Victor reads carelessly"],"a":1,"e":"He anticipates Victor's impatience and tries, impossibly, to prepare him."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Plot","q":"Who was walking with Alphonse on the evening William disappeared?","o":["Elizabeth, Justine and Ernest","Justine and William only","Elizabeth and the servants of the house","Elizabeth, Ernest and William"],"a":3,"e":"'I, my niece, and your two brothers, went to walk in Plainpalais.' Justine was not with them."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Plot","q":"Where did the family first think William might have gone?","o":["Home on his own","To Justine's aunt at Chêne","To the town gates to watch the coaches","To hide in the woods by the lake"],"a":0,"e":"'Elizabeth conjectured that he might have returned to the house. He was not there.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Character","q":"Why does Elizabeth insist on seeing William's body?","o":["She hoped to find the murderer's mark","She wanted to say goodbye properly","She wanted to see the neck","She did not believe he was dead"],"a":2,"e":"She 'hastily examined the neck of the victim', checking for the miniature she had put there."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Alphonse thinks the miniature was 'doubtless the temptation which urged the murderer'. Why is he wrong?","o":["The miniature was worthless and no one wanted it","The motive was revenge","The miniature was found still on William's neck","William had given it to Justine as a present"],"a":1,"e":"The Creature kills William to hurt Victor. Ch. 16 shows he took the picture afterwards."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Theme","q":"Alphonse begs Victor to come home with feelings that 'heal, instead of festering, the wounds'. What does Victor later do?","o":["Forgives the murderer completely","Confesses everything to his father","Persuades the family to leave Geneva","Lets vengeance fester"],"a":3,"e":"Victor's whole later life is devoted to 'brooding thoughts of vengeance'."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Character","q":"How does Clerval react on reading Alphonse's letter?","o":["He suspects the creature","He weeps too","He insists on going with Victor","He stays calm and businesslike"],"a":1,"e":"'Tears also gushed from the eyes of Clerval, as he read the account of my misfortune.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Plot","q":"Does Clerval go to Geneva with Victor?","o":["No; they part in Ingolstadt","Yes, they travel together in a cabriolet","Yes, but he arrives a day later by coach","No, he goes home to his own father instead"],"a":0,"e":"Victor 'hurried into a cabriolet, and bade farewell to my friend'."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Clerval says 'To die so miserably; to feel the murderer's grasp!' Why does Victor remember these words 'afterwards in solitude'?","o":["They are the last words Clerval spoke","Clerval later denies saying them","They foreshadow Clerval's own death","They contain a clue to the killer"],"a":2,"e":"Clerval will die the same way: strangled by the Creature in Ireland."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does Victor slow down near home, though he set out in haste?","o":["Unknown changes frighten him","He is ashamed to face Elizabeth","He wants Clerval to catch up","His horses are worn out"],"a":0,"e":"'A thousand little circumstances might have by degrees worked other alterations.' He fears change he can't name."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor asks the mountains, 'Is this to prognosticate peace, or to mock at my unhappiness?' What does this show?","o":["He has given up science for religion","He is praying for the murderer","He knows a storm is coming","He reads nature as speaking to him"],"a":3,"e":"Victor treats nature as a sign of his fate, as he does throughout."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"Victor says his dark prophecy failed 'only in one single circumstance'. Which?","o":["It got the murderer wrong","It came true too quickly","It named the wrong victim","It underestimated his suffering"],"a":3,"e":"'I did not conceive the hundredth part of the anguish I was destined to endure.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Setting","q":"Secheron, where Victor spends the night, lies how far from Geneva?","o":["Across the lake near Belrive","Half a league","Three leagues","Just inside the walls"],"a":1,"e":"'A village at the distance of half a league from the city.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Setting","q":"Over which part of the lake did the most violent storm hang?","o":["Over Plainpalais itself","Over the town of Geneva","Above Mont Blanc and Chamounix","Between Belrive and Copêt"],"a":3,"e":"'Exactly north of the town, over the part of the lake which lies between the promontory of Belrive and the village of Copêt.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Language","q":"Victor calls the storm 'this noble war in the sky'. How does it affect him?","o":["It fills him with terror","It lifts his spirits","It sends him home","It makes him think of God"],"a":1,"e":"'This noble war in the sky elevated my spirits.' He finds sublime beauty even in grief."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor's whole case against the Creature rests on what?","o":["Footprints and torn clothing","Testimony from Ernest","A confession from the Creature","Seeing it nearby"],"a":3,"e":"He sees it near the spot and instantly 'could not doubt it'. As it happens he is right, yet his reasoning is no better than the court's."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"'Was this his first crime?' What does Victor assume about the Creature's two missing years?","o":["That it learned to speak","That it hunted for him","That it lived in a cave","That it lived by crime"],"a":3,"e":"He assumes 'a depraved wretch, whose delight was in carnage', the opposite of what the Creature later tells."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Sequence","q":"Which happens FIRST after Victor sees the Creature in the storm?","o":["It climbs Mont Salêve","Ernest tells him about Justine","Victor looks at his mother's portrait","Victor enters his father's house"],"a":0,"e":"The Creature climbs Salêve that night; at dawn Victor goes home."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Character","q":"What does Victor tell the servants on arriving at five in the morning?","o":["To prepare breakfast","To fetch Elizabeth","Not to wake the family","To fetch the magistrate"],"a":2,"e":"'I told the servants not to disturb the family, and went into the library.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says six years passed 'in a dream but for one indelible trace'. What is the trace?","o":["The creation and its results","His love for Elizabeth","His friendship with Clerval","His mother's death"],"a":0,"e":"Everything else feels unreal next to what he made."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Character","q":"What is under Caroline's portrait in the library?","o":["Elizabeth's needlework","A miniature of William","A clock from Ingolstadt","Victor's university prize"],"a":1,"e":"'Below this picture was a miniature of William.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Character","q":"What does Ernest wish about Victor's return?","o":["That he'd come three months ago","That he'd brought Clerval","That he'd stayed away","That he had never left"],"a":0,"e":"'I wish you had come three months ago, and then you would have found us all joyous.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Character","q":"Who besides Elizabeth refuses at first to believe Justine guilty?","o":["The magistrate","Ernest alone","No one in the family","Alphonse alone"],"a":2,"e":"Ernest says 'No one would believe it at first… even now Elizabeth will not be convinced.'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Plot","q":"Who found the miniature in Justine's pocket?","o":["A magistrate","A servant","Elizabeth","Ernest"],"a":1,"e":"'One of the servants, happening to examine the apparel she had worn' found it."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Why do the servants go to a magistrate 'without saying a word to any of the family'?","o":["They are terrified of what Justine might do to them","The family might shield her","They hope for a reward from the magistrates","Alphonse ordered them to keep the matter quiet"],"a":1,"e":"Their secrecy hints that the household might shield her, and it seals her fate."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Irony","q":"Alphonse says he'd rather be 'for ever ignorant than have discovered so much depravity' in one he valued. Why is this ironic?","o":["He never valued Justine","Victor's secret is the real depravity","Justine really is guilty","Alphonse is secretly glad"],"a":1,"e":"The one hiding the truth is his son, standing in front of him."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor is calm because he believes no evidence could convict an innocent. What is wrong with this?","o":["The judges are known to have been bribed","He secretly holds the evidence that would clear her","Circumstantial evidence can convict anyone","Justine has already confessed to the murder"],"a":2,"e":"He trusts the system to reach the truth he himself is concealing."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Character","q":"What does Elizabeth hope Victor's arrival will bring?","o":["Comfort for Alphonse","A way to clear Justine","Money for a lawyer","News of the murderer"],"a":1,"e":"'You perhaps will find some means to justify my poor guiltless Justine.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"At what hour does Justine's trial begin?","o":["Eleven o'clock","Dawn","Nine o'clock","Noon"],"a":0,"e":"'We passed a few sad hours until eleven o'clock, when the trial was to commence.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor call the trial?","o":["A public spectacle for the crowd","A necessary evil of the law","A wretched mockery of justice","A fair hearing badly managed"],"a":2,"e":"'During the whole of this wretched mockery of justice I suffered living torture.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Close reading","q":"Why can't Victor confess to the crime himself?","o":["His father forbids him","He is too ill to speak","He fears the creature","He was away when it happened"],"a":3,"e":"'I was absent when it was committed, and such a declaration would have been considered as the ravings of a madman.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Character","q":"How is Justine dressed at her trial?","o":["In prison rags","In mourning","In her servant's apron","In white, like a bride"],"a":1,"e":"'She was dressed in mourning.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does Justine force herself to look courageous in court?","o":["Her confessor told her to","She knows she will be acquitted","She wants to impress the crowd","Her earlier confusion was used against her"],"a":3,"e":"'As her confusion had before been adduced as a proof of her guilt, she worked up her mind to an appearance of courage.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"Who saw Justine near the murder spot towards morning?","o":["A priest","A night watchman","A market-woman","Ernest"],"a":2,"e":"'She… had been perceived by a market-woman not far from the spot.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"When did Justine return to the Frankenstein house?","o":["About eight o'clock","At midnight","Just before dawn","After noon"],"a":0,"e":"'She returned to the house about eight o'clock.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"What turned the court against Justine most strongly?","o":["Elizabeth identifying the miniature","Justine's own confession","The market-woman's testimony","Ernest's evidence"],"a":0,"e":"When Elizabeth proved it was the same picture she had placed on William, 'a murmur of horror and indignation filled the court'."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Setting","q":"Where had Justine spent the evening of the murder?","o":["At her aunt's in Chêne","At the Frankensteins' house","In Plainpalais with the family","At church in Geneva"],"a":0,"e":"'At the house of an aunt at Chêne, a village situated at about a league from Geneva.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"Where did Justine spend the night when shut out of Geneva?","o":["At her aunt's house","Under the trees in Plainpalais","At the inn at Secheron","In a cottage barn"],"a":3,"e":"She 'was forced to remain several hours of the night in a barn belonging to a cottage'."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Connection","q":"What woke Justine in the barn at dawn?","o":["Some steps","A dog barking","Church bells","A storm"],"a":0,"e":"'Some steps disturbed her, and she awoke.' In Ch. 16 the Creature explains he found her sleeping there and planted the picture."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Close reading","q":"Justine asks, 'why should he have stolen the jewel, to part with it again so soon?' What does this argument show?","o":["She reasons clearly","She knows who the killer is","She is confused and rambling","She suspects Victor"],"a":0,"e":"Her logic is good. The court ignores it."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Theme","q":"Why are Justine's character witnesses 'timorous and unwilling'?","o":["Fear and hatred of the crime","They barely knew her","They thought she was mad","They were bribed by the family"],"a":0,"e":"'Fear and hatred of the crime of which they supposed her guilty rendered them timorous.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Character","q":"How long had Elizabeth lived with Justine, by her own account?","o":["Five years, then nearly two","Her whole life","Ten years together","Only a few months"],"a":0,"e":"'At one time for five and at another for nearly two years.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Quote","q":"'I see a fellow creature about to perish through the cowardice of her pretended friends.' Who speaks?","o":["Alphonse","Victor","Justine","Elizabeth"],"a":3,"e":"Elizabeth is the only one who defends Justine in court; Victor stays silent."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Irony","q":"What did Elizabeth's speech actually achieve?","o":["A delay of the trial","Justine's acquittal","More anger at Justine","A confession from Victor"],"a":2,"e":"The approval was for Elizabeth; 'public indignation was turned with renewed violence' on Justine, now called ungrateful."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Language","q":"'The fangs of remorse tore my bosom.' What does Victor compare remorse to?","o":["A poison","A fire","A storm","A wild beast"],"a":3,"e":"The animal image shows remorse as a predator that 'would not forgo their hold'."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"How was Justine's verdict decided?","o":["By a single judge","By ballots, all black","By a show of hands","By a coin toss"],"a":1,"e":"'The ballots had been thrown; they were all black, and Justine was condemned.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Irony","q":"The court officer says judges dislike condemning on 'circumstantial evidence'. Why is this ironic?","o":["The judge was her cousin","Her confession was false","She was never questioned","There was no evidence"],"a":1,"e":"Her false confession is taken as the proof that settles their doubt."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Quote","q":"'All judges had rather that ten innocent should suffer than that one guilty should escape.' Who says it, and how?","o":["Elizabeth, in despair","Victor, bitterly reversing a saying","Alphonse, in sincere belief","The court officer, boasting"],"a":1,"e":"The usual legal saying is the opposite; Victor inverts it in bitterness."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Character","q":"What does Elizabeth say the confession does to her?","o":["Confirms what she suspected","Makes her angry at Victor","Shakes her faith in goodness","Gives her peace at last"],"a":2,"e":"'How shall I ever again believe in human goodness?'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Setting","q":"How is Justine found in her prison cell?","o":["Kneeling in prayer","Writing a letter","Pacing the floor","On straw, manacled"],"a":3,"e":"'Sitting on some straw at the farther end; her hands were manacled.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"Why did Justine confess falsely?","o":["To obtain absolution","To protect Elizabeth","To end the trial quickly","Because she was tortured"],"a":0,"e":"'I confessed, that I might obtain absolution.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Theme","q":"What did Justine's confessor threaten her with?","o":["Torture on the rack","Her family's disgrace","Excommunication and hell fire","Banishment from Geneva"],"a":2,"e":"'He threatened excommunication and hell fire in my last moments if I continued obdurate.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Close reading","q":"'I almost began to think that I was the monster that he said I was.' Which theme does this line touch?","o":["Labels make a monster","Religion always comforts","Guilt always shows","Women are weak"],"a":0,"e":"Justine, like the Creature, is pressured into the role others give her."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Character","q":"How does Justine face death?","o":["Hoping for rescue","In rage and despair","Resigned and unafraid","Blaming Victor"],"a":2,"e":"'I do not fear to die… that pang is past.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Quote","q":"'Learn from me, dear lady, to submit in patience to the will of heaven!' What does it mirror?","o":["Victor's 'Learn from me' to Walton","Caroline's deathbed blessing","The Creature's demand for a mate","Clerval's final letter"],"a":0,"e":"Both say 'learn from me', but Justine teaches patience and Victor teaches the danger of knowledge."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does Justine ask if Victor believes her guilty?","o":["He refuses to look at her","He groaned in the corner","He stares at her coldly","He has testified against her"],"a":1,"e":"His groan from the corner draws her attention."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor feel 'alive in my bosom' in the prison?","o":["A burning coal","The never-dying worm","A caged bird","A cold stone"],"a":1,"e":"An image of endless torment from scripture and Milton: 'the never-dying worm'."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Language","q":"Elizabeth's misery is compared to a cloud passing over what?","o":["The fair moon","A calm lake","The rising sun","A mountain peak"],"a":0,"e":"Innocent grief 'for a while hides but cannot tarnish its brightness'."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Quote","q":"'Live, and be happy, and make others so.' Who says this to whom?","o":["Justine to Elizabeth","Elizabeth to Justine","Caroline to Victor","Alphonse to Ernest"],"a":0,"e":"Justine's farewell is generous even on the eve of execution."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"What happened to Victor's own 'purposed avowal' to the judges?","o":["He made it, and was ignored","He wrote it but never sent it","It died on his lips","He told only his father"],"a":2,"e":"Hearing their 'harsh, unfeeling reasoning… my purposed avowal died away on my lips'."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Language","q":"Victor calls William and Justine 'the first hapless victims' of what?","o":["Cruel fate alone","The courts of Geneva","His unhallowed arts","The Creature's madness"],"a":2,"e":"He takes the blame here: 'my unhallowed arts'."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Structure","q":"In the final paragraph Victor addresses his family directly: 'Ye weep… these are not your last tears!' Why?","o":["He is speaking at their graves","He wants Walton to forgive him","He is writing them a letter","His future victims make it a prophecy"],"a":3,"e":"His 'prophetic soul' knows more deaths are coming, so the passage builds dread."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor call 'more painful to the human mind' than a quick run of events?","o":["Seeing the guilty go free","The dead calm that follows","The long wait for a verdict","The moment the bad news first arrives"],"a":1,"e":"'The dead calmness of inaction and certainty which follows and deprives the soul both of hope and fear.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Close reading","q":"'Justine died, she rested, and I was alive.' What does the short sentence stress?","o":["Justine has peace; Victor does not","Victor is glad to have survived","Justine deserved to die","Victor plans to die soon"],"a":0,"e":"The rhythm sets her rest against his tormented life."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says he 'wandered like an evil spirit', yet his 'heart overflowed with kindness'. What contradiction does he present?","o":["Good intentions, terrible results","Love for Elizabeth and fear of marriage","A wish for revenge and a wish for peace","Faith in God and doubt of heaven"],"a":0,"e":"He insists he began 'with benevolent intentions'. Compare the Creature's 'I was benevolent and good'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Character","q":"What does Alphonse say excessive sorrow prevents?","o":["Proper prayer and Christian faith","Justice for the dead and their families","Sleep and good health in old age","Improvement and daily usefulness"],"a":3,"e":"'Excessive sorrow prevents improvement or enjoyment, or even the discharge of daily usefulness.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Irony","q":"Why is Alphonse's sound advice 'totally inapplicable' to Victor?","o":["Victor no longer trusts his father","It's remorse, not grief","The advice is meant for Elizabeth","Victor never loved William at all"],"a":1,"e":"'If remorse had not mingled its bitterness, and terror its alarm, with my other sensations.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Setting","q":"Why did Victor dislike living inside Geneva?","o":["The streets reminded him of William","The house was too close to the court","The gates closed at ten","The neighbours gossiped about Justine"],"a":2,"e":"'The shutting of the gates regularly at ten o'clock… had rendered our residence within the walls of Geneva very irksome.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor do at night on the lake at Belrive?","o":["Fishes by lantern light with Ernest","Rows to Geneva and back for exercise","Searches the shore for the creature","Drifts and broods for hours"],"a":3,"e":"He sometimes left 'the boat to pursue its own course and gave way to my own miserable reflections'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Close reading","q":"On the lake Victor calls himself 'the only unquiet thing… if I except some bat, or the frogs'. Why the comic exception?","o":["The bats frighten him","Even his despair is being exact","He is losing his mind","The frogs remind him of his experiments"],"a":1,"e":"The precise, almost humorous detail shows him observing even at his lowest."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Plot","q":"What keeps Victor from drowning himself?","o":["His faith and fear of hell","Elizabeth and his family","A promise to Clerval","His duty to the university"],"a":1,"e":"He thinks of 'the heroic and suffering Elizabeth' and of leaving his family 'unprotected to the malice of the fiend'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"Victor fears the creature will commit some 'signal crime'. What will it be like?","o":["A crime against the university","An attack on Victor himself","A theft from his father's house","Bad enough to dwarf the past"],"a":3,"e":"He fears a crime 'which by its enormity should almost efface the recollection of the past'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Close reading","q":"'There was always scope for fear so long as anything I loved remained behind.' What does this predict?","o":["The creature will leave Geneva","He will protect them all successfully","He will stop loving anyone","Each loved one is a target"],"a":3,"e":"The Creature's strategy later is to destroy everyone Victor loves, one by one."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Language","q":"Victor would have pilgrimaged to which mountains to throw the creature down?","o":["The Alps","The Andes","The Himalayas","The Pyrenees"],"a":1,"e":"'I would have made a pilgrimage to the highest peak of the Andes, could I… have precipitated him to their base.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Character","q":"How has Elizabeth changed since Justine's death?","o":["She has turned to science","She blames Victor openly","She has grown hard and bitter","She finds pleasure sacrilegious"],"a":3,"e":"'All pleasure seemed to her sacrilege toward the dead.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Theme","q":"Before Justine's death, how did Elizabeth think of vice and injustice?","o":["As God's punishment","As problems for the law","As tales from books","As common in Geneva"],"a":2,"e":"She saw them as 'tales of ancient days or imaginary evils'. Now 'misery has come home'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Quote","q":"'When falsehood can look so like the truth, who can assure themselves of certain happiness?' Who says it?","o":["Victor","Alphonse","Justine","Elizabeth"],"a":3,"e":"Elizabeth's insight runs through the whole novel: appearances deceive."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Language","q":"Elizabeth feels as if she were 'walking on the edge of' what?","o":["A grave","A battlefield","A precipice","A frozen lake"],"a":2,"e":"'A precipice, towards which thousands are crowding and endeavouring to plunge me into the abyss.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Irony","q":"Elizabeth says the murderer 'walks about the world free, and perhaps respected'. Why does this hurt Victor?","o":["It describes Alphonse","It describes him","It describes Waldman","It describes Clerval"],"a":1,"e":"Victor is free and respected while his creation's victims lie dead. 'I, not in deed, but in effect, was the true murderer.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Character","q":"What does Elizabeth see in Victor's face that makes her tremble?","o":["Love and longing","Guilt and shame","Despair and revenge","Fever and madness"],"a":2,"e":"'There is an expression of despair, and sometimes of revenge, in your countenance that makes me tremble.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Irony","q":"Elizabeth asks, 'What can disturb our peace?' Why does Victor draw closer to her?","o":["He means to confess at last","He wants to kiss her","He is cold by the lake","He fears the destroyer nearby"],"a":3,"e":"'I drew near to her, as if in terror, lest at that very moment the destroyer had been near to rob me of her.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Language","q":"What animal image does Victor use for himself?","o":["A hunted fox","A caged eagle","A wounded deer","A drowning dog"],"a":2,"e":"'The wounded deer dragging its fainting limbs to some untrodden brake, there to gaze upon the arrow… was but a type of me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Plot","q":"About how long after Justine's death does Victor go to Chamounix?","o":["A year","Six months","Nearly two months","The next week"],"a":2,"e":"'It was about the middle of the month of August, nearly two months after the death of Justine.'"},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Plot","q":"Why does Victor switch from horse to mule?","o":["His horse went lame","More sure-footed","It was cheaper","A guide insisted"],"a":1,"e":"He 'hired a mule, as the more sure-footed and least liable to receive injury on these rugged roads'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Theme","q":"In the ravine of Arve, what does the mountains' power make Victor feel?","o":["A wish to climb the peak","That he is nothing at all","Hatred for the creature","He fears only the Almighty"],"a":3,"e":"Sublime nature 'spoke of a power mighty as Omnipotence', and he 'ceased to fear… any being less almighty'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Setting","q":"Which valley does Victor call more beautiful but less sublime than Chamounix?","o":["Arve","Servox","Pélissier","Montanvert"],"a":1,"e":"Chamounix 'is more wonderful and sublime, but not so beautiful and picturesque as that of Servox'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Close reading","q":"'Maternal Nature bade me weep no more.' What role does nature play here?","o":["An enemy to conquer","A cruel judge","A comforting mother","A secret to uncover"],"a":2,"e":"After his ambition treated nature as a citadel, she now consoles him like a mother."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Setting","q":"What does Victor watch from his window at Chamounix before sleeping?","o":["Pale lightning over Mont Blanc","The creature on the ice","Snow falling softly","Stars over the glacier"],"a":0,"e":"'The pallid lightnings that played above Mont Blanc.' Lightning follows him again."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Setting","q":"What does Victor stand beside the day after reaching Chamounix?","o":["The church of Chamounix","The hut on Montanvert","The bridge of Pélissier","The source of the Arveiron"],"a":3,"e":"'I stood beside the sources of the Arveiron, which take their rise in a glacier.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor call the glacier valley?","o":["Imperial Nature's presence-chamber","The tomb of the ancients","The cradle of the world","The cathedral of the gods"],"a":0,"e":"'This glorious presence-chamber of imperial Nature', a king's audience room."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Theme","q":"What do the sublime scenes do to Victor's grief?","o":["Subdue but don't remove it","Turn it into rage","Make it much worse","Remove it entirely"],"a":0,"e":"'Although they did not remove my grief, they subdued and tranquillised it.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Plot","q":"What weather greets Victor the morning he climbs Montanvert?","o":["Pouring rain and thick mist","Bright sun on the snow","A violent thunderstorm","A blizzard"],"a":0,"e":"'The rain was pouring in torrents, and thick mists hid the summits.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Character","q":"Why does Victor climb Montanvert without a guide?","o":["Another person would spoil the solitude","No guide would go in the rain","He could not pay one","He wanted to find the creature"],"a":0,"e":"'The presence of another would destroy the solitary grandeur of the scene.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Setting","q":"What danger lies in one snowy ravine on the path?","o":["Wolves hide in the pines","The path crosses thin ice","Loud speech can start a rockfall","Bandits wait for travellers"],"a":2,"e":"'Even speaking in a loud voice, produces a concussion of air sufficient to draw destruction.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Theme","q":"Victor asks why man boasts of 'sensibilities superior to those apparent in the brute'. What's his point?","o":["Reason makes men free","Animals are crueller than men","Men are gods compared to beasts","Feeling makes us more vulnerable"],"a":3,"e":"With only 'hunger, thirst, and desire, we might be nearly free; but now we are moved by every wind'."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Allusion","q":"The poem Victor quotes ends 'Nought may endure but mutability!' Who wrote it?","o":["Coleridge","Lord Byron","Milton","Percy Bysshe Shelley"],"a":3,"e":"It is 'Mutability' by Percy Shelley, Mary Shelley's husband."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does Victor quote 'Mutability' here?","o":["Nothing in feeling lasts","Because he wrote it himself","To describe the changing glacier","To impress Walton with his learning"],"a":0,"e":"The day's peace vanished overnight: 'Man's yesterday may ne'er be like his morrow.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Plot","q":"How long does it take Victor to cross the sea of ice?","o":["Nearly two hours","Most of a day","All afternoon","Ten minutes"],"a":0,"e":"'The field of ice is almost a league in width, but I spent nearly two hours in crossing it.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Quote","q":"'Wandering spirits, if indeed ye wander… allow me this faint happiness, or take me.' What happens right after?","o":["He hears William's voice","The creature appears","An avalanche falls","The mist covers him again"],"a":1,"e":"He calls on spirits, and the Creature arrives 'with superhuman speed'."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Close reading","q":"How does the creature cross the glacier, compared with Victor?","o":["Bounding where Victor crept","On a dog sledge","By a hidden path","Slowly, using a staff"],"a":0,"e":"'He bounded over the crevices in the ice, among which I had walked with caution.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Character","q":"Which mix does Victor see in the creature's face?","o":["Joy, pride and relief","Anguish, disdain and malignity","Calm, curiosity and kindness","Fear, shame and sorrow"],"a":1,"e":"'His countenance bespoke bitter anguish, combined with disdain and malignity.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Quote","q":"'Begone, vile insect! Or rather, stay, that I may trample you to dust!' What does the insult show?","o":["That the creature is small","Victor's contempt for his creation","Victor's sense of humour","Victor's fear of the creature's size"],"a":1,"e":"Calling an eight-foot being an 'insect' shows Victor's disgust, not reason."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Theme","q":"'All men hate the wretched; how, then, must I be hated.' What social point does the creature make?","o":["God hates sinners","Women hate ugliness","Society hates suffering","Rich men hate the poor"],"a":2,"e":"Misery itself repels sympathy. He is 'miserable beyond all living things'."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Quote","q":"'How dare you sport thus with life?' Who says it?","o":["The creature","Waldman","Victor","Elizabeth"],"a":0,"e":"The Creature turns Victor's own crime against him: Victor played with life by creating him and now wants to kill him."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Plot","q":"What does the creature threaten if Victor refuses his conditions?","o":["To kill his remaining friends","To burn Geneva","To kill himself","To tell the magistrates"],"a":0,"e":"'I will glut the maw of death, until it be satiated with the blood of your remaining friends.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Plot","q":"What happens when Victor springs at the creature?","o":["They grapple and fall","It easily dodges him","Victor wounds it","It knocks him down"],"a":1,"e":"'He easily eluded me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Close reading","q":"'Remember, thou hast made me more powerful than thyself.' Why does the creature switch to 'thou' and 'thee'?","o":["He's mocking Victor","He is quoting Victor's father","He learned old French","Biblical tone toward his maker"],"a":3,"e":"He speaks to Victor as Adam speaks to God, in Milton's language."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Character","q":"What does the creature offer to be, if Victor does his part?","o":["Mild and docile","A protector of Geneva","A scientist like Victor","Victor's servant for life"],"a":0,"e":"'I will be even mild and docile to my natural lord and king if thou wilt also perform thy part.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Quote","q":"'Oh, Frankenstein, be not equitable to every other and trample upon me…' how?","o":["Forever","Alone","In silence","First"],"a":1,"e":"He asks for the justice Victor gives everyone except his own creature."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Character","q":"Where has the creature been living?","o":["In the hut on Montanvert","In the forests of Jura","In an abandoned castle","In the ice caves"],"a":3,"e":"'The caves of ice, which I only do not fear, are a dwelling to me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Theme","q":"The creature says the bleak skies are 'kinder to me than' whom?","o":["Your fellow beings","My creator","The cottagers","The beasts of the forest"],"a":0,"e":"Nature shelters him; humans don't."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Theme","q":"Which legal argument does the creature make for being heard?","o":["Even the guilty may speak","Children cannot be tried","Every man may have a lawyer","Strangers deserve a pardon"],"a":0,"e":"'The guilty are allowed, by human laws, bloody as they are, to speak in their own defence.' This is pointed after Justine."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Irony","q":"'Oh, praise the eternal justice of man!' What is the creature mocking?","o":["Walton's laws at sea","The courts of Ingolstadt","God's justice","Victor's double standard"],"a":3,"e":"Victor accuses him of murder 'and yet you would, with a satisfied conscience, destroy your own creature'."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Character","q":"Victor curses 'the hands that formed you'. What does he add in brackets?","o":["That he is sorry for it","That Waldman taught them","That God made them","That he curses himself too"],"a":3,"e":"'Cursed (although I curse myself) be the hands that formed you!'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature covers Victor's eyes: 'thus I take from thee a sight which you abhor'. What is the gesture?","o":["Bitter grace","A magic spell","An attempt to blind him","A threat of violence"],"a":0,"e":"He answers Victor's 'relieve me from the sight' literally, with bitter courtesy."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Setting","q":"Where does the creature tell his story?","o":["At the inn at Chamounix","In a hut by a fire","In a cave of ice","On the open glacier"],"a":1,"e":"'Come to the hut upon the mountain.' He lights a fire, the element he once discovered."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Character","q":"Which reason does Victor NOT give for agreeing to listen?","o":["To learn if it killed William","Compassion","Fear for his own life","Curiosity"],"a":2,"e":"He mentions curiosity, compassion, wanting to confirm or deny the murder, and 'the duties of a creator'."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Theme","q":"For the first time, what does Victor feel on the glacier?","o":["Fear of death","A creator's duties","Pride in his work","Love for the creature"],"a":1,"e":"'For the first time, also, I felt what the duties of a creator towards his creature were.'"},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Close reading","q":"They enter the hut 'the fiend with an air of exultation, I with a heavy heart'. Why exultation?","o":["He plans to kill Victor","He has won the fight","He's finally being heard","He is drunk"],"a":2,"e":"After years of rejection, being heard is a victory."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature first 'saw, felt, heard, and smelt at the same time'. What does this describe?","o":["Senses not yet separated","Victor's experiment going wrong","A fever caused by electricity","A dream before waking"],"a":0,"e":"It took 'a long time before I learned to distinguish between the operations of my various senses'. It is a newborn's experience."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Context","q":"The creature's account of learning through the senses reflects which philosophy?","o":["Descartes's innate ideas","Plato's theory of ideas","Calvin's doctrine of original sin","Locke: the mind starts blank"],"a":3,"e":"He begins with sensation and builds ideas from experience, as in John Locke's account of the 'blank slate'."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Setting","q":"Where does the creature first rest after leaving Victor's rooms?","o":["The ice caves of Montanvert","The forest near Ingolstadt","A shepherd's hut in the snow","A barn outside Geneva"],"a":1,"e":"'This was the forest near Ingolstadt; and here I lay by the side of a brook.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What had the creature taken to cover himself on leaving Victor's apartment?","o":["Victor's cloak","Nothing at all","Some clothes","A blanket from the bed"],"a":2,"e":"'On a sensation of cold, I had covered myself with some clothes.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Close reading","q":"'I was a poor, helpless, miserable wretch… I sat down and wept.' How does this compare with Victor's view of the night of creation?","o":["It shows the creature planning revenge","A frightened infant, not a monster","It shows he felt nothing at all","It matches Victor's account exactly"],"a":1,"e":"Victor saw a 'demoniacal corpse'; the creature remembers being cold, lost and crying."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Symbol","q":"What is the first thing that gives the creature pleasure?","o":["Birdsong in the trees","The taste of berries","The warmth of a fire","The rising moon"],"a":3,"e":"'A gentle light stole over the heavens and gave me a sensation of pleasure… [The moon].'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"Where does the creature find the cloak he wraps himself in?","o":["In Victor's wardrobe","Under a tree","In a shepherd's hut","On a beggar's body"],"a":1,"e":"'Under one of the trees I found a huge cloak.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Character","q":"Which birds' songs does the creature find sweet, and which harsh?","o":["Blackbird and thrush sweet; sparrow harsh","Thrush harsh; robin sweet","All birds sound harsh to him","Sparrow sweet; nightingale harsh"],"a":0,"e":"'The sparrow uttered none but harsh notes, whilst those of the blackbird and thrush were sweet.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Close reading","q":"Why did the creature's own attempts at sound frighten him 'into silence'?","o":["They attracted wolves and other wild animals","They were uncouth and inarticulate","They hurt his throat, which was not yet formed","They echoed strangely off the trees around him"],"a":1,"e":"He tried to imitate birds but 'the uncouth and inarticulate sounds which broke from me frightened me'."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Theme","q":"What does the creature learn from burning his hand in the fire?","o":["Fire is evil and must be avoided","Humans are cruel to leave fires","One cause, opposite effects","Pain is a punishment for greed"],"a":2,"e":"'How strange, I thought, that the same cause should produce such opposite effects!' Like knowledge itself."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Sequence","q":"Put the creature's fire discoveries in order.","o":["Burns hand, dries wood, fans flame, roasts food","Fans flame, burns hand, roasts food, dries wood","Dries wood, roasts food, burns hand, fans flame","Roasts food, burns hand, fans flame, dries wood"],"a":0,"e":"He burns his hand, learns wet wood dries, invents a fan of branches, then discovers cooking."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What food did roasting improve, and what did it spoil?","o":["Improved meat; spoiled acorns","Improved nuts and roots; spoiled berries","Improved berries; spoiled bread","Improved bread; spoiled cheese"],"a":1,"e":"'The berries were spoiled by this operation, and the nuts and roots much improved.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"Why did the creature leave his first forest?","o":["Food became scarce","Hunters came looking for him","The fire burned out the trees","He was searching for Victor"],"a":0,"e":"'Food, however, became scarce… I resolved to quit the place.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Irony","q":"What did the creature most lament losing when he moved?","o":["The cloak","The fire","The brook","The moon"],"a":1,"e":"He 'exceedingly lamented the loss of the fire… and knew not how to reproduce it'. Even Prometheus's gift can be lost."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"In which direction did the creature travel after leaving the forest?","o":["Towards the setting sun","East to the mountains","North to the ice","Back towards Ingolstadt"],"a":0,"e":"'I struck across the wood towards the setting sun.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What did the shepherd's breakfast contain?","o":["Fish and potatoes","Meat, bread and beer","Bread, cheese, milk and wine","Porridge and honey"],"a":2,"e":"'Bread, cheese, milk, and wine; the latter, however, I did not like.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Allusion","q":"The creature compares the shepherd's hut to Pandæmonium. What is Pandæmonium?","o":["The rock where Prometheus was chained by Zeus","The palace of the gods on Mount Olympus","The garden of Eden before Adam and Eve's fall","The demons' palace in Paradise Lost"],"a":3,"e":"In Milton, the fallen angels build Pandæmonium after the 'lake of fire'. The creature's first shelter is a devil's paradise."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What happens when the creature enters a cottage in the village?","o":["The family offers him bread and lets him stay","Children shriek; he is stoned","A priest comes out and blesses him at the door","He is arrested by soldiers and locked up"],"a":1,"e":"'The children shrieked, and one of the women fainted… some attacked me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Setting","q":"What is the creature's hovel attached to?","o":["A barn by a mill","A church wall","An inn's stable","The back of a cottage"],"a":3,"e":"'It was situated against the back of the cottage and surrounded… by a pig sty and a clear pool.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Language","q":"What does the creature call his hovel when he crawls out of it?","o":["My kennel","My nest","My palace","My cell"],"a":0,"e":"'As soon as morning dawned I crept from my kennel.' He already describes himself like an animal."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"How does light get into the hovel once he has blocked the gaps?","o":["Through a small window","Through the pig sty","By the cottagers' candles","Through the roof"],"a":1,"e":"'All the light I enjoyed came through the sty.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does the creature compare the hovel to 'a paradise'?","o":["It had a fine view of the mountains and lake","It was richly furnished with straw and blankets","The cottagers had left it ready to welcome him","After the forest, anything dry was bliss"],"a":3,"e":"It was 'a paradise compared to the bleak forest… the rain-dropping branches, and dank earth'."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Character","q":"How was the girl he first sees dressed?","o":["Coarse blue petticoat and linen jacket","A shepherd's cloak and boots","A black mourning dress","A silk gown and bonnet"],"a":0,"e":"'A coarse blue petticoat and a linen jacket being her only garb.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What does the young man do when he meets the girl with the pail?","o":["Scolds her for walking so slowly with it","Asks her for money for the farmer","Takes the pail and carries it","Walks past without a word or a glance"],"a":2,"e":"'He took the pail from her head and bore it to the cottage himself.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Character","q":"How does the creature see into the cottage?","o":["From a gap in the thatch of the roof","By climbing up and looking down the chimney","A tiny chink in a boarded window","Through the cottage door, left open in the day"],"a":2,"e":"A window had been filled with wood; 'in one of these was a small and almost imperceptible chink'."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Close reading","q":"Watching the old man raise the kneeling girl, the creature feels 'a mixture of pain and pleasure'. Why both?","o":["He is afraid of being caught","He sees love he cannot share","He is hungry and cold","He dislikes the music"],"a":1,"e":"He experiences tenderness for the first time, and his own exclusion from it."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Character","q":"What does the old man play?","o":["A stringed instrument","The violin and drum","A flute","The organ"],"a":0,"e":"'Taking up an instrument, began to play… sounds sweeter than the voice of the thrush.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What surprises the creature at nightfall?","o":["Tapers to prolong light","Locked doors and shutters","The old man singing","Strangers visiting"],"a":0,"e":"'The cottagers had a means of prolonging light by the use of tapers.'"},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What were the youth's 'monotonous' evening sounds?","o":["Reading aloud","Counting money","Singing","Prayers"],"a":0,"e":"'I since found that he read aloud, but at that time I knew nothing of the science of words.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Plot","q":"What does the creature discover about the old man?","o":["He is Felix's grandfather","He is deaf","He is blind","He is a nobleman in disguise"],"a":2,"e":"'The old man, whom I soon perceived to be blind.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature thinks the cottagers have 'every luxury'. What does this show?","o":["They are secretly rich and hiding their money","He means to rob them once he learns their habits","He judges as a homeless being","He has already been inside the cottage at night"],"a":2,"e":"Fire, food and company seem luxurious to someone who has none."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Theme","q":"What is the first cause of the family's unhappiness the creature discovers?","o":["Felix's lost love","Poverty","Exile","Illness"],"a":1,"e":"'It was poverty, and they suffered that evil in a very distressing degree.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Plot","q":"What does the family live on in winter?","o":["Garden vegetables and a cow's milk","Charity brought from the church by the priest","Fish that Felix catches in the river","Bread bought from the village baker each week"],"a":0,"e":"'Their nourishment consisted entirely of the vegetables of their garden and the milk of one cow.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Character","q":"What sacrifice do the younger cottagers make that moves the creature?","o":["Giving up their beds so he can sleep by the fire","Selling their best clothes to buy him medicine","Feeding the old man first","Working through the night in the cold"],"a":2,"e":"'Several times they placed food before the old man when they reserved none for themselves.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Character","q":"What does the creature do after realising his thefts hurt them?","o":["Steals from the village instead","Confesses to the old man","Leaves the area for good","Lives on berries, nuts and roots"],"a":3,"e":"'I abstained and satisfied myself with berries, nuts, and roots.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Plot","q":"How does the creature secretly help the family?","o":["Leaving coins on the doorstep for them to find","Mending the roof of the cottage in the dark","Gathering firewood at night","Milking the cow before Agatha wakes up"],"a":2,"e":"He takes the youth's tools at night 'and brought home firing sufficient for the consumption of several days'."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Close reading","q":"What does Felix do with the day freed by the creature's wood?","o":["Goes hunting in the forest","Repairs the cottage and garden","Writes letters to Safie","Visits the village inn"],"a":1,"e":"'He did not go to the forest that day, but spent it in repairing the cottage and cultivating the garden.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Language","q":"When he first grasps speech, how does the creature rank it?","o":["A godlike science","A dangerous gift","A human prison","A useless noise"],"a":0,"e":"'This was indeed a godlike science, and I ardently desired to become acquainted with it.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Plot","q":"Which are among the first words the creature learns?","o":["Fire, milk, bread and wood","God, father, sin and death","Love, friend, help and home","Sun, moon, star and sky"],"a":0,"e":"'I learned and applied the words, fire, milk, bread, and wood.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Character","q":"What names does the creature learn for the girl?","o":["Sister or Agatha","Agatha or Felicity","Maria or child","Safie or daughter"],"a":0,"e":"'The girl was called sister or Agatha, and the youth Felix, brother, or son.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Close reading","q":"Which words does the creature recognise but cannot yet use?","o":["Good, dearest, unhappy","Wood, snow, rain","Father, sister, son","Fire, milk, bread"],"a":0,"e":"He 'distinguished several other words without being able as yet to understand or apply them'. They are emotional words."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Character","q":"How does Felix's manner differ from Agatha's, according to the creature?","o":["Always cheerful in face and voice","Happier face, sadder voice","Sadder face, more cheerful voice","Always angry with the old man"],"a":2,"e":"'If his countenance was more sorrowful, his voice was more cheerful than that of his sister, especially when he addressed the old man.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Symbol","q":"What gift does Felix bring Agatha 'in the midst of poverty'?","o":["The first white flower of spring","A letter from Safie","A ribbon from the market","A loaf of fine white bread"],"a":0,"e":"'Felix carried with pleasure to his sister the first little white flower that peeped out from beneath the snowy ground.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Plot","q":"Where does Felix work when he brings home no wood?","o":["For a neighbouring farmer","In the village school","At the mill","For the local priest"],"a":0,"e":"'He worked sometimes for a neighbouring farmer, because he often went forth… yet brought no wood.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Theme","q":"Why does the creature delay revealing himself to the cottagers?","o":["To master their language first","Because he is waiting for Victor to come and find him","Because he plans to steal from them first","Because he fears the old man more than the others"],"a":0,"e":"He hopes language 'might enable me to make them overlook the deformity of my figure'."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Close reading","q":"'When I became fully convinced that I was in reality the monster that I am.' Why is this moment so important?","o":["He discovers he is strong","He sees himself as others do","He realises he can speak","He decides to attack the family"],"a":1,"e":"The pool becomes a mirror: he accepts the label 'monster' before any human has spoken it to him."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Connection","q":"The creature sees himself in a pool and recoils. Which earlier moment does this mirror?","o":["Walton's first sight of Victor","Caroline at her father's coffin","Victor's horror at the creation","Elizabeth's beauty as a child"],"a":2,"e":"He reacts to his own face exactly as his creator did."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Plot","q":"What did the cottagers call the invisible helper?","o":["'Good spirit'","'Our angel'","'The giant'","'The dæmon'"],"a":0,"e":"He heard them 'utter the words good spirit, wonderful' without understanding them."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Irony","q":"The creature hopes to restore the family's happiness. What does he add in brackets about himself?","o":["'(miserable devil!)'","'(poor fool!)'","'(innocent child!)'","'(foolish wretch!)'"],"a":3,"e":"'I thought (foolish wretch!) that it might be in my power to restore happiness.' He narrates with hindsight."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Character","q":"How does the creature view the cottagers?","o":["As enemies whose movements must be watched","As a family he must protect from Victor's anger","As judges of his future","As servants who owe him thanks for the firewood"],"a":2,"e":"'I looked upon them as superior beings who would be the arbiters of my future destiny.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Allusion","q":"The creature compares himself to 'the ass and the lap-dog'. What is the fable's point?","o":["Pride always comes before a fall","Rough manners, good intentions","The strong should always rule over the weak","Pets are loved more than honest working animals"],"a":1,"e":"In Aesop, the ass imitates the lap-dog and is beaten. The creature insists the gentle ass 'deserved better treatment'."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Theme","q":"At the end of Chapter 12, how does the creature feel about the future?","o":["Afraid of death","Full of hope","Indifferent","Bitter and resentful"],"a":1,"e":"'The future gilded by bright rays of hope and anticipations of joy.' It is the high point before his hopes collapse."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature says these events 'have made me what I am'. What claim is he making?","o":["Victor's science made him evil","God decided his fate in advance","He was born wicked","Experience formed his character"],"a":3,"e":"The nurture argument, stated outright as he begins the key part of his story."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Plot","q":"What was Felix's mood just before Safie arrived?","o":["Deeply melancholy","Feverish and ill","Angry with his father","Unusually cheerful"],"a":0,"e":"'The countenance of Felix was melancholy beyond expression; he sighed frequently.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Character","q":"How was Safie dressed when she arrived?","o":["A white gown with gold embroidery","Turkish robes and a turban","A dark suit and thick black veil","A riding cloak over peasant clothes"],"a":2,"e":"'The lady was dressed in a dark suit and covered with a thick black veil.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Plot","q":"What was the only word Safie spoke at the door?","o":["Agatha","Father","Help","Felix"],"a":3,"e":"She 'only replied by pronouncing, in a sweet accent, the name of Felix'."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Character","q":"What did Felix call Safie on her arrival?","o":["His dear sister","His sweet Arabian","His lost bride","His little Turk"],"a":1,"e":"He 'called her, as well as I could distinguish, his sweet Arabian'."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Irony","q":"Safie's beauty is described in detail: raven hair, fair complexion, 'angelic'. How does this matter for the creature?","o":["It reminds the creature of Elizabeth's portrait","It shows that Felix cares only for looks","It makes him jealous and wish to harm her","Her face earns a welcome he can't"],"a":3,"e":"She is a stranger who cannot speak their language, just like him, but her face lets her in at once."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Language","q":"Safie's presence dispels their sorrow 'as the sun dissipates…' what?","o":["The evening shadows","The storm clouds","The winter snows","The morning mists"],"a":3,"e":"'Her presence diffused gladness through the cottage, dispelling their sorrow as the sun dissipates the morning mists.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Plot","q":"How many words did Safie learn at her first lesson?","o":["About twenty","Just one","Over a hundred","About five"],"a":0,"e":"'The stranger learned about twenty words at the first lesson.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Character","q":"How did the old man raise Safie when she knelt to kiss his hand?","o":["He embraced her","He wept","He blessed her","He sent her away"],"a":0,"e":"'He raised her and embraced her affectionately.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Plot","q":"What instrument does the old man play, as named in Chapter 13?","o":["A violin","A harp","A lute","A guitar"],"a":3,"e":"'The old man played on his guitar, and the children listened to him.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Language","q":"What does Safie's singing voice sound like to the creature?","o":["A nightingale","A harp","A thrush","A flute"],"a":0,"e":"'Swelling or dying away like a nightingale of the woods.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Plot","q":"Within how long could the creature understand most of the cottagers' words?","o":["Six months","Two months","Two weeks","A year"],"a":1,"e":"'In two months I began to comprehend most of the words uttered by my protectors.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Character","q":"Who learned the language faster, the creature or Safie?","o":["They learned equally","The creature","Neither learned much","Safie"],"a":1,"e":"'I improved more rapidly than the Arabian, who understood very little and conversed in broken accents.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Character","q":"Why does the creature never go out in daylight?","o":["Sunlight hurts his watery yellow eyes","He fears a repeat of the village","The cottagers would notice the missing wood","He sleeps through the day in the hovel"],"a":1,"e":"He fears 'meeting with the same treatment I had formerly endured in the first village'."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Plot","q":"Why did Felix choose Volney's 'Ruins of Empires' to teach Safie?","o":["It was the standard book for teaching French grammar","It was written in Arabic, Safie's own language","Its style copied Eastern authors","It was the only book the family still owned"],"a":2,"e":"'He had chosen this work… because the declamatory style was framed in imitation of the Eastern authors.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Context","q":"Hearing Volney, over whose fate do the creature and Safie weep?","o":["The ancient Greeks","The Christian crusaders","The early Romans","The original peoples of America"],"a":3,"e":"'I heard of the discovery of the American hemisphere and wept with Safie over the hapless fate of its original inhabitants.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Theme","q":"What question about humanity does Volney raise for the creature?","o":["Why does God allow kings to rule over others?","Why do great empires never seem to fall?","Why are women kept from learning and books?","How can man be so noble and so base?"],"a":3,"e":"'Was man, indeed, at once so powerful, so virtuous and magnificent, yet so vicious and base?'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Language","q":"To the creature, being base and vicious seemed lower than being what?","o":["A wolf or a snake","The blind mole or harmless worm","A slave or a beggar","A savage or a criminal"],"a":1,"e":"It was 'a condition more abject than that of the blind mole or harmless worm'."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Theme","q":"What did the creature at first find impossible to understand about men?","o":["Why the cottagers needed so much sleep","How the cottagers had learned to read","How one man could murder another","Why humans grew old and weak with time"],"a":2,"e":"'For a long time I could not conceive how one man could go forth to murder his fellow.' This is chilling, given what he becomes."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Theme","q":"According to what the creature learned, which two things did society value most?","o":["Strength and courage","Beauty and youth","Noble descent and riches","Education and virtue"],"a":2,"e":"'High and unsullied descent united with riches.' He has neither."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Context","q":"Without descent or riches, what was a man considered?","o":["A vagabond and a slave","A soldier or a sailor","A saint or a hermit","A free and equal citizen"],"a":0,"e":"Such a man was 'considered… as a vagabond and a slave, doomed to waste his powers for the profits of the chosen few'."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Close reading","q":"Which advantage does the creature NOT list among his physical differences from humans?","o":["Tolerance of heat and cold","Greater agility","Ability to eat coarser food","Greater intelligence"],"a":3,"e":"He lists agility, diet, heat and cold, and stature, then asks, 'Was I, then, a monster?'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Quote","q":"'Sorrow only increased with knowledge.' What does the creature wish immediately after?","o":["That he could forget the language he'd learned","That Victor had never given him life","That he had never set eyes on the cottagers","To have stayed in his native wood"],"a":3,"e":"'Oh, that I had for ever remained in my native wood, nor known nor felt beyond the sensations of hunger, thirst, and heat!'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Language","q":"Knowledge clings to the mind 'like a lichen on the rock'. What does this simile suggest?","o":["Knowledge is cold and lifeless","Knowledge is a kind of disease","Knowledge can't be removed","Knowledge grows slowly and beautifully"],"a":2,"e":"'It clings to the mind when it has once seized on it.' Knowledge here is irreversible, just as Victor found."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Theme","q":"What did the creature learn was the only way to overcome pain?","o":["Death","Learning","Sleep","Love"],"a":0,"e":"'There was but one means to overcome the sensation of pain, and that was death—a state which I feared yet did not understand.'"},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Structure","q":"The creature repeats 'were not for me' about Agatha, Safie, the old man and Felix. What is the effect?","o":["It shows he has given up on them","It shows he prefers being alone","It stresses his exclusion","It mocks the cottagers' manners"],"a":2,"e":"The repetition makes his exclusion feel total."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Connection","q":"'No father had watched my infant days, no mother had blessed me with smiles.' Which earlier passage does this deliberately answer?","o":["Walton's letter about his Uncle Thomas","Caroline's death scene","Elizabeth's letter about little William","Victor's description of his own happy infancy"],"a":3,"e":"Victor recalled 'my mother's tender caresses and my father's smile'. The creature had neither."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature calls the cottagers his 'protectors' in 'an innocent, half-painful self-deceit'. What does he admit?","o":["They know about him and protect him","The relationship is one-sided","He thinks they are his parents","He is protecting them"],"a":1,"e":"They do not know he exists; the word is his own wishful fiction."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"What was the De Laceys' situation in France before their ruin?","o":["Merchants trading silks with Turkey","Poor peasant farmers in the French provinces","Respected and well-off in Paris","Servants in a great noble house in Lyons"],"a":2,"e":"They lived 'in affluence, respected by his superiors' in Paris."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Context","q":"Why was Safie's father really condemned, according to opinion in Paris?","o":["His religion and wealth","The murder of a soldier","Smuggling goods","Spying for the Turks"],"a":0,"e":"'It was judged that his religion and wealth rather than the crime alleged against him had been the cause of his condemnation.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Connection","q":"The Turk's trial is 'flagrant' injustice that 'all Paris' sees. How does it echo an earlier trial?","o":["Victor's trial in Ireland","Alphonse's court case","The creature's trial on the glacier","Justine's unjust condemnation"],"a":3,"e":"Two unjust trials, two condemned innocents (or near-innocents), and courts swayed by prejudice."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"When was the Turk arrested?","o":["The night before his planned escape to Leghorn","The day Safie came from Constantinople","The day his first wife died in Constantinople","The moment he tried to leave Paris for Italy"],"a":1,"e":"'He was seized and cast into prison the very day that Safie arrived from Constantinople.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"How did Felix contact the imprisoned Turk?","o":["Disguised as a priest","Through a grated window at night","By bribing a guard","Through Safie's letters"],"a":1,"e":"'He found a strongly grated window in an unguarded part of the building.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"How did Felix respond to the Turk's offers of reward?","o":["Rejected them with contempt","Asked for land in Turkey","Accepted them gladly","Gave the money to the poor"],"a":0,"e":"'Felix rejected his offers with contempt', though Safie herself seemed 'a treasure'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"Who helped Safie write to Felix in French?","o":["An old servant of her father","Agatha","A French governess","A Paris priest"],"a":0,"e":"With 'the aid of an old man, a servant of her father who understood French'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Structure","q":"What does the creature say he will give Victor as proof of his story?","o":["Copies of Safie's letters","Victor's own lab journal","Felix's diary","The miniature of Caroline"],"a":0,"e":"'I have copies of these letters… they will prove the truth of my tale.' At the end Victor shows Walton these letters."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"What was Safie's mother?","o":["A Greek merchant's daughter from Smyrna","A French noblewoman who had married for love","A Christian Arab enslaved by Turks","A Turkish princess from the sultan's court"],"a":2,"e":"'Her mother was a Christian Arab, seized and made a slave by the Turks.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Theme","q":"What did Safie's mother teach her to aspire to?","o":["Wealth and rank through a good marriage","Revenge on the Turks who enslaved her","Intellect and independence","Beauty, obedience and religious devotion"],"a":2,"e":"She taught 'higher powers of intellect and an independence of spirit'. The novel's most explicit feminist note comes from Mary Wollstonecraft's daughter."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Close reading","q":"What did Safie dread about returning to Asia?","o":["A harem and 'infantile amusements'","The long and dangerous sea voyage home","Her father's anger at her disobedience","Forgetting the French she had learned"],"a":0,"e":"She sickened at being 'immured within the walls of a harem, allowed only to occupy herself with infantile amusements'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"How did De Lacey help the escape?","o":["Giving false evidence at the trial","Faking a journey and hiding","Bribing the judges with his own fortune","Sheltering the Turk in his own house"],"a":1,"e":"He quit his house 'under the pretence of a journey and concealed himself, with his daughter, in an obscure part of Paris'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Sequence","q":"Which was the route of the escape?","o":["Paris, Marseilles, Rome, Naples","Paris, Strasbourg, Ingolstadt, Vienna","Paris, Lyons, Mont Cenis, Leghorn","Paris, Geneva, Milan, Venice"],"a":2,"e":"Felix 'conducted the fugitives through France to Lyons and across Mont Cenis to Leghorn'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"Why did the Turk keep pretending he would let Safie marry Felix?","o":["He liked Felix","Felix could still betray him","He owed Felix money","Safie threatened to run away"],"a":1,"e":"'He knew that he was still in the power of his deliverer if he should choose to betray him to the Italian state.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"What did Felix do when he heard his father and sister were imprisoned?","o":["Sent the Turk to free them","Fled to Germany with Safie","Wrote to the king","Gave himself up in Paris"],"a":3,"e":"He 'delivered himself up to the vengeance of the law, hoping to free De Lacey and Agatha'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"Where was Safie to stay if her father escaped before Felix returned?","o":["With a merchant family in Rome","With the old servant","On a ship in the harbour","In a convent at Leghorn"],"a":3,"e":"'Safie should remain as a boarder at a convent at Leghorn.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"How long were the De Laceys imprisoned before trial?","o":["Five months","Five years","Five weeks","One night"],"a":0,"e":"'They remained confined for five months before the trial took place.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"What was the sentence on the De Laceys?","o":["Imprisonment for life in the Bastille","Death by hanging in the public square","Lost fortune and perpetual exile","A heavy fine and loss of their titles"],"a":2,"e":"It 'deprived them of their fortune and condemned them to a perpetual exile from their native country'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Setting","q":"In which country is the De Laceys' cottage?","o":["France","Switzerland","Germany","Italy"],"a":2,"e":"'They found a miserable asylum in the cottage in Germany.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Irony","q":"What did the 'treacherous Turk' send Felix after his ruin?","o":["A letter of thanks","Safie's hand in marriage","A pittance of money","A threat of death"],"a":2,"e":"He quit Italy with Safie, 'insultingly sending Felix a pittance of money'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"Which misfortune did Felix find hardest to bear?","o":["The Turk's ingratitude, losing Safie","Losing his rank and place in Parisian society","Poverty, hunger and exile from France","His father's blindness and failing health"],"a":0,"e":"'He could have endured poverty… but the ingratitude of the Turk and the loss of his beloved Safie were misfortunes more bitter.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Connection","q":"The Turk repays Felix with ingratitude. Which theme does this share with the rest of the novel?","o":["Love conquering all","Nature healing grief","Benefactors betrayed","Science against religion"],"a":2,"e":"Felix saves the Turk and is betrayed; the creature saves the cottagers' winter and the drowning girl and is attacked."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"Why did the Turk suddenly leave Leghorn?","o":["He feared being handed to France","His ship was ready early","Safie fell ill","Felix had come back"],"a":0,"e":"He believed 'his residence at Leghorn had been divulged and that he should speedily be delivered up to the French government'."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"How did Safie learn where Felix was?","o":["From the convent nuns","From her father's papers","From a letter Felix sent her","From her attendant"],"a":1,"e":"'By some papers of her father which fell into her hands she heard of the exile of her lover.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"What did Safie take when she left for Germany?","o":["Only the clothes she wore","Her father's whole fortune","Her mother's Bible","Jewels and some money"],"a":3,"e":"'Taking with her some jewels that belonged to her and a sum of money, she quitted Italy.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"What happened to Safie's attendant on the journey?","o":["She married a German innkeeper","She betrayed Safie to the Turk","She ran off with the money","She fell ill and died"],"a":3,"e":"'Her attendant fell dangerously ill. Safie nursed her with the most devoted affection, but the poor girl died.'"},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Connection","q":"Safie nurses her dying attendant 'with the most devoted affection'. Whom does this recall?","o":["Felix nursing De Lacey","Agatha nursing Safie","Caroline nursing Elizabeth","Victor nursing Clerval"],"a":2,"e":"Women in the novel nurse at their own risk: Caroline, Justine and Safie."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Theme","q":"Who saw to it that Safie reached the cottage safely?","o":["The woman of the house","A merchant from Leghorn who knew Felix","A German soldier on his way home","A priest she met on the road"],"a":0,"e":"'The woman of the house in which they had lived took care that Safie should arrive in safety.' A stranger shows the kindness the creature never receives."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"In which month does the creature find the leather portmanteau?","o":["June","November","March","August"],"a":3,"e":"'A circumstance which occurred in the beginning of the month of August of the same year.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"What was in the portmanteau besides books?","o":["A purse of gold coins and a letter of credit","Letters, a map and a traveller's journal","A pistol, powder and a hunting knife","Some clothes"],"a":3,"e":"He found 'a leathern portmanteau containing several articles of dress and some books'."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Character","q":"Which book taught the creature 'despondency and gloom'?","o":["Volney's Ruins of Empires","Paradise Lost","The Sorrows of Werter","Plutarch's Lives"],"a":2,"e":"'I learned from Werter's imaginations despondency and gloom, but Plutarch taught me high thoughts.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Character","q":"What subject in Werter fills the creature with wonder?","o":["Science and invention","Love and marriage","Death and suicide","War and conquest"],"a":2,"e":"'The disquisitions upon death and suicide were calculated to fill me with wonder.' Werter ends by killing himself."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature quotes 'The path of my departure was free'. Where is this line from?","o":["Coleridge's Ancient Mariner","Plutarch's Lives","Paradise Lost","Percy Shelley's 'Mutability'"],"a":3,"e":"Victor quoted the same poem on Montanvert. Creator and creature share the same lines."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Theme","q":"Which question does the creature NOT ask himself after reading?","o":["What was my destination?","How can I punish my creator?","Who was I?","Whence did I come?"],"a":1,"e":"'Who was I? What was I? Whence did I come? What was my destination?' Revenge comes only later."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Character","q":"Which lawgivers did Plutarch teach the creature to admire?","o":["Numa, Solon, Lycurgus","Romulus and Theseus, the warlike founders","Moses and Solomon, the lawgivers of Israel","Caesar and Alexander, the great conquerors"],"a":0,"e":"He preferred 'peaceable lawgivers, Numa, Solon, and Lycurgus, in preference to Romulus and Theseus'."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Theme","q":"What does the creature say if his first teacher had been a young soldier?","o":["He'd have been killed","He'd have learned faster","He'd have felt differently","He'd have become a lawgiver"],"a":2,"e":"'Perhaps, if my first introduction to humanity had been made by a young soldier, burning for glory and slaughter, I should have been imbued with different sensations.' This is nurture again."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Close reading","q":"How did the creature read Paradise Lost?","o":["As a poem to memorise","As a true history","As a book of magic","As a book about Victor"],"a":1,"e":"'I read it… as a true history.' He takes Milton's story of God and his creatures literally."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Connection","q":"How is the creature like Adam, and how not?","o":["Equally alone, but abandoned","Equally happy, but uglier","Equally created, but from clay","Equally loved, but stronger"],"a":0,"e":"Like Adam he is linked to no other being, but Adam was 'guarded by the especial care of his Creator'."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Character","q":"Why does the creature often find Satan a 'fitter emblem' than Adam?","o":["He also has wings","He also lives in fire","He envies others' bliss","He also rebelled against God"],"a":2,"e":"'Often, like him, when I viewed the bliss of my protectors, the bitter gall of envy rose within me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"What papers did the creature find in the coat taken from the laboratory?","o":["Letters from Elizabeth to Victor at Ingolstadt","Agrippa's secret formulas copied out by hand","Waldman's lecture notes on modern chemistry","Victor's lab journal"],"a":3,"e":"'It was your journal of the four months that preceded my creation.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Close reading","q":"What effect did Victor's journal have on the creature?","o":["It made him pity Victor","It made his loathsomeness indelible","It taught him chemistry","It told him where Victor lived, and nothing else"],"a":1,"e":"Its language 'painted your own horrors and rendered mine indelible'. His creator's disgust becomes his self-image."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Quote","q":"'God, in pity, made man beautiful… but my form is a filthy type of yours.' What makes it 'more horrid'?","o":["Its yellow colour","Its enormous size","The very resemblance","Its strength"],"a":2,"e":"'More horrid even from the very resemblance.' He is a distorted copy of a human being."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Connection","q":"'Satan had his companions, fellow devils… but I am solitary.' What does this lead the creature to demand later?","o":["Forgiveness from God","Victor's death","A home in Geneva","A companion like himself"],"a":3,"e":"His loneliness, sharper than even Satan's, drives the request for a mate."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"Why does the creature postpone meeting the cottagers for some months?","o":["Felix was away working for the farmer for months","Winter made the approach to the cottage impossible","Safie fell ill and the family stayed indoors","Fear of failing; to grow wiser"],"a":3,"e":"He feared failure and wanted 'a few more months' to add to his 'sagacity'."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"What sign of comfort appears in the cottage after Safie's arrival?","o":["A second cow","A new roof","Servants to help them","Letters from Paris"],"a":2,"e":"Felix and Agatha 'were assisted in their labours by servants'."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Theme","q":"'No Eve soothed my sorrows.' Which wish does this anticipate?","o":["Reunion with the cottagers","Death","A return to the forest","A female companion"],"a":3,"e":"He dreams of 'amiable and lovely creatures sympathising with my feelings'."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature says he 'cursed' his creator. What triggers this?","o":["Being shot after saving the drowning girl","Recalling Adam's plea","Reading Werter's thoughts on suicide","Felix's violent rejection at the cottage door"],"a":1,"e":"'I remembered Adam's supplication to his Creator. But where was mine? He had abandoned me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Character","q":"Which season does the creature bear more easily?","o":["Heat","Cold","None of them","Spring rain"],"a":1,"e":"'I was better fitted by my conformation for the endurance of cold than heat.' This explains his flight to the Arctic."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Close reading","q":"'The poor that stopped at their door were never driven away.' Why does the creature mention this?","o":["To explain the servants","It feeds his hope of welcome","To show they were rich","To show they were foolish"],"a":1,"e":"He asks for 'greater treasures than a little food': kindness and sympathy."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"How long after his creation does the creature approach De Lacey?","o":["Six weeks","Two months","A full year of seasons","Two years"],"a":2,"e":"'An entire revolution of the seasons had taken place since I awoke into life.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Theme","q":"Why does the creature think De Lacey's blindness gives him a chance?","o":["The old man is kinder than his children are","A blind man cannot run away from him","His voice isn't terrible","Blind people are never frightened by strangers"],"a":2,"e":"'My voice, although harsh, had nothing terrible in it.' Only sight condemns him."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"Where were the servants during the creature's visit?","o":["In the garden","Gone for good","At church","At a neighbouring fair"],"a":3,"e":"'The servants were gone to a neighbouring fair.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"How does the creature introduce himself to De Lacey?","o":["A beggar asking for bread and a coin","A lost soldier returning from the wars","A friend of Felix from their days in Paris","A traveller wanting rest"],"a":3,"e":"'I am a traveller in want of a little rest.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"What nationality does De Lacey guess from the creature's speech?","o":["German","French","Turkish","Swiss"],"a":1,"e":"'By your language, stranger, I suppose you are my countryman; are you French?'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Quote","q":"'The hearts of men, when unprejudiced by any obvious self-interest, are full of brotherly love.' Why is this tragic?","o":["His children prove it false","Felix overhears it","It is said by Victor","The creature disagrees aloud"],"a":0,"e":"De Lacey's faith is refuted within minutes by Felix's stick."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature says the friends see 'a detestable monster' where they 'ought to see a feeling and kind friend'. What does he call this?","o":["A just judgement","God's will","A fatal prejudice","His own fault"],"a":2,"e":"'A fatal prejudice clouds their eyes.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Character","q":"What does De Lacey say about being 'really criminal'?","o":["It drives them to desperation","Criminals should always be punished harshly","God will forgive every criminal who repents","He would never help a criminal in any way"],"a":0,"e":"'For that can only drive you to desperation, and not instigate you to virtue.' He saw this in his own unjust exile."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"What makes the creature finally cry 'Now is the time!'?","o":["The steps of the returning family","The sound of the guitar","De Lacey reaching for his face","De Lacey's question about his name"],"a":0,"e":"He heard 'the steps of my younger protectors' and seized the old man's hand."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Character","q":"What does Safie do on seeing the creature?","o":["Hides behind Felix","Rushes out of the cottage","Screams for the servants","Faints beside Agatha"],"a":1,"e":"'Agatha fainted, and Safie, unable to attend to her friend, rushed out of the cottage.'"},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Language","q":"Why didn't the creature fight back against Felix?","o":["He was already too injured from the stones","Felix was far too strong for him to resist","His heart sank","De Lacey stepped between them and stopped him"],"a":2,"e":"'I could have torn him limb from limb, as the lion rends the antelope. But my heart sank within me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Quote","q":"'Cursed, cursed creator! Why did I live?' What does the creature say he felt that night instead of despair?","o":["Shame and sorrow","Nothing at all","Fear and confusion","Rage and revenge"],"a":3,"e":"'Despair had not yet taken possession of me; my feelings were those of rage and revenge.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Allusion","q":"'I, like the arch-fiend, bore a hell within me.' Who is the arch-fiend?","o":["Satan","Felix","The Turk","Victor"],"a":0,"e":"It echoes Milton's Satan, 'myself am Hell'. Victor also says 'I bore a hell within me' in Ch. 8."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Connection","q":"Both Victor (Ch. 8) and the creature (Ch. 16) say they 'bore a hell within'. What does the echo suggest?","o":["They are doubles","Both are literally devils","Both read Milton together","Both will go to hell"],"a":0,"e":"The shared phrase ties creator and creature as mirror images."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What does the creature declare after his night of rage in the wood?","o":["That he will leave Europe and go to the Arctic","War on the whole species","That he will kill De Lacey for not protecting him","That he will end his own life that very night"],"a":1,"e":"'From that moment I declared everlasting war against the species.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Character","q":"The next day, what does the creature decide was his mistake at the cottage?","o":["Showing himself too soon","Speaking French","Choosing the old man","Going in daylight"],"a":0,"e":"'I ought to have familiarised the old De Lacey to me, and by degrees to have discovered myself.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Language","q":"Why can't the creature follow the countrymen talking outside the empty cottage?","o":["They whisper so he cannot make out words","They talk too fast for him to follow","The wind drowns out their voices","It is German, not French"],"a":3,"e":"They 'spoke the language of the country, which differed from that of my protectors'. They were German, not French."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What will Felix lose by leaving, according to the landlord?","o":["Nothing, since the landlord will forgive it","Three months' rent, the garden","The deposit they paid and Felix's tools","All their furniture and the family's cow"],"a":1,"e":"'You will be obliged to pay three months' rent and to lose the produce of your garden.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Character","q":"Whom does Felix call 'my wife' as they leave?","o":["Agatha","Safie","Justine","No one"],"a":1,"e":"'My wife and my sister will never recover from their horror.' So Felix and Safie had married."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Quote","q":"'I never saw any of the family of De Lacey more.' What immediately fills the creature?","o":["Fear that the villagers would hunt him down","Hope that he might still find the family","Revenge and hatred","Sorrow and a quiet resignation to his fate"],"a":2,"e":"'For the first time the feelings of revenge and hatred filled my bosom.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Close reading","q":"Why does the creature attack the cottage and garden, not people?","o":["It happened by accident when he lit a torch","He can't hurt anything human","To warn the family never to return there","To keep himself warm through the cold night"],"a":1,"e":"'Unable to injure anything human, I turned my fury towards inanimate objects.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What signal does the creature wait for before firing the cottage?","o":["Dawn","A gust of wind","The moon sinking","The church bell"],"a":2,"e":"He waited 'until the moon had sunk'. When part of the orb was hidden, he 'waved my brand'."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Symbol","q":"The fire's flames 'clung to it and licked it with their forked and destroying tongues'. What is the irony?","o":["Felix started it","His first comfort now destroys","It burns him too","The fire was accidental"],"a":1,"e":"The creature's first discovery, Promethean fire, becomes a weapon."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Close reading","q":"'And now, with the world before me, whither should I bend my steps?' What does this echo?","o":["Walton setting sail","Satan's fall into hell","Victor leaving Geneva","Adam and Eve leaving Eden"],"a":3,"e":"It echoes the end of Paradise Lost: 'The world was all before them'. He is expelled from his Eden."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"How does the creature know to go to Geneva?","o":["Victor's papers named it","Felix told him","A traveller told him","He followed Victor's scent"],"a":0,"e":"'You had mentioned Geneva as the name of your native town.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What guided the creature on his journey besides the sun?","o":["The river Rhine","The stars only","A map of the country","A guide he paid"],"a":2,"e":"'I possessed a map of the country; but I often wandered wide from my path.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Close reading","q":"What does the creature feel toward Victor even as he goes to him for help?","o":["Love and hope","Nothing but hatred","Pity and sorrow","Fear and shame"],"a":1,"e":"'From you only could I hope for succour, although towards you I felt no sentiment but that of hatred.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"In what season does the creature reach the borders of Switzerland?","o":["Midwinter","High summer","Early spring","Late autumn"],"a":2,"e":"'When the sun had recovered its warmth and the earth again began to look green.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Setting","q":"What tree does the creature hide under before the girl falls into the river?","o":["A cypress","A willow","An oak","A pine"],"a":0,"e":"'I heard the sound of voices, that induced me to conceal myself under the shade of a cypress.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"Why was the girl running by the river?","o":["In play, from someone","To fetch water","From the creature","From a wild dog"],"a":0,"e":"She 'came running… laughing, as if she ran from someone in sport'."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"Where did the bullet hit the creature?","o":["His chest","His hand","His shoulder","His leg"],"a":2,"e":"'The ball had entered my shoulder.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Theme","q":"Which feeling made the gunshot wound worse?","o":["Shame at being seen","Fear of dying alone","Hunger and cold","The injustice and ingratitude"],"a":3,"e":"'My sufferings were augmented also by the oppressive sense of the injustice and ingratitude of their infliction.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"How long after his wound healed did the creature reach Geneva?","o":["Six months","Two weeks","A year","Two months"],"a":3,"e":"'In two months from this time I reached the environs of Geneva.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Character","q":"Why did the creature first want to seize William?","o":["To hold him for ransom from the syndic","To kill him at once out of hatred","To ask him the way to Victor's house","To raise him as a friend"],"a":3,"e":"'This little creature was unprejudiced… If, therefore, I could seize him and educate him as my companion.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Close reading","q":"William calls the creature an 'ogre' who wants to 'eat me'. What does this show?","o":["Even a child has learned the prejudice","William has seen him before","William is very brave","The creature looks hungry"],"a":0,"e":"The child is not 'unprejudiced'; he has already absorbed fairy-tale horror."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What makes the creature decide to kill William?","o":["The portrait","William's screaming","Learning he is a Frankenstein","William striking him"],"a":2,"e":"'Frankenstein! you belong then to my enemy… you shall be my first victim.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Quote","q":"After the murder the creature cries 'I too can create…' what?","o":["Fear","A family","Desolation","Life"],"a":2,"e":"'I too can create desolation.' He mirrors his creator's power to make."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Close reading","q":"How does the portrait of Caroline first affect the creature?","o":["It softens and attracts him","It frightens him","It enrages him at once","He does not notice it"],"a":0,"e":"'In spite of my malignity, it softened and attracted me.' Then rage returns as he imagines her disgust."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"Where does the creature find Justine?","o":["In the Frankenstein garden","On the road to Chêne","Asleep on straw in a barn","In a church"],"a":2,"e":"'A woman was sleeping on some straw.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Theme","q":"Why does the creature plant the portrait on Justine?","o":["It slipped from his hand by accident","She must atone for his loss","To hide the evidence from the police","To return the portrait to William's family"],"a":1,"e":"'The murder I have committed because I am for ever robbed of all that she could give me, she shall atone.'"},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Irony","q":"Where does the creature say he learned to 'work mischief'?","o":["From watching the cruel villagers","From reading Victor's laboratory journal","From Satan's schemes in Paradise Lost","Felix's lessons, man's laws"],"a":3,"e":"'Thanks to the lessons of Felix and the sanguinary laws of man, I had learned now to work mischief.' Human justice gives him the method."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Structure","q":"The creature ends his tale with a demand. What is it?","o":["Revenge on Felix","A companion of his own species","A place in Victor's family","Victor's death"],"a":1,"e":"'My companion must be of the same species and have the same defects. This being you must create.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Language","q":"The creature demands a female 'as a right'. What does that word suggest?","o":["He is threatening a lawsuit in Geneva","He thinks Victor owes him","He is quoting human law exactly","He is begging for a favour"],"a":1,"e":"'I demand it of you as a right which you must not refuse to concede.' A creator has duties."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Character","q":"What reason does Victor first give for refusing?","o":["Elizabeth would never forgive him for it","He has forgotten the method entirely","The materials are impossible to obtain","Their joint wickedness"],"a":3,"e":"'Shall I create another like yourself, whose joint wickedness might desolate the world.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Quote","q":"'I am malicious because I am…' what?","o":["Hungry and cold in the ice caves","Made from the bodies of criminals","Miserable","Hated by every person I have met"],"a":2,"e":"'I am malicious because I am miserable.' It is the creature's thesis in five words."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Close reading","q":"'You would not call it murder if you could… destroy my frame.' What double standard is exposed?","o":["Victor kills animals for science","Humans murder each other in war","His killing wouldn't count","Victor murdered Justine in court"],"a":2,"e":"Victor treats killing the creature as no crime while calling the creature a murderer."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Theme","q":"What does the creature say are 'insurmountable barriers' to union with humans?","o":["Language and religion","Law and property","The human senses","Size and strength"],"a":2,"e":"'The human senses are insurmountable barriers to our union.' Humans can't get past what they see."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Quote","q":"'If I cannot inspire love, I will cause…' what?","o":["Fear","Death","Ruin","Pity"],"a":0,"e":"'If I cannot inspire love, I will cause fear.' This becomes the creature's new purpose."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"'I will work at your destruction, nor finish until I desolate your heart.' How does this come true?","o":["He kills those Victor loves","He turns Elizabeth against Victor","He burns down the Frankenstein house","He kills Victor on the glacier"],"a":0,"e":"He goes after Victor's heart, not his body: Clerval, Elizabeth and, indirectly, Alphonse."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature calms himself and says 'you are the cause of its excess'. What does he blame?","o":["Victor's rejection","His own nature","The cottagers' cruelty","Paradise Lost"],"a":0,"e":"He says his rage is out of proportion because Victor provokes it."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Theme","q":"For one creature's kindness, what does the creature say he would do?","o":["Leave Europe for ever","Make peace with mankind","Become Victor's servant","Destroy himself willingly"],"a":1,"e":"'For that one creature's sake I would make peace with the whole kind!'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Character","q":"What does the creature ask the female be like?","o":["Beautiful, like Safie","Gentle, like Agatha","Clever, like Elizabeth","As hideous as himself"],"a":3,"e":"'I demand a creature of another sex, but as hideous as myself.' He is asking only for an equal."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Setting","q":"In Chapter 17, which continent does the creature name as his future home?","o":["South America","North America, beyond the Great Lakes","Africa, south of the great desert","Asia, beyond the Russian steppes"],"a":0,"e":"'I will go to the vast wilds of South America.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Character","q":"What does the creature say he eats?","o":["Fish and roots","Bread and cheese","Lamb and kid","Acorns and berries"],"a":3,"e":"'I do not destroy the lamb and the kid to glut my appetite; acorns and berries afford me sufficient nourishment.' He is vegetarian, a mark of innocence."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Close reading","q":"'Our lives will not be happy, but they will be harmless.' What is modest about this request?","o":["Victor's love as a father to a son","A home in Geneva near his creator","Wealth and land of his own to farm","Only less misery"],"a":3,"e":"He doesn't ask for joy, only freedom from 'the misery I now feel'."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor predict would happen in exile?","o":["He'd forget Victor","He'd return with an ally","He'd starve in the wild","He'd kill his mate"],"a":1,"e":"'You will meet with their detestation… and you will then have a companion to aid you in the task of destruction.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Quote","q":"'How inconstant are your feelings!' Why does the creature say this?","o":["Victor tried to flee","Victor laughed at him","Victor changed the subject","Victor was moved, then hardened"],"a":3,"e":"'But a moment ago you were moved by my representations, and why do you again harden yourself?'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Close reading","q":"What turns Victor's compassion back to horror?","o":["The harsh sound of the creature's voice","Thinking of William's body in the grass","Looking at the creature","The creature's threats against his family"],"a":2,"e":"'When I saw the filthy mass that moved and talked, my heart sickened.' Like everyone else, sight defeats him."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Quote","q":"'My vices are the children of a forced solitude.' What does the creature claim his virtues will come from?","o":["Living with an equal","Victor's forgiveness","Prayer and repentance","Reading good books"],"a":0,"e":"'My virtues will necessarily arise when I live in communion with an equal.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Character","q":"Which consideration does NOT enter Victor's final decision?","o":["His love for the creature","The creature's early goodness","The scorn of its protectors","Its power to evade pursuit"],"a":0,"e":"He weighs early virtue, the blight caused by scorn, and his power and threats. There is no love."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Plot","q":"On what condition does Victor consent?","o":["Returning William's body","Quitting Europe for ever","Confessing to the court","Never killing again"],"a":1,"e":"'On your solemn oath to quit Europe for ever, and every other place in the neighbourhood of man.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Quote","q":"By what does the creature NOT swear?","o":["By the fire of love","By the blue sky","By the sun","By God in heaven"],"a":3,"e":"'I swear… by the sun, and by the blue sky of heaven, and by the fire of love that burns my heart.' Earlier he swore 'by you that made me'."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Close reading","q":"How fast does the creature leave Montanvert?","o":["Faster than an eagle","Crawling over the ice","At a steady walk","Slowly, limping"],"a":0,"e":"'With greater speed than the flight of an eagle.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Plot","q":"How long did the creature's tale take?","o":["All night","The whole day","Two days","About an hour"],"a":1,"e":"'His tale had occupied the whole day, and the sun was upon the verge of the horizon.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Language","q":"'Oh! stars and clouds and winds, ye are all about to mock me.' What is this outburst?","o":["A message to Elizabeth","A prayer to God","A curse on the creature","An apostrophe to nature"],"a":3,"e":"Victor addresses nature directly, a Romantic apostrophe, begging it to 'crush sensation and memory'."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Language","q":"Victor hears every gust of wind 'as if it were a dull ugly…' what?","o":["Avalanche","Siroc","Funeral bell","Thunderclap"],"a":1,"e":"'As if it were a dull ugly siroc on its way to consume me.' A sirocco is a hot desert wind."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Close reading","q":"At home Victor feels 'as if I were placed under a ban'. What does he mean?","o":["Arrested by the syndics","Banned from university","Cut off from his family","Forbidden to leave Geneva"],"a":2,"e":"He feels he has 'no right to claim their sympathies'. His secret isolates him."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"Why does Victor delay starting the female?","o":["He is too ill to work","Repugnance and needed study","Elizabeth watches him closely","He has lost his notes"],"a":1,"e":"He feared the fiend 'yet I was unable to overcome my repugnance', and he needed 'several months to profound study'."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Context","q":"Why does Victor think he needs to go to England?","o":["His father has business affairs in London","English graveyards are easier to rob","Clerval is already studying at Oxford","An English philosopher's work"],"a":3,"e":"'I had heard of some discoveries having been made by an English philosopher, the knowledge of which was material to my success.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Character","q":"How does Victor's health change back in Geneva?","o":["It is much restored","He catches a fever in the boat","It stays exactly as poor as before","It collapses completely"],"a":0,"e":"'My health, which had hitherto declined, was now much restored.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Character","q":"What does Alphonse suspect is the cause of Victor's unhappiness?","o":["He owes money in Ingolstadt","He doesn't want Elizabeth","He blames himself for Justine","He has lost his religious faith"],"a":1,"e":"'You, perhaps, regard her as your sister… you may have met with another whom you may love.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Irony","q":"Alphonse says 'so blind is the experience of man'. Why is this ironic?","o":["He secretly knows about the creature","He's wrong about the cause","He is himself slowly going blind with age","He is quoting De Lacey's very words"],"a":1,"e":"He guesses a love problem; the truth is a promise to build a monster's mate."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Character","q":"Why does the idea of an immediate wedding horrify Victor?","o":["He loves another woman","He thinks Elizabeth is ill","His promise is unfulfilled","He has no money"],"a":2,"e":"'I was bound by a solemn promise which I had not yet fulfilled and dared not break.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Language","q":"Victor calls his promise a 'deadly weight… hanging round my neck'. What does this image recall?","o":["Prometheus's chains","A noose at a hanging","The Ancient Mariner's albatross","Atlas holding the sky"],"a":2,"e":"Coleridge's Mariner wears the albatross around his neck as a sign of guilt. Victor has quoted the poem before."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Theme","q":"Why won't Victor do the work in his father's house?","o":["Ernest would interfere","Fear of being discovered","No laboratory space there","His father forbade it"],"a":1,"e":"'A thousand fearful accidents might occur, the slightest of which would disclose a tale to thrill all connected with me with horror.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Close reading","q":"What does Victor secretly hope might happen during his absence?","o":["His father might forget about the wedding","The creature might die","Clerval might discover the secret and help","Elizabeth might fall in love with someone else"],"a":1,"e":"'Or (so my fond fancy imaged) some accident might meanwhile occur to destroy him.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"How long was the trip to England expected to last?","o":["Exactly six weeks","A few months, a year at most","Two or three years","Until Elizabeth's birthday"],"a":1,"e":"'A few months, or at most a year, was the period contemplated.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"Who arranges for Clerval to join Victor?","o":["Clerval's father","Clerval himself","Alphonse and Elizabeth","Victor"],"a":2,"e":"'He had, in concert with Elizabeth, arranged that Clerval should join me at Strasburgh.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Irony","q":"Victor hopes Henry 'might stand between me and the intrusion of my foe'. Why is this hope bitter?","o":["Henry refuses to travel","Henry betrays Victor","Henry sees the creature","Henry becomes a victim"],"a":3,"e":"Clerval is not a shield but a target. He is murdered in Ireland."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"Victor assumes the creature will follow him and leave his family safe. Why is this reasoning dangerous?","o":["The creature cannot cross water","The creature has promised not to","He assumes only he's at risk","Elizabeth is coming with him"],"a":2,"e":"He misreads the threat as aimed at himself, which he repeats on the wedding night."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"When does Victor leave Geneva for England?","o":["Early spring","August","Late September","Midwinter"],"a":2,"e":"'It was in the latter end of September that I again quitted my native country.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Character","q":"How does Elizabeth say goodbye?","o":["A tearful, silent farewell","A cheerful wave from the window","A letter given at the door","A long speech of advice"],"a":0,"e":"'A thousand conflicting emotions rendered her mute as she bade me a tearful, silent farewell.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"What is the one thing Victor remembers to order before leaving?","o":["Pack his instruments","Ask for his father's blessing on the trip","Buy a pistol for his own protection","Write letters to the English philosophers"],"a":0,"e":"'To order that my chemical instruments should be packed to go with me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"How long does Victor wait for Clerval in Strasbourg?","o":["One night","Two weeks","A month","Two days"],"a":3,"e":"'I arrived at Strasburgh, where I waited two days for Clerval.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Quote","q":"'This is what it is to live; now I enjoy existence!' Who says it?","o":["Victor","Elizabeth","Clerval","Walton"],"a":2,"e":"Henry's joy is set against Victor's gloom in the same boat."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Sequence","q":"What was the route from Strasbourg to London?","o":["Overland through France to Calais","Rhine to Rotterdam, then sea","Down the Danube and round by the Black Sea","By coach through Belgium to Ostend"],"a":1,"e":"'Descend the Rhine in a boat from Strasburgh to Rotterdam, whence we might take shipping for London.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Setting","q":"Below which city does the Rhine become 'much more picturesque'?","o":["Rotterdam","Mannheim","Cologne","Mainz"],"a":3,"e":"'The course of the Rhine below Mainz becomes much more picturesque.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Setting","q":"What season were they travelling in on the Rhine?","o":["The grape harvest","The first snows","The spring floods","Midsummer"],"a":0,"e":"'We travelled at the time of the vintage and heard the song of the labourers.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Character","q":"Where did Clerval feel he'd been transported to?","o":["Ancient Rome","Paradise","Fairy-land","The East"],"a":2,"e":"'He felt as if he had been transported to Fairy-land.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Setting","q":"Which Swiss lakes does Clerval say he has seen?","o":["Lucerne and Uri","Geneva and Neuchâtel","Como and Maggiore","Constance and Zurich"],"a":0,"e":"'I have visited the lakes of Lucerne and Uri, where the snowy mountains descend almost perpendicularly.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Theme","q":"Why does Clerval prefer the Rhine to the Alps?","o":["It is bigger and wilder","It has better roads","Its spirit suits man better","It reminds him of Geneva"],"a":2,"e":"Its spirit 'has a soul more in harmony with man than those who pile the glacier'. He prefers the beautiful to the sublime."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Allusion","q":"Victor describes Henry with lines from which poem?","o":["Milton's 'Paradise Lost'","Coleridge's 'Ancient Mariner'","Wordsworth's 'Tintern Abbey'","Shelley's 'Mutability'"],"a":2,"e":"'The sounding cataract / Haunted him like a passion… [Wordsworth's \"Tintern Abbey\".]'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor call Clerval?","o":["'The glory of mankind'","'My guardian angel'","'A second Adam'","'The very poetry of nature'"],"a":3,"e":"'He was a being formed in the \"very poetry of nature.\"' The phrase comes from Leigh Hunt."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Structure","q":"'And where does he now exist?' What does this outburst reveal to the reader?","o":["Henry is with Walton","Henry abandoned Victor","Henry is still in England","Henry is already dead"],"a":3,"e":"Victor's retrospective narration lets slip Henry's fate before we reach it."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Irony","q":"Victor says Clerval's mind 'formed a world, whose existence depended on the life of its creator'. Why is this pointed?","o":["It echoes Victor's creation","Clerval was secretly writing a novel","It is a reference to God creating Eden","Clerval had helped to make the creature"],"a":0,"e":"The image of a creator and the world that depends on him recalls Victor's own failed creation."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"Why do they travel overland after Cologne?","o":["The river was frozen","Their boat sank","Clerval was seasick","The wind was contrary"],"a":3,"e":"'The wind was contrary and the stream of the river was too gentle to aid us.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"When do they first see the white cliffs of Britain?","o":["Early March","Late September","Late December","Midsummer"],"a":2,"e":"'It was on a clear morning, in the latter days of December, that I first saw the white cliffs.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Context","q":"Tilbury Fort reminds them of which event?","o":["The Spanish Armada","The Great Fire of London","The Battle of Hastings","The execution of Charles I"],"a":0,"e":"'We saw Tilbury Fort and remembered the Spanish Armada.'"},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Setting","q":"Which building towers above London as they arrive?","o":["St Paul's","The Tower","Westminster Abbey","London Bridge"],"a":0,"e":"'The numerous steeples of London, St. Paul's towering above all.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Plot","q":"What did Victor bring to London to reach the scientists?","o":["A fortune in Swiss gold","His university diploma","Letters of introduction","A letter from Waldman"],"a":2,"e":"He 'availed myself of the letters of introduction… addressed to the most distinguished natural philosophers'."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor say the barrier between him and other people was 'sealed with'?","o":["Elizabeth's tears","His own oath to the creature","The secret of creation","William and Justine's blood"],"a":3,"e":"'This barrier was sealed with the blood of William and Justine.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Connection","q":"'In Clerval I saw the image of my former self.' What is the effect of this?","o":["Henry has also made a creature","Henry is also secretly a scientist","Henry shows what Victor lost","Henry looks physically like Victor"],"a":2,"e":"Henry, curious and eager, is what Victor was before ambition consumed him."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Context","q":"What is Clerval's long-term plan?","o":["To go to India","To marry in England","To become a professor","To join the army"],"a":0,"e":"He planned 'to visit India', using his languages to assist 'European colonization and trade'. This is a sign of the novel's colonial moment."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Language","q":"Collecting materials feels to Victor 'like the torture of single drops of water'. What does the simile stress?","o":["Cold indifference","A quick, sharp shock","Slow, endless torment","Cleansing and renewal"],"a":2,"e":"Water torture is unbearable because each drop is small and never stops."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Plot","q":"Who invites them to Scotland?","o":["A professor at Edinburgh","A cousin of Clerval","A former visitor to Geneva","A merchant from Perth they met in London"],"a":2,"e":"'A person in Scotland who had formerly been our visitor at Geneva.' He lives at Perth."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor pack for the trip north?","o":["His instruments and materials","Letters for his father","Only his clothes and books","Gifts for the Scottish friend"],"a":0,"e":"'I packed up my chemical instruments and the materials I had collected.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Plot","q":"On what date did they leave London?","o":["1 January","31 July","14 February","27 March"],"a":3,"e":"'We quitted London on the 27th of March.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"What was new to these 'mountaineers' at Windsor?","o":["Factories and canals","Palaces and soldiers","Oaks, game and deer","Rivers, bridges and boats"],"a":2,"e":"'The majestic oaks, the quantity of game, and the herds of stately deer were all novelties to us.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Context","q":"Which king does Oxford remind them of?","o":["Henry VIII","William the Conqueror","Charles I","George III"],"a":2,"e":"'It was here that Charles I. had collected his forces.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"Which river flows beside Oxford in the novel?","o":["The Isis","The Thames at Greenwich","The Avon","The Cam"],"a":0,"e":"'The lovely Isis, which flows beside it through meadows of exquisite verdure.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Quote","q":"'I am a blasted tree; the bolt has entered my soul.' Which earlier scene does this recall?","o":["William's murder","The fire in the forest","The oak struck by lightning","Prometheus stealing fire"],"a":2,"e":"In Chapter 2 young Victor saw an oak reduced to 'a blasted stump' by lightning. Now he is that tree."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor expects to 'exhibit what I shall soon cease to be'. What is that?","o":["A wreck of a man","A free man","A famous scientist","A married man"],"a":0,"e":"'A miserable spectacle of wrecked humanity, pitiable to others and intolerable to myself.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Context","q":"Whose tomb, and the field where he fell, do they visit?","o":["Goring's","Falkland's","Cromwell's","Hampden's"],"a":3,"e":"'We visited the tomb of the illustrious Hampden and the field on which that patriot fell.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Theme","q":"What ideas do Hampden's monuments briefly stir in Victor?","o":["Revenge and glory","Liberty and self-sacrifice","Love and marriage","Science and progress"],"a":1,"e":"'The divine ideas of liberty and self-sacrifice', but 'the iron had eaten into my flesh'."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Language","q":"'The iron had eaten into my flesh.' What image is this?","o":["A soldier's armour","A blacksmith's anvil","A surgeon's knife","A slave's chains"],"a":3,"e":"He 'dared to shake off my chains', but he is a slave to his promise."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"How does Matlock compare to Switzerland?","o":["Grander and wilder","Exactly the same","Similar, but smaller","Completely different"],"a":2,"e":"'Everything is on a lower scale, and the green hills want the crown of distant white Alps.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Plot","q":"Why does Victor hurry to leave Matlock?","o":["He receives bad news","He sees the creature","Henry mentions Chamounix","The inn is full"],"a":2,"e":"Its curiosities were arranged like those at 'Servox and Chamounix. The latter name made me tremble when pronounced by Henry.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Plot","q":"How long do they spend in Cumberland and Westmorland?","o":["Two months","Two weeks","A single day","Six months"],"a":0,"e":"'We passed two months in Cumberland and Westmorland.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Quote","q":"'I could pass my life here… I should scarcely regret Switzerland.' Where is Clerval?","o":["Oxford","Windsor","Edinburgh","The Lake District"],"a":3,"e":"He says it among the lakes and mountains of Cumberland."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Theme","q":"What 'pain amidst its enjoyments' does the traveller's life involve?","o":["Always leaving what you love","Constant danger from robbers","The expense of travel","Homesickness for one's family"],"a":0,"e":"He must 'quit that on which he rests in pleasure for something new'."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Character","q":"How does Victor behave around Henry when he fears the fiend?","o":["Tells him the whole secret","Avoids him completely","Sends him home to Geneva","Shadows him to protect him"],"a":3,"e":"'I would not quit Henry for a moment, but followed him as his shadow.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Theme","q":"'I was guiltless, but I had indeed drawn down a horrible curse.' What is Victor insisting on?","o":["God is punishing him justly","Cursed but not criminal","The creature is innocent","He is guilty of murder"],"a":1,"e":"He separates guilt from consequence, a distinction the novel keeps testing."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"Why did Clerval like Edinburgh less than Oxford?","o":["Oxford was more ancient","Edinburgh was far too cold and windy","The people there were unfriendly to strangers","He knew nobody at all in the city"],"a":0,"e":"'Clerval did not like it so well as Oxford, for the antiquity of the latter city was more pleasing to him.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"Which Edinburgh sight is NOT mentioned?","o":["Holyrood Palace","St Bernard's Well","The Pentland Hills","Arthur's Seat"],"a":0,"e":"The text lists 'its romantic castle… Arthur's Seat, St. Bernard's Well, and the Pentland Hills'."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Plot","q":"Where does Victor part from Clerval?","o":["Oxford","Perth","Edinburgh","London"],"a":1,"e":"They travel 'along the banks of the Tay, to Perth', where Victor says he will tour Scotland alone."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Irony","q":"Clerval says, 'hasten… to return, that I may again feel myself somewhat at home.' Why is this painful?","o":["Victor never writes to him again","Clerval secretly dislikes Victor","Clerval will travel home without Victor","They'll meet over his body"],"a":3,"e":"The next time they 'meet', Victor is shown Henry's body."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"Where does Victor set up his second laboratory?","o":["A castle in the Highlands","A cave near Edinburgh","An inn in Perth","A remote Orkney island"],"a":3,"e":"'I… fixed on one of the remotest of the Orkneys as the scene of my labours.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"How many people live on the island?","o":["Five","Twelve","One","Fifty"],"a":0,"e":"Its 'inhabitants, which consisted of five persons, whose gaunt and scraggy limbs gave tokens of their miserable fare'."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"How far is the island from the mainland?","o":["A day's sail","Half a mile","About fifty miles","About five miles"],"a":3,"e":"'The mainland, which was about five miles distant.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Plot","q":"What state was the hut in when Victor hired it?","o":["Full of fishing nets","Neat and freshly whitewashed","Burned to the ground","Thatch fallen in, door off"],"a":3,"e":"'The thatch had fallen in, the walls were unplastered, and the door was off its hinges.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Theme","q":"Why did the islanders show no surprise at Victor?","o":["They thought he was a doctor","Poverty had numbed them","They were too frightened","They were paid to stay quiet"],"a":1,"e":"'So much does suffering blunt even the coarsest sensations of men.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"How does Victor contrast Swiss lakes with the Orkney sea?","o":["Cold and grey against warm and golden","Infant's play vs giant's roar","Dark and stormy against bright and clear","Silent and still against loud and musical"],"a":1,"e":"Swiss lakes in storms are 'but as the play of a lively infant when compared to the roarings of the giant ocean'."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Connection","q":"How does the second creation differ from the first?","o":["He works only at night","He works faster and more happily","He works in cold blood","He works with Clerval's help"],"a":2,"e":"'During my first experiment, a kind of enthusiastic frenzy had blinded me… But now I went to it in cold blood.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Character","q":"What is Victor most afraid of on the island?","o":["Drowning in the sea","Running out of materials","The islanders discovering him","Meeting his persecutor"],"a":3,"e":"'Every moment I feared to meet my persecutor.'"},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Close reading","q":"How does Victor feel about completing the female?","o":["Pride in his own skill as a scientist","Calm indifference to the outcome","Hope mixed with dread","Pure joy at being nearly free of his promise"],"a":2,"e":"'A tremulous and eager hope… intermixed with obscure forebodings of evil.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"How many years separate the first creation from the second?","o":["Seven","Three","Five","One"],"a":1,"e":"'Three years before, I was engaged in the same manner and had created a fiend.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Theme","q":"Which fear does Victor NOT list about the female?","o":["She'd prefer human men","She'd kill Victor herself","She'd be more malignant","She'd refuse the compact"],"a":1,"e":"He fears she might be 'ten thousand times more malignant', refuse a 'compact made before her creation', or turn 'to the superior beauty of man'."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Close reading","q":"'She… might refuse to comply with a compact made before her creation.' What is Victor recognising?","o":["She'd have her own will","She would love the creature at once","She would obey Victor only","She would be unable to speak"],"a":0,"e":"A 'thinking and reasoning animal' can't be bound by a deal she never made."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Connection","q":"Victor fears the creature might loathe the female's deformity. What earlier moment does this echo?","o":["William calling him 'ogre'","Felix striking him","The creature at the pool","Victor fleeing from the bedside"],"a":2,"e":"The creature recoiled from his own reflection; he might recoil from his own image in her."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Theme","q":"What is Victor's biggest fear about the pair?","o":["That they'd starve in the wild","That they'd tell the world his secret","Children: a race of devils","That they'd return to Geneva"],"a":2,"e":"'A race of devils would be propagated upon the earth.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor call the creature's earlier arguments?","o":["Sophisms","Prayers","Truths","Riddles"],"a":0,"e":"'I had before been moved by the sophisms of the being I had created.' He means clever but false reasoning."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor fear future ages might call him?","o":["Their saviour","A martyr","Their pest","Their father"],"a":2,"e":"'Future ages might curse me as their pest, whose selfishness had not hesitated to buy its own peace.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"Where does Victor see the creature?","o":["At the window by moonlight","In a boat offshore","In the laboratory doorway","On the beach at dawn"],"a":0,"e":"'I saw by the light of the moon the dæmon at the casement.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Close reading","q":"What does the creature's 'ghastly grin' suggest?","o":["Fear that Victor will turn on him with a weapon","The first signs of madness after years alone","Satisfaction at the work","Pain from the bullet wound in his shoulder"],"a":2,"e":"He grins 'as he gazed on me, where I sat fulfilling the task which he had allotted to me'."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Character","q":"What triggers Victor to tear the female apart?","o":["A sudden noise outside","A letter from Elizabeth","The creature's malicious face","The female moving"],"a":2,"e":"'His countenance expressed the utmost extent of malice and treachery… trembling with passion, tore to pieces the thing.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Language","q":"Victor calls the half-made female 'the thing'. Why does the word matter?","o":["He is quoting the creature","It's how scientists speak","He has forgotten her name","It denies her personhood"],"a":3,"e":"By the end he says it felt like mangling 'the living flesh of a human being'."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"How does the creature react to the destruction?","o":["He attacks Victor at once","A howl, then withdraws","He laughs and leaves","He weeps silently"],"a":1,"e":"'With a howl of devilish despair and revenge, withdrew.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor is 'rooted to the spot' as the door creaks. What comparison does he use?","o":["A deer before a hunter","A soldier frozen in battle","Helplessness in a nightmare","A statue in a church"],"a":2,"e":"'The sensation of helplessness, so often felt in frightful dreams.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"Which journey does the creature NOT mention when he confronts Victor?","o":["The deserts of Scotland","Sailing from Rotterdam","Along the Rhine's shores","The heaths of England"],"a":1,"e":"'I crept along the shores of the Rhine… dwelt many months in the heaths of England and among the deserts of Scotland.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Quote","q":"'You are my creator, but I am your master; obey!' What reversal does this show?","o":["The creature now serves God instead of Victor","Creator and creature have become equals","The creature now has power","Victor at last rules over his creation"],"a":2,"e":"The made thing now commands its maker, as a master to a slave."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Quote","q":"'Shall each man find a wife for his bosom, and each beast have his mate, and I be alone?' What is the argument?","o":["Marriage itself is unnatural and cruel","Even beasts get mates","He secretly wants Elizabeth for himself","Men are much crueller than any wild beast"],"a":1,"e":"He is denied what nature gives to every animal."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Language","q":"The creature says he will watch 'with the wiliness of a snake'. Which allusion does this carry?","o":["The serpent on a doctor's staff","Medusa's hair","Satan in Eden","Cleopatra's asp"],"a":2,"e":"Satan tempts Eve as a serpent; the creature now openly chooses the Satanic role."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Quote","q":"'Beware, for I am fearless and therefore…' what?","o":["Free","Powerful","Alone","Immortal"],"a":1,"e":"'I am fearless and therefore powerful.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"'I shall be with you on your wedding-night.' How does Victor misread it?","o":["He thinks it is an empty threat","He thinks Elizabeth will be warned","He thinks it means a visit to talk","He thinks he'll be killed"],"a":3,"e":"'In that hour I should die.' He never imagines Elizabeth is the target."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Close reading","q":"What makes Victor cry for the first time in months?","o":["His memories of William","Being alone on the island","The creature's threat","Imagining Elizabeth's grief"],"a":3,"e":"'When I thought of my beloved Elizabeth, of her tears… tears, the first I had shed for many months.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"Which way does the creature's boat head?","o":["North to the Arctic","Towards the mainland","Out to the Atlantic","Towards Geneva"],"a":1,"e":"'He had directed his course towards the mainland.' Clerval is on the mainland."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Language","q":"The fiend's words 'rang in my ears like a…' what?","o":["Wedding bell","Trumpet call","Church choir","Death-knell"],"a":3,"e":"'The words of the fiend rang in my ears like a death-knell.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"What does Clerval's letter ask Victor to do?","o":["Come straight to London","Wait on the island","Meet him at Perth","Write to Geneva"],"a":2,"e":"'He besought me, therefore, to leave my solitary isle and to meet him at Perth.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"Why must Clerval head south soon?","o":["Elizabeth has summoned him","His money has run out","His father is ill","His Indian venture needs him"],"a":3,"e":"London friends 'desired his return to complete the negotiation… for his Indian enterprise'."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Close reading","q":"Entering the lab, Victor felt 'as if I had mangled the living flesh of a human being'. What does this suggest?","o":["She seems almost human","He thinks he has killed one of the islanders","He has cut himself badly on his instruments","He is beginning to lose his mind entirely"],"a":0,"e":"The 'thing' he destroyed now seems human to him, which complicates his choice."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor do with the remains of the female?","o":["Leaves them for the creature","Buries them on the beach","Sinks them in a weighted basket","Burns them in the hut"],"a":2,"e":"'I accordingly put them into a basket, with a great quantity of stones… to throw them into the sea.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says 'a film had been taken from before my eyes'. What is he now sure of?","o":["The creature is evil beyond any redemption","Another would be selfish","He should sail home to Geneva at once","God has forgiven him for the first creation"],"a":1,"e":"To create again 'would be an act of the basest and most atrocious selfishness'."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"How far out does Victor sail to dump the basket?","o":["Half a mile","About four miles","Just past the rocks","Twenty miles"],"a":1,"e":"'I… sailed out about four miles from the shore.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Irony","q":"While dumping the basket, Victor feels 'as if I was about the commission of a dreadful crime'. Why is this ironic?","o":["He's about to be accused of one","He is pretending to fish","He is in fact a criminal already","He has just been forgiven"],"a":0,"e":"Within a day he lands in Ireland and is charged with murder."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"Why can't Victor steer back to the Orkneys?","o":["The creature cuts the sail","The rudder breaks off","He has lost the oars","The wind would swamp the boat"],"a":3,"e":"'If I again made the attempt the boat would be instantly filled with water.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Quote","q":"'Fiend, your task is already fulfilled!' Why does Victor say this?","o":["He thinks he'll die at sea","He has found a body","He hears of Clerval's death","He has seen the creature"],"a":0,"e":"He thinks the sea 'was to be my grave', leaving his family unprotected."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Theme","q":"'How strange is that clinging love we have of life even in the excess of misery!' What prompts this?","o":["Clerval's letter","The creature's threats","Waking on the island","His joy at seeing land"],"a":3,"e":"He sees 'a line of high land' and 'tears gushed from my eyes'."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"How does Victor make a new sail?","o":["From part of his clothes","From a blanket","From the basket","From the boat's cover"],"a":0,"e":"'I constructed another sail with a part of my dress.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Character","q":"Why does Victor make himself stand tall before the crowd?","o":["To hide his wound","To impress the magistrate","Out of pride","To avoid looking guilty"],"a":3,"e":"'That no physical debility might be construed into apprehension or conscious guilt.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Quote","q":"'It is the custom of the Irish to hate villains.' What has Victor just assumed?","o":["That he was in Scotland","That the man was drunk","That he was being welcomed","That he was in England"],"a":3,"e":"He asked if it was 'the custom of Englishmen to receive strangers so inhospitably'."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Character","q":"Who is Mr Kirwin?","o":["A doctor","A magistrate","A priest","An innkeeper"],"a":1,"e":"'Mr. Kirwin is a magistrate, and you are to give an account of the death of a gentleman.'"},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Structure","q":"Victor ends the chapter 'I must pause here'. What does this pause do?","o":["Suspense before Henry's death","It signals that Victor is about to die","It marks the change to a new narrator","It lets Walton add a letter of his own"],"a":0,"e":"He breaks off to steel himself, holding back the 'frightful events' he must relate next."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Character","q":"How is Mr Kirwin first described?","o":["Young, harsh and suspicious","Proud and aristocratic","Drunk and careless","Old, benevolent and mild"],"a":3,"e":"'An old benevolent man with calm and mild manners.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Character","q":"What was Daniel Nugent's relationship to the first witness?","o":["Neighbour","Son","Cousin","Brother-in-law"],"a":3,"e":"The witness was fishing 'with his son and brother-in-law, Daniel Nugent'."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"How did the fisherman find the body?","o":["He tripped over it","It washed into his net","He saw it from the boat","His dog found it"],"a":0,"e":"'He struck his foot against something and fell at his length on the ground.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Close reading","q":"Why did the men realise the man had not drowned?","o":["He had a knife wound","He was far from the sea","His clothes weren't wet","He was still breathing"],"a":2,"e":"'They found that the clothes were not wet and even that the body was not then cold.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"About how old was the murdered man?","o":["About twenty-five","About forty","About fifty","About eighteen"],"a":0,"e":"'A handsome young man, about five and twenty years of age.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Connection","q":"What mark on the body links this murder to William's?","o":["A broken skull","A portrait in his pocket","A stab wound in the chest","Black finger marks on the neck"],"a":3,"e":"'The black mark of fingers on his neck.' The creature strangles his victims."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Irony","q":"Why does Kirwin view Victor with suspicion during the evidence?","o":["He is found to be carrying a hidden knife","He refuses to answer any of Kirwin's questions","He nearly faints","He tries to run away through the crowd"],"a":2,"e":"Victor's agitation is grief and recognition, but it looks like guilt."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"What does Daniel Nugent swear he saw?","o":["Two men fighting on the sand by lantern light","A giant figure striding along the beach","A woman running away from the creek","A lone man in a similar boat"],"a":3,"e":"'He saw a boat, with a single man in it… the same boat in which I had just landed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"Who was sent for to try to revive the body?","o":["An apothecary","A surgeon from Dublin","A magistrate","A priest"],"a":0,"e":"'Daniel went to the town for an apothecary, but life was quite gone.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Theme","q":"How do the witnesses explain Victor landing near the body?","o":["He came back on purpose to look for the body","He meant to return and bury it secretly","He was lost in a sea fog off the coast","The wind drove him back"],"a":3,"e":"They thought he had 'beaten about for many hours and had been obliged to return nearly to the same spot'."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Close reading","q":"Why is Victor 'perfectly tranquil' going to view the body?","o":["He doesn't care if he dies","He has an island alibi","Kirwin has promised to free him","He thinks it's a stranger"],"a":1,"e":"'I had been conversing with several persons in the island… about the time that the body had been found.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Quote","q":"'Have my murderous machinations deprived you also, my dearest Henry, of life?' Why is this so damaging?","o":["It is spoken in French, which they distrust","It insults Kirwin and the Irish witnesses","It sounds like a confession","It reveals the creature's existence to all"],"a":2,"e":"To the onlookers, 'murderous machinations' sounds like guilt."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"How long does Victor lie in fever?","o":["Six months","Two weeks","Three days","Two months"],"a":3,"e":"'I lay for two months on the point of death.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Irony","q":"Why don't Victor's ravings convict him?","o":["Everyone ignored him","He spoke too softly","Only Kirwin knew his language","He only raved at night"],"a":2,"e":"'As I spoke my native language, Mr. Kirwin alone understood me.' He called himself 'the murderer of William, of Justine, and of Clerval'."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Language","q":"Victor compares his repeated shocks to 'the turning of the' what?","o":["Seasons","Key","Wheel","Tide"],"a":2,"e":"'Like the turning of the wheel, continually renewed the torture.' He means the torture wheel."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Character","q":"Who is the nurse at the prison?","o":["Elizabeth in disguise","Kirwin's sister","A turnkey's wife","An Irish nun"],"a":2,"e":"'She was a hired nurse, the wife of one of the turnkeys.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Quote","q":"'I do my duty with a safe conscience; it were well if everybody did the same.' What is ironic about the nurse's words?","o":["She is secretly kind","She is the murderer","She is quoting the Bible","She shows no compassion"],"a":3,"e":"She is dutiful but heartless, the opposite of the novel's nursing heroines."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Theme","q":"'Who could be interested in the fate of a murderer but the hangman who would gain his fee?' What does Victor later learn?","o":["Nobody had helped him","Kirwin had been very kind","His father had paid the doctor","The hangman had been paid"],"a":1,"e":"'I soon learned that Mr. Kirwin had shown me extreme kindness.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"What did Kirwin provide for Victor?","o":["A private house outside the town","Best room, doctor, nurse","A free passage home to Geneva","A lawyer brought over from Dublin"],"a":1,"e":"'He had caused the best room in the prison to be prepared for me… provided a physician and a nurse.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Connection","q":"Victor considers declaring himself guilty, 'less innocent than poor Justine'. What links them?","o":["Both were tried at Geneva","Both confessed falsely","Both wrongly accused","Both died in prison"],"a":2,"e":"Victor now stands where Justine stood, accused of a murder the creature committed."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"In which language does Kirwin speak privately to Victor?","o":["French","Latin","German","English"],"a":0,"e":"'He drew a chair close to mine and addressed me in French.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"How did Kirwin learn who Victor was?","o":["From Clerval's luggage","From Victor's ravings","From the nurse","From papers on him"],"a":3,"e":"'All the papers that were on your person were brought me… one which I discovered… to be from your father.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Close reading","q":"Kirwin says the body was 'placed, as it were, by some fiend across your path'. What is ironic?","o":["Kirwin is lying","The nurse placed it","Kirwin suspects the creature","That's literally true"],"a":3,"e":"Kirwin is speaking figuratively, but a fiend did exactly that."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"Who does Victor think the visitor is?","o":["Ernest","Elizabeth","The creature","A lawyer"],"a":2,"e":"He thought 'the murderer had come to mock at my misery'."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Character","q":"How does Kirwin react to 'Take him away!'?","o":["He laughs","He calls the guard","He leaves at once","He suspects guilt"],"a":3,"e":"'He could not help regarding my exclamation as a presumption of my guilt.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Quote","q":"'You travelled to seek happiness, but a fatality seems to pursue you.' Who says it?","o":["Elizabeth","Alphonse","Clerval's father","Kirwin"],"a":1,"e":"Victor's father says it in the Irish prison."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Language","q":"What does Victor call his father's arrival?","o":["His good angel","His judgement","His salvation","His last hope"],"a":0,"e":"'The appearance of my father was to me like that of my good angel.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"How long had Victor been in prison when the assizes came?","o":["Three weeks","Three months","One year","Six months"],"a":1,"e":"'I had already been three months in prison.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"How far did Victor travel to the court?","o":["Nowhere; it came to him","About ten miles","Across the sea to Dublin","Nearly a hundred miles"],"a":3,"e":"'I was obliged to travel nearly a hundred miles to the country town where the court was held.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"Why does the grand jury reject the charge?","o":["He was in the Orkneys","Kirwin paid them","A witness confessed","The creature was seen"],"a":0,"e":"'It being proved that I was on the Orkney Islands at the hour the body of my friend was found.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Symbol","q":"Whose eyes haunt Victor after his release?","o":["Elizabeth's and Caroline's","William's and Justine's","His father's and Kirwin's","Henry's and the monster's"],"a":3,"e":"Henry's 'languishing in death' and 'the watery, clouded eyes of the monster'."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Language","q":"Victor feels a devouring 'maladie du pays'. What is it?","o":["Madness","Guilt","Homesickness","Fever"],"a":2,"e":"It is homesickness for 'the blue lake and rapid Rhone'."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Character","q":"What makes Victor finally want to live and go home?","o":["To marry Elizabeth as his father wishes","To clear his name in a Geneva court","To see the lake and the Rhone again","To protect his family"],"a":3,"e":"'One duty remained… to watch over the lives of those I so fondly loved and to lie in wait for the murderer.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"To which port do they sail from Ireland?","o":["Marseilles","Calais","Rotterdam","Havre-de-Grace"],"a":3,"e":"'We took our passage on board a vessel bound for Havre-de-Grace.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"What drug does Victor take every night?","o":["Quinine","Brandy","Laudanum","Opium smoke"],"a":2,"e":"'I had been in the custom of taking every night a small quantity of laudanum.'"},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Close reading","q":"On the ship, what dose of laudanum does Victor take?","o":["None at all","Half his usual","Double his usual","His usual dose"],"a":2,"e":"'I now swallowed double my usual quantity.' Even then the nightmare comes: 'the fiend's grasp in my neck'."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Theme","q":"Waking from the nightmare, Victor feels 'a truce' with the future. What does he say about the mind?","o":["It never forgets anything","It is stronger than the body","It is ruled by dreams","It easily forgets for a while"],"a":3,"e":"'A kind of calm forgetfulness, of which the human mind is by its structure peculiarly susceptible.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Close reading","q":"'I abhorred the face of man. Oh, not abhorred!' What does Victor's self-correction show?","o":["He loves people but feels unworthy","He is confused about his own words","He fears that people will attack him","He truly hates all of humanity"],"a":0,"e":"He sees them as 'creatures of an angelic nature', but 'I had no right to share their intercourse'."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Character","q":"What does Alphonse think is troubling Victor in Paris?","o":["Love for another woman met abroad","Fear of returning home to Geneva","Grief for Henry Clerval alone","Wounded pride at the charge"],"a":3,"e":"'He thought that I felt deeply the degradation of being obliged to answer a charge of murder.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Quote","q":"'William, Justine, and Henry—they all died by my hands.' How does Alphonse take this?","o":["As a riddle","As delirium","As a joke","As a confession"],"a":1,"e":"He 'appeared to consider it as the offspring of delirium'."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Theme","q":"Which reason does Victor NOT give for keeping his secret?","o":["He'd be thought mad","He'd be arrested","He can't bring himself to tell it","It would horrify his hearer"],"a":1,"e":"He fears being 'supposed mad', and would not 'make fear and unnatural horror the inmates of his breast'."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Quote","q":"'I could not sacrifice the whole human race.' What is Victor referring to?","o":["Marrying Elizabeth","Telling the secret","Killing the creature","Refusing to make the female"],"a":3,"e":"He means he could not save his loved ones by creating a mate for the creature."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Irony","q":"What convinces Alphonse that Victor's mind is 'deranged'?","o":["The one part that is true","Victor's violent behaviour","Victor's laughter","Victor's refusal to eat"],"a":0,"e":"Victor's most rational sentence, about the human race, sounds the maddest."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"Where are they when Elizabeth's letter arrives?","o":["In Paris","In Geneva","At sea","In Ireland"],"a":0,"e":"'A few days before we left Paris on our way to Switzerland, I received the following letter.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Character","q":"What does Elizabeth ask Victor directly in her letter?","o":["Will he come home?","Is he ill?","Does he love another?","Did he kill Clerval?"],"a":2,"e":"'Answer me, I conjure you by our mutual happiness, with simple truth—Do you not love another?'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Connection","q":"Elizabeth's question echoes whose earlier worry?","o":["Caroline's in Chapter 3","Clerval's in Chapter 5","Justine's in Chapter 8","Alphonse's in Chapter 18"],"a":3,"e":"Alphonse also asked if Victor regarded her 'as your sister' or loved another."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Theme","q":"What does Elizabeth say would make her 'eternally miserable'?","o":["A long engagement with no date fixed","Seeing Victor die before she does","Having to live far away from Geneva","A marriage without free choice"],"a":3,"e":"'Our marriage would render me eternally miserable unless it were the dictate of your own free choice.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Language","q":"Which word does Elizabeth fear Victor will use to stifle love?","o":["Honour","Duty","Promise","Family"],"a":0,"e":"'You may stifle, by the word honour, all hope of that love and happiness.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Character","q":"How does Elizabeth ask Victor to reply?","o":["In person at the wedding","Only when it doesn't pain him","Through his father","At once, by return of post"],"a":1,"e":"'Do not answer tomorrow, or the next day, or even until you come, if it will give you pain.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"What date is on Elizabeth's letter?","o":["1 January","25 December","18 May","18 March"],"a":2,"e":"'Geneva, May 18th, 17—'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor imagines the 'freedom' of victory as like that of which peasant?","o":["One whose family is massacred","One who has paid his rent","One who wins the lottery","One who escapes a prison"],"a":0,"e":"'Homeless, penniless, and alone, but free.' This freedom is empty."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Allusion","q":"'The apple was already eaten, and the angel's arm bared to drive me from all hope.' Which story is this?","o":["Prometheus and the fire","Pandora's box","The judgement of Paris","The Fall from Eden"],"a":3,"e":"Victor casts himself as Adam after the Fall, driven out by the angel."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Close reading","q":"Why doesn't Victor delay the wedding to stay safe?","o":["His father is too old and ill to wait","Elizabeth insists the wedding be held soon","He wants to die as quickly as possible","Worse revenge would follow"],"a":3,"e":"If the creature 'should suspect that I postponed it… he would surely find other and perhaps more dreadful means of revenge'."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"When does Victor promise to tell Elizabeth his secret?","o":["Before the wedding","On the wedding night","Never","The day after the wedding"],"a":3,"e":"'I will confide this tale of misery and terror to you the day after our marriage shall take place.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Irony","q":"Victor writes 'there must be perfect confidence between us'. What makes this ironic?","o":["He withholds the secret","He is lying about loving her","She already knows","He has already told her"],"a":0,"e":"He calls for perfect confidence in the same letter where he puts off telling her."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Character","q":"How has Elizabeth changed when Victor returns?","o":["Thinner, less lively","Older and harder","Ill with fever","Happier than before"],"a":0,"e":"'She was thinner and had lost much of that heavenly vivacity.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Quote","q":"'It is well for the unfortunate to be resigned, but for the guilty there is no…' what?","o":["Peace","Pardon","Rest","Hope"],"a":0,"e":"'For the guilty there is no peace.' Resignation helps sufferers, not the guilty."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Character","q":"What does Alphonse hope will replace the lost family members?","o":["New children","New friends","Religion","Travel"],"a":0,"e":"'New and dear objects of care will be born to replace those of whom we have been so cruelly deprived.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"How many days until the wedding are agreed?","o":["Ten","Thirty","Three","Seven"],"a":0,"e":"'The ceremony should take place in ten days.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"'When I thought that I had prepared only my own death, I hastened that of a far dearer victim.' What narrative technique is this?","o":["A letter inside the narrative","Retrospective foreshadowing","A dream sequence","An unreliable lie"],"a":1,"e":"The older Victor tells us outright what the younger Victor could not see."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Character","q":"Who sees through Victor's pretended cheerfulness?","o":["The servants","Alphonse","Ernest","Elizabeth"],"a":3,"e":"It 'hardly deceived the ever-watchful and nicer eye of Elizabeth'."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"What has been restored to Elizabeth by the Austrian government?","o":["Part of her inheritance","Her father's title","A house in Milan","Her mother's jewels"],"a":0,"e":"'A part of the inheritance of Elizabeth had been restored to her by the Austrian government.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Setting","q":"Where is the Villa Lavenza?","o":["Lake Geneva","Lake Lucerne","Lake Como","Lake Maggiore"],"a":2,"e":"'A small possession on the shores of Como belonged to her.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor carry to protect himself?","o":["A hunting rifle loaded with ball","A sword borrowed from his father","Nothing, trusting to his own strength","Pistols and a dagger"],"a":3,"e":"'I carried pistols and a dagger constantly about me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Irony","q":"How does Alphonse interpret Elizabeth's sadness on the wedding day?","o":["Doubt about Victor","Grief for Clerval","A bride's shyness","Illness"],"a":2,"e":"He 'only recognised in the melancholy of his niece the diffidence of a bride'."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Setting","q":"Where do the couple plan to spend their wedding night?","o":["Belrive","Evian","Geneva","Chamounix"],"a":1,"e":"'Sleeping that night at Evian and continuing our voyage on the following day.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Close reading","q":"'Those were the last moments of my life during which I enjoyed the feeling of happiness.' Where is Victor?","o":["At the altar","At Belrive with his father","In the inn at Evian","On the lake after the wedding"],"a":3,"e":"He says it of the boat journey across the lake."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Symbol","q":"The Jura is called 'an almost insurmountable barrier to the invader'. Why mention this here?","o":["The boat must avoid the Jura","Victor's defences will fail","Switzerland is about to be invaded","Elizabeth fears soldiers"],"a":1,"e":"The imagery of a protecting barrier and an invader hangs over the wedding night."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Character","q":"What does Elizabeth say 'whispers' to her?","o":["That Victor is lying","That she will die","Not to trust their future","That the creature is near"],"a":2,"e":"'Something whispers to me not to depend too much on the prospect that is opened before us.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Close reading","q":"Elizabeth points out fish and every pebble on the lake bed. Why?","o":["To mock Victor's science","To change the boat's course","To distract them both","To show she is a naturalist"],"a":2,"e":"She 'endeavoured to divert her thoughts and mine from all reflection upon melancholy subjects'."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Setting","q":"Which river do they pass before Evian?","o":["The Drance","The Arve","The Rhine","The Rhone"],"a":0,"e":"'We passed the river Drance and observed its path through the chasms.'"},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Close reading","q":"What smell does the breeze carry as they land?","o":["Smoke and pine","Flowers and hay","Rain and earth","Salt and seaweed"],"a":1,"e":"It 'wafted the most delightful scent of flowers and hay', a moment of beauty before the horror."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"At what time did Victor and Elizabeth land at Evian?","o":["Noon","Six in the morning","Midnight","Eight o'clock"],"a":3,"e":"'It was eight o'clock when we landed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Symbol","q":"What does the weather do on the wedding night?","o":["A violent wind and storm","Thick fog rolls in","Snow falls on the lake","It stays calm and clear"],"a":0,"e":"The wind 'now rose with great violence in the west… a heavy storm of rain descended'. Storms accompany each disaster."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Language","q":"The clouds swept across the moon 'swifter than the flight of the' what?","o":["Arrow","Vulture","Eagle","Swallow"],"a":1,"e":"'Swifter than the flight of the vulture.' It is a bird of death."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"What is Victor holding as night falls?","o":["Elizabeth's hand","A dagger and a candle","A pistol hidden in his bosom","A lantern"],"a":2,"e":"'My right hand grasped a pistol which was hidden in my bosom.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Quote","q":"'This night, and all will be safe; but this night is dreadful, very dreadful.' What does Victor wrongly believe?","o":["Elizabeth is the one in real danger tonight","The creature has given up and left Switzerland","He himself will be killed tomorrow, not tonight","Surviving it means safety"],"a":3,"e":"He thinks only this one night, and only his own life, is at stake."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Irony","q":"Why does Victor send Elizabeth to bed alone?","o":["He is angry with her","He wants to sleep","To spare her the fight","She is tired"],"a":2,"e":"He wanted to spare her 'how fearful the combat… would be to my wife', and left her alone."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Close reading","q":"When Victor hears the scream, 'the whole truth rushed into my mind'. What truth?","o":["The inn was on fire","He was being robbed","Elizabeth was the target","His father was dying"],"a":2,"e":"At that moment he understands what 'I will be with you on your wedding-night' meant."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Language","q":"Elizabeth is 'flung by the murderer on its bridal bier'. What do the words combine?","o":["Love and war","Wedding and funeral","Science and religion","Life and creation"],"a":1,"e":"A bier carries a corpse; 'bridal bier' fuses the wedding bed with a coffin stand."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Quote","q":"'Life is obstinate and clings closest where it is most hated.' Why does Victor say this?","o":["The creature can't be killed","His father won't die","He survives what should kill him","Elizabeth still breathes"],"a":2,"e":"He wonders why he did not 'then expire'."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Connection","q":"How was Elizabeth killed?","o":["Stabbed","Poisoned","Strangled","Drowned"],"a":2,"e":"'The murderous mark of the fiend's grasp was on her neck.' This is the same method used on William and Clerval."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Close reading","q":"After Elizabeth is moved, why might Victor 'have supposed her asleep'?","o":["The room was dark","She looked peaceful","A handkerchief covers her face","Her eyes were closed"],"a":2,"e":"'Her head upon her arm and a handkerchief thrown across her face and neck.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"What does the creature do at the window?","o":["Climbs into the room and seizes the body","Shouts a new threat against Victor's father","Grins and points","Weeps at the window and begs for forgiveness"],"a":2,"e":"'A grin was on the face of the monster; he seemed to jeer, as with his fiendish finger he pointed towards the corpse.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"How does the creature escape the pistol shot?","o":["He is wounded but flees","By climbing the roof","By plunging into the lake","By running into the woods"],"a":2,"e":"He 'leaped from his station, and running with the swiftness of lightning, plunged into the lake'."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Theme","q":"What do most of the searchers believe about the creature?","o":["It was a robber","It drowned","It was a bear","Victor imagined it"],"a":3,"e":"'Most of my companions believing it to have been a form conjured up by my fancy.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"What thought makes Victor rush back to Geneva?","o":["He wants to report the murder to the syndics","Fear for his father and Ernest","He believes the creature has swum to Geneva","He must arrange Elizabeth's burial at Belrive"],"a":1,"e":"'My father even now might be writhing under his grasp, and Ernest might be dead at his feet.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"Why must Victor return by the lake?","o":["No horses could be got","The boat was faster","The roads were flooded","He feared the woods"],"a":0,"e":"'There were no horses to be procured, and I must return by the lake.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Connection","q":"On the way back Victor sees 'the fish play in the waters'. Why does this hurt?","o":["He is hungry","They remind him of his experiments","They look like the creature","Elizabeth pointed them out"],"a":3,"e":"Elizabeth had pointed out the 'innumerable fish' the day before."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Quote","q":"'Nothing is so painful to the human mind as a great and sudden…' what?","o":["Shock","Loss","Fear","Change"],"a":3,"e":"'Nothing is so painful to the human mind as a great and sudden change.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Language","q":"Victor says he has reached the 'acme' of his horrors. What does acme mean?","o":["The highest point","The explanation","The middle","The beginning"],"a":0,"e":"'Mine has been a tale of horrors; I have reached their acme.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"How does Alphonse die?","o":["Of grief, in Victor's arms","Killed by the creature","In a fall","Of fever in prison"],"a":0,"e":"'The springs of existence suddenly gave way… in a few days he died in my arms.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Character","q":"Who of Victor's family survives?","o":["Ernest and Elizabeth","Ernest","Alphonse","No one"],"a":1,"e":"'My father and Ernest yet lived.' Ernest is never mentioned again."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"Where is Victor kept after his father's death?","o":["In a solitary cell","In a convent","At Belrive","In a hospital in Geneva"],"a":0,"e":"'For they had called me mad, and during many months… a solitary cell had been my habitation.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Theme","q":"What gives Victor's freedom a purpose?","o":["Science","Revenge","Faith","Grief"],"a":1,"e":"'Liberty, however, had been a useless gift to me, had I not… at the same time awakened to revenge.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"About how long after his release does Victor go to the magistrate?","o":["A month","A year","A week","A day"],"a":0,"e":"'About a month after my release, I repaired to a criminal judge in the town.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Close reading","q":"How does Victor tell his story to the magistrate?","o":["In a written letter","Calmly, with exact dates","Weeping and shouting","Through a lawyer"],"a":1,"e":"'Briefly but with firmness and precision, marking the dates with accuracy and never deviating into invective.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Theme","q":"When does the magistrate's disbelief return?","o":["When asked to act","When Victor raises his voice","When he hears of Clerval","When he sees no proof"],"a":0,"e":"He listened with 'half kind of belief… but when he was called upon to act officially… the whole tide of his incredulity returned'."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Character","q":"What does Victor suggest the creature can be hunted like?","o":["A bear","A chamois","A fox","A wolf"],"a":1,"e":"'He may be hunted like the chamois and destroyed as a beast of prey.' A chamois is an Alpine goat."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Quote","q":"Victor admits revenge is 'a vice'. What does he call it?","o":["A duty owed to his murdered family and friends","His soul's only passion","A sin he will confess to the priest before he dies","A burden laid on him by God as punishment"],"a":1,"e":"'While I allow it to be a vice, I confess that it is the devouring and only passion of my soul.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Connection","q":"Victor's 'haughty fierceness' is compared to that of whom?","o":["Milton's Satan","The ancient Romans","Napoleon's soldiers","The martyrs of old"],"a":3,"e":"'That haughty fierceness which the martyrs of old are said to have possessed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Irony","q":"'Man, how ignorant art thou in thy pride of wisdom!' Why is this ironic from Victor?","o":["The magistrate is uneducated","His own pride caused all this","Victor is quoting the creature","The magistrate is younger"],"a":1,"e":"Victor's own 'pride of wisdom' made the creature. He is accusing the magistrate of his own fault."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Connection","q":"The magistrate soothes Victor 'as a nurse does a child'. Which earlier character also dismissed Victor's truth as delirium?","o":["His father in Paris","Clerval at Ingolstadt","Kirwin in Ireland","Elizabeth at Geneva"],"a":0,"e":"Alphonse treated Victor's confession as 'the offspring of delirium'."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor says revenge let him be 'calculating and calm'. What would he otherwise have faced?","o":["Delirium or death","Prison and disgrace","Poverty and exile","Madness and the asylum"],"a":0,"e":"'It moulded my feelings and allowed me to be calculating and calm at periods when otherwise delirium or death would have been my portion.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What does Victor take with him when he leaves Geneva?","o":["Elizabeth's portrait and her last letter","His chemical instruments and Victor's journal","His father's old sword and a pair of pistols","Money and her jewels"],"a":3,"e":"'I provided myself with a sum of money, together with a few jewels which had belonged to my mother.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Setting","q":"Where does Victor make his vow of revenge?","o":["On Montanvert","At the inn at Evian","At his family's graves","In the Geneva court"],"a":2,"e":"'The cemetery where William, Elizabeth, and my father reposed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"By which of these does Victor NOT swear his vow?","o":["His love for Elizabeth","The sacred earth","Night and its spirits","The shades of the dead"],"a":0,"e":"'By the sacred earth on which I kneel, by the shades that wander near me, by the deep and eternal grief… and by thee, O Night.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"How is Victor's vow answered?","o":["A ghost's voice","A loud fiendish laugh","A clap of thunder","Total silence"],"a":1,"e":"'I was answered through the stillness of night by a loud and fiendish laugh.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'I am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have determined to live.' Why is the creature 'satisfied'?","o":["He wants to be forgiven","Victor will suffer on","The chase is over","Victor will kill him"],"a":1,"e":"A living, pursuing Victor keeps suffering, which is exactly what the creature wants."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"Where does the creature hide to escape across the sea?","o":["A ship bound for England","A ship to the Black Sea","A fishing boat to Ireland","A barge on the Rhine"],"a":1,"e":"'I saw the fiend enter by night and hide himself in a vessel bound for the Black Sea.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Setting","q":"Through which regions does Victor follow the creature?","o":["India and Persia","Tartary and Russia","Egypt and Arabia","Spain and Portugal"],"a":1,"e":"'Amidst the wilds of Tartary and Russia… I have ever followed in his track.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Irony","q":"Why does the creature sometimes leave marks to guide Victor?","o":["Out of pity for Victor's suffering","So Victor won't give up","To lead him into a trap among the crevasses","To lure him towards the Russian police"],"a":1,"e":"He 'feared that if I lost all trace of him I should despair and die'. He needs Victor alive to suffer."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Irony","q":"Victor credits 'spirits' for the food left for him. Who really left it?","o":["Probably the creature","Walton's sailors","Victor himself","The peasants"],"a":0,"e":"He thinks it 'set there by the spirits', but the creature writes, 'You will find… a dead hare; eat and be refreshed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Character","q":"How does Victor win over the villagers on his route?","o":["By telling stories of Geneva by the fire","Money and shared game","By threatening them with his pistols","By treating their sick with medicine"],"a":1,"e":"He 'gained the friendship of the villagers by distributing' money and gave them food he had killed."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"When is Victor happy during the pursuit?","o":["Never","When he kills game","Only in sleep","When he sees the creature"],"a":2,"e":"'It was during sleep alone that I could taste joy… in sleep I saw my friends, my wife, and my beloved country.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Close reading","q":"In his dreams, revenge seems 'a task enjoined by heaven'. What happens to his hatred?","o":["It doubles","It turns on himself","It turns into pity","It dies, briefly"],"a":3,"e":"'At such moments vengeance, that burned within me, died in my heart.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"Where does the creature leave written messages?","o":["On tree bark and stone","In letters to Geneva","On Victor's tent","On Walton's ship"],"a":0,"e":"'He left marks in writing on the barks of the trees or cut in stone.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'You live, and my power is complete.' What is the creature's logic?","o":["He has won the race","Victor's life is his torment","Victor must kill him","They should be friends"],"a":1,"e":"Victor alive and suffering is the creature's victory."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"Why does the creature lead Victor north?","o":["He is lost and has no idea where he is going","Victor can't bear cold","He wants to find a new home among the ice","He hopes to meet Walton's ship and get help"],"a":1,"e":"'I seek the everlasting ices of the north, where you will feel the misery of cold and frost, to which I am impassive.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'Wrap yourself in furs and provide food… your sufferings will satisfy my everlasting hatred.' What tone is this?","o":["Frightened","Pleading","Mocking","Grieving"],"a":2,"e":"It is a taunt disguised as advice, from the 'scoffing devil'."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Allusion","q":"Victor compares seeing the frozen ocean to which ancient sight?","o":["The Greeks seeing the sea","Columbus seeing America","Moses seeing the promised land","Caesar crossing the Rubicon"],"a":0,"e":"'The Greeks wept for joy when they beheld the Mediterranean from the hills of Asia.' This is Xenophon's 'Thalatta!'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What lets Victor start gaining on the creature?","o":["A boat","A shortcut through the mountains","A sledge and dogs","A faster horse"],"a":2,"e":"'I had procured a sledge and dogs and thus traversed the snows with inconceivable speed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What did the creature take from the seaside cottage?","o":["Their only child, as a hostage","Winter food and dogs","Their furs, money and guns","Their fishing boat and nets"],"a":1,"e":"'He had carried off their store of winter food… he had seized on a numerous drove of trained dogs.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Character","q":"How was the creature armed, according to the villagers?","o":["Only his hands","A spear","A gun and many pistols","A sword and shield"],"a":2,"e":"'A gigantic monster… armed with a gun and many pistols.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"About how long had Victor been on the ice when he sighted the creature's sledge?","o":["Three days","Three months","Three weeks","A year"],"a":2,"e":"'I should guess that I had passed three weeks in this journey.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"How close does Victor get before the ice breaks?","o":["A day's journey","Within arm's reach","Ten miles","Under a mile"],"a":3,"e":"'I beheld my enemy at no more than a mile distant.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Close reading","q":"What does Victor do on seeing the sledge in the distance?","o":["Prays for strength","Fires his pistol","Turns back","Weeps aloud with joy"],"a":3,"e":"'Warm tears filled my eyes… until, giving way to the emotions that oppressed me, I wept aloud.' Hatred has become his only joy."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"How does Victor reach Walton's ship?","o":["A sailor rows out","Swimming","Oars made from his sledge","The dogs pull him across"],"a":2,"e":"'I quickly destroyed part of my sledge to construct oars.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What would Victor have done if Walton's ship had been sailing south?","o":["Fought the sailors","Gone aboard anyway","Stayed on the ice","Asked them to wait"],"a":2,"e":"'I had determined, if you were going southwards, still to trust myself to the mercy of the seas.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'He is eloquent and persuasive… trust him not.' What does Victor ask Walton to do if he meets the creature?","o":["Thrust a sword into his heart","Take him to England","Listen to his story","Make him a companion"],"a":0,"e":"'Call on the names of William, Justine, Clerval, Elizabeth, my father… and thrust your sword into his heart.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Structure","q":"What two things convince Walton the story is true?","o":["The journal and the portrait","The letters and the sighting","Victor's tears and eloquence","The sailors' testimony"],"a":1,"e":"'The letters of Felix and Safie… and the apparition of the monster seen from our ship.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Character","q":"What does Victor refuse to tell Walton?","o":["Elizabeth's last words","Where the creature is","Where he was born","How he made the creature"],"a":3,"e":"'Sometimes I endeavoured to gain from Frankenstein the particulars of his creature's formation, but on this point he was impenetrable.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Structure","q":"What did Victor do to Walton's notes on his story?","o":["Signed every page as a true account","Burned them in the cabin stove","Corrected and expanded","Ignored them as a sailor's foolishness"],"a":2,"e":"He 'corrected and augmented them… principally in giving the life and spirit to the conversations'. This affects how reliable the narrative is."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Allusion","q":"'Like the archangel who aspired to omnipotence, I am chained in an eternal hell.' Whom does Victor compare himself to?","o":["Prometheus","Adam","Lucifer's angels","Satan"],"a":3,"e":"Satan, who aspired to God's power, as in Paradise Lost."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'I trod heaven in my thoughts.' What is Victor describing?","o":["His dreams during the creation","His religious faith","His love for Elizabeth","Climbing Mont Blanc"],"a":0,"e":"He means his reveries 'while the work was incomplete', the intoxication of ambition."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"Why does Victor say childhood friends have a special power?","o":["They are related to you","They share your secrets","They know your true motives","They are more beautiful"],"a":2,"e":"They 'can judge of our actions with more certain conclusions as to the integrity of our motives'."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What is Walton's fear on 2 September?","o":["A mutiny","Running out of food","The creature boarding","Losing his maps"],"a":0,"e":"'I almost dread a mutiny caused by this despair.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Language","q":"Victor makes the sailors believe the icebergs are what?","o":["Mole-hills","Clouds","Mountains of glass","Castles"],"a":0,"e":"They 'believe these vast mountains of ice are mole-hills which will vanish before the resolutions of man'."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What do the sailors' deputation demand?","o":["More food and rum","To abandon the ship","A promise to turn south","Victor's removal"],"a":2,"e":"They insist 'that if the vessel should be freed I would instantly direct my course southwards'."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'Be men, or be more than men.' What irony lies in Victor urging this?","o":["He is himself a coward who fled the ice","The sailors are only boys, not men","Overreaching ruined him","He is simply repeating Walton's words"],"a":2,"e":"Overreaching the limits of humanity is exactly what destroyed Victor."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Language","q":"Victor says the ice 'is mutable and cannot withstand you'. Which earlier word does this recall?","o":["'Mutability'","'Paradise'","'Prometheus'","'Sublime'"],"a":0,"e":"It echoes Shelley's 'Mutability', quoted on Montanvert."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"On what date does Walton agree to turn back?","o":["26 August","7 September","2 September","12 September"],"a":1,"e":"'September 7th. The die is cast; I have consented to return.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Character","q":"How does Walton describe his own decision to return?","o":["A relief","A wise act of mercy","Hopes blasted by cowardice","Victor's victory"],"a":2,"e":"'Thus are my hopes blasted by cowardice and indecision; I come back ignorant and disappointed.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Character","q":"What happens when Victor tries to leave his bed to pursue the creature?","o":["He reaches the deck","He falls overboard","He shoots at the sailors","He faints"],"a":3,"e":"'He endeavoured to spring from the bed, but the exertion was too great for him; he fell back and fainted.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"On his deathbed, does Victor think his conduct blamable?","o":["No","He refuses to say","Only the creation itself","Yes, entirely"],"a":0,"e":"'I have been occupied in examining my past conduct; nor do I find it blamable.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"Which duty does the dying Victor rank highest?","o":["To his own species","To his creature","To his family alone","To science"],"a":0,"e":"'My duties towards the beings of my own species had greater claims… I did right in refusing.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'Seek happiness in tranquillity and avoid ambition.' What does Victor immediately add?","o":["Science is evil","Never go north","Return to Margaret","Another may succeed"],"a":3,"e":"'Yet why do I say this? I have myself been blasted in these hopes, yet another may succeed.' His final moral is contradictory."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Close reading","q":"How does Victor die?","o":["Out on the ice, still chasing the creature","Raving and cursing the creature's name","In his sleep, alone in the cabin","Smiling, pressing Walton's hand"],"a":3,"e":"'He pressed my hand feebly, and his eyes closed for ever, while the irradiation of a gentle smile passed away from his lips.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What alerts Walton that someone is in Victor's cabin?","o":["A crash of glass","A hoarse, human voice","The dogs barking","A scream"],"a":1,"e":"'A sound as of a human voice, but hoarser; it comes from the cabin where the remains of Frankenstein still lie.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Language","q":"What does the creature's hand look like to Walton?","o":["Like marble","Like a child's","Like a bear's paw","Like a mummy's"],"a":3,"e":"'One vast hand was extended, in colour and apparent texture like that of a mummy.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'That is also my victim!' What does the creature call Victor next?","o":["My enemy","Generous and self-devoted","My father","Cruel and selfish"],"a":1,"e":"'Oh, Frankenstein! Generous and self-devoted being!'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Character","q":"What stops Walton from killing the creature as Victor asked?","o":["The sailors begging him not to try","He had no weapon in the cabin with him","Curiosity and compassion","Fear of the creature's gigantic size"],"a":2,"e":"'A mixture of curiosity and compassion.' This is the same mix that made Victor listen."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"According to the creature, who suffered more during the murders?","o":["The creature","Elizabeth","Clerval","Victor"],"a":0,"e":"'He suffered not in the consummation of the deed. Oh! Not the ten-thousandth portion of the anguish that was mine.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Character","q":"What turned the creature's pity for Victor back into vengeance?","o":["Victor's illness","Victor hoping to marry","Victor's threats","Victor's escape from Ireland"],"a":1,"e":"'When I discovered that he… dared to hope for happiness… impotent envy and bitter indignation filled me.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'I was the slave, not the master, of an impulse.' What did the creature tell Victor in Chapter 20?","o":["'I am your master'","'I am your slave'","'I am your equal'","'You are my father'"],"a":0,"e":"There he said 'You are my creator, but I am your master'. Now he admits he too is enslaved."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Allusion","q":"'Evil thenceforth became my good.' This echoes which character?","o":["Milton's Satan","Werter","Victor","Prometheus"],"a":0,"e":"It echoes Satan in Paradise Lost: 'Evil be thou my good.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Language","q":"Walton accuses the creature of throwing 'a torch into a pile of buildings'. What does he mean?","o":["He is an arsonist","He destroyed Geneva","He mourns ruin he caused","He set fire to the cottage"],"a":2,"e":"'When they are consumed, you sit among the ruins and lament the fall. Hypocritical fiend!'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"Which two humans does the creature say deserve blame too?","o":["Alphonse and Ernest","Walton and his crew","Felix and the rustic","Kirwin and the nurse"],"a":2,"e":"'Why do you not hate Felix… Why do you not execrate the rustic who sought to destroy the saviour of his child?'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Quote","q":"'Am I to be thought the only criminal, when all humankind sinned against me?' What is the creature's claim?","o":["Victor alone is guilty","God is to blame","He is entirely innocent","Society shares the guilt"],"a":3,"e":"He admits his crimes but insists humanity's cruelty made him."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Language","q":"What harsh word does the creature use for himself?","o":["A miracle","A machine","An abortion","A ghost"],"a":2,"e":"'I, the miserable and the abandoned, am an abortion, to be spurned at, and kicked, and trampled on.' It is a failed, unfinished birth."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Allusion","q":"'The fallen angel becomes a malignant devil. Yet even that enemy of God and man had…' what?","o":["Friends in his desolation","A heaven to return to","A creator who loved him","Victory over God"],"a":0,"e":"'Yet even that enemy of God and man had friends and associates in his desolation; I am alone.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"How does the creature plan to die?","o":["Throwing himself into the freezing sea","A funeral pyre in the far north","Asking Walton to run him through","Starving slowly on the ice raft"],"a":1,"e":"'I shall collect my funeral pile and consume to ashes this miserable frame.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"Why does the creature want his body burned to ashes?","o":["So no one can copy him","So no one fears him","So Victor's ghost can rest","So he can be reborn"],"a":0,"e":"'That its remains may afford no light to any curious and unhallowed wretch who would create such another as I have been.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Connection","q":"The creature's funeral pyre returns to which of his first discoveries?","o":["The moon","Language","Reading","Fire"],"a":3,"e":"He first found fire in the forest near Ingolstadt; he will end in it."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Close reading","q":"What does the creature say he would once have wept to lose?","o":["The love of his creator, Victor Frankenstein","The books he found in the leather portmanteau","Summer and birdsong","The company of the De Lacey family"],"a":2,"e":"'When I felt the cheering warmth of summer and heard the rustling of the leaves and the warbling of the birds… I should have wept to die.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Structure","q":"How does the novel end?","o":["The creature lost in darkness","The creature's death in flames","Walton reaching England","Victor's funeral"],"a":0,"e":"'He was soon borne away by the waves and lost in darkness and distance.' We never see him die."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Who was Mary Shelley's mother?","o":["Hester Thrale, Johnson's friend","Jane Austen's cousin Eliza","Lady Caroline Lamb, the novelist","Mary Wollstonecraft"],"a":3,"e":"Wollstonecraft wrote A Vindication of the Rights of Woman and died days after Mary's birth."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"To whom was the 1818 edition dedicated?","o":["Percy Bysshe Shelley","William Godwin","Lord Byron","Samuel Taylor Coleridge"],"a":1,"e":"It was dedicated to her father, the radical philosopher William Godwin, author of Political Justice."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"How was the first edition published in 1818?","o":["Under Percy Shelley's name","Under Mary Godwin's name","Anonymously","As a serial in a magazine"],"a":2,"e":"It appeared anonymously in three volumes. Many readers assumed Percy Shelley wrote it."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Which volcanic eruption caused the cold 'year without a summer' of 1816?","o":["Mount Tambora","Krakatoa","Etna","Vesuvius"],"a":0,"e":"Tambora erupted in 1815. The gloomy summer kept the Diodati party indoors telling ghost stories."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Which member of the Diodati party wrote 'The Vampyre' from the same contest?","o":["Percy Shelley","Lord Byron himself","Claire Clairmont","John Polidori"],"a":3,"e":"Byron's doctor Polidori wrote 'The Vampyre' (1819), from a fragment by Byron."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Which scientist's experiments made frogs' legs twitch with electricity?","o":["Humphry Davy","Benjamin Franklin","Luigi Galvani","Isaac Newton"],"a":2,"e":"'Galvanism' was the electrical science that seemed to promise reanimation."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Giovanni Aldini publicly 'galvanised' what in London in 1803?","o":["A drowned child","A live horse","A sheep's heart","A hanged criminal's body"],"a":3,"e":"Aldini made a hanged man's face and limbs move, a real event behind the novel's fears and hopes."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"What was added to the novel in its 1831 edition?","o":["A sequel about the creature","A new final chapter","Illustrations by Byron","An introduction by Mary Shelley"],"a":3,"e":"In the 1831 introduction she tells the story of the 'waking dream' that inspired the book."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Whose initials match 'Margaret Walton Saville' (M.W.S.)?","o":["Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley","Mary Wollstonecraft Godwin","Margaret Wordsworth Shelley","Mary Walton Seymour"],"a":0,"e":"The letter-reader shares the author's initials, a playful signature inside the frame."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"The creature's story sits at the centre of the novel. What does this placing do?","o":["Puts his voice at the heart","Lets Walton judge him","Makes Victor the villain","Hides him from the reader"],"a":0,"e":"Chapters 11–16 sit in the middle of the nesting, where readers hear the 'monster' speak."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Which document does Victor show Walton as proof of his tale?","o":["His laboratory notebook","Elizabeth's last letter","Copies of Safie's letters","The female creature's body"],"a":2,"e":"'The letters of Felix and Safie, which he showed me.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Which story is nested deepest in the novel?","o":["Victor's childhood","Walton's voyage","The creature's education","Safie's mother's story"],"a":3,"e":"Walton > Victor > creature > De Lacey history > Safie's letters > her mother. It is the innermost ring."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Why is the novel called epistolary?","o":["It is told in diary entries","It has several authors","It is framed by letters","It is written as a play"],"a":2,"e":"Walton's letters to Margaret frame the whole novel."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Who does Victor tell the whole story to before Walton?","o":["His father","Elizabeth","A Geneva magistrate","Mr Kirwin"],"a":2,"e":"He 'related my history briefly but with firmness' to the criminal judge, who did not act."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"What name does the creature have in the novel?","o":["Victor","Adam","None","Frankenstein"],"a":2,"e":"He is never named. The popular confusion with 'Frankenstein' came later, through stage and film."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Language","q":"Which word does Victor NOT use for his creation?","o":["'Fiend'","'Wretch'","'Adam'","'Dæmon'"],"a":2,"e":"Victor uses dæmon, fiend, wretch, devil and monster. Only the creature mentions 'Adam'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which statement best fits the creature's own account of himself?","o":["Born good, made evil","Evil only by choice","Evil from creation","Never truly evil"],"a":0,"e":"'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Both Walton and Victor are driven by what?","o":["A desire for revenge","Love of a woman","Need for money","Ambition for discovery"],"a":3,"e":"Walton seeks 'the dominion I should acquire'; Victor seeks to 'pioneer a new way'. Their ambition doubles."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"How does Walton's ending differ from Victor's?","o":["He continues north","He dies on the ice","He builds a creature","He turns back"],"a":3,"e":"Walton gives up his voyage at his crew's demand, avoiding Victor's fate, though 'blasted by cowardice'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Who, like the creature, learns a new language as an outsider at the cottage?","o":["Agatha","Safie","Justine","Felix"],"a":1,"e":"Both learn from Felix's lessons, but beauty lets only Safie in."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Justine and Victor are both put on trial. What is the difference?","o":["He confesses; she doesn't","She's executed; he's freed","Both are freed","Both are executed"],"a":1,"e":"The innocent servant dies; the guilty creator, with an alibi, walks free."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Who nurses Victor through his first fever after the creation?","o":["Justine","Clerval","Elizabeth","Alphonse"],"a":1,"e":"Henry 'was my only nurse'. Nursing is a recurring act of love."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which character dies of an illness caught while nursing someone?","o":["Caroline","Justine","Agatha","Elizabeth"],"a":0,"e":"She caught scarlet fever nursing Elizabeth."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Symbol","q":"What is usually shining when the creature appears at a window?","o":["The sun","The moon","A candle","Lightning"],"a":1,"e":"The moon lights him at Ingolstadt, the Orkneys and Evian: 'by the light of the moon'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Symbol","q":"Windows recur in the novel. What do they often represent?","o":["Seeing in, shut out","Wealth and comfort","Escape to freedom","Science and clarity"],"a":0,"e":"The creature watches the De Laceys through a chink and grins through windows: he is always outside looking in."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Symbol","q":"Fire both warms and burns the creature. What does it stand for?","o":["Anger, which destroys","Knowledge, which helps and harms","God, who punishes","Love, which comforts"],"a":1,"e":"'How strange… that the same cause should produce such opposite effects!' This is Prometheus's gift."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Symbol","q":"What does light usually represent in the novel?","o":["Knowledge and discovery","Wealth and power","Death and grief","Love and marriage"],"a":0,"e":"Victor seeks 'a torrent of light into our dark world'. Walton heads for a land of 'eternal light'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"Where does the novel begin and end?","o":["Montanvert","Ingolstadt","The Arctic","Geneva"],"a":2,"e":"Walton's frozen frame opens and closes the novel. Ice is the setting of isolation and obsession."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"Which landscape style dominates Victor's darkest moments?","o":["Gentle farmland and vineyards","Busy cities and universities","The sublime: Alps, glaciers, ice","Calm seas and beaches"],"a":2,"e":"Montanvert, Mont Blanc and the Arctic are vast, awe-inspiring and dangerous."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Which literary movement does the novel's love of nature and feeling belong to?","o":["Modernism","Romanticism","Neoclassicism","Realism"],"a":1,"e":"It is Romantic: the sublime, emotion, the individual, nature's healing power, and quotes from Wordsworth and Coleridge."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Which genre's features—graves, monsters, storms, dread—does it also use?","o":["Satire","Pastoral","Gothic","Picaresque"],"a":2,"e":"It is a Gothic novel and often called the first science fiction novel."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What does the novel suggest about appearance?","o":["Beauty reflects goodness","It doesn't matter at all","Ugliness reveals evil","People judge by it, unjustly"],"a":3,"e":"Only the blind De Lacey judges the creature fairly; sighted people all recoil."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which character judges the creature fairly?","o":["De Lacey","Felix","Walton","William"],"a":0,"e":"Being blind, he hears 'something in your words which persuades me that you are sincere'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What is Victor's greatest failure, according to many readers?","o":["Abandoning his creation","Building it at all","Robbing graves","Studying Agrippa"],"a":0,"e":"He flees the bedside; the creature calls himself 'abandoned' and 'deserted'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What sort of justice does the novel show?","o":["Harsh but true","Divine and certain","Fair and reliable","Flawed and prejudiced"],"a":3,"e":"Justine is executed, the Turk wrongly condemned, the De Laceys exiled, Victor nearly hanged."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor finally decide about his duty?","o":["His species comes first","His science comes first","His family comes first","His creature comes first"],"a":0,"e":"'My duties towards the beings of my own species had greater claims.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which family is presented as the ideal of loving poverty?","o":["The Frankensteins","The Clervals","The Moritzes","The De Laceys"],"a":3,"e":"They share food and care despite poverty. The creature calls them his 'protectors'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Which three characters are killed by strangling?","o":["Justine, William, Alphonse","Caroline, Clerval, Victor","William, Clerval, Elizabeth","Elizabeth, Justine, Ernest"],"a":2,"e":"Each bears 'the mark of fingers' on the neck. Justine is hanged; Alphonse dies of grief."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Who is the only surviving member of Victor's immediate family?","o":["Elizabeth","William","Ernest","No one"],"a":2,"e":"Ernest lives but vanishes from the story after Chapter 23."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What does the novel suggest about solitude?","o":["It brings wisdom and peace","It cures grief","It is needed for genius","It breeds misery and crime"],"a":3,"e":"'My vices are the children of a forced solitude.' Victor's secret work also needs isolation."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"How do the creature and Victor become alike by the end?","o":["Both forgive each other","Both find peace","Both live for revenge","Both marry"],"a":2,"e":"They chase each other across the ice, each consumed by vengeance."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Allusion","q":"What is the novel's subtitle?","o":["A Romance of the North","The Modern Prometheus","The Man Who Made Life","A Tale of Terror"],"a":1,"e":"Prometheus stole fire for mankind and was punished; Victor seizes the 'spark of being'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Allusion","q":"Which epic does the creature read as 'a true history'?","o":["The Divine Comedy","The Aeneid","The Iliad","Paradise Lost"],"a":3,"e":"Milton's poem gives him the roles of Adam and Satan."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Allusion","q":"The 1818 epigraph begins 'Did I request thee, Maker, from my clay…'. Who speaks it in Milton?","o":["Eve","Adam","Satan","The Son"],"a":1,"e":"Adam complains to God, as the creature does to Victor."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Allusion","q":"Which Coleridge poem is quoted most in the novel?","o":["The Ancient Mariner","Christabel, the tale of the witch Geraldine","Frost at Midnight, written to his infant son","Kubla Khan, the dream of Xanadu"],"a":0,"e":"Walton mentions 'the land of mist and snow'; Victor walks 'like one who, on a lonely road'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Allusion","q":"What do Walton, the Mariner and Victor share?","o":["A love of chemistry and natural philosophy","A doomed voyage, a tale","A terror of fire and of lightning","A wife murdered on their wedding night"],"a":1,"e":"The Mariner tells his tale to a stranger; Victor tells his to Walton."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Language","q":"Who in the novel is most praised for eloquence?","o":["Felix","Ernest","The creature","Alphonse"],"a":2,"e":"Victor warns: 'He is eloquent and persuasive… trust him not.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Irony","q":"Victor warns Walton the creature is persuasive. Why is this ironic?","o":["Victor is a poor speaker","The creature cannot speak","Walton never meets the creature","Victor's own eloquence moves the crew"],"a":3,"e":"His own speech 'Be men, or be more than men' moves the sailors."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What is the novel's warning about knowledge?","o":["It can be dangerous","It always brings happiness","It belongs to God alone","It is only for men"],"a":0,"e":"'Learn from me… how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Who also learns that 'sorrow only increased with knowledge'?","o":["Elizabeth","The creature","Walton","Clerval"],"a":1,"e":"Like Victor, the creature finds knowledge brings pain."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What do the women in the novel tend to do?","o":["Lead the action","Become scientists","Suffer passively and die","Seek revenge"],"a":2,"e":"Caroline, Justine and Elizabeth die; Safie is the rare active exception."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Which female character acts most independently?","o":["Agatha","Elizabeth","Safie","Justine"],"a":2,"e":"She crosses Europe alone to find Felix, defying her father."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor's destruction of the female deny?","o":["A woman's own existence","Elizabeth's dowry","Safie's freedom","The creature's right to work"],"a":0,"e":"He destroys a being 'whose dispositions I was alike ignorant' before she can choose anything."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"In which city was Victor born?","o":["Geneva","Naples","Ingolstadt","Belrive"],"a":1,"e":"'I… was born at Naples.' He was raised in Geneva."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"What is Alphonse's public role?","o":["Banker","Professor","Syndic","Magistrate"],"a":2,"e":"William says: 'My papa is a syndic.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"At which university does Victor study?","o":["Ingolstadt","Paris","Oxford","Geneva"],"a":0,"e":"He leaves Geneva for 'the university of Ingolstadt'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Which professor first inspires modern science in Victor?","o":["Agrippa","Waldman","Krempe","Kirwin"],"a":1,"e":"M. Waldman's lecture sets him on his path. Krempe is 'squat' and unkind."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"What languages does Clerval study at Ingolstadt?","o":["Persian, Arabic and Sanskrit","German and English","French and Italian","Greek and Latin"],"a":0,"e":"He studies 'the Persian, Arabic, and Sanskrit languages'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Sequence","q":"Put these deaths in order.","o":["Clerval, William, Justine, Elizabeth","William, Clerval, Justine, Elizabeth","William, Justine, Clerval, Elizabeth","Justine, William, Elizabeth, Clerval"],"a":2,"e":"Then Alphonse dies of grief, then Victor on Walton's ship."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Sequence","q":"Which comes first in the story's timeline?","o":["Justine's trial","The creature's birth","Caroline's death","William's murder"],"a":2,"e":"Caroline dies before Victor leaves for Ingolstadt."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Sequence","q":"Which of these happens first?","o":["The creature saves a drowning girl","The creature kills William","Safie arrives at the cottage","The creature meets Victor on the glacier"],"a":2,"e":"Safie arrives (Ch13), he is rejected (Ch15), saves the girl and kills William (Ch16), then meets Victor (Ch10, told in flashback)."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Sequence","q":"Which of these happens last?","o":["Victor destroys the female","Victor meets Kirwin","The creature promises to go to South America","Clerval leaves Victor at Perth"],"a":1,"e":"The promise (Ch17), Perth (Ch19), the female's destruction (Ch20), then Kirwin (Ch21)."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor most often blame for his ruin?","o":["Destiny","Waldman","His own pride","His father"],"a":0,"e":"He speaks of 'destiny' and 'fate' rather than his choices."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which emotion drives both Victor and the creature at the end?","o":["Fear","Joy","Love","Revenge"],"a":3,"e":"Victor's 'devouring and only passion' mirrors the creature's 'insatiable thirst for vengeance'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature calls Victor 'my father, my creator'. What theme does this raise?","o":["Social class","Religious faith","Parental responsibility","Scientific method"],"a":2,"e":"Victor fails as a parent, a creator who abandons his 'child'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature says he ought to be Victor's Adam. Who does that make Victor?","o":["A fallen angel","An innocent victim","A failed God","A loving father"],"a":2,"e":"He creates life like God but withholds care."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What role does nature most often play for Victor?","o":["Irrelevant","Hostile and cruel","Something to conquer","Healing and calming"],"a":3,"e":"Mountains and lakes 'subdued and tranquillised' his grief."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"How does the novel present ambition?","o":["Dangerous, yet admired","Unimportant beside love and family","Wholly good, the mark of every great mind","Wholly evil, a sin to be punished by God"],"a":0,"e":"Victor warns Walton yet says 'another may succeed'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Language","q":"Which narrative technique does Victor use most to build dread?","o":["Dramatic dialogue","Shifting into the present tense","Flashbacks to Caroline","Foreshadowing with hindsight"],"a":3,"e":"'Little did I then expect…' He knows the ending as he narrates."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"What date is on Walton's first letter?","o":["11 December","5 September","26 August","18 May"],"a":0,"e":"'St. Petersburgh, Dec. 11th, 17—.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"Where does Walton write his first letter from?","o":["St Petersburg","Archangel","Geneva","London"],"a":0,"e":"'St. Petersburgh.' He later writes from Archangel."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"In which century is the story set?","o":["The sixteenth","The eighteenth","The nineteenth","The seventeenth"],"a":1,"e":"Letters are dated '17—'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which of these is a 'double' of Victor?","o":["Kirwin","Daniel Nugent","Ernest","Walton"],"a":3,"e":"Both are ambitious, isolated, and tell their stories in writing. Walton longs for a friend who 'would sympathise with me'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Elizabeth's line 'falsehood can look so like the truth' sums up which theme?","o":["Scientific progress","Religious doubt","Deceptive appearances","Romantic love"],"a":2,"e":"Innocence looks guilty (Justine, Victor); goodness looks monstrous (the creature)."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Who rescues Victor from the ice?","o":["Russian peasants","Clerval","The creature","Walton"],"a":3,"e":"He is found 'on a large fragment of ice' and taken aboard Walton's ship."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Who travels on a sledge pulled by dogs in Walton's first sighting?","o":["An Inuit hunter","The creature","Walton","Victor"],"a":1,"e":"'A being which had the shape of a man, but apparently of gigantic stature.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Language","q":"Who calls himself 'the slave, not the master' of an impulse?","o":["Felix","The creature","Victor","Walton"],"a":1,"e":"The creature says it over Victor's body."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Who says, 'I am content to suffer alone'?","o":["The creature","Victor","Walton","Elizabeth"],"a":0,"e":"The creature, at the end, gives up seeking sympathy."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What do the creature's final words promise?","o":["To burn himself","To kill Walton","To return to Geneva","To find a mate"],"a":0,"e":"He will 'ascend my funeral pile triumphantly'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Who among the creature's victims is a child?","o":["Elizabeth","Clerval","William","Justine"],"a":2,"e":"William, Victor's youngest brother, is the first victim."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which murder is indirect, through the law?","o":["William's","Clerval's","Justine's","Elizabeth's"],"a":2,"e":"The creature frames her by planting the portrait. She is hanged."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"In which two places does Victor do his creating?","o":["Ingolstadt and the Orkneys","Chamounix and Perth","Paris and London","Geneva and Oxford"],"a":0,"e":"His first lab is an Ingolstadt garret; the second is an Orkney hut."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Language","q":"What is Victor's laboratory called at Ingolstadt?","o":["A 'house of learning'","A 'chapel of light'","A 'workshop of filthy creation'","A 'temple of science'"],"a":2,"e":"'My workshop of filthy creation.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Close reading","q":"Victor once says he would have made 'a new species'. What does he imagine it would do?","o":["Destroy mankind","Rule Geneva","Bless him as its creator","Live in the Arctic"],"a":2,"e":"'A new species would bless me as its creator and source.' The reality is the opposite."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Irony","q":"Victor wanted to 'banish disease' and conquer death. What did his work cause?","o":["Immortality","A new university","A cure for fever","Many deaths"],"a":3,"e":"He hoped to 'renew life where death had apparently devoted the body to corruption'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"What material did Victor collect from charnel houses?","o":["Bones","Gold","Plants","Chemicals"],"a":0,"e":"'I collected bones from charnel-houses.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"How tall is the creature?","o":["Average height","About six feet","Ten feet","About eight feet"],"a":3,"e":"Victor made it 'about eight feet in height'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Language","q":"What colour is the creature's skin described as?","o":["Grey","Yellow","Green","Blue"],"a":1,"e":"'His yellow skin scarcely covered the work of muscles and arteries.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What did Victor choose for the creature's features?","o":["Beauty","Smallness","Strength","Ugliness"],"a":0,"e":"'I had selected his features as beautiful.' The beauty he chose became horror once the creature was alive."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Which science-fiction 'first' is the novel often credited with?","o":["The first machine that travels through time","The first mechanical man, or robot","A scientist creating life","The first journey to the Moon by balloon"],"a":2,"e":"Life is made by experiment, not magic, which is why critics call it the first true science fiction."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"What finally ends Victor's life?","o":["A pistol shot","Exhaustion and illness","Drowning","The creature's attack"],"a":1,"e":"He dies aboard Walton's ship, worn out by the pursuit."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Why does the creature mourn over Victor's body?","o":["Joy at his death","Fear of Walton","To mock him","Grief and remorse"],"a":3,"e":"He calls Victor 'generous and self-devoted' and asks for pardon."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which word best describes the creature's feelings for Victor at the end?","o":["Pure hatred","Indifferent","Loving","Conflicted"],"a":3,"e":"He hates him, mourns him, pities him and condemns himself."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I shall commit my thoughts to paper, it is true; but that is a poor medium for the communication of feeling.' Who writes this?","o":["Victor","Elizabeth","The creature","Walton"],"a":3,"e":"Walton laments he has 'no friend', a longing Victor will fill and the creature never can."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I am by birth a Genevese.' Who says this?","o":["Clerval","Elizabeth","Justine","Victor"],"a":3,"e":"These words open Victor's own narrative in Chapter 1."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'Life and death appeared to me ideal bounds, which I should first break through.' Who thinks this?","o":["Waldman","The creature","Walton","Victor"],"a":3,"e":"Victor's ambition at Ingolstadt: to 'pour a torrent of light into our dark world'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'The modern masters promise very little.' Who says this?","o":["Alphonse","Krempe","Victor","Waldman"],"a":3,"e":"Waldman's lecture contrasts the 'ancient teachers' who 'promised impossibilities'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I beheld the wretch—the miserable monster whom I had created.' When does Victor see this?","o":["On the glacier","At William's murder scene","Waking after the creation","In the Orkneys"],"a":2,"e":"He wakes from his nightmare to find the creature holding up the bed curtain."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I seemed to have lost all soul or sensation but for this one pursuit.' Who says it?","o":["The creature","Victor","Walton","Clerval"],"a":1,"e":"He describes his total absorption while building the creature."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'A selfish pursuit had cramped and narrowed me, until your gentleness and affection warmed and opened my senses.' Whom is Victor praising?","o":["Clerval","Elizabeth","Walton","Waldman"],"a":0,"e":"He says it to Henry on their spring walk after his recovery."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'Everywhere I see bliss, from which I alone am irrevocably excluded.' Who says it?","o":["The creature","Felix","Victor","Walton"],"a":0,"e":"He says it on Montanvert as he begins his plea."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'Remember that I am thy creature.' What does the creature say next?","o":["'Forgive me'","'I will destroy thee'","'I ought to be thy Adam'","'I am thy master'"],"a":2,"e":"'Remember that I am thy creature; I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I will be with you on your wedding-night.' In which setting is this threat made?","o":["An Orkney hut","The Montanvert hut","Victor's Ingolstadt rooms","The Geneva cemetery"],"a":0,"e":"It is said on the Orkney island after Victor destroys the female."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'You are my creator, but I am your master.' In which setting?","o":["An Orkney hut","A Geneva prison","Walton's ship","The sea of ice"],"a":0,"e":"The creature says it after the female is destroyed."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I am alone and miserable; man will not associate with me.' What does the creature immediately demand?","o":["A home in Geneva","Victor's death","A companion of his species","Revenge on Felix"],"a":2,"e":"'One as deformed and horrible as myself would not deny herself to me.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I may die, but first you, my tyrant and tormentor, shall curse the sun.' Who speaks?","o":["The creature","Walton","Victor","Felix"],"a":0,"e":"It is the creature's threat in the Orkneys."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'Seek happiness in tranquillity and avoid ambition.' To whom is this said?","o":["Walton","The creature","Ernest","Clerval"],"a":0,"e":"These are Victor's dying words to Walton."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I am a blasted tree.' Where is Victor when he says this?","o":["Geneva","The Orkneys","Oxford","Ingolstadt"],"a":2,"e":"He says it during the tour of England, near Oxford."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'Learn from me… how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge.' To whom is Victor speaking?","o":["The creature","Walton","Clerval","His father"],"a":1,"e":"He interrupts his story to warn Walton."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.' What does the creature ask next?","o":["'Follow me'","'Forgive me'","'Kill me now'","'Make me happy'"],"a":3,"e":"'Make me happy, and I shall again be virtuous.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Quote","q":"'Hateful day when I received life!' What has the creature just read?","o":["Werter","Victor's journal","Paradise Lost","Safie's letters"],"a":1,"e":"He has just read 'your journal of the four months that preceded my creation'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Victor and the creature both describe themselves as 'bearing a hell within'. In which two chapters?","o":["8 and 16","1 and 24","12 and 20","5 and 10"],"a":0,"e":"Victor after Justine's trial (8); the creature after the cottage (16)."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Who speaks of 'the bitter gall of envy'?","o":["Victor","Walton","Elizabeth","The creature"],"a":3,"e":"He says it about watching the cottagers' bliss, like Satan."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"When the creature first comes to Victor's bedside, what lights the scene?","o":["Moonlight through the shutters","A flash of lightning over the town","A candle Victor had left burning","The first grey light of dawn"],"a":0,"e":"'By the dim and yellow light of the moon, as it forced its way through the window shutters.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Which two scenes both feature an innocent condemned by a court?","o":["Elizabeth and Safie","Victor and Walton","Clerval and Felix","Justine and the Turk"],"a":3,"e":"Justine (Ch8) and Safie's father (Ch14) both face flagrant injustice."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Which two characters both lose their mothers?","o":["Felix and Ernest","Elizabeth and Justine","Clerval and Walton","Victor and Safie"],"a":3,"e":"Caroline dies; Safie's mother 'died, but her lessons were indelibly impressed'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Elizabeth and Justine were both taken in by which family?","o":["The De Laceys","The Moritzes","The Clervals","The Frankensteins"],"a":3,"e":"Caroline adopted Elizabeth; Justine came as a servant 'loved… as if she were her own'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Connection","q":"Victor's dream after the creation turns Elizabeth into what?","o":["A statue","A child","Caroline's corpse","The creature"],"a":2,"e":"'I thought that I held the corpse of my dead mother in my arms.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"Victor's first nightmare joins Elizabeth with death. When does this come true?","o":["Caroline's funeral","Clerval's murder","The wedding night","Justine's trial"],"a":2,"e":"He dreams of kissing Elizabeth and her lips becoming 'livid with the hue of death'. She dies on the wedding night."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which family member dies of grief, not violence?","o":["William","Alphonse","Caroline","Ernest"],"a":1,"e":"He 'could not live under the horrors that were accumulated around him'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which character's death is caused by an illness, not the creature?","o":["Elizabeth","Caroline","Clerval","William"],"a":1,"e":"Caroline dies of scarlet fever before the creature exists."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature tells Walton 'I have murdered the lovely and the helpless'. Which victim does 'helpless' most fit?","o":["Alphonse","Victor","William","Clerval"],"a":2,"e":"A child is the most helpless; he 'grasped to death his throat who never injured me'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which institution most clearly fails both Justine and the creature?","o":["The army","The church choir","The law","The university"],"a":2,"e":"Courts condemn Justine and 'the sanguinary laws of man' teach the creature mischief."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Who is the creature's 'first victim', in his own words?","o":["Justine","Felix","Clerval","William"],"a":3,"e":"'You belong then to my enemy… you shall be my first victim.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Which of Victor's friends is most like his former self?","o":["Kirwin","Walton","Clerval","Ernest"],"a":2,"e":"'In Clerval I saw the image of my former self.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"What 'great and sudden change' does Victor say is most painful?","o":["His mother's death","Elizabeth's death","His first fever","Leaving Geneva"],"a":1,"e":"He says this on the lake the morning after the wedding night."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"In which country is Clerval murdered?","o":["Ireland","Switzerland","England","Scotland"],"a":0,"e":"His body is found on the Irish shore where Victor lands."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"In which country does the creature watch the De Laceys?","o":["France","Switzerland","Germany","Italy"],"a":2,"e":"'They found a miserable asylum in the cottage in Germany.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"Where does Victor meet the creature for their long talk?","o":["Montanvert","The Jura","Chamounix","Belrive"],"a":0,"e":"They go to 'the hut upon the mountain' after crossing the sea of ice."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Plot","q":"How many times does Victor fall seriously ill after the creation?","o":["Only at the end","Several times","Never","Once"],"a":1,"e":"After the creation, after Clerval's death, after his father's death and at the end."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Plot","q":"Whom does Victor promise to tell the secret to, but never does?","o":["Elizabeth","His father, on the journey from Paris","Clerval, before leaving him at Perth","Mr Kirwin, once he is freed in Ireland"],"a":0,"e":"He promises to tell her 'the day after our marriage', but she dies first."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Plot","q":"Which of these characters never sees the creature?","o":["William","Felix","Alphonse","Walton"],"a":2,"e":"Walton sees him over Victor's body; William and Felix see him and react with horror. Alphonse never does."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor consider worse than his own death?","o":["Losing Elizabeth","Poverty","Shame","Prison"],"a":0,"e":"'Death was no evil to me if the loss of Elizabeth were balanced with it.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Irony","q":"Victor calls the living creature 'the demoniacal corpse'. Why is 'corpse' apt?","o":["It had already died once in the lab","It was buried the following day","It never moved again after that night","It was built from corpses"],"a":3,"e":"He assembled it from the dead, collecting 'bones from charnel-houses'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which feeling does Victor NOT show toward the creature at some point?","o":["Hatred","Horror","Love","Compassion"],"a":2,"e":"He feels horror, hatred, brief pity on the glacier, but never love."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Close reading","q":"The creature says his heart was 'fashioned to be susceptible of love and sympathy'. What does 'fashioned' remind us of?","o":["He is fashionable","He was made","He is French","He is a tailor"],"a":1,"e":"He was literally fashioned by Victor's hands."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which creature act shows goodness after the De Laceys reject him?","o":["Saving the drowning girl","Sparing Felix","Helping Victor","Feeding William"],"a":0,"e":"He rescues her 'with extreme labour' and is shot for it."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What reward does the creature get for each good deed?","o":["Friendship","Violence or rejection","Forgiveness","Money and thanks"],"a":1,"e":"He gets stones from the village, Felix's stick, a bullet from the rustic."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which of these does the creature NOT learn at the cottage?","o":["How to speak French","The history of empires","How life is made","How to read and write"],"a":2,"e":"He learns speech, letters, history and society. Creation stays Victor's secret."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Who wrote the journal that tells the creature of his origin?","o":["Clerval","Waldman","Victor","Walton"],"a":2,"e":"He found Victor's papers in the coat taken from the laboratory."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Language","q":"Which repeated word does Victor use for his creature more than any other?","o":["Son","Fiend","Child","Companion"],"a":1,"e":"'Fiend' appears again and again, sometimes alongside 'dæmon' and 'wretch'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Victor calls himself 'the true murderer'. Who agrees in the novel?","o":["No one else","Kirwin","Elizabeth","Alphonse"],"a":0,"e":"Everyone either doubts him or thinks him delirious."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which Romantic idea does the creature's early life illustrate?","o":["The superiority of cities","The innocent 'natural man'","The divine right of kings","Science as salvation"],"a":1,"e":"Like Rousseau's natural man, he begins good and is corrupted by society."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Which philosopher's 'natural man' is born good and corrupted by society?","o":["Descartes","Hobbes","Locke","Rousseau"],"a":3,"e":"The creature's story echoes Rousseau's idea; Hobbes thought natural life was 'nasty, brutish, and short'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Mary Shelley's parents argued for which cause?","o":["Strict religion","Reform and women's rights","Slavery","Royal power"],"a":1,"e":"Godwin and Wollstonecraft were radicals; the novel questions injustice and women's roles."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"How old was Mary Shelley when she began the novel?","o":["Thirty","Fourteen","Twenty-five","Eighteen"],"a":3,"e":"She was 18 in summer 1816 and 20 at publication."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"At which villa on Lake Geneva did the ghost-story contest happen?","o":["Villa Lavenza","Villa Borghese","Villa Diodati","Villa Medici"],"a":2,"e":"Byron rented Villa Diodati; the novel's Villa Lavenza is fictional, on Como."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Which poet challenged the group to write ghost stories?","o":["Polidori","Coleridge","Lord Byron","Percy Shelley"],"a":2,"e":"Byron proposed 'We will each write a ghost story'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What modern issue is the novel often used to discuss?","o":["Climate change","Social media","Space travel","Scientific responsibility"],"a":3,"e":"Cloning, AI, genetic engineering: making life without caring for it."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which statement best sums up the novel's view of science?","o":["Powerful; needs responsibility","Less important than art","Always good","Always evil"],"a":0,"e":"Science isn't condemned outright; Victor's failure of care is."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Close reading","q":"'If I cannot inspire love, I will cause fear.' Which theme does this best show?","o":["Science breeds evil","Nature heals","Rejection breeds violence","Love conquers all"],"a":2,"e":"His violence is a response to being refused love."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Why might Mary Shelley give the creature such an eloquent voice?","o":["To fill space","To win the reader's sympathy","To mock Victor","To show he is human"],"a":1,"e":"Readers hear his reasoning in his own words, so they judge him differently from the characters who only see him."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Why is it important that Walton sees the creature himself?","o":["It makes Walton the hero","It confirms Victor's tale","It shows Walton is mad","It explains the ice"],"a":1,"e":"'The apparition of the monster seen from our ship' supports the story."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Which chapters form the creature's narrative?","o":["11 to 16","1 to 6","5 to 10","17 to 22"],"a":0,"e":"He begins in Chapter 11 ('original era of my being') and ends with his demand in 16."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"Victor's narrative is told to whom?","o":["Walton","Elizabeth","Margaret","The creature"],"a":0,"e":"Victor tells Walton, who writes it down for Margaret."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What makes the creature's crimes different from Victor's?","o":["He knows he is doing wrong","They are legal","They are forgiven","They are accidental"],"a":0,"e":"He commits them deliberately; Victor's crime is neglect."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which character changes most across the novel?","o":["The creature","Ernest","Alphonse","Kirwin"],"a":0,"e":"From innocent newborn to scholar to murderer to self-condemning mourner."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Close reading","q":"'The fallen angel becomes a malignant devil.' Which earlier hope does this end?","o":["To marry","To be human","To be famous","To be Adam"],"a":3,"e":"He wanted to be 'thy Adam' but became 'rather the fallen angel'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Why does Victor stay silent while Justine is tried?","o":["He believes she is guilty","The judges forbid him to speak","He has forgotten the creature","He'd be thought mad"],"a":3,"e":"He says his tale 'would have been looked upon as the ravings of a madman'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which two characters both carry a secret burden alone?","o":["Victor and the creature","Felix and Safie","Clerval and Walton","Elizabeth and Justine"],"a":0,"e":"Both are cut off from human sympathy."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"Where does Victor build his first creature?","o":["A Geneva cellar","A Paris attic","An Orkney hut","An Ingolstadt garret"],"a":3,"e":"'A solitary chamber, or rather cell, at the top of the house.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Which professor sneers at Victor's old alchemy books?","o":["Agrippa","Waldman","Kirwin","Krempe"],"a":3,"e":"M. Krempe: 'Every instant that you have wasted on those books is utterly and entirely lost.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"Which three writers fascinate the young Victor?","o":["Volney, Rousseau, Voltaire","Agrippa, Paracelsus, Albertus Magnus","Newton, Locke, Galvani","Milton, Plutarch, Goethe"],"a":1,"e":"He reads these alchemists before modern science 'destroyed all my visions'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Allusion","q":"'Paradise Lost', 'Plutarch's Lives' and 'Werter'. Who reads all three?","o":["Clerval","The creature","Walton","Victor"],"a":1,"e":"He finds them in a leather portmanteau in the woods."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What makes the creature a 'Romantic hero' to some readers?","o":["Faith and obedience","Wealth and noble birth","Science and reason","Passion, isolation and rebellion"],"a":3,"e":"Like Byron's heroes and Milton's Satan, he is a solitary outcast in revolt."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"What does the novel suggest about revenge?","o":["It destroys the avenger","It is rare","It brings peace","It is just"],"a":0,"e":"Both Victor and the creature are consumed and destroyed by vengeance."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which pair end the novel alone with each other in the Arctic?","o":["Victor and the creature","Felix and Safie","Elizabeth and Justine","Walton and Margaret"],"a":0,"e":"Creator and creature, locked together in the ice."}],"ov":{"context":[["Author","Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley (1797–1851). Daughter of feminist Mary Wollstonecraft and philosopher William Godwin; married to poet Percy Bysshe Shelley."],["Written","Begun June 1816 at Lake Geneva during a ghost-story contest with Lord Byron and John Polidori, in the cold, dark 'Year Without a Summer'. Mary was 18."],["Published","1818 (anonymously). Revised edition 1831 — the version in this audiobook (Elizabeth is adopted, not a cousin)."],["Genre","Gothic novel, Romantic novel, and often called the first science-fiction novel."],["Subtitle","'The Modern Prometheus' — the Titan who stole fire from the gods for humans and was eternally punished. Victor seizes the 'spark' of life."],["Science behind it","Galvanism (Galvani's twitching frog legs; Aldini's electrified corpse, 1803), debates over 'vitalism', and the rise of modern chemistry."],["Big influences","Milton's Paradise Lost, Coleridge's 'Rime of the Ancient Mariner', Rousseau (natural goodness), Locke (the mind as a blank slate), Wollstonecraft (women's education)."]],"structure":"Three nested narrators, like boxes inside boxes: Robert Walton's letters to his sister Margaret (frame) → Victor Frankenstein's story told to Walton (Ch. 1–10, 17–24) → the Creature's story told to Victor (Ch. 11–16, the heart of the book). Each narrator has reasons to shape the truth, so ask who is speaking and why.","summaries":[["Letters 1–4","Walton sails north seeking the pole and glory, lonely for a friend. His crew sees a giant on a sledge, then rescues a dying stranger — Victor — who agrees to tell his story as a warning."],["Chapter 1","Victor's privileged Geneva childhood. His father rescues and marries Caroline Beaufort; they adopt Elizabeth, whom Caroline calls a 'present' for Victor."],["Chapter 2","Harmonious childhood with Elizabeth and Clerval. Victor discovers the alchemists (Agrippa, Paracelsus) and sees lightning destroy an oak."],["Chapter 3","Caroline dies of scarlet fever. Victor goes to Ingolstadt; Krempe mocks his alchemy, but Waldman's lecture fires his ambition to 'unfold the deepest mysteries of creation'."],["Chapter 4","Two years of obsessive study. Victor discovers the secret of life and builds an eight-foot being from charnel-house parts, neglecting health, nature, and family."],["Chapter 5","November night: the Creature wakes. Victor flees in horror, has a nightmare of Elizabeth turning into his mother's corpse, and collapses into a months-long fever; Clerval nurses him."],["Chapter 6","Elizabeth's letter introduces Justine. Victor recovers, avoids science, studies languages with Clerval, and is happy on a spring walking tour."],["Chapter 7","William is murdered. Returning to Geneva in a storm, Victor glimpses the Creature and knows it's the killer — but stays silent. Justine is accused."],["Chapter 8","Justine's trial. Elizabeth defends her; Victor says nothing. Justine falsely confesses under pressure and is executed."],["Chapter 9","Victor sinks into guilt and isolation, considers suicide, and seeks comfort in the Alps at Chamounix."],["Chapter 10","On the Mer de Glace, the Creature confronts Victor: 'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.' Victor agrees to hear his story."],["Chapter 11","The Creature's earliest memories: senses, cold, fire, rejection by villagers. He hides in a hovel beside the De Lacey cottage."],["Chapter 12","He watches the poor, loving De Laceys, stops stealing their food, secretly helps them, discovers language, and is horrified by his reflection."],["Chapter 13","Safie arrives. The Creature learns language and history alongside her and realizes he has no family, money, or place in society."],["Chapter 14","The De Laceys' backstory: exiled for rescuing Safie's father, who then betrayed them. Safie defied him to find Felix."],["Chapter 15","He reads Paradise Lost, Plutarch, and Werter; finds Victor's journal. He wins blind De Lacey's kindness — then Felix beats him away."],["Chapter 16","The family flees; he burns the cottage, is shot after saving a girl, kills William, frames Justine, and demands a female companion."],["Chapter 17","Victor refuses, then — moved by the Creature's arguments — agrees, if the pair will leave Europe forever."],["Chapter 18","Victor delays, then travels to England with Clerval (Rhine journey). The wedding to Elizabeth will follow his return."],["Chapter 19","Travels through London, Oxford, the Lakes, and Scotland. Victor leaves Clerval and builds the female on a bleak Orkney island."],["Chapter 20","Fearing a 'race of devils', Victor destroys the female. The Creature vows, 'I shall be with you on your wedding-night.' Victor drifts to Ireland and is arrested."],["Chapter 21","The murder victim is Clerval. Victor falls ill in prison, is visited by his father, and is acquitted."],["Chapter 22","Elizabeth's letter; they return to Geneva and marry. Victor arms himself, believing he is the target."],["Chapter 23","On the wedding night the Creature strangles Elizabeth. Alphonse dies of grief. Victor, called mad, vows revenge."],["Chapter 24","Victor chases the Creature to the Arctic and dies on Walton's ship. Walton turns back. The Creature mourns Victor and leaves to burn himself at the pole."]],"characters":[["Victor Frankenstein","Brilliant, ambitious Genevan scientist who creates life then abandons it. Secretive, self-pitying, blames 'destiny'. Tragic hero (or villain?)."],["The Creature","Never named. Born gentle and curious; learns language and literature; turned violent by rejection. Eloquent, intelligent, and the novel's moral challenge."],["Robert Walton","Arctic explorer and frame narrator; Victor's double. His decision to turn back is the lesson Victor never learned."],["Elizabeth Lavenza","Victor's adopted 'more than sister' and bride. Kind, brave (defends Justine), but largely passive. Killed on her wedding night."],["Henry Clerval","Victor's best friend; loves languages, poetry, and people. Represents a healthy, balanced mind. Murdered in Ireland."],["Alphonse Frankenstein","Victor's loving, respected father. Dies of grief after Elizabeth's death."],["William Frankenstein","Victor's young brother; the Creature's first victim."],["Justine Moritz","Servant raised by the Frankensteins; framed for William's murder and executed after a coerced confession."],["Caroline Beaufort","Victor's mother; dies of scarlet fever nursing Elizabeth."],["M. Krempe / M. Waldman","Ingolstadt professors: Krempe blunt and 'repulsive'; Waldman gentle, inspiring Victor's turn to chemistry."],["The De Laceys","Old blind father, Felix, and Agatha — exiled French family the Creature watches and loves."],["Safie","Arabian woman who travels alone to find Felix; the Creature learns language alongside her."],["Mr. Kirwin","Kind Irish magistrate who treats Victor fairly."]],"themes":[["Dangerous knowledge & ambition","Victor and Walton pursue glory past all limits. 'Learn from me… how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge.'"],["Creator's responsibility","Victor gives life but no love or care. 'I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel.'"],["Nature vs. nurture","The Creature is made good and turned evil by rejection. 'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.'"],["Isolation & the need for companionship","Walton wants a friend; Victor isolates himself; the Creature begs for a mate. 'I am malicious because I am miserable.'"],["Appearance & prejudice","Everyone (except the blind De Lacey) judges the Creature by his face. 'A fatal prejudice clouds their eyes.'"],["Revenge","Revenge consumes both creator and creature until they are mirror images in the Arctic."],["Nature & the sublime","Mountains, glaciers, and storms heal, terrify, and judge. Nature restores the characters but punishes those who try to master it."],["Justice & injustice","Justine and the De Laceys suffer for crimes they didn't commit; Victor's silence lets injustice happen."]],"symbols":[["Fire / light","Knowledge and its danger (Prometheus). Warms the Creature, burns him, and later destroys the cottage."],["Lightning","Nature's power and the 'spark' of life — the blasted oak and Victor's own ruin."],["Ice / the Arctic","Isolation, emotional coldness, the limits of ambition; frames the whole novel."],["Paradise Lost","Creation, rebellion, and the Fall — Victor as God/Satan, the Creature as Adam/Satan."],["The miniature of Caroline","Lost motherly love and the false evidence that condemns Justine."],["Eyes & seeing","The Creature's 'dull yellow eye', the blind De Lacey who 'sees' truly, judgment by sight."],["The moon","Watches over key moments — the Creature's birth, his first nights, the window at Elizabeth's death."]],"essays":[["Who is the real monster — Victor or the Creature?","Use Victor's abandonment (Ch. 5), his silence (Ch. 7–8), and the Creature's speeches (Ch. 10, 17, 24)."],["How does Shelley use the nested narrative structure?","Consider reliability, sympathy, and how the Creature's voice sits at the center."],["Is Victor punished for seeking knowledge, or for failing his responsibilities?","Compare Ch. 4's warning with the Creature's 'parental' claims and Victor's last words."],["How does the novel explore isolation?","Trace Walton's letters, Victor's lab, the Creature's hovel, and the Arctic ending."],["What role do women play in Frankenstein?","Elizabeth, Justine, Caroline, Safie, and the destroyed female — passive victims or quiet critiques?"],["How does nature function in the novel?","The sublime Alps, the seasons Victor ignores, storms, and ice."]]}};
